\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Why Some New College Graduates Have Seen Their Job Offers Disappear\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Brian Cheung \par \uc2 539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Unemployment is near 50-year lows, but some employers are getting worried about a slowing economy and taking on new hires. And it`s falling on the newest members of the workforce with some college grads losing job offers after they accept them. Brian Cheung has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHEUNG, NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND DATA REPORTER (voiceover): Weeks after graduating from the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 , Loyana Nobhaan`s (ph) bags are packed to move across the country for her first ever job at a tech company in San Diego.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, I was just mostly excited to get started with my career.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (voiceover): But just days before graduating, the company that hired her said it was pulling her job offer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hearing that I was going to have to go back to the drawing board and re-find a job was, you know, obviously it`s really disheartening, especially being so close to my college graduation.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (voiceover): Nobhaan is among a group of young job seekers increasingly sharing stories about landing jobs only to have those job offers rescinded. Rescinded offer comments have risen 50 percent from last year on Fishbowl, a networking platform operated by job site \uc2 Glassdoor\uc2 . Companies like \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  have acknowledged pulling job offers as they also lay workers off with concern about a slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL ZHAO, LEAD ECONOMIST, \uc2 GLASSDOOR\uc2 : It`s largely a reflection of what we`re seeing in the broader job market. So, this tends to be more white- collar related roles and it tends to be interns or new grads.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (voiceover): \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  says hiring for entry-level jobs requiring a college degree is down 45 percent this year. And some with firm offers are being told to wait to start. A study from intelligent.com says 28 percent of business school grads have had their start dates pushed to next year. Experts advice to grads nervous about those early offers, don`t stop job searching, research the company`s financial standing, and cast a wide net.\par \par \uc2 ZHAO: Make sure that the skills you`re working on are transferable to other industries or other jobs.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (voiceover): Nobhaan is moving to San Diego anyway for a fresh start.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Pick up a way to sing job somewhere or maybe just take some time to kind of pivot and really figure out what I want to do.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (voiceover): Not the new start she was hoping for, but a new start nonetheless.\par \par \uc2 Brian Cheung, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: When we come back here tonight, there are trailblazers and then there`s Rue McKenrick. We join him on his epic trek around the country to inspire America.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230607ej66000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for June 6, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Dana Griffin, Molly Hunter, Emilie Ikeda, Miguel  Almaguer, Tom Costello, Gabe Gutierrez, Meagan Fitzgerald, Brian Cheung,  Gadi Schwartz \par \uc2 3,923 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Just breaking tonight, late details in a mass shooting at a high school graduation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): Shots ringing out on the VCU College campus near a high school graduation. Witnesses saying they got down on the ground as people started running. The investigation just beginning tonight.\par \par \uc2 The catastrophe unfolding in Ukraine, a critical dam destroyed, putting hundreds of thousands in danger. The new fears about the potential impact on a nearby nuclear plant with Russia and Ukraine trading blame for the blast.\par \par \uc2 Plus, air quality alerts across the U.S., 100 million at risk. Why it`s so bad out there. And what you need to know to stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Golf world shake up. A merger blindsiding top players and fans alike as the PGA unites with its bitter rival. But will all be forgiven as backlash blows across the globe?\par \par \uc2 Prince Harry makes history taking the stand in a high-stakes trail against British tabloids. His riveting testimony and what he said about his mother, Princess Diana.\par \par \uc2 Chris Christie`s campaign kick-off. The one-time Trump loyalist laying into the former president, as former vice president \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  readies to get in the race.\par \par \uc2 Despite a red-hot job market, why some employers are pulling job offers already promised to new college grads.\par \par \uc2 And taking the scenic route. One man`s walk around America inspiring us along the way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening and welcome everyone. It has happened again. We are monitoring what appears to be another mass shooting. This one as students and their loved ones who are gathering for a high school graduation in Richmond, Virginia on the campus of \uc2 Virginia Commonwealth University\uc2 . Police say there are multiple injuries.\par \par \uc2 Dana Griffin is following the story and joins me now. What`s the latest, Dana?\par \par \uc2 DANA GRIFFIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Lester, we understand a high school graduation ceremony was underway at the theater on the corner of the VCU campus. Now, the public schools there says that that`s when the shooting happened outside of that theater. Richmond police are only reporting multiple injuries at this point. We do not know the extent of those injuries.\par \par \uc2 Now, people inside say it sounded like fireworks and everyone started running. And alert to students on the VCU campus says there is no ongoing threat to the community. All Richmond public schools will be close tomorrow as this investigation begins. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Dana Griffin leading us off tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Ukraine, the apparent purposeful destruction of a major dam has left up to 80 towns at risk from deep and fast-moving floodwaters. The attack has already forced 16,000 people from their homes. Ukraine`s president calling it a war crime, but Russia accusing Ukraine for the attack. It comes amid signs Ukraine is back on the offensive. Molly Hunter is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY HUNTER, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, water still racing through the Kakhovka Dam in southern Ukraine. Entire neighborhoods in Kherson flooded. Rescuers evacuating more than 1,000 people. When the dam exploded this morning, dramatic videos showing the entire middle section swallowed up by the Dnipro River. These satellite images showing the devastating aftermath.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has intelligence that is leaning toward Russia as the perpetrator of the attack, according to two U.S. officials and one Western official, but no final determination yet.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS COORDINATOR, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: We cannot say conclusively what happened at this point. What we absolutely can say is that the damage to the Ukrainian people and to the region will be significant.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER (voiceover): Ukraine pointing the finger directly at Moscow. President Zelensky calling it a terrorist attack and a war crime, saying tonight up to 80 Ukrainian villages could be at risk. Russian state media first denied, then blamed Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: The Kakhovka Dam is one of six dams on the Dnipro River. It provides vital drinking water, it provides power, and it provides water for the cooling pools at the \uc2 Zaporizhzhia Nuclear Power Plant\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HUNTER (voiceover): One analyst estimates some 300,000 civilians could be without clean drinking water by tonight, and some 500,000 will be affected by power outages and flooding. Tonight, Ukraine`s nuclear energy chief telling NBC News the Zaporizhzhia Plant occupied by Russia has enough water for now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There is no immediate territory in there. The level is normal. Their presence inside, and this is my biggest concern. They just showed us today what they can do. And they say do not stop.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER (voiceover): Meanwhile, tonight, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports that three months before the attack on Russia`s Nord Stream gas pipeline, the Biden administration learned from a close ally that the Ukrainian military had planned a covert attack targeting it. The paper citing multiple officials familiar with the matter, which does not say if the plan was carried out. NBC News has not confirmed the report. Ukraine has previously denied involvement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:05:13]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Molly, if we could circle back to the attack of the dam. I know Ukraine believes Russia was trying to derail its long-awaited effort to take back Ukrainian territory from Russia.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER (on camera): Lester, that`s right. Ukrainian officials saying tonight that they believe Russia was attempting to cut off that southern route for Ukrainian troops. But President Zelensky, very clear tonight, saying the attack did not hinder any counter-offensive plans. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Molly Hunter in Ukraine, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning down to the air we breathe, with 100 million people at risk under air quality alerts today, blamed on pollution and even smoke from wildfires hundreds of miles away. Emilie Ikeda has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, hazy skies are blanketing wide swaths of the country, from Boston to Philadelphia to New York City, where you can barely make out the skyline.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m coughing, chest is tight, asthma.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): And near Syracuse, an eerily yellow tin. In the south and Midwest, 35 million under air quality alerts as a result of high concentrations of ozone or pollutants paired with heat. And in more than a dozen states, 65 million facing poor air quality due to smoke from out-of- control blazes in Canada. Already this year, nine million acres torched there.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TRUDEAU\uc2 , PRIME MINISTER, CANADA: This is a scary time for a lot of people, not just in Alberta, but right across the country. Smoke from those towering flames, drifting hundreds of miles into the U.S. and raising alarm bells among medical experts.\par \par \uc2 PETER DECARLO, ENVIRONMENTAL HEALTH AND ENGINEERING ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR, \uc2 JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY\uc2 : When we`re breathing wildfire smoke, that smoke is getting into our lungs, and they may feel irritation, a tingling sensation in their throat. Sometimes their eyes will tear.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): Experts say poor air quality can be hazardous to anyone, but especially seniors, children, and those with respiratory conditions. The health risk posed by wildfire smoke continues to worsen in the U.S., as the number of people experiencing at least one day of unhealthy air quality has increased 27-fold over the last decade. To stay safe --\par \par \uc2 DECARLO: Limit the time you spend outdoors and limit the amount of physical activity, the strenuous physical activity that you`re doing.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): As the threat continues this week, all eyes remain on the sky.\par \par \uc2 Emilie Ikeda, NBC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Now to a seismic shakeup for the golf world tonight. The PGA uniting with its bitter rival, Saudi-backed LIV Golf. Miguel Almaguer now on why the fallout from the new partnership goes far beyond the fairway.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Striking a deal that blindsided the sports world, today`s merger between the PGA and Saudi-backed LIV Golf transcends the game. Some of the sport`s biggest names joined LIV just a year ago, launching a bitter rivalry on the fairways, pitting top players against each other, creating geopolitical tension, dividing fans, and triggering a slew of lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TIRICO, NBC SPORTS COMMENTATOR: I think the golf world, including the biggest names in the sport, was stunned. I don`t think anybody saw this coming.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Why did it happen?\par \par \uc2 TIRICO: Money. At the end of the day, money. And discovering down the line that was ahead with lawsuits that were pending for both sides.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voiceover): With billions of dollars to be invested by Saudi Arabia, often criticized for human rights violations, \uc2 Yasir Al-Rumayyan\uc2  becomes chairman of the new entity.\par \par \uc2 JAY MONAHAN\uc2 , COMMISSIONER, PGA TOUR: We`ve recognized that together we can have a far greater impact on this game than we can working apart.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voiceover): For PGA Commissioner \uc2 Jay Monahan\uc2 , a dramatic reversal from a year ago when tour players were leaving for lucrative deals with LIV, angering 9/11 families.\par \par \uc2 MONAHAN: I would ask any player that has left or any player that would ever consider leaving, have you ever had to apologize for being a member of the PGA Tour?\par \par \uc2 JIMMY ROBERTS, NBC SPORTS COMMENTATOR: I don`t know the answer to how palatable this will ultimately be, but you know, there are a lot of people who aren`t going to be happy about it. But I think it was really damaging to the game of golf the way the situation existed for the last, what was it, year and a half.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voiceover): Star players like Phil Mickelson and Brooks Koepka joined LIV and were suspended from the PGA.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS KOEPKA, PROFESSIONAL GOLFER: I understand I`m going to get (BLEEP) for it. And I`m totally cool with that.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (voiceover): Tonight it appears they`ll be welcome back. The news cheered by some players like Mickelson while others caught off guard and angry. The question now, will fans embrace or tee off on the new tour?\par \par \uc2 Miguel Almaguer, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We are learning more tonight about the passengers and pilot aboard that doomed private jet that crashed Sunday in a Virginia forest, including the unusual connection of mom and her baby on board had with the couple who own the aircraft. Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The tragic end to Sunday`s private plane crash is only compounded with the details of the victims who died. The owners of the plane, John and Barbara Rumpel of Florida tell NBC News they had adopted 49-year-old Adina Azarian when she was 40 because she reminded them of another daughter they`d lost in a scuba accident.\par \par \uc2 Azarian was headed home to New York with her 2-year-old child, Aria, and their nanny, Evadnie Smith. The question, why did the pilot, Jeff Hefner, fail to respond to air traffic controllers within 15 minutes of departing, then fly for two hours, presumably on autopilot? The F-16 fighter jets that intercepted Hefner`s plane could see he was slumped over and unresponsive. Did he suffer a medical emergency? Was he rendered unconscious due to a sudden loss of cabin pressure? And were his passengers also incapacitated?\par \par \uc2 No one radioed a Mayday call, and the fighter pilots reported the passenger window shades were down. Hefner was a retired \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  captain.\par \par \uc2 JOHN COX, NBC NEWS AVIATION ANALYST: I knew him personally.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voiceover): NBC News Aviation Analyst John Cox says Hefner should have received a cockpit warning if the cabin was losing pressure.\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : At 14,000 feet, you will automatically get the deployment of oxygen mask.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voiceover): Without oxygen, hypoxia can quickly set in, headaches, and nausea, trouble concentrating, then loss of consciousness. For a second day, investigators today have been inspecting and documenting the crash scene in a remote and rugged Virginia forest.\par \par \uc2 ADAM GERHARDT, SENIOR AVIATION ACCIDENT INVESTIGATOR, NATIONAL TRANSPORT SAFETY BOARD: The airplane, pilot qualifications, maintenance records, basically, everything is on the table.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): Investigators plan to use a helicopter to remove the wreckage within a few days. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Tom Costello, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  is now the latest former ally of President Trump to openly take him on for the Republican nomination. Gabe Gutierrez has late details on a big field getting more crowded.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  making it official, kicking off his White House bid in New Hampshire. Back in 2016, Christie endorsed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: There is no better fighter than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): But now, the brash former New Jersey governor is a fierce critic, arguing he`s the strongest to take on Trump and slamming other Republicans for not doing the same.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE: They kind of cozy up to him. They hope that he implodes and that if they`re nice to him, that they`ll inherit his voters.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): He joins a growing GOP field, 10 Republican candidates by the end of this week, with former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  expected to announce tomorrow, with former President Trump remains the clear front runner.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They`re at one percent. Some are at zero. I hear Chris Christie`s coming in. What`s the purpose? And he`s polling at zero.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): But some New Hampshire Republicans tell us they`re looking for a Trump alternative.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Would you prefer \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Right now, I`m afraid yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Why?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Too many issues where he`s too self-absorbed.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): Others backing the former president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I like everything he`d done about the wall. I love immigrants, but I just like them coming in legally.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): Trump`s top rival, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , today touting his economic record in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: In Washington, they`re adding trillions of more in debt. In Florida, we`re running budget surpluses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  is staking his campaign here in New Hampshire, but he faces an uphill battle. The growing GOP field may end up giving Trump an advantage. The Christie campaign tells us that it plans to engage Trump and act as a counter-punch. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe Gutierrez in New Hampshire, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 Seconds, Prince Harry`s history-making testimony. What he said about the tabloids, his privacy, and Princess Diana?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back now with Prince Harry`s historic testimony in a London courtroom today, lashing out of the tabloids, he says, have invaded his privacy for most of his life. Meagan Fitzgerald was there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEAGAN FITZGERALD, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, a media frenzy outside of London courthouse as Prince Harry arrived. He`s the first senior British Royal to testify in more than 130 years, taking on mirror group newspapers, one of several tabloid media outlets he and wife Meghan accused of invading their privacy and gathering information unlawfully.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:17]\par \par \uc2 The Duke of Sussex came out swinging in the tenth courtroom, describing what he calls incredibly invasive media coverage since he was a child. And in a written statement, alleging journalists and editors had blood on their hands. That constant tabloid intrusion, inciting of hatred and harassment led to his and Meghan`s decision to flee to the U.S., and that journalists had hacked his mother`s voicemail, which he said was vile and made him physically sick.\par \par \uc2 But the newspapers attorney hit back, exposing gaps in Harry`s memory. The prince admitting he couldn`t recall if he had even read some of the articles he said caused him distress in his written statement. The attorney denied mere journalists hacked Harry or Diana`s phone and said articles about the prince were based on legal sources like Royal press releases, tips from those close to him, and information from the public.\par \par \uc2 Soft spoken, Harry remained calm, though at times appeared visibly flustered during hours of intense interrogation.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: How did it go in court for Harry today?\par \par \uc2 DAISY MCANDREW, NBC NEWS ROYAL CONTRIBUTOR: I think given that this was a more aggressive form of questioning, he will be quite pleased with how he performed. He did keep his composure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD (on camera): The prince is expected to be back in court again tomorrow in what should be his last day on the stand. Lester?\par \par \uc2 All right, Meagan Fitzgerald, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a lot more to tell you about. Still ahead, the serious warning for parents about a popular baby lounger. And later, while some companies are struggling to find workers, why some new college graduates have seen their job offers disappear.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back with a disturbing update for parents after at least 10 deaths were linked to a popular baby lounger since 2015. In 2021, the \uc2 CPSC\uc2  recalled more than three million Boppy loungers after eight infant deaths. NBC News recently did an investigation into these types of baby loungers and found they were associated with more than twice as many deaths than federal regulators previously announced.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment is near 50-year lows, but some employers are getting worried about a slowing economy and taking on new hires. And it`s falling on the newest members of the workforce with some college grads losing job offers after they accept them. Brian Cheung has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHEUNG, NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND DATA REPORTER (voiceover): Weeks after graduating from the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 , Loyana Nobhaan`s (ph) bags are packed to move across the country for her first ever job at a tech company in San Diego.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, I was just mostly excited to get started with my career.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (voiceover): But just days before graduating, the company that hired her said it was pulling her job offer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hearing that I was going to have to go back to the drawing board and re-find a job was, you know, obviously it`s really disheartening, especially being so close to my college graduation.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (voiceover): Nobhaan is among a group of young job seekers increasingly sharing stories about landing jobs only to have those job offers rescinded. Rescinded offer comments have risen 50 percent from last year on Fishbowl, a networking platform operated by job site \uc2 Glassdoor\uc2 . Companies like \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  have acknowledged pulling job offers as they also lay workers off with concern about a slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL ZHAO, LEAD ECONOMIST, \uc2 GLASSDOOR\uc2 : It`s largely a reflection of what we`re seeing in the broader job market. So, this tends to be more white- collar related roles and it tends to be interns or new grads.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (voiceover): \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  says hiring for entry-level jobs requiring a college degree is down 45 percent this year. And some with firm offers are being told to wait to start. A study from intelligent.com says 28 percent of business school grads have had their start dates pushed to next year. Experts advice to grads nervous about those early offers, don`t stop job searching, research the company`s financial standing, and cast a wide net.\par \par \uc2 ZHAO: Make sure that the skills you`re working on are transferable to other industries or other jobs.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (voiceover): Nobhaan is moving to San Diego anyway for a fresh start.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Pick up a way to sing job somewhere or maybe just take some time to kind of pivot and really figure out what I want to do.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (voiceover): Not the new start she was hoping for, but a new start nonetheless.\par \par \uc2 Brian Cheung, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: When we come back here tonight, there are trailblazers and then there`s Rue McKenrick. We join him on his epic trek around the country to inspire America.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, tonight, we`re taking the scenic route around the entire perimeter of the United States. Gadi Schwartz with how one conservationist is blazing his own trail and inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GADI SCHWARTZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): To say ultra hiker, Rue McKenrick, has a thirst for adventure would be an understatement.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Do you run across a lot of like dangerous animals?\par \par \uc2 RUE MCKENRICK, HIKER: I had a mountain lion that was continuing to come into camp and I felt the need at some point just to pack up and walk through the night.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, hold on. Hold on. You decided to pack up and then walk into the darkness?\par \par \uc2 MCKENRICK: Yes, I felt like I`d make it on my terms.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voiceover): But that is the price you pay when you have a dream as big as his. You see, Rue is on a mission to pave the way for the greatest trail America has ever seen. A 14,000-mile American Perimeter Trail. Think the Pacific Crest Trail made famous by Reese Witherspoon in Wild.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh my God. What have I done?\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voiceover): But the APT would be over five times longer, circumnavigating the entire U.S. A track that took him three years to complete the first time he did.\par \par \uc2 MCKENRICK: In some ways, I got the easy part out of the way already.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Walking all around the United States.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voiceover): The hard part now, doing it again to lay the groundwork for what he hopes could be a national trail system in the future.\par \par \uc2 MCKENRICK: We have a highway system, and you can drive across the country if you -- if you like. I think it`s reasonable that we also have trails that mimic that kind of system.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: To connect us.\par \par \uc2 MCKENRICK: To connect us.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voiceover): And when it finally came time for us to part ways, the sheer magnitude of his dream came into view.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: I`ve never ended an interview like this, but I guess this could buy, right?\par \par \uc2 MCKENRICK I think this is the end of the road.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: I`m headed to the car. You`re literally headed to Oregon.\par \par \uc2 MCKENRICK: Yes, I`m just going to start walking that way. Maybe I`ll beat you there.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Rue McKenrick, a man who doesn`t mind taking the long way home. Gadi Schwartz, NBC News, San Jacinto, California, 900 miles away from Bend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Tuesday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230607ej6600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Just Hours To Go Before Threatened Hollywood Writer Strike, Will Your Favorite Tv Shows Be Halted?\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Miguel Almaguer \par \uc2 536 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: The biggest drama in years is playing out in Hollywood. The industry, bracing for a writer strike just hours from now that could shut down your favorite shows. Miguel Almaguer explains what`s at stake.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Tonight`s suspenseful Hollywood drama unfolding behind the scenes has come down to the wire with just hours left for the Writer`s Guild of America to reach a deal with the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , which represents studios.\par \par \uc2 At odds over streaming residuals, higher pay, and consolidated jobs, the men and women who write for your favorite television shows and movies are set to walk off the job at midnight.\par \par \uc2 CODY BLUE SNIDER, MEMBER, WRITERS GUILD OF AMERICA: Nobody wants a strike, but they have to meet us. They have to meet. We`re not asking for much. We`re asking for just a livable wage and for our jobs to be protected and for us to make residuals on the content we`re creating. If we don`t stand up to these bullies, we`re going to just keep getting bullied our whole lives and nothing`s going to change.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: If pencils go down and picket signs go up, we could see a sequel to the 2007-2008 strike that lasted 100 days, cost an estimated $3 billion, and abruptly cut into show production.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA LITTLETON, CO-EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, VARIETY: If a strike begins this week, the first thing that viewers at home will notice that their favorite late-night shows will go into reruns. If there is a strike, the longer it goes, the more significant the impact, because nothing happens without a script.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: A shutdown trickles down well beyond writers. Production props, even craft service teams would be locked out. That`s tens of thousands of jobs. With negotiations still underway, the studio reps say its priority is the long-term health and stability of the industry.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the clock is ticking on this real-life Hollywood cliffhanger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Miguel joining me here in our Los Angeles studios. You`ve been talking to insiders. Are they surprised that the talks have gone on so late?\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): Not surprised, Lester. These negotiations typically go into the 11th hour, but people that were optimistic are now pessimistic that a deal will be reached. And they worry that if there is a strike, it could last months.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Miguel Almaguer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, rocker John Fogerty back on tour and back playing some of the biggest hits of all time, his own.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230502ej51000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Is Hollywood About To Go On Strike? The Negotiations Going Down To The Wire\b0\par\par\uc2 Kate Snow, Marissa Parra \par \uc2 622 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW: It is a real-life Hollywood suspense story going down to the wire. Members of the Writers Guild of America are threatening to go on strike as early as tomorrow night. Marissa Parra reports on the last-minute negotiations and what a writer`s strike could mean for your favorite shows.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Hollywood is holding its breath as signs of a writers` strike are written across Tinsel Town.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE SANCHEZ-WTIZEL, WGA NEGOTIATING COMMITTEE: We are really prepared to do what we have to get a fair deal.\par \par \uc2 PARRA (voiceover): In a stand-off with the alliance representing the studios they write for, nearly 98 percent of the Writers Guild of America support a strike. Saying, the vote represents collective solidarity and demand for meaningful change.\par \par \uc2 [18:45:00]\par \par \uc2 RAPHAEL BOB-WASBERG, WGA BOARD MEMBER: We want enough money to make a basic living, doing what we love, which is writing. Profits for the companies are up, the cost of living is up, and payment to writers is down.\par \par \uc2 PARRA (voiceover): The \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  have remained largely tight lipped. Saying in statements, their goal continues to be to reach a fair and reasonable agreement. But if they don`t --\par \par \uc2 DOMINIC PATTEN, DEADLINE HOLLYWOOD SENIOR EDITOR: It is going to have a massive ripple effect that you could borderline call a tsunami effect.\par \par \uc2 PARRA (voiceover): It`s not only writers affected. Those in front and behind the camera will likely stand in solidarity, refusing to cross the picket line, bringing production to a grinding halt. Late night TV would feel the impact first, just like the last time writers went on strike 15 years ago when Stephen Colbert famously quipped --\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT, "THE COLBERT REPORT" HOST: I got a problem here, there are no words in my prompter.\par \par \uc2 PARRA (voiceover): Last week, Seth Meyers showing his support.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS, "LATE NIGHT" HOST: What the writers are asking for is not unreasonable.\par \par \uc2 PARRA (voiceover): Writers argue there is a lot on the line.\par \par \uc2 PATTEN: If you want to put a big word up in the sky in flaming letters, it`s streaming.\par \par \uc2 PARRA (voiceover): Streaming has changed, not only how movies and TV are viewed, but how they`re made. Fewer episodes, longer production time, and less pay, which writers say is not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 PATTEN: We can break it down very clearly. More money, more information, more security, and less freebies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PARRA (on camera): And make no mistake, halting Hollywood can be expensive. The last Hollywood strike cost billions. Kate.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Marissa Parra, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We are back in a moment with a new kind of identity theft, using artificial intelligence to clone a singer`s voice. Do you think you can tell the difference?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: More than a dozen college students are recovering from injuries after a roof collapsed last night at a home near \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2  in Columbus. The roof covered a porch and first responders say, it was not strong enough to support all the people who were standing on it, as many as 45 people. Some had to be rescued from under the debris.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230502ej4u000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Positive Signs, A New Move To Avoid That Looming Rail Strike In The Lead Up To Christmas\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Tom Costello \par \uc2 1,248 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:17]\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: And a good Thursday morning. Check it out now, we can say the holiday season is officially underway.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: She looks good back there. And its arrival is coming with encouraging news when it comes to the economy. It is December 1st. This is Today.\par \par \uc2 Positive signs, a new move to avoid that looming rail strike in the lead up to Christmas. This morning, the action now being taken on Capitol Hill and new evidence the push to control record inflation and soaring prices could be working. We`ve got everything you need to know and the impact on your bottom line.\par \par \uc2 Center of the storm, the CEO once hailed as the king of crypto speaks out about the sudden collapse of his company.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAM BANKMAN-FRIED, FORMER FTX CEO: Look, I screwed up like I was CEO. We messed up big.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Straight ahead, his message to investors who lost billions and his response to allegations they were deliberately misled.\par \par \uc2 Holding court, the Prince and Princess of Wales stepping out at the \uc2 NBA\uc2  game as they begin their U.S. tour, a visit being overshadowed by new allegations of racism surrounding the royal family back home. We`ll have the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Save the dates, the holiday shipping frenzy is heating up, just ahead the deadlines you need to know and what you can do to save on all those packages you have to mail.\par \par \uc2 All that, plus, remembering Christine McVie. Tributes pouring in for the star behind some of Fleetwood Mac`s most iconic songs led by the band surviving members, their touching messages as we celebrate McVie`s life and music.\par \par \uc2 And let there be light.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Three, two, one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The Rockefeller Center Christmas tree brought to life during a festive, star-studded celebration, marking the official arrival of the most wonderful time of the year. Today, Thursday, December 1st, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, boy, is it good to see you this Thursday morning. We`re happy to be out here together. But we`re also happy to be here with that lady behind us in all her glory, our tree after the lighting ceremony last night.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, isn`t she lovely, Hoda Kotb? It was an evening of lights and stars filled with truly incredible performances. And there`s always the highlight. Of course, that moment we switch the lights on for the very first time this season.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Loved it. It was beautiful. A lot of folks coming to check out this tree. Meantime, there also appear to be some bright spots when it comes to the nation`s economy as the holiday season is heating up.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: In fact, take a look at these new numbers folks, the economy growing faster than expected last quarter, with a GDP rising to almost 3 percent. Wall Street approving yesterday the Dow up more than 700 points and Americans appear eager to spend nearly 200 million of his shopping over Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But we are keeping our eye on a looming economic threat. That`s a possible railroad workers strike. It couldn`t start next week. It could affect virtually every aspect of the supply chain. NBC`s Tom Costello is inside Studio 1A. He`s got it covered for us. Hey, Tom, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey guys, good morning. So for most of this year, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been waging an aggressive campaign to bring down 40-year high inflation. It`s been rising, raising interest rates at a pace not seen in decades. And of course, that impacts mortgage rates, credit cards, new loans, now new evidence that inflation may be starting to wane. And in the meantime, the possibility of a rail strike now hinges on what Congress does.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): With the holiday season in full swing, this morning, Congress has taken steps to stop that looming rail strike. The House passing a bill that forces an agreement between rail companies and their workers, it now goes to the Senate where it faces a harder path but leaders there, working to pass it quickly to avoid disrupting holiday shipping and potentially rocking the economy, which right now is showing new signs of strength despite record inflation.\par \par \uc2 Revised government stats showed the economy grew faster than expected in the third quarter, with GDP rising at an annual rate of 2.9 percent. And there`s evidence the job market is cooling off. A new labor department survey shows job openings fell in October to 10.3 million from 10.7 million, the previous month. And that`s actually good news because the shortage of workers has been driving up wages and feeding inflation.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:17]\par \par \uc2 Hopeful signs for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  following its aggressive series of interest rate hikes, while it tries to tamp down inflation. Now the Fed Chairman \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  was signaling the central bank will slow the pace of those rate increases this month, from three quarters of a point hike to a half point hike.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , FEDERAL RESERVE CHAIRMAN: It makes sense to moderate the pace of a rate increases as we approach the level of restraint that will be sufficient to bring inflation down.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): That was welcome news on Wall Street with the Dow surging more than 700 points on Wednesday. Still, Chairman Powell says rates will have to remain high for an extended period. If the U.S. manages to avoid an economic downturn, experts say it will be driven by strong consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZANDI\uc2 , CHIEF ECONOMIST AT MOODY`S ANALYTICS: For out there spending, the economy should be able to move forward without an economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): It comes to shoppers are bringing holiday cheer to retailers. \uc2 The National Retail Federation\uc2  says nearly 197 million people flocked to stores from Thanksgiving through Cyber Monday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have shopped like never before I think the prices are really good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And Tom, all of this is happening as we`re seeing gas prices ticked down?\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Yes. And pretty dramatically too, you know, the national average right now on gasoline is now averaging 3.47 a gallon. Yes, we know almost $5 a gallon out in California. But the national down 28 cents in a month. That`s pretty significant. And a year ago we were 3.35. So look where we are right now. It`s really an amazing come down from $5 gas back in June, down to where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Trending the right way. All right, Tom Costello, Tom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Good to see you Tom.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221210eicc00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for December 1, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Peter Alexander, Tom Costello, Sam Brock, Meagan  Fitzgerald, Gabe Gutierrez, Keir Simmons, Priscilla Thompson \par \uc2 3,921 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Breaking news tonight, Congress successfully averting a rail strike that could have crippled the U.S. economy before the holidays.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): The Senate voted to impose a deal on the rail unions a day after it cleared the House but rejecting the paid sick leave for workers that unions wanted. The bill now goes to President Biden for his signature.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, the President hosting his first State Dinner for France`s Emmanuel Macron. What the two leaders said today about Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The major win for the DOJ. A federal appeals court overturning the special master review of the classified documents seized at Mar-a-Lago. But is that the end of the fight.\par \par \uc2 The school bus crashing into a home pinning a car underneath it. Multiple students injured.\par \par \uc2 Police outside the home of Antonio Brown attempting to arrest the former \uc2 NFL\uc2  star. The allegations of domestic violence he faces.\par \par \uc2 William and Kate visiting Boston but facing backlash back home. Our interview with a woman who says William`s godmother grilled her with racist questions.\par \par \uc2 NBC News investigates potential cancer-causing chemicals in a neighborhood in a major U.S. city.\par \par \uc2 And the young athlete beating the odds to become Sports Illustrated`s Sports Kid of the Year.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone. President Biden tonight hailing a Senate vote this afternoon that will steer the country away from the economic blow of a major rail strike. The Senate late today moving to stop a threatened December 9 strike in its tracks a day after the House gave its okay to the bill. It opposes a labor contract previously brokered by the White House. President Biden in a statement saying we have spared this country a Christmas catastrophe in our grocery stores, in our workplaces, and in our communities. But the Congress imposed deal leaving many union members with a bad taste tonight.\par \par \uc2 Peter Alexander is in Washington where the President is also engaging in some high-level foreign diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, as President Biden prepares for the pomp and pageantry of his first State Dinner with a foreign leader, French president \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , a domestic crisis now averted. Late today, the Senate approving a deal to avoid a crippling railroad strike that the White House warned could have single-handedly triggered a recession.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The joint resolution is passed.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The bipartisan bill the first time in 30 years Congress has intervened to stop a rail strike, will force rail union workers to accept an agreement brokered by the White House months ago, including a 24 percent pay raise over five years, but only a single paid sick day. Right now, workers get none. Some unions rejected the deal because of that.\par \par \uc2 Today, the President was pressed why he did not push for more paid sick time.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I negotiated a contract no one else could negotiate. The only thing he was left out was whether or not it was paid leave. You know, I`ve been trying to get paid leave not just for rail workers, for everybody.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Tonight, a Senate vote to include additional paid leave failed. Freight conductor Dan Banks in Ohio says rail crews deserve much more.\par \par \uc2 DAN BANKS, FREIGHT CONDUCTOR: My brothers and sisters in the crew room with me, we`re all just shark`s teeth. One of those falls out, they shove another one right up in the place. There`s no -- it don`t miss a beat.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: On Ukraine, presidents Biden and Macron tonight showing the united front against Russian aggression. Mr. Biden saying, he has no plans to contact Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , but opening the door to a face-to-face meeting.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m prepared to speak with Mr. Putin if in fact there is an interest in him to sign that he`s looking for a way to end the war. He hasn`t done that yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Peter, tonight, some mixed legal headlines for the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Lester, that`s right. In a win for the Justice Department, an appeals court has now ended the special master`s independent review of thousands of documents seized from former President Trump`s Mar- a-Lago estate.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, a setback for President Biden`s proposed student debt forgiveness program. The Supreme Court will not block an injunction. And we`ll hear arguments in February. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Peter Alexander well-guarded tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Some encouraging new economic signs today including healthy spending by Americans heading into the holidays despite high inflation and higher interest rates. And new signs the Fed may ease up on those rate hikes. Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Just over two weeks until Hanukkah, three util Christmas, and the latest data suggests Americans are still spending with 40-year high inflation continuing to ease. October consumer spending just out shows the biggest gain since June even as families pay more for rent food and new cars. Retailers report nearly 200 million people flocked to stores from Thanksgiving through Cyber Monday.\par \par \uc2 In Atlanta, Whitney and Joe Novak own Kazoo Toys and say shoppers are inflation sensitive but still buying.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:16]\par \par \uc2 JOE NOVAK, OWNER, KAZOO TOYS: We`ve had a good year. We`re positive. I mean, we`re keeping people employed. We`re not concerned. Toys is one of the nice industries where we`re not going to have that. that Huge ups and downs roller coaster of (INAUDIBLE), kids are always going to want toys.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Inflation in October running at six percent, slightly lower than September. Fed Chairman \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  now says the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  could soon slow the pace of its interest rate hikes aimed at taming inflation.\par \par \uc2 JAY \\POWELL, CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: The time for moderating the pace of rate increases may come as soon as the December meeting.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: That could mean a half-point rate hike in two weeks rather than another three-quarter-point hike. Meanwhile, gas prices are now averaging $3.47 gallon, down 28 cents and a month. Third quarter GDP even stronger than first reported.\par \par \uc2 STEVE LIESMAN, CNBC SENIOR ECONOMICS REPORTER: We`re kind of like waiting for the recession like they were waiting for Godot. It hasn`t happened yet. And each month that goes by, the economy proved itself to be more resilient. Consumers keep spending.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): Tomorrow we`ll get the November jobs report. 200,000 new jobs expected. Data the Fed will use when deciding whether it really can slow the pace of rate hikes in December. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tom Costello, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A pretty disturbing scene today in a suburb just north of New York City when a school bus crash into a house after hitting two parked cars. Police say the bus driver and seven children were hurt. Most of the injuries were minor, thankfully. Two 5-year-old boys had injuries that were more serious, but not life-threatening.\par \par \uc2 In Tampa, Florida tonight, more trouble for former \uc2 NFL\uc2  star Antonio Brown as police tried to arrest him on a misdemeanor charge stemming from an alleged domestic dispute. Sam Brock has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Tampa Police say they`re trying to arrest former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player Antonio Brown, making multiple attempts outside this home to execute an arrest warrant for battery domestic violence. The department telling NBC News he`s "not communicating with police officers."\par \par \uc2 It`s the latest twist in the 34-year-old`s saga that`s gone well beyond the gridiron. The charges stemming from an incident Monday involving brown and the mother of four of his children. According to court documents, Brown threw a shoe at the woman striking her in the ponytail. The victim had no injuries but believe the shoe was meant for her head."\par \par \uc2 In the same document, police describe learning Brown tossed the woman`s belongings out of the house, and then made threats via speakerphone saying that if she got in the house, he would shoot her in front of police. It also says the kids did not want to go inside because they were afraid of their dad when he gets in these destructive moods.\par \par \uc2 After the incident, a petition to remove Browns guns and ammunition under a risk protection order was rejected by a Florida judge citing lack of physical action.\par \par \uc2 How is it possible there wasn`t enough evidence to take his guns away, but there is enough evidence potentially prosecute him for domestic violence?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PHANG, LEGAL ANALYST: The judge got this one wrong. The Florida statute that allows for these risk protection orders gives a series of factors that can be considered by the court. He met a number of them. Antonio Brown is a risk to not only himself, but to the members of the community, and obviously to the mother of his children.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: According to the petition, he kept two firearms in the house, a handgun and a rifle. NBC News has not been able to reach Brown`s legal team for comment. Tonight, he remains a wanted man by Tampa Police.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Sam, what do we know about what steps police are willing to take in this situation?\par \par \uc2 BROCK (on camera): Yes, Lester, Tampa Police tell me at this point they`re not going to risk officer safety for a misdemeanor arrest warrant. Now, obviously, that could change if Brown escalates the situation. As for the woman and children, police say they are no longer at the home. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Sam Brock, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Anticipation building tonight for Team USA`s match in the Knockout Round of the World Cup on Saturday as the hero of Tuesday`s win speaks about his injury and that next big game. Here`s Meagan Fitzgerald.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: USA!\par \par \uc2 MEAGAN FITZGERALD, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With the countdown on for Team USA`s high stakes game in years. Superstar Christian Pulisic has a message for fans.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN PULISIC, MEMBER, U.S. MEN`S NATIONAL TEAM: I will do everything in my power to you know work with this medical team and make sure that I can -- I can play.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Pulisic was injured during Tuesday`s match against Iran as he scored the winning goal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In the middle, Pulisic scores.\par \par \uc2 PULISIC: The opportunity was there to just beat the defender to the ball. And I was just able to do that and you know paid the price for it a bit.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: That price, a pelvic injury which kept him out of the second half. Pulisic later sharing this picture from the hospital where he anxiously watched the final minutes of the game on a phone.\par \par \uc2 PULISIC: They were checking my -- you know, my blood sugar and everything and I was flying through the roof but it wasn`t because of anything. It`s just me stress watching the game.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Pulisic says he`s on the mend but Team USA says his status is still day-to-day.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:02]\par \par \uc2 GRANT WAHL, SPORTS JOURNALIST, MEADOWLARK MEDIA: I think Christian Pulisic will play in this game this is what he`s been looking forward to his entire life.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: That fighting spirit is exactly Team USA will need in the knockout round.\par \par \uc2 PULISIC: I hope people watching, you know, especially back home in the states can see, wow, these guys are really, you know, giving everything for each other for this country. And that`s what really makes it special.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD (on camera): Team USA will be the underdogs in the game against the Netherlands on Saturday. If they win, it will be the first time the team has advanced to the quarterfinals since 2002. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Meagan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 He was known as the king of crypto, but now, \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  is apologizing for a historic financial collapse as potentially more than a million investors struggle to get their money back. As Gabe Gutierrez reports, Congress is now investigating.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Weeks after its massive cryptocurrency exchange, FTX collapsed. \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  is on an apology tour.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What are your lawyers telling you right now? Are they suggesting this is a good idea for you to be speaking?\par \par \uc2 SAM BANKMAN-FRIED, CEO, FTX: No, they`re very much not.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: But the 30-year-old former billionaire says he had no intent to commit fraud and says management failures and sloppy accounting caused FTX to implode.\par \par \uc2 BANKMAN-FRIED: I screwed up.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: In early November, crypto media outlet CoinDesk reported on potential problems with Bankman-Fried`s other company, Alameda, a hedge fund. Soon, concern investors were demanding their money from FTX causing what amounted to a run on the bank. It forced the exchange into bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 BANKMAN-FRIED: Clearly, I made a lot of mistakes or things I would give anything to be able to do over again. I didn`t ever try to commit fraud on anyone.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Congress is now investigating FTX`s collapse.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEBBIE STABENOW (D-MI): These events uncovered an alarming lack of internal controls and egregious governance failures.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: The key question is whether FTX misappropriated customer funds when it made loans to Bankman-Fried`s hedge fund.\par \par \uc2 BANKMAN-FRIED: I knowingly commingled funds.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: But many of FTX`s investors aren`t buying it.\par \par \uc2 MANNY BAUTISTA, INVESTOR, FTX: Like, he screwed over so many people.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Manny Bautista says he can`t access his assets with FTX valued at about $35,000, and he`s driving for a rideshare service to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 What do you think should happen to him?\par \par \uc2 BAUTISTA: I think he should go to jail. And there should be some way to have users recover their funds.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Bankman-Fried was once estimated to be worth more than $20 billion. He says he`s now down to $100,000 and one credit card. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, we`ll hear from the woman at the center of a royal scandal over racism. What she says about those troubling questions put to her by Prince William`s godmother.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back now with William and Kate`s visit to the U.S. while controversy is raging back home in the U.K. Tonight, the woman who says William`s godmother asked her a series of racist questions during an event is speaking out to our Keir Simmons.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, as the Prince and Princess of Wales visited a Boston nonprofit helping vulnerable young people --\par \par \uc2 NGOZI FULANI, CHARITY EXECUTIVE: I let my guard down.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: British charity executive Ngozi Fulani speaking out about her treatment by Prince William`s godmother at Buckingham Palace event this week.\par \par \uc2 FULANI: I`m in there five minutes when this lady comes up to me. And the first thing she does is take my hair and move it. I`m like I don`t know who she is. But she`s felt already that she has the right to put her hands on me physically to move my hair. So she can see my name but --\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Then she says she was questioned for eight minutes about her ethnicity.\par \par \uc2 FULANI: Lady, I`m bold, yes, and I`m British. Yes, but what`s your nationality?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : A spokesman for Prince William has said, there`s no place for racism in our society. Do you think that`s what this was?\par \par \uc2 FULANI: Do I think or do I know? I know what racism is.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Lady Susan Hussey who was close to Queen Elizabeth has resigned her role as aide to Camilla, Queen Consort.\par \par \uc2 FULANI: Where are am I safe from this? When are we going to confront this kind of behavior instead of excusing it?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: British newspapers calling it a royal disaster. And now, there`s this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No one sees what`s happening behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Harry and Meghan releasing this trailer for their documentary.\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2 , \uc2 DUKE\uc2  OF SUSSEX: I had to do everything that I could to protect my family.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Tonight, we reached out to \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and to the Duke and Duchess` representatives about the timing with no response.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (on camera): Tomorrow, the prince and princess will meet the President, staying focused on their causes, not the controversy. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Keir Simmons, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the volcano danger in Hawaii as lava flows toward a key highway. And a neighborhood where cancer is common, are chemicals above and below ground the cause? Our investigation is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: New images in tonight of the eruption of Mauna Loa. Lava from the world`s largest active volcano now threatening to cut off the main highway on Hawaii`s big island. And there are growing health concerns as the volcano spews ash and acidic gases into the air.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to an NBC News investigation into potential cancer-causing chemicals found in a Houston neighborhood. And residents are fighting to hold one of the largest rail companies responsible. Priscilla Thompson reports.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:09]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LATONYA PAYNE, SON DIED OF LEUKEMIA: He loved to cook and he loved to rap in the shower.\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): LaTonya Payne still clings to memories of her son Corinthian since he died of leukemia last year at just 13 years old.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It was hard. He was coughing up blood from his lungs. He was on dialysis. They tried everything and nothing worked.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Two months after his death, LaTonya was diagnosed with breast cancer for the second time.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Our community is suffering from this cancer cluster.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: In 2019, state health officials found higher rates of certain adult cancers in Houston`s fifth ward. A year later, another bombshell. Children in the area getting leukemia at a rate nearly five times the state average.\par \par \uc2 SYLVESTER TURNER\uc2 , MAYOR, HOUSTON: The second report from the state health department was even more disturbing because that involved the children.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Right. But they never listed a cause.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: They never listed a cause.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: For years, the historically Black and Latino community has blamed the higher rates of cancer on the rate yard now owned by \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  which sits blocks from many homes.\par \par \uc2 Decades ago, workers used creosote to preserve wooden rail ties. That site shut down in 1984, but the chemical believed by the CDC to cause cancer leaked into the soil and groundwater, remaining under more than 100 homes and businesses, according to the \uc2 Texas Department of State Health Services\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Is this environmental racism?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes. Should it be occurring in any community? No.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  declined NBC`s request for an interview but says, over the last 25 years, they`ve implemented remediation efforts in the area, including installing more than 120 wells, removing impacted soil, and monitoring groundwater.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA EDWARDS, FAMILY DIED FROM CANCER: This man here, Mr. Dawn, he died from cancer. This lady here, her husband died from cancer.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Sandra Edwards lost her grandfather to bone cancer a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 EDWARDS: I`ve got a ziploc bag full of obituaries from fifth ward.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: All cancers?\par \par \uc2 EDWARDS: All cancers.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Edwards is a community activist.\par \par \uc2 Why stay?\par \par \uc2 EDWARDS: I have nowhere else to go. This is what I can afford. And my family worked hard to keep this house.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: In September, a significant discovery. The Houston Health Department finding another likely carcinogen in the area, Dioxin, this time on the soil`s surface.\par \par \uc2 LOREN HOPKINS, CHIEF ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENCE OFFICER, HOUSTON HEALTH DEPARTMENT: The fact that we`re finding dioxin on the surface soil where residents can be exposed to it is what`s so alarming.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  says in part the dioxin levels in the city`s samples are well below state requirements and that attributing the contamination solely to their site is unreasonable and inaccurate. Lawyers representing families in the neighborhood say thousands have filed lawsuits against \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  including LaTonya Payne.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Like, compensation is not going to remove the crying at night, but the justice is when the ones at hand are held accountable.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Priscilla Thompson, NBC News Houston.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next, how autism became his superpower and helped a young golfer become SportsKid of the Year.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, he`s in the pages of Sports Illustrated, even has his own clothing line. But for one inspiring young athlete, golf is so much more than a game.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): The game of golf teed up a new world for 11-year-old Carter Bonas.\par \par \uc2 CARTER BONAS, SPORTSKID OF THE YEAR, SPORTS ILLUSTRATED: The first experience playing golf was really amazing, and I just liked hitting the golf ball.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Before he started playing, Carter was struggling. He says he was bullied at school and having trouble engaging socially. Carter has a form of autism.\par \par \uc2 THELMA TENNIE, CARTER`S MOM: Carter`s type of autism is high-functioning Asperger`s. he is able to communicate, but his social skills are not where they should be.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Medical experts say some kids with Asperger`s tend to focus all their energy on one thing at a time. Carter used that to his advantage on the golf course.\par \par \uc2 BONAS: Golf definitely helped me with my anger management. And when I had a bad day, I would just go to the golf course, and that made me feel nice.\par \par \uc2 TENNIE: Golf really did save his life because now he has something to look forward to, something that he was good at.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Carter was just named SportsKid of the Year. He hopes to one day play in the PGA and has his own clothing line. Part of the proceeds, Carter donates to an organization that helps families and children with autism.\par \par \uc2 BONAS: It feels amazing that I can help other kids because I really, really want to help other people if they need help.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A young role model hoping to inspire other kids just like him.\par \par \uc2 BONAS: And I will continue to be a good advocate and help people stay positive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): That`s NIGHTLY NEWS. For more of Carter`s story, tune in to this Saturday`s "NIGHTLY NEWS" KIDS EDITION" here on NBC. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221202eic100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Race To Avert A Rail Strike That Could Cripple The U.S. Economy Ahead Of The Holidays\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Tom Costello \par \uc2 553 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: And in Congress tonight, support for blocking a potentially catastrophic rail strike. But to do it, they`ll have to impose a controversial contract on unions representing more than 100,000 rail workers. Tom Costello has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, rare bipartisan agreement that the lame duck Congress must intervene to head off a potentially crippling rail strike on December 9th from the President --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s not an easy call but I think we have to do it. The economy is at risk.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: To the Republican Senate Minority Leader.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I thinks some may be inclined to vote against it and others are arguing that the -- that the economic price doing that is too great.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The showdown comes after eight unions agree to a White House broker deal with freight rail operators, but four unions voted no. If one goes on strike, they all do. Congress has the authority to impose a deal but Democrats risk alienating a core constituency.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): I don`t like going against the ability of unions to strike, but weighing the equities, we must avoid a strike.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The chief concern, a rail strike could cost the economy anywhere from half a billion dollars a week to $2 billion a day depending on who is doing the analysis, stalling the supply chain filled with everything from food to fertilizers, clothing to appliances, car parts to Christmas gifts.\par \par \uc2 LORI ANN LAROCCO, CNBC SENIOR EDITOR: When you look around your house, the majority of items that are in your home came on rail.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Some commuter railroads and \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  could also be affected. The White House negotiated deal offers the union`s a 24 percent pay hike though many workers say it still fails to provide sufficient time off or paid sick leave.\par \par \uc2 TONY CARDWELL, PRESIDENT, BMWED-IBT: It doesn`t matter how much money you make if you can`t spend time at home or you can`t be home sick.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: But the unions may soon have contract that many actually voted against with the House expected to act first thing in the morning to end a strike before it even starts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:09]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Tom joining me here now. Let`s talk about the senators` late word about the status of a deal.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Well, Senator Bernie Sanders is right now raising concerns. He says he will block consideration of the bill until there was a vote on giving that paid sick time to rail workers. Meanwhile, there are also several leading Republicans who are now voicing opposition to the deal in addition to Bernie Sanders, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Tom Costello, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221206eibt0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Black Friday Is Here, Officially Kicking Off The Holiday Shopping Rush; President Biden Announcing Renewed Efforts To Ban Assault Rifles; \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Workers Expected To Strike Today; This Morning COVID Cases Hitting An All-Time High Across China; Team USA Preparing For Game Two At The World Cup Today; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  granting amnesty for suspended accounts. Aired 7-8a ET\b0\par\par\uc2 Kristen Welker, Peter Alexander, Emilie Ikeda, Kristen Dahlgren,  Somara Theodore, Kelly O`Donnell, Janice Mackey, Frayer, Meagan Fitzgerald,  Sam Brock \par \uc2 7,069 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC TODAY HOST: Good Friday morning on this day after Thanksgiving, and for millions of Americans this day is all about three things.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC TODAY HOST: Leftovers, shopping, and the long trip home. This is November 25, and it`s TODAY. It`s on. Black Friday is here, officially kicking off the holiday shopping rush. More than half the country expected to take advantage this weekend. And after weeks of deals leading up to the big day, how will those sales stack up? We have the guide to the best bargains and shopping strategies to check those gifts off your list.\par \par \uc2 Buckle Up. Good weather helped tens of millions of Americans get to their Thanksgiving destination without too much hassle, but the trip home could be a different story. Our team is standing by with everything you need to know to get back as easily and as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 Doubling Down. President Biden announcing he`s renewing his effort to ban assault rifles in the wake of a string of mass shootings. We go inside the president`s Thanksgiving Day push and whether or not he can get Congress on board.\par \par \uc2 Violent Protests.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The Chinese city that`s home to the world`s largest iPhone factory placed under strict COVID lockdown after angry workers clashed with police over pay. How it could impact supplies ahead of the holidays.\par \par \uc2 Those stories plus you say you want a revolution. World Cup fever running ramped ahead of Team USA`s match against England today. It comes after an incredible kick has made highlight reels around the globe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Created the goal. Running in (inaudible)!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We`re live in Doha with the latest. And for the Winston.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Tonight the National Dog Show Best in Show Winner is the French Bulldog.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Meet the little French Bulldog who won big at the National Dog Show, and now Winston will join us live on the plaza this morning after capturing hearts and first place. TODAY, November 25, 2022.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb, live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Good morning. Welcome to TODAY. Thanks for joining us on this Friday morning of your Thanksgiving weekend. We hope you all had a wonderful holiday. I`m Peter Alexander alongside my friend, Kristen Welker. Hoda and Savannah are off today. And it was tight. Cooper, the English Toy Spaniel, really brought it down to the wire, but Winston is your big winner last night.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Winston pulled out this big victory. He is adorable. He`s small but mighty, and he`s won a lot of competitions it turns out.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: He`s our little stocking stuffer this holiday.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Exactly. We sure do look forward to meeting him, but we do begin this morning as we usually do on this day with Black Friday shopping coast- to-coast. And while stores and online retailers have been advertising early Black Friday deals for weeks now, 166 million Americans are expected to shop in stores and online this weekend, so keep an eye out for those deals because it comes as inflation is at 7.7 percent. A major concern for Americans this holiday season. And as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in around 40 countries, including here in the U.S., are planning to strike today.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Meantime, those 55 million Americans who travelled over the holiday, well guess what? They have to head home at some point, likely this weekend, just as some severe weather is going to hit parts of the country. Don`t worry, though. We`ve got it all covered for you today, and we start with NBC`s Emilie Ikeda. She`s is at The Mills at Jersey Gardens Mall in Elizabeth, New Jersey where the rush is already on. Emilie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning. This place is buzzing with excitement, and this is something we haven`t seen all to of in the past few years, lines on Black Friday as more people return to pre-pandemic shopping habits. And a top priority this morning is finding the best deal, families in search of bargains as inflation shortens holiday gift lists.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The biggest shopping event of the year is finally here, and families are anticipated to take advantage of the deep discounts in record fashion. A whopping 166 million shoppers to fill their baskets this weekend.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Looks like there are going to be some good sales, and we`re excited about that.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: But there could be some hiccups for one of the biggest Black Friday players. Some \uc2 Amazon\uc2  worker are expected to go on strike today around the world, including in the U.S., calling for better wages an worker conditions. NBC News reached out to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  overnight but has not heard back, as many consumers gear up to dive into deals in store and online.\par \par \uc2 Mom of six, Tiffany Meyers, will take part in the Black Friday rush for her first time.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:02]\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY MEYERS, MOTHER OF SIX: Because of inflation, we`re absolutely not able to spend as much as we have in the past.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: While still strong, retail sales are expected to grow at a slower pace than last year`s historic demand, weighed down by sky-high inflation. Major players like Target, Kohl`s, and Macy`s have seen a pullback in demand in recent months. Now they along with \uc2 Nordstrom\uc2  are pulling out all the stops for customers this Black Friday.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WANLASS, NORDSTROM NEW YORK CITY VP AND GENERAL MANAGER: We are ready to go with the best products at the best prices.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Steep sales have been underway at many retailers for weeks, but experts say even deeper discounts are dropping in today`s countdown to Christmas. Snag fine jewelry at Macy`s for 70 percent off. Save $230 on this smart \uc2 Google\uc2  TV, and get second-generation AirPods Pros for their lowest price ever.\par \par \uc2 With the pandemic fading to this degree, do you think there`s a lot more excitement about actually buying things in person?\par \par \uc2 ETHAN CHERNOFSKY, PLACER.AI VP OF MARKETING: Well online shopping is amazing and such a critical channel for retailers. Discovery is still more effective in the store.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Should shoppers expect lines today?\par \par \uc2 CHERNOFSKY: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: As shoppers celebrate the season of savings.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: And to avoid those lines, consider using curbside pickup or look to see if a retailer allows you to check out an item in store using its app to help save time. Also, keep in mind, before you leave your home, take a look at this. I got this here. My list. Make your list. Compare prices. I`m really speaking to myself when I say set a budget and stick to it. Guys, back to you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, we`re going to let you go and start that process right now. Emilie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With stores bracing for close to pre-pandemic levels of holiday traffic, consumers are navigating their way through the deals and through the crowds as well. We wanted to take a look at how malls are getting ready for this year`s big rush.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: is the Co-President of Malls for Simon, and The Mills at Jersey Gardens is one of their properties. Eric, good morning. Hope you had a great Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SADI, CO-PRESIDENT OF MALLS, \uc2 SIMON PROPERTY GROUP\uc2 : Good morning. Hope you guys did as well. Thanks so much for having me today. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We`ll we`re so happy you can be here, and here we are on Black Friday. Of course, the sales kicked in back in October, but, of course, the big sales are happening today. So how important are today`s sales at predicting how strong this season is going to be overall for retailers?\par \par \uc2 SADI: Well obviously Black Friday historically has been a very important day. I think we`re seeing today that people are very excited about getting out of the house and doing some shopping. And listen, this is an important season for all retailers. I think what we`re going to see this year and early signs are it`s going to be better than the last few years.\par \par \uc2 And so, we`re feeling very positive so far. Too early to call the game, but we`re very excited what we`re seeing so far.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Eric, obviously Americans are now moving past COVID, but they are dealing with record inflation right now. And while you`re optimistic, about one in three American families say that financially they`re in a tougher place this year than they were one year ago. What specifically are you guys doing to help lure in those shoppers, to bring them back?\par \par \uc2 SADI: Well listen, I think people like to engage with their friends and their family. So we`re making sure we have, you know, the guy in the big red suit, Santa. We have a lot of events around Santa. We`re partnering with local schools for concerts, et cetera, from their local programs to come to the mall and engage.\par \par \uc2 And so, we`re doing a lot of traditional things that we do, but also I think our retailers are - have great product. They have great merchandise, and I think that`s going to be a big difference is our retailers really have good product this season as well. So I think that`s why people are showing up, and I think it was very evident this morning at 6 a.m., and I got here a bit before that and the lines were already building.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And Eric, just very quickly, obviously there has been a growth of online shopping in recent years in addition to COVID. How do you actually get people back to the malls? And what`s your message about the strength of malls in general?\par \par \uc2 SADI: Listen, malls, you know, I use a quote from Mark Twain that`s one of my favorite quotes, and it`s, "The rumors of my death have been greatly exaggerated," okay? The mall has been - has been called dead many, many times, but yet here we are having a record year. Traffic is better than last year. Sales are better than last year. Sales are better than pre- pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think the mall is very much alive. We`re investing in our properties. Our retailers are investing, and I think that`s really the important message is to win in today`s market the consumer demands more and we have to deliver them more. And so, that`s our big focus at our company is making sure we have a great experience when you`re here. We have great dining. We have an eclectic mix of shopping.\par \par \uc2 And so, the malls have evolved and the malls have changed certainly for the better, and I think that`s our message really here at Simon.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well it sure is great to see things getting back to normal. Eric Sadi, thank you so much for joining us. Happy holidays.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:03]\par \par \uc2 SADI: Thank you. Happy holidays to you. Thanks for having me, again.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We appreciate it, Eric, and good luck to you guys this season. Meantime, if you`re one of the millions of people travelling over the Thanksgiving holiday weekend, the chances are you`ve had a relatively decent trip so far. Most have with clear skies. Airports have not been reporting many delays, but that could actually change. Some storms are coming in the northeast. We have it all covered, and we start with NBC`s Kristen Dahlgren at Newark Airport this morning. Kristen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey. Good morning, Peter. Yes, from a busy Newark airport this morning we`ve seen the lines of travelers growing, and we are also seeing some delays trickle onto the board with the storms moving across the country. What started as such an easy trip for millions maybe about to take a turn.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: At major airports across the country, passengers thankful to find mostly smooth sailing on Turkey Day. At LAX, which is expecting some 200,000 travelers per day on average through the weekend, the terminals were nearly empty Thursday afternoon. Some flights even arriving early on Thanksgiving. FlightAware`s tracking maps mostly showing green, but at the Houston airport some weren`t so lucky. 11-hour delayed flight this TikToker posting and still waiting at Bush Airport. The region dealing with heavy rainfall.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Over six inches of rain.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: It`s a far cry from the major summer travel chaos that saw countless flights cancelled and delayed. According to the travel app, \uc2 Hopper\uc2 , the average cost of a domestic flight this holiday was up 5 percent from 2019. 