erent World Cups. And this stunning scissor kick from Brazil against Serbia capturing the world`s attention yesterday.\par \par \uc2 But there is more to watch than just the games. Plenty of controversies have been kicking up, too. This morning, these Iranian fans seen inside the stadium, joining in protest sparked by the death of 22-year-old student Mahsa Amini, stopped by security, confiscating their flags.\par \par \uc2 The German players covering their mouths in a team photo, sending a message to FIFA that they`re denying them a voice. This after soccer`s governing body threatened to penalize players wearing OneLove armbands to promote diversity and inclusion. A World Cup with a lot to focus on, but tonight it`s all about Team USA.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN MALE: We`re going to win it all.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN MALE: We win it all. We win -- we`re going to win it all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [07:35:04]\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD (on camera): That`s the spirit, right? Well listen, guys, we are hearing back from the Qatari Government about those accusations of discrimination. I want to read for you (ph) in part their statement here. They say, our message has always been that everyone is welcome, but FIFA has not gotten back to us with a comment about those protests inside the stadium at the Iran game.\par \par \uc2 Guys?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Quite a backdrop to the games this year. Megan, thank you so much. It`s so great to see those American fans cheering on Team USA.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Goalkeeper Matt Turner may have his hands full today. Hopefully, he has a great day, and the rest of the team as well.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, everyone is going to be watching. And you can catch the USA/England match at 2:00 p.m. Eastern on Telemundo and streaming on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Harry Smith brings us a story of college students raising awareness about difficult issues through a very unique performance.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Plus, how the rising trend in subscription services could change the way you travel. We`re going to break down the deals being offered today so you can find out what is worth buying. That`s right after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:40:08]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: In Today`s "Consumer Confidential" we`re talking cost savings and convenience. That`s the reason many people choose subscription services for everything from grocery deliveries to music and also streaming.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Listen to this, though. This Friday morning, Black Friday, the trend is growing for air travel as well, including one at \uc2 Frontier Airlines\uc2  that`s announcing a package for unlimited flights. But there is a catch to know about before you buy. NBC`s Sam Brock has the story from \uc2 Miami International Airport\uc2 . Sam --\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Peter and Kristen, good morning. At a time that airfare has increased 40% year-over-year, travelers right now are just looking for some relief. \uc2 Frontier Airlines\uc2  program has unlimited flights for $799 through Cyber Monday, and it does certainly offer that but with plenty of parameters. Now for flexible flyers that could work as these programs are becoming more and more common.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Whether you`re jet setting across parts of the country or kicking back in the Caribbean on vacation, the new GoWild! Pass from \uc2 Frontier Airlines\uc2  is looking to capitalize on a couple of current trends: skyrocketing prices, travelers with more flexible schedules, and surging demand.\par \par \uc2 BARRY BIFFLE\uc2 , \uc2 FRONTIER AIRLINES\uc2  CEO: A third of our customers today are flying five times or more per year, and that`s actually double the rate that we had prior to the pandemic. And so, that`s why we created this product to, you know, tailor to their needs.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The offer at $799 through Cyber Monday then jumps to nearly $2,000 annually, plus added taxes and fees per flight. The unlimited package only lets you book domestic flights 24 hours in advance, international flights 10 days in advance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s like you can`t plan anything.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s more for last minute I want to just jet off and go somewhere tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Ian Grant is 31 from Miami and figured why not give this a shot?\par \par \uc2 IAN GRANT, GOWILD! PASS SUBSCRIBER: I probably spent over 5 grand in travelling, so I saw it as an opportunity to be able to reduce that cost and then have the flexibility to travel more often.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: It`s part of a growing trend in subscription travel, a concept not necessarily new. For years, loyalty credit cards have offered unlimited access to certain airport lounges and hotel perks, but economic pressures and a renewed interest in travel have sparked new programs.\par \par \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  unveiled a similar annual subscription service earlier this year focusing on western states and using an entry level $49 per month plus taxes and fees for six roundtrip flights a year. Popular travel brands like \uc2 Tripadvisor\uc2  and Travel + Leisure offer discounted rates and deals for a monthly or annual fee. Enterprise is testing rental subscription in three different states. Travel experts say hotel chains are next.\par \par \uc2 MARK ELLWOOD, TRAVEL EXPERT: Keep an eye out for more hotel companies rolling out subscription deals which will be very attractive and great value.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The savings, of course, will ultimately shape consumer interest with \uc2 Frontier\uc2  saying they`ve already seen an early rush.\par \par \uc2 BIFFLE: If you have the flexibility, if you`re an active retiree or someone who works from home, has a lot of flexibility and you can travel once a week, this is a no brainer even at $1,999.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BROCK: \uc2 Frontier Airlines\uc2 ` program is available 300 plus days of the year, but there are some blackouts during high-demand times like the holidays. Financial services firm, \uc2 UBS\uc2 , projecting the subscription services industry to reach $1.5 trillion by 2025. Kristen and Peter, back to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Sam, appreciate it very much. I love that the Frontier guy says if you can travel once a week --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: -- that`s a pretty good gig.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That`s a pretty good gig. I think flexibility is the key --\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: -- with that one. All right. Now let`s get another check of the forecast with Somara. Somara, if you are going to jet set somewhere, how`s it looking out there?\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: Well I`ll tell you something. You may not want to jet set to Texas because they`re looking at snow in far western Texas unless you want to see some nice winter weather. Here`s a look. We have winter alerts for Carlsbad, New Mexico down into Alpine, right on into Lubbock. They actually are going to be seeing up to eight inches of snow in some parts there. And as we head throughout your weekend, you can see where the higher elevations are. That`s where we`re going to see more of the snow.\par \par \uc2 And believe it or not, the same storm system that`s bringing the snow is also bringing severe weather to parts of New Orleans and farther east in Texas, so something to consider if you`re hitting the road today. That`s a look at the weather across the country. Let`s see what`s going on a little closer to home after this break.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: And that`s a look at your local forecast. Peter, Kristen --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Somara. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [07:45:02]\par \par \uc2 And up next our series Inside the Game. We`re going to take you behind the scenes of the \uc2 NFL\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You`re going to love this. I got an inside look of the Vikings pregame ritual that brings that Minnesota magic for players and fans. That`s the Skol Chant. Check it out. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Oh.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We are back now with our Inside the Game series, and our special Thanksgiving edition of NBC Sunday Night Football saw a big win for the Vikings over the Patriots last night, but some of the top highlights came before the game even started, right?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: So in Minnesota they call this show time. The Vikings and their fans have made that stadium a bucket list destination, so we headed to the Twin Cities to see what makes Minnesota so magical.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Want to see the most electric pregame in pro sports? Head north. The \uc2 Minnesota Vikings\uc2  are dazzling fans.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, the Skol Chant and the Gjallarhorn, there is no better.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And gaining new ones.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The electricity in the stadium is absolutely amazing. I see it on TV all the time from San Diego.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Skol! Skol!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Skol! Skol!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Skol!\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Even before the opening kickoff.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s the best. And I go to away games, too, and there is nothing better than this place.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We headed to Minneapolis to get an inside look at the pregame that was just named the best overall production in all of American sports.\par \par \uc2 GREG BOSTROM, VIKINGS SENIOR DIRECTOR OF ENTERTAINMENT MARKETING: We want it to be an incredible experience. Not just a football game but a full experience the whole time we`re here.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The most obvious of those is this, 18 feet taken straight from Norse mythology, the Gjallarhorn.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN HARPER, VIKINGS VICE PRESIDENT OF CONTENT AND PRODUCTION: The Gjallarhorn is a horn that the Vikings sounded to start a battle, so we thought that it fit really well.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: This one is sounded by special guests like Hall of Famer John Randle.\par \par \uc2 [07:50:04]\par \par \uc2 You sounded the Gjallarhorn at the firm home playoff game for the Vikings in the new stadium. That moment when you get behind it, what are you thinking?\par \par \uc2 JOHN RANDLE, FOOTBALL HALL OF FAMER: Unbelievable. It gets the crowd roaring. It gets everybody in the right frame of mind, and it`s just such an exciting feeling.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Even louder is the massive carved drum pounded by a member of the team`s Skol Army.\par \par \uc2 JAY MATUZAK, VIKINGS SKOL ARMY DRUMMER: When it`s the stadium doing the Skol Chant and we got this drum rocking and this place is going, it`s quite incredible to be a part of this.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And then there`s the chant. 66,000 strong. Skol, which basically means "cheers" in Scandinavian countries, has been a part of Vikings fan culture for decades. But it got new intensity once the team paired it with a popular cheer from European soccer.\par \par \uc2 HARPER: Everybody talked about it. It kind of went viral, and we got 100 emails from fans asking us, like you`ve got to do this.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The Vikings got the blessing of Iceland`s team captain to adopt it. And a new Viking war cry was born.\par \par \uc2 HARPER: Sports is a communal experience, right. And you feel something when you`re here in the stadium for the snow and for the horn and the Skol Chant all part of it. That adds to the home field advantage.\par \par \uc2 HARPER: And the icing on the cake, 55 fake snow machines providing some Minnesota atmosphere even inside their dome.\par \par \uc2 BOSTROM: And then the art form is making sure you hit as many people as you can.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Local pride has never sounded louder.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That is cool.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: So, of course, we came home bearing props. This one`s for you. I`d like to see what that looks like, Goldilocks.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Thank you. Peter, let`s see. Looking pretty good. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Let`s go (ph).\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Hopefully the Eagles won`t disown me for wearing this, but this is a strong pregame --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We should do the rest of the broadcast this way. In my final broadcast --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: No, this is so much fun. I`m a die-hard football fan for my whole life. I didn`t even know about this tradition.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: This is great.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And check out who did it yesterday. You`ll recognize this face.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Okay, let`s see.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Suni Lee, Minnesota`s own, the gold medalist of the (ph) United States gymnasts.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Wow! Way to go, Suni! That is so cool.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes. And, of course, they beat the Patriots, so I`m guessing she`ll be back again soon.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I bet she will. It is so powerful to see all of the fans clapping and cheering at the same time, and that drum and Gjallarhorn. Great stuff.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, it just makes your heart beat a little bit faster.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Coming up right here, this little nugget was just named Best- in-Show, and his first stop afterwards is our plaza. We can`t wait to introduce you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Oh.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But first, your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:11]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221125eibp000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hashtag Meltdown, A Mass Exodus From \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , Hundreds Of Employees Resigning, Rejecting An Ultimatum From \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  To Work Hardcore Or Leave\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie, Craig Melvin, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 1,505 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: It is 7:15, a lot more to get to with Craig, including. I don`t know whether we should call it turmoil at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  or something more.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Chaos.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s a big deal, yes.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: It sounds like both. Hoda, Savannah, good morning. Good morning to you as well. So we had a memo to staffers, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s new owner, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , he gave an ultimatum commit to quote extremely hardcore work or leave the company. This morning, it looks like hundreds of employees chose the latter. NBC`s Stephanie Gosk is here with that story. Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good morning, Craig. He sent that e-mail to his remaining employees on, Wednesday, telling them they had until 5:00 p.m. Thursday to sign on to his vision for what he calls \uc2 Twitter\uc2  2.0. Now after weeks of layoffs and resignations, the future of the popular social media platform may be hanging in the balance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): This morning, the doors at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  are locked, amid fresh turmoil and a mass employee Exodus. Employees told the company`s offices will be temporarily closed and badge access will be restricted through Monday, according to CNBC. On Thursday, a fresh wave of resignations rocked \uc2 Twitter\uc2  again, after billionaire boss, Musk, sends an e-mail to employees detailing his vision for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  2.0. It read in part, we will need to be extremely hardcore. This will mean working long hours at high intensity, only exceptional performance will constitute a passing grade. The ultimatum a 5:00 p.m. deadline on Thursday. Respond yes, if you decide to stay if not Musk writes, anyone who has not done so will receive three months of severance.\par \par \uc2 TIM HIGGINS, CNBC CONTRIBUTOR AND TECH REPORTER FOR THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: This kind of hardcore make your decision if you`re going to stay or go not something that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  people are accustomed to.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): That e-mail from Musk prompted hundreds of employees to step down, according to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  setting to people with knowledge of the situation. Some employees who quit tweeting the salute emoji meant to convey the message thank you for your service. Adding to the confusion, Musk now backtracking on his hardline remote work policy, after initially telling employees it was no longer allowed. He now says all that is required for approval is that your manager takes responsibility for ensuring that you are making an excellent contribution.\par \par \uc2 The latest wave of resignations coming after Musk already laid off thousands of staff when he officially took over the company. Melissa Engel worked as a senior data scientist at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . She says she was fired just days before Musk delivered his latest ultimatum, but has coworkers who resigned yesterday. She believes \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s future is hanging in the balance.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA ENGEL, SENIOR DATA SCIENTIST, \uc2 TWITTER\uc2 : It just seems that with current staff levels it cannot and that`s just flat out, cannot be saved.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): Musk has been tweeting overnight posting a skull and crossbones flag, a meme seeming to show \uc2 Twitter\uc2  in the grave and telling his followers quote, how do you make a small fortune and social media? Start out with a large one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK: A very large one. It`s not clear exactly how many workers opted for the severance package. But reports put the number in the hundreds possibly much higher. It comes after Musk already cut 50 percent of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s workforce in that mass layoff when he took over the company. NBC News has reached out to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on this but we have not heard back.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So he locked up the offices, that would --\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Yes. Does not want them in there, ostensibly because he`s worried they would do something.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: It`s going to be interesting to see how this plays out at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you Steph.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Yes, a lot of people wondering if they get on their phones if it`s just going to be gone.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Gone, yes.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Right.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, thank you. Thanks Steph. All right, let`s get a check of Dylan`s forecast. Hey, Dylan.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Hey, guys. Yes, the big story is that lake effect snow storm that we are going to see last through the weekend but elsewhere across the country. Look at this, there`s not a whole lot going on. It`s cold, it`s windy temperatures running about 20 to 30 degrees below average. And all of this cold air is going to settle in behind this cold front even down into parts of Florida and along the Gulf Coast, highs today, only in the 50s and low 60s.\par \par \uc2 [07:19:41]\par \par \uc2 That`s look at the weather across the country. We`ll get to your local forecasts in the next 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 DREYER: And that`s your latest forecast.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, thank you, Dylan.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, new clues in the mysterious murders of four Idaho college students, what a report from the coroner is now revealing as the \uc2 FBI\uc2  joins in on the investigation with the killer still on the loose. We have the very latest and a live report.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And then we`ll take a closer look at the Taylor Swift ticket debacle. Today`s public sale now canceled. \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  under more fire than ever before, some are calling for a Justice Department investigation. We`ll have everything you need or want to know. But first this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Coming up, a football moment so epic it is simply known as, the play.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes, Cal`s legendary kickoff return to stun Stanford through that series of laterals in the middle of the cardinal marching band. Remember that 40 years later, Harry Smith on the field with the players and band members who was there. It`s a truly amazing story. We`ll have it for you after your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:26:21]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:25]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Back now 7:30. It`s beginning to look a lot like Christmas in our plaza. Check it out, the world famous Radio City Rockettes, they`re giving us a taste of the Christmas Spectacular today. Tonight is the grand opening. It`s got to be in the 30s or 40s and they`re out there like that.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Every year. I feel like every year, the Rockettes come to the preview. It`s 30 degrees outside.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know. But they needed like a sequence snow suit or something for this Today Show appearances but we so appreciate it, really gets us in the mood. We`ll get out there in a minute.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get to your headlines here at 7:30 disgraced \uc2 Theranos\uc2  founder \uc2 Elizabeth Holmes\uc2  will learn her fate today. A sentencing hearing is scheduled for later this morning in San Jose, California. In January she was convicted on four counts of investor fraud and conspiracy. The prosecution is asking for a 15-year prison sentence. Prosecutors also want Holmes to pay her fraud victims $804 million in restitution.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: The man suspected of killing 10 black people at a Buffalo grocery store back in May plans to plead guilty to all state charges against and that`s according to an attorney representing the victim`s families, 19- year-old Payton Gendron is scheduled to appear in court on Monday. Back in June, he was indicted on a list of charges including domestic terrorism motivated by hate and 10 counts of first degree murder. Gendron also faces federal hate crime charges connected with that shooting.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: To Thursday night football now, the \uc2 Green Bay Packers\uc2  desperately needed a win to keep their playoff hopes alive. But the \uc2 Tennessee Titans\uc2  had plans with their own.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Dontrell hangs in the pocket, fires caught, touchdown to Hilliard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Dontrell Hilliard with a touchdown on the opening drive for the Titans. They would never trail the rest of the game. Tennessee goes on to win that 127 to 17, Aaron Rodgers and the Packers now four and seven on the season. Ouch.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes, rough season for the Packers.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221122eibi000dy\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The TODAY Show for October 25, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb; Craig Melvin; Savannah Guthrie; Stephanie Ruhle; Jacob  Soboroff; Al Roker; Carson Daly, Brianna Dahlquist \par \uc2 9,172 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: It`s 8:00 on "TODAY." Coming up, pushed to the brink. Some hospitals over capacity over that tripledemic threat. An unprecedented surge of flu, respiratory illnesses, and new COVID variants.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA, WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR: It is concerning. We have three respiratory viruses that are circulating, and all of them going up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: From what doctors are saying to what the White House is doing about it. We`re live with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Then up close and personal. The secret service agent assigned to Jacqueline Kennedy shares a never-before-seen side of the former first lady.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: How many people got to have that view of her?\par \par \uc2 CLINT HILL, FORMER U.S. SECRET SERVICE AGENT ASSIGNED TO FIRST LADY JACQUELINE KENNEDY AND CO-AUTHOR OF "MY TRAVELS WITH MRS. KENNEDY": Very few. Very few people saw her like that. It showed how she really lived.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: His firsthand and revealing look at the American icon.\par \par \uc2 Plus, remembering Leslie Jordan. We look back at the life of the beloved actor known for his role on "Will & Grace."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LESLIE JORDAN, ACTOR: Well, well, well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And his \uc2 Instagram\uc2  videos that got us through the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: A little song that I`m going to sing to cheer everybody up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And home and Garten. Ina Garten is back in Studio 1A, whipping up some delicious dishes from her new cookbook. The "Barefoot Contessa" herself sharing with us some easy go-to dinners that you don`t want to miss. Today, Tuesday, October 25, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Shout out to Spearfish, South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On a girls` trip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Sioux City, Iowa.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Visiting from San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Celebrating our 20th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From Rocky Top, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: It`s Lily Jay`s (ph) birthday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On fall break.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Lexington, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Landgrove, Vermont.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Here for Penny`s (ph) 70th birthday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Pharmacist students.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From \uc2 Wilkes University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CROWD: In Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wanted to surprise my mom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Who`s watching back in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On her favorite show.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We made it on "TODAY".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: OK.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, you did. Welcome in. Welcome Wilkes-Barre. Welcome everybody. We`re happy that you`re with us on a Tuesday morning. We`ll be outside in just a bit. Feel a little bit --\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: I know. Plaza`s got coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We got a lot represented out there.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s a busy, busy morning. Let`s get right to your news at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 The nation`s health care system still recovering from the COVID pandemic is feeling the strain once again. This time the potential tripledemic of COVID variants, the flu, and the RSV, that`s a respiratory illness that strikes kids. Pediatric hospitals are already filling up and flue and COVID numbers are on the rise.\par \par \uc2 We spoke this morning with Dr. Ashish Jha. He`s the White House COVID-19 Response Coordinator and this is what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA, WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR: It is concerning. We have three respiratory viruses that are circulating. The good news here is we have terrific vaccines against two of them, both influenza vaccines, as well as new updated COVID shots. If people got those two, they can really keep them out of the hospital, prevent them from getting seriously ill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: President Biden, by the way, set to get his Omicron booster shot today. Just to remind Americans to keep their protection up to date.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: With just two weeks to go until the midterm elections, the state of the economy, top of mind for voters. And one of the biggest concerns, a looming recession. NBC`s Stephanie Ruhle sat down with treasury secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  for an exclusive interview. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, NBC NEWS SENIOR BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: So, where we are right now, we hear from business leaders every day, from Jamie Dimon to a Jeff Bezos to brace ourselves, and a recession is coming. Do you believe it is or are we in one right now?\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY: Well, we -- for three and a half percent on unemployment rate and have had over 300,000 jobs a month for the last three or four months or longer. That is not what most people think of as a recession.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Steph, welcome. So, what is Yellen saying here? Is she saying that we`re definitely not in a recession?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, she`s not ruling it out.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It is a possibility but she says given the path that we are on, she believes inflation will lower. But we`re on the path. We are not on the fast track. And that is certainly what Americans would like, this to move a lot quicker. It`s tough.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, we have not seen inflation come down, even though the Fed has raised interest rates, it`s poised to do so again, which many are concerned, is going to drive the economy into the recession. Did she weigh in on that?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, she did. And she`s traveling the country right now trying to talk about all the things the Biden administration has done. And they have done a lot on a bipartisan basis, right. There`s the Infrastructure Bill. We know there`s big projects going on. The Inflation Reduction Act is going to lower health care costs. It`s going to lower energy costs.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the problem. Those are fundamental long-term, building block projects that take a long time.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 In the short term, we know the president is releasing barrels from the oil reserve to try to deal with gas prices but it`s really tough right now. And we`ve got the midterms in less than two weeks.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Sure. Did the treasury secretary -- we heard her boasting about the unemployment rate. Did she address the labor shortage in this country?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, listen, the labor shortage kind of is what it is. It`s an immigration problem that we have had since before the pandemic. There is a mismatch with the amount of jobs and the amount of available workers. We need immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 However, her big issue right now, and this administration`s issue, we are facing an election in less than two weeks.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: True.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And will the economy be the issue that hurts them the most? It could be. So, she`s talking about all the great jobs that we`ve brought back, right. 10 million jobs since the pandemic. The United States has bounced back better than any other developed country. That`s a huge win. But we have very short-term memories, right? We forget where we were just two years ago.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It was really bad. And we need to possibly remember that, or at least that`s what she`s hoping for.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you, Steph.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you, Steph.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Well, here we go again, folks. The Powerball Jackpot, growing again this morning. Since nobody matched all six numbers last night, Wednesday`s Jackpot now grows to an estimated $680 million, and that, that makes it the seventh largest prize in Powerball history. Cash option gets you about $326 million before taxes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Let`s get a "Morning Boost".\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. I got one for you.\par \par \uc2 All right. A three-year-old, a little kid named Louie (ph), had never ever tasted a lemon before. He was curious. So, his mom said, OK. You can try a slice. Now, you got to watch until the very end.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Some lemon?\par \par \uc2 LOUIE (PH): Thank you. I like it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you?\par \par \uc2 LOUIE (PH): Yes. I don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It was always at first. It was a deal-breaker when he took that second bite. By the way, how about a kid who says, thank you, mom, when he got her the lemon. What a good kid.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Sweet boy.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: At least he tried it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: At least he tried it.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead here on a Tuesday morning, the star-studded tributes that are pouring in after the loss of beloved Emmy winning actor, Leslie Jordan. We`re going to celebrate his career and the many laughs he gave us throughout the years.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But first, Jacob Soboroff is here in the studio bringing us a new look at the life of Jackie Kennedy through the eyes and images of her secret service agent. Jacob.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Hoda. That man`s name is Clint Hill. He was assigned to protect Mrs. Kennedy. He was with her and President Kennedy on that fateful day in Dallas. He`s now sharing new personal stories and new images for the first time. You`re not going to want to miss this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re back at 8:10 now with a "TODAY" exclusive. Clint Hill is best known for being the secret service agent assigned to First Lady Jacqueline Kennedy Onassis` detail.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And most notably, you can see him in this iconic image. Shielding Mrs. Kennedy and her husband`s body in the moment after President Kennedy was shot in Dallas. Well, now he`s telling more of his and also her story in a new book that`s out today.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: NBC`s Jacob Soboroff had a chance to sit down with him. Hi, Jacob. Morning.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Hi, you guys. Good morning. To you, Savannah, I know this is someone that you know well. Clint Hill is an extraordinary individual. The book is called "My Travels with Mrs. Kennedy." And while 90-year-old Hill is facing health challenges of his own, his memory of his time with her are as crystal as day. The book takes a nostalgic look back at Hill`s time on Jackie Kennedy`s secret service detail. It is personal and revealing. It`s an amazing look at Mrs. Kennedy at times as we`ve never seen her before with the stories behind some of the images that we all know so well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): From 1960 to 1964, wherever Jacqueline Kennedy went, Clint Hill was never far behind.\par \par \uc2 CLINT HILL, FORMER U.S. SECRET SERVICE AGENT ASSIGNED TO FIRST LADY JACQUELINE KENNEDY AND CO-AUTHOR OF "MY TRAVELS WITH MRS. KENNEDY": We had a wonderful relationship. She trusted me. I trusted her. She would look out for me if necessary and she knew I would look out for her.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): As a young secret service agent, Hill was assigned to the first lady`s detail, an assignment that didn`t exactly thrill him.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): You get the assignment to protect Mrs. Kennedy. You think to yourself what?\par \par \uc2 HILL: My whole career is gone.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): And in terms that you were in the middle of one of the most consequential moments in American history?\par \par \uc2 HILL: Very true. I didn`t think it would be interesting at all. And I was completely wrong.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): Hill was by Jackie`s side on November 22, 1963 when President John F. Kennedy was shot. He revisited one of the America`s and his darkest days with Savannah in Dallas in 2013.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And you carry this place with you, I guess, the rest of your life?\par \par \uc2 HILL: Yes, I do. It`s one thing that I`ll never rid myself of.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): The images and the memories of that day still haunt him.\par \par \uc2 HILL: It`s a lot of sadness. Guilt. We let him down, his family down, Mrs. Kennedy down by not protecting him. I`ve never gotten over that and I never will.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): Hill`s revealing for the first time he attempted suicide shortly after Kennedy`s assassination. Walking into the waters off Palm Beach, Florida, one night, writing, I wanted the water to swallow me up. A police officer intervened, saving his life.\par \par \uc2 HILL: When you were given a responsibility to do something, you got to do it to the bitter end no matter what it takes, and I didn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): That harsh memory and gentler ones filled the pages of his latest book, "My Travels with Mrs. Kennedy." A collection, including some never-before-seen pieces of American history.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): Wow. These are the photos that you took with the camera?\par \par \uc2 HILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): How does it feel loo took loot to look at these?\par \par \uc2 HILL: You know, it`s emotional. It`s memories. Its days gone by.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): If not for his wife and co-author, Lisa McCubbin Hill, these treasures might have remained hidden in an old trunk in Hill`s garage or worse.\par \par \uc2 LISA MCCUBBIN HILL, CLINT HILL`S WIFE AND CO-AUTHOR OF "MY TRAVELS WITH MRS. KENNEDY": He would find these pictures of Jacqueline Kennedy in Italy and say, well, I don`t know. Do we keep these or throw them? And I`m like, oh, my God. Everything that he had was part of history.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): American history, yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): Hill is sharing the incredible stories behind some of his favorite photos taken by himself and others. Like, this one, the grinning and elegant Jackie Kennedy with her husband at a White House State dinner with France`s minister of culture, Andre Malraux.\par \par \uc2 HILL: That night, she convinced him when he went back to Paris is to convince de Gaulle to allow the "Mona Lisa" to come to the United States.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): For people who might not know this, the "Mona Lisa" for a time, was in the National Portrait Gallery in the United States.\par \par \uc2 HILL: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): And it was Jackie Kennedy who convinced the French government to allow it to come to U.S.?\par \par \uc2 HILL: That`s exactly right. It was her doing.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): And you were there for that?\par \par \uc2 HILL: I was there for that.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): And these from a diplomatic mission to India. Mrs. Kennedy surrounded by locals clamoring to meet her. Hill says these shots of Mrs. Kennedy and her sister, Lee, on top of a camel on a visit to Pakistan most captured her essence.\par \par \uc2 HILL: There was nothing that she wouldn`t try or do. She had loved life and wanted to live it to the fullest amount, fullest extent possible. And that was the kind of thing she loved.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): Among his most treasured pictures are the candid and intimate images of Mrs. Kennedy. Private moments of the woman he knew so well.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): How many people got to have that view of her?\par \par \uc2 HILL: Very few. Very few people ever saw her like that. It showed how she really lived.\par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): On his many travels, occasionally, the lens caught the secret service man himself, who preferred to blend into the landscape. There you are. Looks like you are not having a bad time in Italy?\par \par \uc2 HILL: No, that was -- I was relaxing. I am on a security boat and Mrs. Kennedy is on a sailboat that is anchored nearby.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voiceover): Opening that trunk brought back a flood of memories for Hill. Many that he had buried for decades. But ultimately, he says it`s been a healing journey.\par \par \uc2 HILL: There were a lot of good days when I was assigned to Mrs. Kennedy. There were some of the worst days, one that could imagine. But there were so many good days. And that`s what I remembered, was the good days.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): What a man. The photos -- honestly, that really spoke to me were the simple ones. The images of Mrs. Kennedy eating gelato, she was water skiing with Caroline, even relaxing in a corner. It felt like anybody could have taken them on their family vacation like we were all there with them. And for Clint Hill, he really was.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, part of the family there.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: An -- I mean, you had so much time with him. You know, he`s -- what a remarkable guy.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, definitely. And you do -- you still feel that weight that he carries.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I mean, he said it. He`ll always carry it. He`ll always feel that responsibility, you know. Even though he, of course, did the best that he could.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: A piece of American history.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Thank you, Jacob.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Beautiful piece.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That was a beautiful story, Jacob.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Guys, again, the book is "My Travels with Mrs. Kennedy" and it is out today.\pa