ger bowl. \\ GARTEN: We need a bigger bowl for that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Tell me about the book because you said it`s kind of go-to recipes. This is not fancy pants stuff.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: You know what, in the in the pandemic, everybody was cooking so much. Even I got sick of cooking. And so, I decided I was going to write a book of really simple recipes, and also new ways to serve dinner. Like maybe it can just be breakfast for dinner.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I like that.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: I mean, who wouldn`t like that, right?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So you`re going to make a couple of dishes, once a mac and cheese.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What makes yours different?\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: You don`t have to make a room for it. You just assemble everything. You put it in the refrigerator overnight, and you put -- and then throw it into the oven.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK, all right. That`s great.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Savannah wants to know, OK. So first, we need lots of cheese.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: So you can, I mean, as most people do, you can use one of these graters.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: But you can also use a food processor. I`m going to show you how fast it is. Just put the whole thing in --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Even for a hard -- what kind of cheese is that?\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: It`s Gruyere and Cheddar. It`s both.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK, OK.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Isn`t that crazy?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wait. What is it -- what`s (inaudible) know this? Or does he even know this?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Why don`t we do this (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: I don`t know. It`s so much easier.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh yes, because when I make it, it`s like a sweatshop cheese grating, you know, for hours.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Me too.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: And you just do it like that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK. So now you got --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I love you already. She`s great. Done and done.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: So then you take a big bowl and it`s got a lot of cream in it. And you put all the cheese in into this.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And that`s just heavy cream and cheese.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: And cheese. It`s got cheddar and Gruyere, and seasonings a little nutmeg.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Your secret ingredient.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Well the French like in a (inaudible), they like to do nutmeg. It`s just that kind of under flavor. Salt and pepper, really simple.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Let me do this.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`re in charge of pepper, set them up.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: That`s perfect. And then what I do is I take hot pasta. I use cavatappi and you just put it right into the into the bowl. Isn`t it good?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: It`s fantastic.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: It`s great. And you toss the whole thing together just like this.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: So all the flavors in the cheeses are all together.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So is that kind of what`s making your (inaudible) even though you`re not really making --\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: And then what you do is you cover it and put it in the refrigerator overnight. And the pasta, the starch from the pasta gets into the cream and it makes the room. So you never have to cook --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: When does the cheese get all melty?\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: When it goes in the oven.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, in the oven.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Make sure that you cover it like --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Nobody likes a cold mac and cheese.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: And then what I do -- I like -- I love when something creamy and something --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, creamy taste good.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: I do bread crumbs on the top. So I`m going to put --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: (Inaudible) bread crumbs of course.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Well I think fresh bread crumbs are just so great.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Now, do you have to have stale bread like they always say?\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: No. Any kind of bread, it doesn`t matter. Whatever you have around.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: And then I just make bread crumbs out.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: So that`s bread crumbs at the top.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh this is it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So good morning for the food processor.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Yes. And so then, what I do is I take a little bit of butter. This is the bread crumbs and just toss them together. And then, this isn`t -- that goes into the dish.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s going to go into the oven.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Just like that. And that`s --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Do you have to turn the oven on?\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: You have to pre-heat the oven.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK, this is good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Leave us alone.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So we got Mac and cheese, and then we put some cheese on top.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: You can never have too much cheese, right? OK. So we have cheddar and Gruyere on top, and these are the buttered bread crumbs on top.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Look at this.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: And that`s (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So it`s like creamy underneath that one?\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: It`s creamy underneath and it`s crispy on top, and it -- doesn`t it look delicious?\par \par \uc2 [08:50:06]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK. Tell us how do we like it.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: I don`t know. You might like it, you might not like it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: The bread crumbs also (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: And wasn`t that easy?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: That was really easy, right?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I`m going to try this. (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So good.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: So sits overnight in the fridge. And if you have time left over, there`s more cheese, so.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What do you have here?\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: I love to do when people are coming over, it`s just like little toast with whatever I can find in the refrigerator. And so these are, it`s a stale baguette or fresh baguette, whatever. And what I do is I brush it with olive oil. And it`s just so easy. Just brush each one with olive oil. And then salt and pepper. I mean, most people don`t season toasts like this, but it makes such a difference because it`s really flavorful.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: And then you just put it in the oven --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Do you have a little garlic on --\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: That`s afterwards when it comes out.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I have to stay out of it.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: When it comes out, I cut -- so they come out like this. And you just rub it on. So you get the flavor without biting into garlic.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: That`s just a really simple.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: And then just take a little bit of goat cheese, room temperature goat cheese.\par \par \uc2 DALY: All these little bits of love, that`s what makes this so good. All the detail, all the --\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Any kind of a creamy cheese, even cream cheese, and just little --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Like that.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: That these are (inaudible) but whatever kind of olives you have, and a little salt and pepper.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yum, I like this.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: You have a little buffet over there.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I need that other plate.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, he means the one with the whole mac and cheese in it. Ina, you`re amazing.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: These are so good.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you for coming and hang out with us.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Anytime.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I don`t know (inaudible). All good though.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She`s going to show us even more Go-To Dinners on our 4th Hour. You can get those recipes at today.com/food. We are back in a moment. Thank you, Ina. We love you. This is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: And we`re back with Today`s Plaza Pics. We took a couple. You`ll see one I took there with Jessica. We`ll put it up here. She`s in Salt Lake City on her first trip away from her four kids, so she`s having fun in the city. It was great to meet Jessica today.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I found a few new friends too. I got Fred and Regina from Buffalo New York. Happy they came to visit us.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And we got Marshall and Amy visiting New York. This is their last trip in a while because they are expecting a baby.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right, congrats. We celebrate them. How about we celebrate some birthdays?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Love to do it. Let`s start spinning those Smucker`s Jar, see who we`ve got up. Well first up, a happy 101st birthday to Mr. Mike Welker of St. Louis. He says the secret to longevity to two donuts a day keeps the doctor away. I like that. Iris Reece is 100, a Bingo champ from Brooklyn, New York, the proud mom of 14 kids. Bingo! I love that you have time to play bingo.\par \par \uc2 [08:55:00]\par \par \uc2 Happy 100th birthday to Louella Mitchell of Wenatchee, Washington. Louella can fix anything from sprinkler heads to electric cords, this fix it. Celebrating 100 years, Humberto Castro of Brownsville, Texas earned two degrees in agriculture from the University of Missouri. Midge Cannon of Naperville, Illinois is 100 loves going on cross country road trips with the girlfriends. And happy 100th birthday to Angelo Murabito, a World War II vet from Oswego, New York. Yes, Angelo. For his 90th birthday, Angelo went ziplining so the family is a little afraid to ask what he has in mind to celebrate this milestone.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I love it, Roker.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I like that.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Coming up in just a few minutes on the 3rd Hour of Today, one of our favorites here. We`re going to catch up live with Olympic legend Lindsey Vonn.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Plus, Grammy winner Baby Face, he`s going to perform on both the 3rd and the 4th Hours. He`ll be here live. I`m looking forward to that. Hope you guys have a great day. We`ll be back after your local news and weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS AND WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221025eiap000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Finding The Balance Between Work And Family Time Now That Your Kids Are Back At School\b0\par\par\uc2 Kristen Welker, Peter Alexander \par \uc2 1,872 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:12:11]\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER: Well, September didn`t just mark the return to school for kids across the country. It also saw many parents returned to the office on a more regular basis for the first time in two and a half years, if you can believe it. And that means a lot less family time that many of us have grown so accustomed to lately.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER: No doubt. So, the questions include how can you still get that quality time together or you can just finally enjoy some old moments.\par \par \uc2 Grace Bastidas is the editor-in-chief of Parents Magazine. Grace, it`s so nice to have you here, the mom of a nine and second row pair of girls just like we have. So, I`m extra grateful for your anecdotes and advice here.\par \par \uc2 GRACE BASTIDAS, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, PARENTS MAGAZINE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It feels like this has been such an adjustment for so many Americans for families during the pandemic, you had that time at home, you`re all hunkered down, together. And now we`re all back at it. Kids at school, parents at the office. How do you find that work life balance?\par \par \uc2 BASTIDAS: So yes, that line between work and family life was certainly blurred during the pandemic with so many people working from home. But as we transition back to in person, it`s very important to set boundaries. And don`t wait to see how things shake out, talk to your manager be upfront and specific say, hey, Peter, I can`t take meetings after 5:00 p.m. anymore because that`s my family time. And if you`re taking time during the workday day to say go to your kids soccer practice, block off the time on your calendar, put an out of office e-mail out there, your colleagues will appreciate that you`re human, that you value both career and family. And once you do get that reclaim time, make it meaningful. As parents will always emphasize quality over quantity.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 BASTIDAS: So, power off the devices and be present (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Eye contact instead (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Right, quality is so important. And of course, we`re talking about kids too. My little Margot started a playgroup this past week. So, we are so excited. That`s us taking pictures of her before she went. What are some good tips Grace for how families can get their kids adjusted into good routines heading back into the school year?\par \par \uc2 BASTIDAS: So even if school is in full swing or you`re back in the office, it`s never too late to reboot your routine. Start at night. Prepare as much as possible, get the lunchbox boxes ready, lay out everyone`s clothes, put your work bag in the backpacks by the door so that they`re ready to go. And if you have a big kid, delegate some of those tasks to them. They`re plenty capable.\par \par \uc2 And in my house, we have a whiteboard in the kitchen with everyone`s schedule so that my daughters know what to expect. And it also helps to keep the grownups on track.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, the delegating tasks remains in negotiation for the Alexanders. Some advice for some fun activities families can do to enjoy that time together?\par \par \uc2 BASTIDAS: Well, first of all, in terms of activities, you want to give everyone a say in what you`re going to do by giving even the kids, you`re letting them know that their opinions and voices matter. And in terms of activities, maybe designate one night a week for a themed dinner taco night, fondue Friday, dessert for dinner.\par \par \uc2 [08:15:05]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Fondue Friday, we`ll be there.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That`s a new one. That sounds good.\par \par \uc2 BASTIDAS: Our kids love a theme meal and we`ll be excited the whole day or maybe it`s movie night, or regular movie night or regular game night, everyone takes turns picking what they`re going to do. And if you have a little one, I love pajama walks. You know that hour before bed is hectic. So, get everyone in their pajamas, faces wash, teeth brush and go out and meander the neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Love that such great tips. Grace, it`s so great to see you. Thanks for being here with us.\par \par \uc2 BASTIDAS: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We try to do early to bed Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, but that hasn`t (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Good luck with that. All right.\par \par \uc2 BASTIDAS: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Thanks, Grace.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Good stuff. And we should mention our Peter just wrote to Parents Magazine about how you spend your time Peter at home with your wife and two daughters. You can find that on our website today.com. And obviously that`s something that`s real important.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes. My dad taught us family first. The rest are details. So, it`s so important for everybody.\par \par \uc2 We want to check in with Somara now who`s keeping an eye on the forecast. Somara?\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: Hey, everyone, we`ve got a lot going on out there. Right now, we`re going to start in California though, where they are expecting a good dousing of rain. Right along Northern California coastline, Sacramento, San Francisco all the way up to Redding, we could see one or two inches of rain easy on the East Coast. I`ve got something for you, tons of sunshine. Oh, it`s going to be a beautiful day out there. For much of the country but keep an eye out for the severe storms in the Midwest. I want in -- specific I want to tell you all you have a threat for tornadoes today. So please be careful.\par \par \uc2 That`s look at the weather across the country. Let`s see what`s going on a little closer to home.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: That`s the weather in your neighborhood. Back to you, Kristen. Peter.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right. Somara, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still to come, Joe is back with a look at what`s ahead in "Popstart".\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Hey, they`re coming up, big Broadway news about Phantom of the Opera the longest running show ever. That`s right after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We are back this morning with "Popstart" and that`s all you Joe.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Hey there. So first up, of course is the Phantom of the Opera. The longest running show in Broadway history just announced that it will be closing next year. Phantom is going to celebrate its 35th anniversary on Broadway in January before the curtain goes down to the final time on February 18.\par \par \uc2 Now like many shows on Broadway, Phantom has struggled a bit to regain its footing after that long pandemic closure, but it has done quite well over the three plus decades seen by almost 20 million people and grossing $1.3 billion since opening. Phantom is still running in other parts of the world, you can catch it on London`s West End, Australia or even in in China where the first Mandarin language production is set to open next year.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:22]\par \par \uc2 So, Peter and Kristen, my angels of music next time you`re in New York, we`re going to have to catch it one last time, right?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We sure will. I love that the props are back, first of all, Joe. Second of all, I think you need to know that Peter is an aspiring theater actor, so this is particularly devastating for him.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, no doubt, music of the night is Alexander family classic. We love that thing.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I know.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But they`ve had a pretty good run after 35 years.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It sure did. Yes. So good.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Yes, exactly. And maybe you can still be the Phantom on the West End. Peter Will hold out --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: All right, up next, live from New York, it is almost time for Saturday Night Live. Our favorite comedy sketch show is gearing up for its 48th season and they just announced four new featured players. Marcello Hernandez, Molly Kearney, Michael Longfellow and Devon Walker will all be joining the cast in Studio 8H. Now Molly Kearney, who most recently appeared in Amazon`s A League of Their Own remake is making history as SNL is first non-binary cast member. This news all comes after many fan favorites announced they were leaving the show, that includes Kate McKinnon, Aidy Bryant, and Pete Davidson. These new cast members do have some pretty big shoes to fill but you can catch the season 48 premiere of SNL on October 1st, that of course, is right here on NBC.\par \par \uc2 And finally, Ana De Armas. She caught up with our friend Willie for this week Sunday sit down. They talked about her new movie Blonde, a fictionalized take on the life of Marilyn Monroe, and what it took to transform into the Hollywood icon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIS GEIST, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: People have preconceived notions about Marilyn Monroe or they know a few things about her. They know Happy Birthday, Mr. President, they know Gentlemen Prefer Blondes.\par \par \uc2 ANA DE ARMAS, ACTRESS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: But where did you begin to try to get to know her so you can play this part?\par \par \uc2 DE ARMAS: So, it was kind of like a slow process of discovering who not just the movie star was but who was Norma Jean. Just a lot of, you know, conversations and just questioning everything pretty much, not so much about what we already know about Marilyn.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: So much buzz around that one. Well, you can catch Willie`s full conversation with Ana De Armas tomorrow morning on "Sunday Today." And that Peter and Kristen is your "Popstart".\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, I`m looking forward to that. Looks like a great conversation and I feel like you want to see that movie, Pete?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It`s going to be fun one to watch. Joe thank you so much. We`re back right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:26:17]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That is going to do it for us on a Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, shaping up to be a nice one in some parts where it`s starting to feel like fall, Somara.\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: Yes. Out west, it`s really going to be feeling like fall. Here on the East Coast, we are going to be seeing temperatures in the 90s.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Nice. Then get out and enjoy it. And have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Enjoy your weekend.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220920ei9h00105\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last-Minute Deal Brokered By The Biden Administration To Avert A Catastrophic Rail Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Miguel Almaguer \par \uc2 527 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: A huge sigh of relief tonight after the White House announced a last-minute agreement to avert a nationwide rail strike that could have worsened supply chain problems and hurt the economy even more. Miguel Almaguer has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The tentative agreement keeps 7000 freight trains on the tracks the American economy, our supply chain chugging along, and nationwide passenger service on the rails. After a grueling 20-hour negotiation session with White House leaders, the union`s representing some 60,000 active freight train conductors and engineers reached an 11th hour deal at 2:00 a.m. with rail companies.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The American people, this agreement can avert a significant damage than any shutdown would have brought.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: With a vote from union members expected to ratify the deal, conductors and engineers are set to receive annual bonuses totaling $5,000 over a five-year term, and a staggered pay increase year after year amounting to a 24 percent raise.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LINDSEY, RAILROAD ENGINEER, \uc2 UNION PACIFIC\uc2 : It`s about quality of life. It`s about the ability to actually take care of your family, to take days off when you need the opportunity to.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: As a striker threatened to disrupt the supply chain exactly 100 days before Christmas, the cost to the U.S. economy could have soared to $2 billion a day. Still, workers held their ground.\par \par \uc2 KENT WONG, DIRECTOR, UCLA LABOR CENTER: All across the country, workers are rising up. They`re fed up with low wages, unsafe work conditions, and they see record profits on the corporate ledgers.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Still the handshake agreement did come at a cost because 97 percent of the rails they ride on are owned by freight companies, yesterday, \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  fearing a strike, canceled all of their long-distance routes. By tomorrow, service on the web of rails will be restored. But not soon enough for passengers like David Paperman and his wife.\par \par \uc2 DAVID PAPERMAN, AMTRAK PASSENGER: We`re in Eugene airport --\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Who today are flying instead of riding 850 miles when their trip was canceled at the last minute.\par \par \uc2 PAPERMAN: They saw the train coming in the tunnel and they didn`t -- they didn`t tell us.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Tonight, a setback for some but our nation now on track to keep goods and passengers moving.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): Union members expect to take a vote on this new contract in the coming weeks if not months. They tell us they are cautiously optimistic. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Miguel Almaguer in Los Angeles, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220917ei9f0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Number Of Workers Who Quit Their Jobs During The Pandemic Now, Kind Of Wish They Hadn`t\b0\par\par\uc2 Craig Melvin, Hoda Kotb, Tom Llmas, Gadi Schwartz \par \uc2 2,897 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:40:38]\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN: We are back. 7:40 now with Today`s Consumer Confidential where every day around the same time, we bring you stories that really impact your family`s bottom line.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Yes, this morning, an update on something we`ve talked about a lot during the pandemic. We are talking about the great resignation.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: Yes, tens of millions of workers quit their jobs in search of better ones. But new research is revealing the grass is not always greener. NBC`s Gadi Schwartz joins us now with more. Gadi, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GADI SCHWARTZ: Hey, good morning, guys. Yes, there are a lot of reasons why so many people quit their jobs over the last two years. In this red hot labor market, you can find bigger salaries, more benefits, more flexibility, and more job fulfillment. At least that`s what a lot of people expect to find. But it turns out the reality of this great resignation isn`t always as rosy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): Call it the great resignation regret, more than 40 million Americans left their jobs last year in hopes of finding a career with better paying hours and more satisfaction. A new survey finds nearly half of people who quit their old jobs for new ones during the pandemic say, the switch has not met their expectations.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER MASTEN (ph), FOOD SERVER: It was just kind of a slow burn of, oh, this is taking a lot out of me. I miss my old life.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): For years. 39-year-old Skyler Masten (ph) worked as a server at one of L.A.`s hottest restaurants. But when the pandemic hit, he started thinking about jumping ship.\par \par \uc2 MASTEN (ph): Waiting tables can be difficult. And with the compounded aspect of COVID that you`re in a mask and a face shield and it`s sweaty. It was exhausting.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): Skyler (ph) also wanted a more stable career because he and his wife hoped to start a family. So he quit and took a job teaching high school English where the pay was 20 percent less, but the fulfillment would be higher or so he assumed.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Was teaching what you thought it was going to be.\par \par \uc2 MASTEN (ph): It was not necessarily what I expected to realize that, oh, I`m making less money, but I`m probably working 80 to 90 hours a week, grading papers. There is a great sense of, is this really what I want.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): Many other workers have shared that disappointment.\par \par \uc2 TONY FRANCIS (ph), CAREER COUNSELOR: Some will leave their jobs thinking that the grass is greener and you know sometimes grass is grass.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): Career Counselor Tony Francis (ph) says her firm sees a lot of job jumpers moved to new careers that may pay more but are less satisfying.\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS (ph): The work experience and the work life balance and the company culture, is not aligning with their personal goals and priorities.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): And just when Skyler (ph) thought he was stuck, his former restaurant called offering him a management job with higher pay and benefits and less stress, so he took it. Experts say it`s a national trend known as boomeranging. And boomerangers now make up about one-third of all new hires. Some companies wowing back those former employees with better positions and perks, employees and workers perhaps both learning lessons from the great resignation chaos.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: And there is a bright side to all this, that survey also found that about 75 percent of people who quit their jobs are now happier with their current situation and they don`t regret taking that leap. Guys?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, but Gadi, if you find yourself in a new job that didn`t turn out as well as you expected, I mean, what should you do?\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Well, some experts say first don`t panic with the demand for workers still sky high in a lot of sectors, it is still very much an employee`s market. It`s good to start by sharing your concerns with your employer also you want to network with contacts and even reach out to a previous employer to see if they might offer something better because a lot of times they will. Guys?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right, Gadi, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I mean in this new job, this is what you expected, right?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Oh, it`s beautiful, I mean heaven. I was like part of that one goes and like people are like boomeranging, what is that?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`re board with that. And by the way, can you believe that that guy was making more money waiting tables that he was teaching schools.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: That is the problem.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s a separate story.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Anyway, all right, let`s get a check of the weather Ms. Dreyer.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: OK. Our grass is just fine.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes. Nice and green.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: OK, but it is very, very hot in the Northeast once again. We`re just hanging on to the last bits of summertime here and with the heat and humidity comes some thunderstorms especially tomorrow for the interior northeast where we could see wind gusts up to 60 miles per hour some large hail, can`t rule out a tornado but just want to keep that in mind for your Friday afternoon and evening as that cold front moves in.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime though, enjoy the heat if you like it, New York 90 degrees for a high today but not that humid, so it`ll actually feel like 88, still hot though, Binghamton 86, 90 in Baltimore, a little more humid where it`ll feel like 92, Richmond, Virginia will feel like 94. Tomorrow it is hot once again. Philly gets in the lower 90s, same in Washington, D.C., Salisbury, Maryland 92 degrees when you factor in the humidity.\par \par \uc2 [07:45:12]\par \par \uc2 Then let`s push a little farther to the west where temperatures are still going to stay hot. In St. Louis upper 80s to low 90s all through the weekend into Monday, Cincinnati is hot too, Nashville as well. And really, we are going to hang on to this heat for just a few more days before that cold front is clear things out a little bit for the northeast at least. That`s a look at the weather across the country. Now here`s a peek out your window.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 DREYER: And that`s your latest forecast.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Dylan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, do not cram the song of the summer just yet because we got a late entrant in the battle. Ready? We are all over the rise of the adorable corn kid, one of the internet`s catchiest songs, coming right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [07:50:30]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For me, I really like corn.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What do you like about corn?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ever since I was taught that corn was real. It tasted good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did you think corn wasn`t real?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But when I tried it with butter everything changed. I love corn. Corn.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Don`t you just love him? Guys, that kid, he`s taking the internet by storm all thanks to his love of corn.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Things just tastes better when they`re real. I think that`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: And a touch of butter.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s right. Well, that young man`s name is Tariq but he`s earned nicknames deservedly so like hashtag corn kid and the CEO of corn. He`s even inspired a song that now has 30 million views and counting on TikTok alone. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I really like corn.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What do you like about corn?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s corn. A big lump with knobs, it has the juice. I can`t imagine a more beautiful thing. It`s corn. I can tell you all about it. I mean look at this thing but when I tried with butter everything changed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 DALY: There you have it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Look at this thing. I love that.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Corn.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: I wonder if he has ever had Jersey corn because that would blow his mind even more.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wait, what`s going on with Jersey corn?\par \par \uc2 DREYER: It`s just the sweetest most delicious corn on the planet.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And this, I think this corn is from Jersey.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Well, it is hard to eat with lipstick.\par \par \uc2 DALY: I like a little Mexican street corn. We have some that last night. Little --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: -- he`s missing that that front tooth which is like perfect for eating that corn.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: I think that would make it harder.\par \par \uc2 DALY: My 13-year-old has braces and he was slicing, he was taking a knife to the corn last night because he can`t eat it with his braces. But gets his braces off next month, he`s like I can`t wait for that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wait a minute super sweet, super buttery, super salty, and warm.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: With the butter.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That is really good.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Tariq you`re on to something, it`s gorgeous, great.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Can he do this?\par \par \uc2 DREYER: And it`s real.\par \par \uc2 DALY: This is a T.V. corn. This is like real corn.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s something special there.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: I don`t care about the lipstick because there`s one.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Eating corn.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: At 8 o`clock in the morning.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s good.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What else we got Carson?\par \par \uc2 DALY: Well, Tariq by the way his catchphrases have a corntastic day, so we should end by saying have a corntastic day everybody.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on PopStart, if you like Wordle you`re going to like the -- and you like Christmas story the classic film, and if you happen to like Tom Hanks, who does all of those things, we`re going to give you some insider too.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Carson this is -- you love this. We brought pickleball to the Plaza. We`re going to test out our skills, help you master the nation`s fastest growing sports.\par \par \uc2 DALY: The headline is Hoda is a ringer because we`ve done this before. You`re so good.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But first, your local news.\par \par \uc2 DALY: She`s quiet because she`s so good.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:57:08]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: It`s 8:00 on "TODAY". Coming up, breaking overnight. Uvalde School Police Chief fired after an emotional school board meeting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Turn in your badge and step down. You don`t deserve to wear one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The decision coming three months to the day after that horrific shooting. The details and reaction, just ahead.\par \par \uc2 Then, new treatment. The psychedelic medicine helping people struggling with alcohol abuse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL BOGENSCHUTZ, DIRECTOR, NYU LANGONE CENTER FOR PSYCHEDELIC MEDICINE: It could impact millions and millions of lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Harry Smith`s conversation with patients and the promising new information about how this therapy helps.\par \par \uc2 Plus, hidden treasures. How this summer`s drought is unearthing ancient artifacts, some not seen in centuries. We will bring you around the world for a look at some of these incredible revelation revelations.\par \par \uc2 And pretty big deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Pickleball is so open to everybody and it`s such a nice community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re playing pickleball on the plaza. The sport sweeping the nation makes a stop outside our studio. With some of the game`s biggest stars here to help you step up your swing. Today, Thursday, August 25, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sending love to my husband, Dennis, watching from Truitt, Oregon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Love you, dad.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: What`s up Papa (ph), Washington?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Hello to our hometown Kremmling, Colorado.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Best friends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Celebrating 40 years of friendship.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From South Vacherie (ph), Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Girls` trip on the plaza.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Visiting from Paducah, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because it`s our 50th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Atlanta, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We`re turning 10.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: All right.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Welcome back. Welcome back. We`re so happy you guys are here on this Thursday morning. Savannah`s off. I -- we got a lot of matching t-shirts.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We do.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: A lot is happening out there.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: I love matching t- shirts.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Cool.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Happy Thursday to -- by the way before we get to headlines. Hoda, you got a really special story for us tomorrow. And it`s really, sort of, celebrating a beloved children`s book.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You all know the one, "Goodnight Moon".\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Goodnight, Bear. Goodnight chairs.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The whole thing -- anyway it`s written by Margaret Wise Brown. It`s a classic. That wasn`t always the case. And in fact, some libraries and things wouldn`t even carry that book. There`s a great story behind it. I had the privilege of doing the audiobook for "Good Night Moon". So, it`s cool. So, we`re going to talk about that and meet --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, that`s --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: -- we going to -- I`m going to introduce you to Margaret Wise Brown, who since passed away, but I think her backstory might surprise you, too.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I did not know that was an instant classic. I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No, it`s a time. It`s a time.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: The quiet little bunny, right? That`s how it ends.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Shh.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: That`s the best. It`s the best.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Let`s get to our news at 8:00. Exactly three months after that tragic shooting in Uvalde, the school`s police chief, Pete Arredondo has been fired. The school board voted last night and it was unanimous. Arredondo has been widely criticized for the delayed response in taking down the gunman. He did not attend the meeting but one young speaker lashed out at him before the vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have messages for Pete Arredondo, and all the law enforcement that were there that day, turn in your badge and step down. You don`t deserve to wear one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Arredondo`s attorney called the meeting a, "Public lynching" and requested that his client be reinstated with back pay and with benefits.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: The deadly storm system that`s been sweeping across the south is expected to bring more flooding this weekend. It dropped up to a foot of rain on parts of Central Mississippi yesterday. Emergency crews had their hands full, evacuating residents from a nursing home. And children and staff members from daycare as the floodwaters rushed in. It was also, as you see here, a possible tornado sighting.\par \par \uc2 Dylan`s got the latest on where those storms are headed next. We`re talking about more rain?\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: More rain. The ground is totally saturated. Dallas now, and Jackson, Mississippi the wettest August on record. So, the rain has nowhere to go except to run off into the rivers, to streams and just create more and more flooding.\par \par \uc2 So, we do have flood watches in effect for the same area, like we saw yesterday. And the storms themselves producing about three inches of rain per hour are very slow moving. So, when you have three inches per hour, sitting over the same area for several hours at a time, you can see how quickly that adds up. And we could end up with an additional six or more inches of rain in some areas.\par \par \uc2 The storm itself will eventually rain itself out. Unfortunately, it`s raining itself out over the same area because it`s part of a stalled front that`s not moving anywhere. So, flash flooding remains a concern through the day today, through the day on Friday. And then eventually, again, some areas picking up about three to six inches when you see that rain falling at such a significant rate. So, more flooding is likely. Tom.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right. Dylan, we thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220826ei8p0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Educators In Ohio`s Largest District Are On Strike And In Philadelphia, Thousands Of Custodians And Bus Drivers Are Preparing To Do The Same\b0\par\par\uc2 Craig Melvin, Maggie Vespa \par \uc2 623 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN: Also this morning, a major issue is facing millions of families as the new school year gets underway, a shortage of teachers and other staff. Educators in Ohio`s largest district just went on strike. Meanwhile, in Philadelphia, thousands of custodians and bus drivers are preparing to go on strike, all of them facing concerns that are plaguing schools nationwide. NBC`s Maggie Vespa has the very latest. Maggie, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Craig, good morning. As you know, we`ve covered it all across the country. Those teacher shortages leading to larger class sizes in this profession with notoriously low pay and also lately cultural and political tensions are kind of infiltrating classrooms. Well, cut to this week, this is peak back to school season and teachers and staff and a growing number of districts say they have hit their breaking point.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): This morning, with back to school in full swing nationwide. Teachers are testing districts by marking themselves absent.\par \par \uc2 REGINA FUENTES, COLUMBUS EDUCATION ASSOCIATION SPOKESPERSON: There is no substitution for good and great teachers.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): In Columbus, Ohio, the state`s largest district, public school teachers are on strike for the first time in nearly 50 years. Their demands include smaller class sizes and air conditioning in every school. With classes set to start tomorrow, the district is scrambling to find substitutes.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER ADAIR, COLUMBUS BOARD OF EDUCATION PRESIDENT: Our community`s children are the board`s priority. And our final offer we felt did reflect that.\par \par \uc2 FUENTES: We are doing this for the students.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): The Columbus flash, the latest classroom flashpoint, over the weekend, roughly 2,000 custodial and transportation workers in Philadelphia public schools also voted to strike, demanding better pay and.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): You all want active shooter training.\par \par \uc2 GABE MORGAN, \uc2 SERVICE EMPLOYEES INTERNATIONAL UNION\uc2  32BJ PENNSYLVANIA VICE PRESIDENT: They`re asking for the training that you think they`d be getting already.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): A sobering stressor for modern teachers who also balance ever changing COVID restrictions and soaring rates of anxiety among students. It`s taking a toll with an estimated 300,000 public school teachers and staff having left the field between February 2020 and May 2022. A recent NBC News poll finding just 25 percent of parents are confident public schools will be adequately staffed this year.\par \par \uc2 In Columbus after years of remote learning mom, Kristen McCormick (ph), worries how her first grader Ezra (ph) will feel when his teacher isn`t in class, Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN MCCORMICK (ph), MOTHER: We have struggled as a family to maintain a sense of normalcy and the routine. So this is just sort of another log on the fire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VESPA: And of course, a lot of teachers say that he complaint, that common complaint is that notoriously low pay. So to put this in perspective, according to one study, public school teachers make on average 20 percent less than professionals with similar education levels and experience. Craig?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes, and it`s really quite sad. Maggie Vespa for us there, Maggie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220824ei8n000jj\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Booming Popularity Of Trade Schools\b0\par\par\uc2 Willie Geist, Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 2,192 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:40:00]\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST: It is an age-old question, is the ever pricing price of a four-year college worth it? For many, the answer is yes. But for an increasing number of high school students graduating into an economy with some 11 million open jobs, the quickest path to a well-paying career is by learning a trade. NBC News Senior Business Analyst Stephanie Ruhle has more in our "Sunday Spotlight."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, NBC NEWS SENIOR BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At a refurbished firehouse in New York City, Jessica Martelly (ph) is learning how to build a wall. She`s a student at NEW, \uc2 Nontraditional Employment for Women\uc2 . It trains women for jobs in construction. But it is not what the 22- year-old had originally planned after high school.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (on camera): Did you want to go to college or did you think that was a path everyone takes?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA MARTELLY, NEW STUDENT (PH): I thought since I was the first generation in my family, I would be the first generation to graduate college.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): But instead, she left college early and took a job in retail. Then, she learned about this training. And now, she`s considering a job as a sheet metal worker.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (on camera): As we`re having this conversation, your eyes are lighting up, you are smiling ear-to-ear.\par \par \uc2 MARTELLY (ph): Yes. I`m really excited.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): It`s a similar story for this Ailover Tolaskow (ph). First a year of college and a retail job. Now, she`s an apprentice carpenter on a 58-story high rise in New York.\par \par \uc2 AILOVER TOLASKOW, APPRENTICE CARPENTER (PH): When I was in high school, it was what college are you going to? Did you get your admissions letter yet? And I never thought to go into the trades, because it wasn`t a conversation that I had with anybody.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): After decades of societal pressure to go to college, a growing number of students are looking for alternative career paths, especially ones that don`t carry significant loan debt.\par \par \uc2 Last spring, college enrollment dropped nearly 5 percent from a year earlier, and attendance overall is down more than 9 percent compared to pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (on camera): Is going to a trade school now considered an alternative to going to college?\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN CULHANE, PRESIDENT OF (NEW) NONTRADITIONAL EMPLOYMENT FOR WOMEN: It is, and it should be even more so, right? It is the other four-year degree, is what we say. You`re earning while you`re learning and you`re not carrying the debt.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): In 2021, more than 500,000 apprentices were learning skills like carpentry and plumbing and but also, I.T. and health care. That`s up 66 percent from 10 years earlier. Nationwide, there are more apprenticeship programs supported by federal aid from both Republican and Democratic administrations.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The first thing is, is a great attitude.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): Increasingly, kids under 18 are interested in these careers. At Berkmar High School, north of Atlanta, there are the usual classes, math and English, but also, construction. As early as ninth grade, students learn about carpentry, plumbing and masonry.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MAHAFFEY, HEAD CONSTRUCTION TEACHER AT BERKMAR HIGH SCHOOL: This is the kind of program that needs to be in every single high school in America. It`s one of those that can give students just another way of looking at what possibilities are out there.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): Yet many students and parents, along with employers, still worry about not having a college degree.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (on camera): Do we put too much pressure on kids to go to college?\par \par \uc2 JEAN EDDY, PRESIDENT/CEO OF AMERICAN STUDENT ASSISTANCE: Absolutely. It`s the big default, which almost as if you don`t go to college, there is a stigma. There are plenty of worthwhile jobs, in fact, jobs right now that can`t be filled that pay $70,000, $80,000, $90,000 a year to start, not requiring a college degree. College is not for everybody. And the sooner that we realize that, the better off this country is going to be.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thanks to this program, like from the very beginning to the very end, it`s just pushing me to where I want to be.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): For "Sunday TODAY," Stephanie Ruhle, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Steph, thank you very much. You can see more of the series, "Kids Under Pressure," all week across the outlets of NBC News.\par \par \uc2 This week, we highlight another life well lived. We take for granted today that we can walk into a doctor`s office and lie down in or just step into a machine that sees clearly into our bodies. But that concept was revolutionary when a doctor conceived of it a half century ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 In 1974, Dr. Raymon Damadian received a patent for a scanner that would become the MRI machine. An essential medical tool that can diagnose cancer and look into patient`s brains, lungs, and bones without exposing them to radiation.\par \par \uc2 [08:45:00]\par \par \uc2 A native of New York City who studied the violin at the prestigious Julliard School before turning to science. Damadian used magnetic resonance imaging to produce vivid imaging superior to x-rays or CT scans. He performed his first scan in July of 1977, and his company, \uc2 Fonar\uc2 , sold its first commercial scanner in 1980. To protect his patent, Damadian sued corporations like Johnson & Johnson and General Electric when they entered the market with their own versions of his machine.\par \par \uc2 In 1988, President Reagan awarded Dr. Damadian the National Medal of Technology. The next year, he was inducted into the National Inventor`s Hall of Fame. That first scanner he built was sent to the Smithsonian.\par \par \uc2 Damadian protested vigorously in 2003 when a pair of other doctors won the Nobel prize in medicine for their contributions to MRI technology. He acknowledged the importance of their work, but never got his prize. Damadian spent his career perfecting his invention, creating the standup and open MRI scanners to alleviate the claustrophobia caused by the original.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Raymond Damadan whose machine revolutionized medicine by offering a window into the human body died this month in Woodbury, New York. He was 86 years old.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEIST (on camera): It is time for the highs and lows of the week, and our first high goes to the miraculous recovery of a 12-year-old ballplayer at the Little League World Series in Williamsport, Pennsylvania. This morning, Easton Oliverson is talking and walking just six days after falling from the top bunk of a bed at the player`s dormitory and suffering fractures to his skull and face.\par \par \uc2 Now, out of sedation, the star pitcher and outfielder has received an outpouring of support from across the country, including from his baseball hero, \uc2 Los Angeles Dodgers\uc2  star Mookie Betts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOOKIE BETTS, \uc2 LOS ANGELES DODGERS\uc2  STAR: Hey, this is Mookie Betts. I just want you to know that we are praying for you, thinking of you and I hope to see you soon, my man.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [08:50:00]\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Attaboy, Mookie. Easton`s team from the Snow Canyon Little League in Utah played its first game on Friday. And Easton`s spot in the lineup was filled by his little brother, Brogan. With Easton cheering from his hospital bed, 10-year-old Brogan stepped to the plate in the bottom of the fifth inning to a standing ovation. Snow Canyon lost the game, but they`re not done yet. They`ve got a win or go home game later today. Easton`s dad says, it is a miracle he survived that fall and we are so glad he`s on the mend this morning.\par \par \uc2 Our first low is to the nasty slide at a Major League game that came off of the diamond. \uc2 Los Angeles Dodgers\uc2  reporter David Vassegh was excited when the team traveled to play the \uc2 Milwaukee Brewers\uc2  this week because it meant, after years of waiting, he finally could take a ride down the slide from Bernie`s Chalet just over the outfield wall.\par \par \uc2 Brewer`s mascot, Bernie the Brewer, takes a celebratory slide every time the home team hits a home run. But as he learned very painfully, David Vassegh is no Bernie the Brewer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID VASSEGH, \uc2 LOS ANGELES DODGERS\uc2  REPORTER: All right. Here we go. Holy crap. Holy crap. Holy crap.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Dave? Dave?\par \par \uc2 VASSEGH: Hi, guys.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Cut to cast on arm. A trip to the emergency room revealed two fractures in Vassegh`s right wrist and six cracked ribs after that slide. Dodgers third baseman, Justin Turner, who was interviewed by the injured Vassegh before that trip to the ER later tweeted this photo, complete with a commemorative chalk outline and two words that will follow Vassegh back to Los Angeles, holy crap.\par \par \uc2 A sidenote, by the way, that Brewers` tradition, much better back in the day when Bernie would slide into a 20-foot mug of beer. Bring is back, Brew crew.\par \par \uc2 Out next high is a flag waiting celebration of the best of the best among America`s business in the front, party in the back set. The competition stiff again this year at the USA Mullet Championships. Yes. where Americans of all ages stake their claim to our nation`s finest mullet hairstyle. Let us meet a few of this year`s youngest contestants for best in show.\par \par \uc2 In the kid`s division, we have a boy with a mullet as cool as his name. Oh, yes, this is Epic. That`s his name. Going for the triple threat, that`s business, party, and a mohawk, Axel, Bowden and Miles. Looking good fellows. Landry`s mom got fancy with the clippers with these stars and stripes. Love that. And little Rustin doesn`t look old enough to have that much glorious hair.\par \par \uc2 Well, this morning, we are pleased to announce the champion in the 2022 kid`s division. He is, Emmett Bailey of Menomonie, Wisconsin. Yes, just bringing the heat right there. Congratulations, young man. And Wisconsin just showing out, also winning in the teen division on the strength of this mullet from Cayden Kershaw or Wausau, closing the deal, obviously, with that nice feathering up top. Just tremendous work, fellows. You have made 1990s Billy Ray Cyrus and Andre Agassi very proud.\par \par \uc2 Our final low goes to the high a brown bear in Turkey experience after accidentally getting into some hallucinogenic honey. Who among us is not? Let`s start by saying the bear cub is just fine after a bad trip on something called DeliBal, a honey native to the region that can induce euphoria, hallucination and intoxication, as you can see here. She just got into a bad batch.\par \par \uc2 Turkish officials helped the bear to a veterinarian where she recovered quickly, and is now in good health, thank goodness. Though, she has put up black lights and tapestries in her den and started listening to a lot of Pink Floyd.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back with your "Sunday Mug Shots."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:55:00]\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We`ve got more of your "Sunday TODAY Mug Shots" this week. Starting straight across the top, there is Ricardo relaxing in Cozumel, Mexico. Looking good, Ricardo. Here`s David and Ladonna on safari in Botswana joined by some elephant friends. Love it. There is Gina in Chesapeake, Virginia. Hey, Gina. Kim and Cathy, co-workers and faculty members at Fair Lawn High School, right next door to my hometown in New Jersey. Go, Cutters.\par \par \uc2 Down along the bottom, Zach and Lulu checking in Oak Island, New Carolina. Edgar and Irene honeymooning in Lisbon, Portugal. Congratulations, guys. And yes, we`ve been waiting a long time for this one. There is Petey the peacock in Mt. Juliette, Tennessee. We finally got an NBC peacock.\par \par \uc2 Send us a photo of you and your mug with the hashtag, #SundayTODAY, you might see yourself next week. And remember, you can get that big old "Sunday TODAY" mug online at today.com/shop.\par \par \uc2 A programming note, Major League Baseball now is streaming on Peacock. And it gets going early again this morning. Our MLB Sunday Leadoff Coverage of the \uc2 Chicago White Sox\uc2  and \uc2 Cleveland Guardians\uc2  begins today at 11:30 a.m. Eastern on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for spending part of your morning with us. We will see you right back here next week on "Sunday TODAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220823ei8l000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Trend Tied To Working From Home That`s Also Here To Stay, Surveillance Software\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie, Sheinelle Jones, Vicky Nguyen \par \uc2 2,645 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:40:26]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Welcome back. In the wake of the -- you got it right Vicky, in the wake of the pandemic hybrid and remote jobs are here to stay.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: They`re playing Vicky Nguyen`s song. And apparently worker tracking is a part of all of this. A growing number of companies now monitoring their employees even down to the keystroke or the mouse click and the question is, are we OK with that?\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES: Well, our senior consumer investigative correspondent Vicky Nguyen joins us with a closer look at the pros and cons. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN: Yes, believe it or not, there are two sides to this. Good morning. Having your boss serve as big brother, well, it may sound like grounds for a new job. But some experts say this is productivity monitoring and it is here to stay. It`s a tradeoff for the flexibility of working from home to keep it from backfiring though. They say employers should be transparent and treat workers fairly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): For many Americans, working from home is now more feasible than ever. One recent survey showing 58 percent of respondents say they can work remotely at least one day a week, with 35 percent having the option to work from home the entire week. And with more people operating outside of the office, some companies are stepping up worker tracking or so called tattleware or surveillance software. That is supervisors monitoring daily activities through company issued devices, including keyboard usage, screen time, clicks, and more.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would definitely leave that company and try to get job elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If it was a company I really admired and worked for, I wouldn`t be bothered by it.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): A recent "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " investigation found eight of the 10 largest private employers in the U.S. track the productivity metrics of their workers, many of them in real time, leading to many employees being subjected to scores, trackers, idle buttons and more, which can lead to lost wages, and in some cases, even terminations according to "The Times." Earlier this year, we chatted with business ethics Professor J.S. Nelson, who told us people working somewhere with more than 500 employees shouldn`t be on high alert.\par \par \uc2 J.S. NELSON, BUSINESS ETHICS PROFESSOR: We need to think about being photographed, listening through your microphone, keystroke logging software. So recording what you`re typing in at any given time, what websites you`re going to, who you`re communicating with.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): As increased productivity and transparency have been cited as benefits of everyday worker tracking. Experts say workers being treated fairly is crucial.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): When an employee doesn`t feel the ability to control what`s happening. How does it affect their behavior at work?\par \par \uc2 CHASE THIEL, PH.D., BILL DANIELS CHAIR OF BUSINESS ETHICS AT UNIVERSITY OF WYOMING: They feel less responsibility to do good work, to be the best employee they can be to fully engage themselves and monitoring strips that away. It takes them away from their work in that sense, because they`re not as engaged in it.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): And with more and more employees already feeling burned out, some workers argue not every job or task can be monitored digitally, often leading to real work falling outside of tracking data, including face to face meetings.\par \par \uc2 THIEL: You simply can`t track all the behaviors that go into a successful workday. You have to continue to focus on employees work outputs, not necessarily the inputs.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): And while there are creative options out there that might be able to help.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If you work from home, you need a mouse jiggler, it jiggles your mouse to make it seem like you`re working. Some are weighing whether to keep their jobs or take their talents elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Keep it moving.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So then our employee or the company is obligated to tell their workers that they`re monitoring them.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: This is a gray area sort of depends on where you live, Sheinelle. But legal experts, ethics experts have long said, look, if you`re an employer and you`re using it, the best practice is be upfront of that, tell your employees what the expectations are. And if you`re a worker, have that conversation, talk to your boss talk to your H.R. department asked how the data is being collected. How is it being used. At the end of the day, if everybody is doing the right thing? We`re good, but this stuff is here to stay?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, and those are computers probably that are owned by the company that you`re using.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: They can`t put it on your personal devices but so many of us take home our work tablet and laptop and phone so expect it, especially if your company is 500 people or over --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Expected but I think it is the right thing to do for companies to be like just FYI. We can monitor you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, I`m letting you know.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s the right thing to do for employees to do their jobs.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you, Vic.\par \par \uc2 All right, what do you say we switch gears, check in with Mr. Roker. Hey, Al.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. Yes are watching you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You`re definitely being monitored.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I just realized that. Anyway, for today, we are looking at some severe weather from Missouri all the way down into Mobile, Alabama isolated winds low tornado risk, a lot of rain from especially parts of the Springfield all the way down to Little Rock, Memphis, one to two inches of rain per hour possible. We`re going to be watching that. And here in the northeast, we got a bit of a drought going on. Massachusetts, 95 percent of the state is in a drought and you look out west, 71 percent of the country is in severe drought.\par \par \uc2 [07:45:22]\par \par \uc2 And in fact, Lake Mead which is in Nevada, surprise 20 million people with water from Nevada, Arizona, California. July 2000, that water elevation was at 1,200 feet near maximum capacity. Well look at today, 22 years later, it is down to 27 percent of capacity, the lowest levels since 1937. And this southwest mega drought is the driest 22-year period in 1,200 years. So we`re watching this very closely. We need the rain out west.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest weather. Ladies?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, thank you Al.\par \par \uc2 You guys are going to love this one. OK, coming up next, the T.V. anchors using their talents from work to update the world on their baby girl. They`re hilarious. And we`re going to introduce you to them but first check out these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [07:50:52]\par \par \uc2 JEANNETTE REYES, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, speaking of diaper changes, we have some breaking news, an explosion at the diaper station. Fortunately, no injuries. Have been reported and authorities tell us they do have a suspect in custody. Unfortunately, that suspect is not speaking at the moment, but they have linked her to hundreds of other explosions.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT BURTON, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: JR, is, she considered a flight risk?\par \par \uc2 REYES: No, look at her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s so cute.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And by the way, the baby is like the star of that video. Guys,