r fitness giant Peloton. The company announcing plans to hike prices, cut hundreds of jobs, and close a number of locations. NBC`s Stephanie Gosk is here with more on this.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Steph. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Hoda. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Peloton plans to lay off around 780 employees and shutter many of their brick-and-mortar locations in what the CEO is calling an aggressive reduction. And that`s not all.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): Once the leader of the pack, this morning, Peloton is racing to get back on track.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You all look good. You all look focused today.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): The popular fitness giant is cutting hundreds of jobs, closing some retail locations, outsourcing delivery, and raising prices too. It`s Peloton Bike Plus going up $500 and the treadmill by $800. CEO, \uc2 Barry McCarthy\uc2 , who took the reins in February stating, these changes are essential if Peloton is ever going to become cash flow positive.\par \par \uc2 The trendy workout from home business exploded during the pandemic, its stock and popularity soared. But as gyms reopened and the landscape changed, Peloton hit a bumpy stretch. Its stock, tumbling more than 60 percent this year. Peloton has also battled safety issues. Recalling certain treadmills last year after more than 70 incidents, involving the machines were reported, including injuries to children and a six-year-old boy who died.\par \par \uc2 In turn, the company responded by offering full refunds and free services to relocate its treadmills and committed to working on a fix. Peloton also faced a hit after the "Sex in the City" reboot where Mr. Big died of a heart attack following a workout.\par \par \uc2 SARAH JESSICA PARKER, ACTRESS: John.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS NOTH, ACTOR: To new beginnings.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): Peloton striking back with an 