aling with ongoing inflation, every penny counts. So, can you actually skip these supplements and save some money?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): Americans love their supplements. Taken by 31 percent of adults in the U.S., a whopping 80 million people, these pills, powders, and liquids are part of a $50 billion industry domestically. But, are you getting your money`s worth?\par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Preventive Services Task Force\uc2 , which is a nongovernment, independent panel of evidence-based medicine experts reviewed 84 studies, involving hundreds of thousands of people over 30 years. The task force concluded there was little or no benefit to in using vitamin and mineral supplements to prevent cancer, cardiovascular disease, and death.\par \par \uc2 DR. PIETER COHEN, ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR OF MEDICINE, \uc2 HARVARD MEDICAL\uc2  SCHOOL: You really don`t need to take them.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): Dr. Pieter Cohen studies the safety of supplements at \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 . He believes they do have an important place in medicine. Like for people with certain digestive diseases, following specific restrictive diets, or on medications that interfere with nutrient absorption. But he says they`re not for everyone.\par \par \uc2 DR. COHEN: I`m a big fan of multivitamins. But it`s when patients really need them. For most healthy people, there`s just no reason to take these every day.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): Not everyone agrees with the task force`s findings.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA WONG, SENIOR VICE PRESIDENT, \uc2 COUNCIL FOR RESPONSIBLE NUTRITION\uc2 : There are narrow, specific recommendations for healthcare providers.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): Andrea Wong, A Ph.D. researcher leads scientific and regulatory affairs for the \uc2 Council for Responsible Nutrition\uc2 , a trade group that represents 180 manufacturers and suppliers of dietary supplements. Those studies are limited. 