st saying, you`re just so easy to be around. You`re like the easiest, nicest most down to earth legend I`ve ever met.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL CROW, AMERICAN MUSICIAN: Oh my gosh. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: What was it like to have a documentary? I mean that`s exciting. And I`m sure it`s flattering, but also kind of scary, right?\par \par \uc2 CROW: It was kind of terrifying. I mean, especially now because I have people that come up to you. I mean, the most unexpected people come up and say I watched your documentary and, you know, you kind of are laying it all out, all the good stuff and all of the heavy stuff.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What was the most difficult part to reveal?\par \par \uc2 CROW: There was a lot of stuff I`d never talked about. I mean there have been some deaths along the way and that have really affected me certainly John O`Brien`s death from leaving Las Vegas. And, you know, my battle with the real low lows and having to go get help. And, yes, I mean it`s all in there. And actually to be honest, it was really liberating. Finally just talking about it because people think you have like "All I Want To Do" and "Everything is A Winding Road" and you`re upbeat and you`re happy all the time and soak up the sun. But, you know, ultimately it`s your deep cuts that tell the story, so.\par \par \uc2 [08:40:11]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You still write music. You`re still playing music. Your boys are teenagers now.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I can`t.\par \par \uc2 CROW: I can`t.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: How is that even possible?\par \par \uc2 CROW: I don`t know. I mean, it`s --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Do they like your songs?\par \par \uc2 CROW: I don`t know if I`m on their playlist to be honest with you. It`s like Morgan Wallen and like, you know, I don`t know if I`m on their playlist, so.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But they`re musicians too, right?\par \par \uc2 CROW: They`re both very musical, but I have one that`s really into sports. And one that`s really into nature. And I just constantly tell them, you -- music will, you know, help you remember all the memories of your life. But find your passion and go toward that because that`s, that brings joy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And this song that you`re going to sing for us in a little bit is for them?\par \par \uc2 CROW: It is. You know, I`ve never let my kids be in social media. In fact, they don`t have social media. I`m a mean mom. My kids were like, you were in the 1870s. But they`re in the documentary. And I finished the documentary with a song that was really inspired by a conversation with my teenager about what it feels like to be tough stress, you know. They are living through a pandemic. They are worried about their planet. They`re worried about going to school and not being safe, you know, with guns at all. And I didn`t have that stress.\par \par \uc2 And the song is really to remind all of us that every moment is a gift and we have to stay in that moment. Don`t be distracted with a phone in between you and your loved ones. And, you know, make the most of every moment so, I wrote it for them.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`re going to hear it in a moment. I don`t know if you heard us talking with Carson about this Woodstock 99, which everyone is talking about with this \uc2 Netflix\uc2  documentary.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You were there. You experience some of this awfulness. What is it like that suddenly people are talking about it again. You`ve seen the documentary.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Yes. You know, it`s funny, the first Woodstock was monumental in my life and as an inspiration for me. I was at the second Woodstock, which totally changed the trajectory of my career in 1996. And then this one, I think, was a perfect illustration of how ugly greed can be. The whole thing was set up, you know, and trying to make money and it really played into a lot of fear and just a bad energy. So and we still, you know, it`s like they said in the \uc2 HBO\uc2  documentary tying it to the January 6th, and the anger and unrest. When you get a mob, it certainly exacerbates it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s interesting to look back at it, but we`re looking forward.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Literally we`re looking forward because in a few moments after this break, you`re going to sing us your new song "Forever." It`s coming up. But first this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We are back. We are so ready. The amazing Sheryl Crow is here performing her latest single "Forever." Sheryl, take it away.\par \par \uc2 [08:45:03]\par \par \uc2 CROW: Two, three, four.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That is so good. I know. We`re all crying. Sheryl Crow, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You sound great. Beautiful harmony. Sheryl is going to come back in our next half hour, looking forward.\par \par \uc2 Carson, over to you.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Well, I`m talking to Matt Abdoo. We`re about to make this delicious sandwich. You are clapping for Sheryl Crow. But you have this, you have the --\par \par \uc2 MATT ABDOO, CHEF AT PIG BEACH BARBECUE RESTAURANT: Tough to follow a Sheryl Crow. But we got Italian beef sandwich. We`re ready to rock you guys how easy it is to do at home.\par \par \uc2 DALY: The Bears, this show that nobody seems to be watching that`s making the spread famous and it`s very easy to make. We`ll show you how coming up. But first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:51:25]\par \par \uc2 DALY: It was a great morning. Sheryl Crow, we`ll about to eat some Italian beefs, good times. Today Food, we are back. And did you guys know that Italian beef, the sandwich, it`s booming in shops all across the country all thanks to the show, the series on FX called The Bear. It`s about a young superstar chef who leaves the world of fine dining to take over his family sandwich shop in Chicago. Now the house specialty of the shop is Italian beef. And people on the show and in real life are dying to get their hands on this sandwich. We have our buddy Matt Abdoo, who literally Uncle Al and I talked about Pig Beach once a week around here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: Should we go see Matt this weekend? We are always talking about you.\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Yes, you should.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Executive Chef of Pig Beach probably in new locations in New York and West Palm Beach. The Bear is a show that you like to watch too.\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Oh God, it was so good. It was so true to form on so many ways that when that show came out, I was just like, oh my god. It crushed the whole season in two nights streaming. The main actor just did such an incredible job. It was so cool to watch.\par \par \uc2 DALY: What does the history of Italian beef, like it came into my life much later through, we would order it from Portillo`s, which in other places in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Well, it`s one of the most iconic places that didn`t again have its huge claim to fame. But essentially, it`s like a hot roast beef sandwich. It`s dipped in this beautiful beef broth and finished nice crusty bread from like this famous bakery in Chicago. And it`s just all those great things. You got that beautiful beefy meat. You got these pickled vegetables on top of over the heat.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Let`s get into it. How do you make this at home, Matt?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Let`s do it. So this is our version. This is what we`ve been running at our Pig Beach Queens location for a happy Armenian. This show inspired us to start doing this dish at our restaurants so we`re super excited for people to come in and try it. Our starts with this beautiful wet rub.\par \par \uc2 Now, traditional is probably more of like Italian seasonings and whatnot. But in this bowl here we got some plain old yellow mustard and some honey. And what we add to it is some fresh chopped garlic, some black pepper, some Oregano, brown sugar and chili flakes and rosemary, sorry.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to just mix all that together and make what we call a wet rob which is in this bowl right here. And with this wet rub is beautiful. It`s perfect for steaks.\par \par \uc2 DALY: What is this?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: That`s a beef paste. That also has to go in here. That`s a -- it`s a beef base that kind of like better than bouillon or beef paste. It`s beefy broth flavor add water to it but if you do this --\par \par \uc2 DALY: And then it turns out here, yes?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: That`s right. So we`re going to take this paste.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Is this something you`re doing like the night before or hours before?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Yes, you`re going to all have this night before we can let it actually marinate with this on it. You`re going to smear all this wet rub all over the meat. It`s top round or top sirloin or iron work is great. And we`re going to take this. We`re going to roast in the oven really, really hot in 450 degrees, for few munites --\par \par \uc2 DALY: What is the beef, I go into a butcher and I say I`m going to make the sandwiches, what I`m asking for?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Top round or eye round or top sirloin --\par \par \uc2 DALY: A roast?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Yes, exactly, because you`re basically making roast beef, you`re making homemade roast beef.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 DALY: How is it, guys, I know you`re eating sandwich, just jump in here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You can hear all the noises.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, it`s so good.\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: So once he comes out of the oven nice and roasty, we`re going to make our broth and what we`re basically doing is fortifying a beef broth. Now you can go through the efforts of roasting bones and making it from scratch. Or you can just go buy some that pre made beef broth that comes in the car in, sweat out some garlic and onions. We`re going to add our roasted beef roast.\par \par \uc2 DALY: What did you cook this for I`m sorry, I missed that part. How long was it cooked?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: The onions and --\par \par \uc2 DALY: No other meat?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: This is going to be roast in the oven hot heat for about 20 minutes to sear it.\par \par \uc2 DALY: OK.\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: And then while that searing, we`re making our broth --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Finish cooking into that so to that garlic and onions are starting out writing more of that beef paste is going to fortify that broth. Some Italian seasonings is the trick, you get all that Italian seasonings favorites, it`s parsley, it`s rosemary, it`s thyme, it`s chili flake, it`s garlic, and then we`re going to put it to broth and so covers up halfway and you`re going to put it back in the oven and let this cook for about another hour or so until the internal temperature gets down to 130 which is like medium to medium rare.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: You`re kind like brazing.\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Yes, it`s like half roast, half braids. This is a slow cooking, and keep that meat nice and juicy. So when you cool it down the next day after --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Savannah is going at it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: When you cut it that is this like a grain situation again for this matter?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Yes, always whenever you`re cutting any kind of meat, you want to cut it against the grain for tenderness but what you basically want to do is get as thin as you possibly can, most people do this on a deli slicer in restaurants you can get it super, super thin you don`t need perfect sizes, you just need it thin.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 [08:55:08]\par \par \uc2 DALY: You do make this out at home?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: No.\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: If you ever want to make it at home and you want to kind of cheat, like don`t tell anybody that you buy like deli roast beef and basically make this broth and then when the time come --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Yes, so this broth from the meat was roasted in. We`re going to take it, strain it, we`re going to add our meat back into that broth to get it nice and cooked and hot. And then in this pot over here, we some of the meat. And the biggest thing guys, the Italian beef sandwich you got to get all that broth in there and. And now at the restaurants they have a separate wet dip or dry and they usually take the whole bed, they put it into that broth, get it nice and wet. And then they top it with some sweet peppers. And some --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: You need a roll that can handle that. What do you suggest?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: So there`s a famous bakery in Chicago, I believe it`s called Toronto bakery, and it`s kind of like an in between French bread, Italian bread, it`s nice and crusty and hearty that can hold up to the juiciness of it. But literally when you eat this sandwich in Chicago, it`s like falling apart because it`s so wet from all that broth, you rolled up nice and tight and people like eat it with a knife and fork because it`s going to fall apart. But --\par \par \uc2 DALY: It`s so good.\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Whatever you want, however you enjoying it, broth inside, dip it, but those pickled vegetables, right, they added a nice --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Brightness to that hearty meat flavor of that sandwich and the sweetness from the sweet peppers and everything that`s in there. It`s just perfect sandwich.\par \par \uc2 DALY: How is your happy hour version of this going?\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Amazing. We can`t wait for more people to come and check it out and have it from 4:00 to 7:00 every day. We`re doing a happy hour with this sandwich, being one of the featured items on it. So we look forward --\par \par \uc2 DALY: And you recommend this show the --\par \par \uc2 ABDOO: Oh, it was so good. Jeremy Alan White, the main actor, did such a great job. There`s so many like a fine dining chef, my days adult post believing that to do a barbecue restaurant there`s somebody like --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220816ei8f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for August 5, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kelly O`Donnell, Ali Vitali, Anne Thompson, Sam  Brock, Stephanie Gosk, Andrea Mitchell, Emilie Ikeda, Boyd Huppert \par \uc2 3,836 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the surprising new blockbuster report on jobs.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Over half a million new jobs created doubling predictions. The unemployment rate matching the 50 year low we saw just before the pandemic dampening immediate recession fears though Americans still struggling with crippling inflation and a key Senate Democrat now supporting the massive climate and economic bill.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the pressure mounting on the President with NBC News reporting some key Democratic donors are frustrated ahead of 2024.\par \par \uc2 Just in, the breaking verdict in the Alex Jones trial ordered to pay $45 million in damages.\par \par \uc2 The severe weather leading to more than 1000 flight cancellations nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the latest on that deadly lightning strike. The rising death toll.\par \par \uc2 The house fire leaving 10 dead including three children. The latest on the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Demanding justice. The team who died under the weight of police officers. His family saying he was George Floyd before George Floyd. My investigation tonight.\par \par \uc2 Indiana poised to become the first in the country to pass a near-total ban on abortion after Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 And the unforgettable umpire who`s flipping the script and inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening everyone. Pessimism about the state of the American economy turned to welcomed surprise today with new numbers confirming the nation is still cranking out jobs at a brisk pace. A whopping 528,000 new jobs added last month. The unemployment rate dropping to 3.5 percent tying the pre-pandemic low. The figures suggesting the country has not edged off into a recession.\par \par \uc2 Not to be ignored however, inflation still eating into paychecks at a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  poised to raise its key interest rate next month for the fifth time this year in an effort to slow the economy and bring prices down. After suffering bad economic headlines for weeks, an enthusiastic President Biden today trumpeting the job gains along with a steady drop in gas prices. Kelly O`Donnell reporting now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: An unexpected summer scorcher, the hot jobs market. Employers added twice the number of jobs predicted for July, 528,000. Unemployment fell to 3.5 percent, taking the U.S. back to pre-COVID Times.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today, there are more people working in America than before the pandemic began. In fact, there are more people working in America at any point in American history.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading this job growth, leisure and hospitality followed by professional services, health care, transportation and warehousing.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH, U.S. SECRETARY OF LABOR: And it shows that there is some signs this economy that is still very strong.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: But that undeniably good news overshadowed for millions of Americans by 40-year high inflation. In South Carolina, Angelina Scott faces painful choices.\par \par \uc2 ANGELINA SCOTT, RESIDENT, SOUTH CAROLINA: The economy right now is making me choose between my medication and food, between my medication, food, and bills.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: She and her husband Matthew are both working but can`t cover all their rising expenses.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I feel like the economy is really suffocating and slowly. It feels like no one cares. It doesn`t matter.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: A July survey finds 51 percent of Americans now don`t have an emergency fund that can cover three months of expenses. And about half of the country`s elderly now struggle to pay for essentials. While a President`s political fate is often linked to the state of the economy, new NBC News reporting finds a restlessness among Democratic donors who would prefer a different nominee for 2024.\par \par \uc2 One democratic fundraiser speculated about the president stepping aside saying there`s going to be a lot of pressure coming up post-midterms, yet most see President Biden as the strongest contender in a potential rematch against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As Americans cope with higher prices, another critical snapshot on the economy is expected Wednesday, the consumer price index, the latest inflation number will be released. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Kelly O`Donnell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, Senate Democrats are voicing optimism about passing their big climate tax and health care bill. Ali Vitali is at the Capitol. Ali, how did this finally come together?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  now in lockstep with the rest of her caucus after Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  accepted her changes to this bill including removing a provision that would have closed the carried interest tax loophole which benefits wealthy hedge fund managers, plus adding billions in drought relief funds.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s full steam ahead. Democrats sprinting headlong into a rare Saturday session to pass the signature climate and deficit reduction package. Schumer telling me today he`s confident that his party will stand together to pass this legislation, which at the earliest would be Sunday. Lester?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:26]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Ali, thank you. And just breaking, a jury in Austin, Texas ordering Alex Jones to pay millions in punitive damages.\par \par \uc2 Anne Thompson joins me now. Anne, what happened in court today?\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, this is a big win for the victim`s parents. A Texas jury today ordering Alex Jones and his company to pay more than $45 million in punitive damages to the parents of Jesse Lewis who died in the Sandy Hook School massacre. That`s on top of yesterday`s decision, which means Jones and his company must pay Jesse`s parents a total of $49.3 million. That`s a lot of money but a fraction of the $135 to $270 million a forensics economist says Jones and his business are worth.\par \par \uc2 Jones faces two similar lawsuits from other victims` families. He did not show up in court for today`s verdict. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Anne Thompson, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, some of the busiest airports in the country from D.C. to New York are on ground delays as severe storms move in Thousands of flights delayed or canceled nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, tens of millions of Americans are under heat alerts in the Northeast and the plains. Temperature surging today into the 90s and triple digits. And increasingly severe weather is being tied to climate change. Here`s Sam Brock.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The grueling heat that`s gripped the country is showing little sign of letting up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Central air is key.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: More than 60 million people tonight against sweltering under heat alert, with cities in the Northeast seeing their warmest temperatures in a decade. Dallas has now topped triple digits 40 times this year, almost doubling the annual average. Then, there`s the storms. Earth cam video from the Washington Monument showing lightning strikes around the same time four people were struck across from the White House killing three, including a couple in their 70s.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I was just in a state of shock. I just couldn`t believe it.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The ramp and cruelty of a changing climate felt deeply on the Isle de Jean Charles about 90 minutes outside of New Orleans. The signs are everywhere figuratively and literally for the Native American groups who`ve lived here for generations.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Were standing with one foot in the water and one foot on land.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Chris Pranay (PA) has spent all 57 years of his life on this land. Now, the Choctaw Nation is part of an unprecedented relocation receiving nearly $50 million in a federal grant to help around 100 people seek higher ground.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You being forced because of erosion and hurricanes to move inland.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The move is completely voluntary. But Mother Nature is clearly closing in. The island transforming from 22,000 acres in 1963 to just 300 by 2017.\par \par \uc2 Chris`s house somehow managed to go 20 years without damage from a hurricane until Ida which not only left its mark here, but rip through most of the remaining properties on the island as the barrier of land that used to blunt storms continues to erode.\par \par \uc2 Now, a pathway to a new aisle some 40 houses in Gray, Louisiana which families will occupy any day now.\par \par \uc2 PAT FORBES, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, LOUISIANA OFFICE OF COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT: They can literally withstand 150-mile-an-hour winds.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: In what could be a playbook for future cities.\par \par \uc2 FORBES\uc2 : We know there are more occurrences like this come and not just in Louisiana but around the country and around the world. So, we`ve got to start learning how to do it right.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The harsh reality of Coping With Climate displacement as communities like Isle de Jean Charles look to preserve their past. Sam Brock, NBC News New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A House fire overnight in eastern Pennsylvania killed 10 people including three children. According to a local paper, a volunteer firefighter who responded to the tragic scene said that the victims were all relatives. Tonight, there is an ongoing investigation into what caused that fire.\par \par \uc2 Tonight the battle over abortion is playing out in Indiana as that state moves toward banning almost all abortions. Stephanie Gosk now with late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Indiana is on the cusp of passing a ban on abortion, the first state to take that step since the supreme court overturn Roe v. Wade medical. The Senate still has to weigh in one last time. It has been anything but a smooth process. The battle waged over the exceptions,\par \par \uc2 KAREN ENGLEMAN, REPUBLICAN STATE REPRESENTATIVE, INDIANA: Ending the life of an unborn child is neither necessary nor an evidence-Pbased treatment for rape.\par \par \uc2 RITA FLEMING, DEMOCRATIC STATE REPRESENTATIVE, INDIANA: Does this apply to a fifth grader who has been a victim of incest?\par \par \uc2 [19:10:04]\par \par \uc2 ENGLEMAN: It does.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: In the end, the ban includes exceptions for the mother`s health and for victims of rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 DR. NANCY WOZNIAK, OBGYN: The inclination of people is to -- the pregnancy is a result of this, that this is just going to be something that compounds her trauma. And I would just respectfully say, not always.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Dr. Nancy Wozniak is an OBGYN against abortion. She worries some may take advantage of the exceptions.\par \par \uc2 WOZNIAK: There`s always a risk of somebody exploiting that ability to justify getting an abortion.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: On the other side, Dr. Caitlin Bernard argues exceptions for rape and incest don`t really protect the victims. She performed an abortion on a 10- year-old rape victim whose story captured headlines and caught President Biden`s attention.\par \par \uc2 From your experience, do women who are the victims of rape and incest want to come forward and talk about it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD, OBGYN: Not even slightly. You know, they`ve been through something that is probably the most traumatic thing they`ve been through.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: When it comes to the health of the mother, there are challenges there as well.\par \par \uc2 It sounds pretty straightforward. You`re allowed to get an abortion if your health is compromised by the pregnancy or your life. Is it straightforward?\par \par \uc2 DR. AMY CALDWELL, OBGYN: Not at all. There is no way that we could come up with a list of exceptions for every possible scenario.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Dr. Amy Caldwell works one day a week at Planned Parenthood but she spends most of her time in the hospital where doctors worry about criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: We are frightened. There have been a lot of group chats. There`s been discussions about you know, do we stay? Do we go?\par \par \uc2 GOSK: You mean, do you leave all the other?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Leave Indiana? Leave the hospital?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The law will likely get passed in the coming days before going to the governor for the final say. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, Indianapolis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, we are keeping a close eye on soaring tensions between China and the U.S. as China ramps up a massive show of force near Taiwan. Here`s Andrea Mitchell.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): China`s military escalation at a fever pitch, its highest in memory on all fronts, air, sea, land, cyber, and space. Fighter jets circling Taiwan, missiles fired over the island, nine landing in waters off Japan, detouring commercial flights and rattling a major U.S. ally. All of it a furious response to House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s visit supporting the democratic government in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 China claims Taiwan is its territory.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: These provocative actions are a significant escalation. China has chosen to overreact and use Speaker Pelosi`s visit as a pretext to increase provocative military activity in and around the Taiwan Strait.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: All these raising U.S. fears of an accidental confrontation.\par \par \uc2 ADM. \uc2 JAMES STAVRIDIS\uc2  (RET), NBC NEWS CHIEF INTERNATIONAL SECURITY AND DIPLOMATIC ANALYST: Upping those fighters that are potentially going nose to nose in and around Taiwan, that`s not Tony Blinken. You know, it`s Goose and Maverick. These are young men and women. They`re fired up.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: In the Taiwan Strait, a Chinese nuclear submarine below the water, warships above.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: Within these zones, they`re firing missiles, torpedoes, naval gunfire. It`s a kind of a de facto blockade.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And China has cut off all lines of communication with the U.S. No high level military or naval talks. No cooperation on illegal migration, drug trafficking, or climate change. And China sanctioned Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and her family. Pelosi`s message to China tonight.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): But they will not isolate Taiwan by preventing us to travel there. They are not doing our travel schedule.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Andrea, if I can change subjects slightly here and talk about the tensions with Russia. There`s news following Britney Griner`s prison sentence.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (on camera): Indeed, Lester. Now that the trial is over, Russia`s Foreign Minister now says for the first time they are willing to talk about a prisoner swap but only in private. So, the U.S. is going to test whether \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is really serious. President Biden said today, I`m hopeful we`re working hard. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Andrea Mitchell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the baby formula crisis. Why some parents are still struggling to find food for their children? What families need to know?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, now to a story we have been following closely, the baby formula shortage that left many families scrambling. Tonight, many of them say they are still not out of the woods and all this. Here`s Emilie Ikeda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The nightmare that has been ruling parents lives for months --\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Parents are desperately scrambling to find baby formula.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Isn`t over yet. We spoke with mom Felicia Edwyn back in May.\par \par \uc2 FELICIA EDWYN, MOTHER OF TWO: It`s just not a good feeling to be on edge at every moment.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Since then, she and her husband Toby say there`s been little relief.\par \par \uc2 TOBY EDWYN, FATHER OF TWO: It really gets down to alright we only have about a day or twos worth. What`s our next move going to be?\par \par \uc2 F. EDWYN: And that in itself is nerve-racking.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: While the situation has improved some, our look at store shelves this week found big gaps remain where formula should be. The latest nationwide data shows a 27 percent out-of-stock rate for powdered formula. That`s despite the reopening of Abbott`s critical Sturgis manufacturing plant which resumed production earlier this summer, but not yet for all products.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has had domestic manufacturers ramp up production and flown in the equivalent of nearly 64,000,000 eight-ounce bottles of formula from abroad. The White House this week announced more flights to come but also acknowledge that for many families the problem persists.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: This has not gone away and we do understand that there`s a lot more work to do.\par \par \uc2 SARAH CHAMBERLAIN, MOTHER: Back ordered, cancelled.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: In New Jersey, Sarah Chamberlain hasn`t been able to order a single can of specialty formula online since April. She now depends on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  groups to track down the products which her 8-year-old who suffers from a metabolic disorder relies on for 70 percent of her nutrition.\par \par \uc2 What`s it like to be a mother in this kind of situation?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERLAIN: It`s difficult. I mean, her health and her development is at risk without this formula. And that is an incredible burden for parent.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Industry experts anticipate supply to normalize in the fall, a long road ahead for families still weathering a day-to-day struggle. Emilie Ikeda, NBC News Montclair, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next tonight, my report of the death of an unarmed Black teenager in the wake of the George Floyd case where his parents say his death deserves another look.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back now with the case of a black teenager who died after a struggle with police nearly four years ago, one that Anton Black`s family says has similarities to the death of George Floyd. But Anton Black`s case is not widely known outside Maryland. And now, there are questions about why his death was ruled an accident.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): In Greensboro, Maryland, September 2018, an unarmed Black teenager flees from police. There`s a chase and a struggle all caught on body cam. 19-year-old Anton Black under the weight of police officers crying out to his mother.\par \par \uc2 ANTON BLACK, KILLED IN POLICE CUSTODY: How are you guys doing? My name is Anton Black.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Anton was a star athlete and a budding model.\par \par \uc2 ANTONE BLACK, FATHER OF ANTON BLACK: This is my baby boy, Anton.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And now, his father says Anton is an example of a broken system outraged that his son`s death was declared an accident.\par \par \uc2 ANTONE BLACK: You begging for your life and they don`t get off of him. How is that an accident?\par \par \uc2 My son was George Floyd before George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: There is at least one connection between George Floyd`s case and Anton Black`s. Dr. David Fowler, Maryland`s former chief medical examiner who signed off on Anton`s autopsy report testified two years later for officer Derek Chauvin`s defense that Floyd`s death was undetermined.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROGER MITCHELL, FORMER CHIEF MEDICAL EXAMINER, WASHINGTON D.C.: If Anton Black is an accident, and George Floyd is undetermined, then how many other cases that we have no idea about are accidents and undetermined?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Dr. Roger Mitchell is an expert in in-custody police deaths.\par \par \uc2 How many in-custody deaths are there in the U.S. each year?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: No one knows.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: There are roughly 2000 medical examiners and coroners in America. Dr. Mitchell says there is no central database so no one knows how many people like Anton die in police custody every year. But he says he has a solution, adding a single box on a death certificate.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: A checkbox on the U.S. standard death certificate that allows us to see from a physician`s hand who`s dying in custody no matter what the manner of death so that we can have any objective public health measure of this issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Dr. Fowler declined to speak with NBC News. Don`t miss the new episode of "DATELINE" tonight at 10:00 p.m. Eastern, 9:00 Central.\par \par \uc2 When we come back here tonight, how one beloved umpire`s unique style on the field is changing the game and inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: While summer softball season hits a fever pitch, one umpire is calling games unlike any other. Boyd Huppert from our KARE 11 in Minneapolis has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LOUIS WILLIAMS IV, UMPIRE: Three balls, two strikes.\par \par \uc2 BOYD HUPPERT, REPORTER, KARE 11: By tradition, umpires are an anonymous bunch. It`s their nature.\par \par \uc2 You know, the umpires name?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, I don`t.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: But one field over at the Minnesota girls 10 and under state softball tournament --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Are you ready?\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: -- everyone knows this name.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: My name Blue Lou and you know what I got.\par \par \uc2 KIDS: What do you got?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I got a team that`s hotter than hot.\par \par \uc2 KIDS: How hot is hot?\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: And loves his strike calls.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Strike.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He does like the one who read balls.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: Part game official.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I believe that we will win.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: Part motivational speaker, part acrobat. Everyone knows Blue Lou but who is he?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Louis Williams IV.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: Louis Williams IV came to Minnesota to play football at St. Cloud State University.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, I had like a year and a half left of school. They cut the team because of budget issues.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: Louis stayed and finished his engineering degree, struck a pose with his mother, and went to work in his field. The field of manufacturing engineering and the infield.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Last year, around this time, I read a book it`s called The Compound Effect. And a book basically was talking about if you got a talent and put it on display for the world to see.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: You might say Louis jumped at the opportunity. How much do they love him?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can he switch?\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: That`s a team pleading to have Louis move from another game to theirs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please, please.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: The girls will encounter plenty of umpires they won`t remember and one they will never forget.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: As long as the kids have fun, I do 100 backflips.\par \par \uc2 HUPPERT: Boyd Huppert, NBC News, Becker, Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s terrific. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Friday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good ni