ch. Sightings of the enormous animals increasing in recent weeks with this encounter caught on camera. Where all of these whale sightings are happening and what you should know before you head into the water.\par \par \uc2 Plus, protecting pandas. Researchers in China working on a new facial recognition software for the adorable bears.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANIS MACKEY FRAYER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Is this the same panda?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (on camera): A different one?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Different one, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: How they`re hoping this technology could help conservation efforts while also helping panda fans identify their furry friends.\par \par \uc2 And sophomore sailor. We will introduce you to the 16-year-old who just sailed across the Atlantic Ocean entirely by himself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAL CURRIER, TEEN SAILOR CROSSED THE ATLANTIC ON HIS OWN: I`ll miss all of you. I`ll miss any humans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: He`ll join us live to take us inside his epic 28-day adventure. Today, Tuesday, July 26, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Hamilton, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Celebrating our 40th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Lebanon, Indiana.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: And Waco, Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sending love to our daughter Charlotte.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Back in Long View, Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Las Vegas and Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For my mom`s birthday.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Mother/daughter trip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Santa Rosa, California.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Hi to our Mimi in Memphis, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We love you.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Hello Anniston, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Erickson (ph) family vacation.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Minneapolis to the "TODAY" show.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: Love when they bring props.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We do. We do. The props and the t-shirts make it. Welcome back. It`s Tuesday, guys. We`re so happy you`re starting your day with us. Craig has the morning off. Mr. Tom Llamas joins us at the table.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: How are you, guys?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Good morning. By the way, if you missed the first hour, don`t worry, you can catch up anytime. Just remember to set your DVR every morning at 7:00 a.m. and you can always rewind.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Let`s get to our news at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 Anxious Americans and investors closely watching the state of the economy this morning with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  expected to raise interest rates to get inflation under control. NBC`s Tom Costello is at the Fed`s headquarters in D.C. for big meetings there. They start today.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Tom, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hoda, good morning.\par \par \uc2 It is no surprise what the Fed is going to do. It has telegraphed this very widely and clearly. It intends to raise rates again tomorrow, likely by three-quarters of a percentage point to bring inflation under control. As we`ve said many times, inflation running at the hottest level since 1981. We`re paying more for gasoline, for food, for clothing, for transportation, you name it. And as a result, the Fed has to bring that under control without tipping the economy into a recession, which brings up the second equation for this week.\par \par \uc2 Is the economy already in recession? We should find that out this week with the second quarter GDP numbers because typically, a recession is defined as two quarters of contracting economic activity. Contracting growth. That`s an oxymoron, but it means the economy is shrinking. So, we should find out this week if the economy has already tipped into a recession. If it has, it is the strangest recession that the Fed has ever seen because unemployment is near a 50-year low, wages are still going up, modestly, consumer spending still strong, and the overall state of the economy seems to be in pretty good shape.\par \par \uc2 The question then becomes, well, if this is really a recession, is it the true definition of a recession. Nonetheless, higher interest rates mean we`re all going to pay more, of course, for credit cards, for car loans. And the Fed also influences mortgage rates as well. Guys, back to you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Tom, while we have you. Let`s talk for a quick second about the pilot shortage. We`ve talked a lot about that over the past weeks. And it looks like Congress may be taking some action when it comes to the age limit.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Yes, Senator Lindsey Graham in the Senate is posing that they raise the retirement age. The mandatory rage -- raise, rather -- or age, rather, from 65 to 67. But still require older pilots get a medical checkup every six months or so. That would help address the national shortage we have, the national shortage of pilots.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is also a similar suggestion in the House. The question is, might this move forward to the point that Congress, in fact, raises the retirement age. We know that we`re going to lose 14,000 pilots over the next four years because of early -- because of retirements required at the age of 65.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And a lot of pilots say, wait a minute, I`m 64 and nine months and I am just as good as ever. I want to keep flying. Why not raise it to 67 and help address the pilot shortage. The big pilots` union that`s representing pilots, they are against this right now, Hoda.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Tom Costello for us there at the Fed in D.C. To, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let`s move to the weather. The extreme heat setting new records from coast to coast this week. And those high temps are helping fuel this season`s largest wildfire in California. The oak fire is now threatening thousands of homes near Yosemite National Park, enforcing residents to evacuate. The fire is so large now it is visible from space and the smoke is choking the San Francisco Bay area too nearly 200 miles away.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: The White House physician says President Biden`s COVID symptoms are almost completely resolved. The president made his first public appearance in several days with a virtual meeting yesterday. And during that event, he said he is feeling better and hopes to be back to work in person by the end of the week. The president is taking the antiviral drug Paxlovid for his symptoms.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`ve been on shark watch in recent weeks. But now, a different creature of the sea is turning heads. As we told you yesterday, there was a huge humpback whale caught on camera. It soared out of the water, it landed right on a small boat. NBC`s Emilie Ikeda is in Massachusetts where the jaw-dropping encounter took place.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Emilie. It`s just hard to believe that nobody got hurt in that.\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Hoda, remarkable is right. Especially when you consider the size of humpback whales which can grow to about the length of that large boat behind me. Right now, an estimated 1,000 of the whales are swimming just off of New England shores, looking to get their fill. And when whales are feeding, they`re not paying attention to anything else. That`s why experts say, if you`re heading out on the water, be alert.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): This morning, beachgoers on whale watch. And Plymouth, Massachusetts has a front-row seat to the feeding frenzy. One boat getting a little too close to the action.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I just saw the boat go freaking flying. It was crazy. It was nuts. I was like -- I couldn`t believe that thing was still floating after that.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): A humpback whale breaching the surface, Sunday, in what`s called lounge feeding.\par \par \uc2 LAURA HOWES, DIRECTOR OF RESEARCH AND EDUCATION, UNIVERSITY OF MASSACHUSETTS BOSTON: So, they`re feeding on very schools of fish. And to do that, they need to rush up at the surface.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): And what goes up, must come down. All 40 tons of it. The same schools of fish drawing crowds of boaters are attracting whales, too, creating a precarious situation according to researcher Laura Howes.\par \par \uc2 HOWES: It does pose a risk for human safety and for the whale`s safety. Because when whales are feeding, they tend to be a bit distracted just like a human might be on Thanksgiving when you`re eating a big feast.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): Remarkably, no one was injured in the Sunday morning surprise. But the close call amongst a torrent off recent whale sightings off of Massachusetts Coast. Michael Manfredi has been watching in awe from his paddleboard the last few weekends.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MANFREDI, HAD CLOSE ENCOUNTER WITH WHALE: And all of a sudden you see the front of the whale, just the mouth opening up like this, and then it opened wide and is coming out of the water, and there`s fish flowing out of the whale`s mouth.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): The New York City area experiencing its own surge in whale activity in recent years according to a new study. Reporting, they`re lingering for longer periods of time, too.\par \par \uc2 HOWES: If there is food around, it`s plentiful, the whales will find it. So, it`s not unordinary for whales to sometimes be close to shore.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): While most humpback whales are no longer endangered, a quarter of their deaths on the East Coast are blamed on boats, Howes says. That`s why you should always be alert on the water. Avoid feeding marine animals. And most importantly, create a buffer zone of 100 feet from whale activity so glimpses of the wild wonder aren`t taking away more than your breath.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (on camera): Well, the safest place to see whales is simply from solid ground. You can try to seek a broader perspective by going to a higher vantage point. Also, consider using tools like binoculars or cameras with zoom lenses to improve your whale watching experience. Guys, back to you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Emilie, thank you. All right. So, should we do a little "Boost", guys?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Let`s hit it. All right. It`s pretty common for teenagers to be completely embarrassed when their parents do something for no apparent reason. But there is a teenager who deserves our sympathy for her dad`s action. Take a look at the scene from a California restaurant.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Excuse me, my daughter thinks that you are so cute.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Watch it. Oh, my God. 15-year-old, Sophie (ph) --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, my God. I love her.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: -- slid under the table after that comment. It turns out Sophie`s brother was the one who gave the dad the idea. After --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK. Now, look at the brother.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The brother --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: His looks all innocent.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: He`s trying --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But, no.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know, he was like, what? Oh. So, the dad heard Sophie whisper it and then the -- then the son told the dad. Anyway, Sophie now says she`s on high alert around her brother. She can`t say anything.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She can`t.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: With what?\par \par \uc