in a sailboat. Currier beginning the trip in Marion, Massachusetts last month.\par \par \uc2 CAL CURRIER, SAILED THE ATLANTIC OCEAN: I`ll miss all of you. I`ll miss any human.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: departing on his 30 foot sailboat, The Argo, and after about 28 days alone at sea, the California teen finally reuniting with his parents in Lagos, Portugal. From start to finish, Currier ultimately travelling nearly 4,000 miles across the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 CURRIER: So we`re heading north, to the east of Cape Cod.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: While Currier grew up around boats, he only started taking sailing lessons seriously this January. But a love for the sea runs in his family, both Currier`s father and grandfather are transatlantic sailors. The teen saying they both inspired him to make the trip across the world`s second largest ocean.\par \par \uc2 CURRIER: Time is short. Life is awesome, so do awesome things right now.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So when a sailboat he bought for $12,000 from 90 year old Sandy Van Zandt.\par \par \uc2 CURRIER: And there`s the stuff that Sandy brought over.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From the `50s?\par \par \uc2 CURRIER: From the `50s.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And he crossed the Atlantic alone. Now Currier is likely the youngest person to ever sail solo across the Atlantic from west to east.\par \par \uc2 CURRIER: I want to show other kids, they aren`t too young to do amazing things. Just think it up, ask for help, make it happen.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And he`s doing just that, reaching this ambitious goal all before starting his junior year of high school.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And Cal Currier, he joins us now live from Lagos in Portugal. Cal, before we get to everything that happened and how you`re feeling. We want to know, first of all, how your parents told you it was OK for you to do this when you just started taking lessons in January?\par \par \uc2 CURRIER: So, first off, my dad is very adventurous and my mom is also quite adventurous, and the whole brainstorming process for what I was going to do this summer happened with my dad. So, he was kind of there for me deciding I was going to do it. And my mom took a little bit more convincing. But after a couple long conversations and a couple long nights, we convinced her that it was safe to do.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, Cal, what was it like because an Atlantic crossing is not easy sailing or smooth sailing, is it?\par \par \uc2 CURRIER: So regularly, the safest way is from east to west. There`s nicer winds and less storms that way. I went west to east partly because I was already on the East Coast, and partly out of some level of ignorance. In this certain crossing, I was extremely lucky with the wind and with the weather, so I only actually had one rainy night and only a couple of nights of dangerously high winds.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I mean, one rainy night is enough for all of us in the middle of the ocean. Tell us what was the most physical or the most difficult part. I know physically, it`s tough. Mentally, I read at one point you`re only supposed to sleep in 90 minute increments and you overslept.\par \par \uc2 CURRIER: Yes. So the physical aspect isn`t all that difficult. The mental aspect is definitely the hardest part by leagues. Loneliness and boredom, and sleep deprivation are the greatest challenges that I had to deal with. Boredom because I mis-planned and didn`t bring enough books or entertainment, loneliness because I`m alone for 28 days which isn`t easy for anyone, and sleep deprivation because you can`t sleep very long, and.\par \par \uc2 [08:40:00]\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Cal, you know, being out there on the open ocean at night, it is some of the darkest spots on this planet. What did you learn about yourself?\par \par \uc2 CURRIER: I`d say what I learned most about myself was just how important people are to me. I`ve always considered myself to be somewhat of an introverted extrovert, where I like to be with myself, but at the end of the day I love people. And that was reaffirmed with that. So I just really missed people the whole time. So I don`t think I`ll do any large solo expedition anymore. It`s just more fun to do with people.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Just to underscore something that I just the sleeping piece of it because when I was a teenager, we used to sleep till 10:00 or 11:00. You couldn`t drag yourself out of bed. But I was thinking for 28 days in a row, barely sleeping and having to wake up, how did you physically do that?\par \par \uc2 CURRIER: Alarms. And sleeping during the day was really important, but there were a lot of days that I didn`t get more than three hours of sleep.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh my goodness.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, I`m sure your parents are very excited that you`re back on dry land, and can`t wait to put their arms around you. Congratulations, what a feat.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: What were we doing at 16?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: I was going to say driving to like 7/11.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We had a super soaker party in my birthday.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I don`t want to say, the statue limitations hadn`t run. (Inaudible) when I was 16.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right. Up next, say goodbye to sore feet. We`ll walk through Good Housekeeping`s list of this season`s best sandals. But first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re back with Start Today, OK. So tens and thousands of us have been walking all summer long and that`s with the help of our 31 day plan. So in that spirit, we wanted to know what are the best sandals that help you rock those daily walks. So this is what I love.\par \par \uc2 Our good friends at Good Housekeeping came up with a list. Lexie Sachs is the executive textiles director at Good Housekeeping Institute and she`s here with some of the magazine`s top fashionable and functional choices.\par \par \uc2 Lexie, I love when you guys run these things through tests. It helps us know. So let`s get started with the best overall walking sandal. What did y`all pick?\par \par \uc2 LEXIE SACHS, EXECUTIVE TEXTILES DIRECTOR, GOOD HOUSEKEEPING INSTITUTE: OK. So we love Vionic shoes because they have built in orthotics but they don`t look like orthotic footwear.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SACHS: And what`s also great about this particular pair is all of these straps on them are so easily adjustable.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, they`re velcro.\par \par \uc2 SACHS: Right. So you have four straps here. You really get that perfect fit. So they`re super comfortable to wear really soft foot bed.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Love it, comfortable, all right. What`s the best value if you`re looking for a sandal?\par \par \uc2 SACHS: OK. So these from Sanuk are a good alternative to your traditional flip flops, only $35. And get this --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: How do they work?\par \par \uc2 SACHS: Feel the bottom here, Hoda, they have a yoga mat material. So literally, if you`re walking on a yoga mat, and even the straps here are these like knit stretchy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So this go behind your foot?\par \par \uc2 SACHS: Exactly. So you have the support all the way around, it`s like wearing yoga pants on your feet honestly.\par \par \uc2 [08:45:02]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, those are comfy, all right. What`s the most popular walking sandal?\par \par \uc2 SACHS: So Birkenstock sandals have been around for decades, and they`re loved for good reason. The cork foot bed molds to the shape of your foot, so they get more comfortable as you continue to wear them. I also want to point out here, you can see there`s a deep cut in the heel and also a wide area of your toe box. It`s very generous to your foot.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. And it`s comfortable in between your toes too because that`s one.\par \par \uc2 SACHS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Let`s pretend I want to go walking all day long in a town, what`s the best all day walking shoe?\par \par \uc2 SACHS: So the Teva sandals here are great for that. They have really good shock absorption. And if you look at the bottom, there`s really good traction here. Good grip for slip resistance. So if you`re on uneven streets, even a light trail may be but like cobblestone, they`re really great for that. And again, these have the adjustable straps in the front too, so you get that close fit.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Is this a big investment? How much is this shoe?\par \par \uc2 SACHS: These are at $80.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: $80, OK. All right. So, what do we have here? What are these ones?\par \par \uc2 SACHS: OK. These are Naots. These are great for traveling. They add a little bit of the fashion into the mix. You have the really pretty straps comes in lots of colors. You still have that adjustable strap around the ankle --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s velcro, right?\par \par \uc2 SACHS: Exactly. These also I love because they come in a wide range of sizes. They go from four to 13. So if you have a hard to find size, there`s a lot of options here. And again, this foot bed will mold to your foot so you can wear it over time and we`ll get more and more comfortable.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And I like it has a little heel, so you --\par \par \uc2 SACHS: Yes, it`s got that wedge there.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. My kids love water shoes that look exactly like these but these are for the sporty person.\par \par \uc2 SACHS: Exactly. These are going to be your adventure sandals. And what`s great is you have that protection around your toe. It`s not open. You have this bungee cord here so that`s going to give you a good fit. And these materials are quick dry so they can get wet. You can wear them in the water all your sports and still have a sandal. And then again, of course, that`s supportive foot bed, that`s going to be anatomically shaped for your foot.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Lexie, bring us home.\par \par \uc2 SACHS: All right. So these are from Dr. Scholl`s Islander and what`s great about these especially if you have heel pain, they have insole technology. So again, that arch support, that shape of your foot, it`s not just a flat sandal. And another thing I love about these are these straps. So it`s slip on, the really stretchy but these are really uncomfortable but they lie flat on it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Comfortable. They hit the right spot.\par \par \uc2 SACHS: Exactly. No annoying straps there. So we love this.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Lexie, thank you. Thanks to Good Housekeeping. You can check out all the sandals at today.com/shop. Don`t forget to join our Start Today group on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . You can start our 31 day walking plan at any time. All right, Savannah, over to you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Thank you so much. Coming up next, we`ve got a top chef winner in the house, \uc2 Jeremy Ford\uc2  with some (inaudible) crowd pleasing vegetarian dishes, crowd pleasing and vegetarian. Jeremy is going to tell us how to do it. It`s a showstopper. But first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. We are back. Today Food, summer does not have to be all burgers and hotdogs, you know, and it can still be delicious. If you are looking to bring more veggies into the rotation, you`re in luck. All the way from the Butcher`s Club at Palm Beach`s PGA National Resort, we`ve got Top Chef season 13 winner \uc2 Jeremy Ford\uc2  with some veggie recipes to spice up the dinner. Hi, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY FORD\uc2 , TOP CHEF SEASON 13 WINNER: Yes, hi. I`m such a huge fan.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I`m a fan.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Veggies. Now, look, I`m a little skeptical because I`m a carnivore but this all looks delicious.\par \par \uc2 [08:50:07]\par \par \uc2 FORD: But all this goes with carnivore stuff.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK, great.\par \par \uc2 FORD: So you`re good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So show me what you got.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes. This is one of my favorite dishes from the Butcher`s Club at PGA. And we basically take these beautiful sweet Vidalia onions.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: And we put them in a pot.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Skin on.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, skin on.\par \par \uc2 FORD: And we have a little bit of milk, water in there, some aromatics such as thyme, a little rosemary, some black pepper corn.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You just put (inaudible) all, I love that.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes. How easy can that be?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I mean, to the ease of that, OK.\par \par \uc2 FORD: And the reason we do this is because we`re basically poaching the inside of the onion slowly, so we`ll bring us up to a boil and let it cook for like five or six minutes. And then we`ll just check it by, you know, pulling it out and doing a little squeeze.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And what do you want, to be kind of soft?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Just a little softer than a raw onion, so like for up to seven minutes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. We got our tasters over there.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Get verdict --\par \par \uc2 FORD: Hey.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We already got an OMG.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: So good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK. So now what happens?\par \par \uc2 FORD: So basically now we take off the root end, without taking off my finger.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. That`s always tricky.\par \par \uc2 FORD: We need those. And then we just pull the center out. So it`s really, really easy once it`s soft to pull this out, right? Take off the outside because we`re going to stuff -- there we go. I know, right? So we`ll pull all these layers off in the outer two. And if you want to make it look cool, you`ll need a little top on.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, that`s neat.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Little hat.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK. And then, we`re going to stuff it with this, like potato salad.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes, yes. So in here, there`s a little bit of horseradish, some heavy cream, potatoes, you know, a light lunch.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, I know, my goodness. We didn`t say it was a low fat. We just said it`s vegetarian.\par \par \uc2 FORD: We just said it was going to be easy and fun. We stuff our gluten here.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Rotten as we would say (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I actually learned from my mentor Dean Max, taught me years and years ago.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s a beautiful recipe. OK, then what? Now, what do we do?\par \par \uc2 FORD: So now, that stuffed in there. Are you trying to stuff some?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, I feel like this guy needs to be stuffed. I don`t want to waste my time here. Yes, I don`t want you to, you know, waste the onion. Not my time. I meant to say I don`t want to waste this beautiful onion. I have all the time in the world.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, let`s do it. So then, what? We roast it?\par \par \uc2 FORD: So then we just pop this thing in the oven. Let it get all crispy and delicious like that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This is so yummy, you guys.\par \par \uc2 JONES: This is so good.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Fun, right? Like there`s gratin but you never had gratin in --\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: The horseradish, it`s perfect.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, OK.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Cool. And then we pick that off and we roast it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Then we can move on.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And then, you can serve it with this cream spinach. So how do we make that?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes. The cream spinach is my claim to fame. At the Butcher`s Club in PGA, this is our top selling dish.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh OK, wow.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Everyone loves this dish. It`s amazing. Even at Stubborn Seed, at my other restaurant in South Beach, we run this dish.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 FORD: So everyone`s had creamed spinach, right? So simple, shallots, garlic --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Usually from a box, to be honest.\par \par \uc2 FORD: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I will come to your house and cook it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK, OK. I`m going to learn from you.\par \par \uc2 FORD: OK, OK, good, good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So what are we sauteing there?\par \par \uc2 FORD: So that`s shallots, garlic and onion, right? So you get that nice base, which is kind of like the base for most cream spinaches, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`re adding a little flour.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Adding a little flour.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Is this a (inaudible)?\par \par \uc2 FORD: This is (inaudible). OK, you`re hired.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Second French word, (inaudible), OK.\par \par \uc2 FORD: And then we cook that out for like five minutes, make sure --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That looks kind of dry and crumbly.\par \par \uc2 FORD: It`s dry and crumbly until we go ta-da. We add the heavy cream and let that cook for a little while.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 FORD: What makes our so cool, though, right? So typically, you have your spinach and your cream. Which is this cooked out?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. And you already cooked your spinach.\par \par \uc2 FORD: We already cooked our spinach in boiling water shocked in ice water so we keep that beautiful green color. But what makes ours funky and cool, is tarragon basil until -- so it gives you this -- she knows.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I mean, we`re about to be finished over here.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But wait a minute, you take these herbs and you just put them in the blender or the food processor?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes. And then also ice it. So yes, so I`ve got to tell you, ice it before you went that way, doesn`t continue to turn brown.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh.\par \par \uc2 FORD: If you want nice bright green spinach like this --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK. We`re going to put all these specifics. You`re going to share this recipe.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK, good.\par \par \uc2 FORD: The secret has been revealed, the world will know.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: (Inaudible)?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: It`s not too soupy. It`s got perfect texture.\par \par \uc2 FORD: The right amount of flour into your heavy cream mix, that`s just going to give you the perfect consistency. So I always like a little bit more just in case so doesn`t get too runny, right?\par \par \uc2 JONES: This is amazing.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I`m glad you`re enjoying.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You`re cooking it up, and then here`s a dumb question. Do you put it in the oven after this step?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes, if you want to. You could serve it hot out of this part right here onto a dish but I liked these little casserole dishes get a really hot.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. I didn`t notice it like brown off on the top or something. Could you have a little cheese on top?\par \par \uc2 FORD: You could put a little -- hey, you know what let`s put some cheese on top. Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: And then you bake it off.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Bake it off a little brown pot.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Need to put the boiler on.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, the boiler.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Now, we`re talking. What`s happening? Clean plate --\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Who miss the meat?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`re about to ask you this. Anyone miss --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No.\par \par \uc2 JONES: No.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That onion is insane.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I`ve never had anything like it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: It`s such a fun reiteration of our classic favorite two things, right? Gratin, creamy spinach but just done a little differently, right?\par \par \uc2 [08:55:03]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Say gratin again, gratin.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Gratin.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Gracias. Chef Jeremy, thank you so much. If you want to find these recipes and a lot more, got to today.com/food. Sheinelle, over to you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh my goodness, thank you. Coming up in the 3rd Hour (inaudible) that and more.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Right here on Today.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS AND WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220726ei7q000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News, Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Beautiful Rural Corners Of America Where Former City Dwellers Liberated By Remote Work Are Arriving In Droves\b0\par\par\uc2 Willie Geist, Maggie Vespa \par \uc2 2,015 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 June 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:40:00]\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST: The COVID pandemic now two and a half years old has changed not only the way we live but where we liv. With so many American companies allowing employees to do their jobs remotely, there has been a flight from big expensive cities to a slower life in beautiful rural areas like Queen Creek, Arizona, Meridian, Ohio, and Harriman, Utah.\par \par \uc2 But how is that migration changing those towns? NBC`s Maggie Vespa visited one of them for our "Sunday Spotlight."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Nestled among snow- capped mountains, Lisa Walters makes breakfast for five.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Bye, mom.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): Kids off to school, Walters to work, commuting minutes to a new co-working space to meet clients hundreds of miles away.\par \par \uc2 LISA WALTERS: Denver and some in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): Walters does marketing remotely from Bozeman, Montana, her family moved from costly Oakland, California days before COVID lockdowns forced life online.\par \par \uc2 WALTERS: Just so much more family time. I`m home every day at like 4:30, which was unheard of. The pandemic shifted the way work is.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): It shifted Bozeman, too, one of the fastest growing areas across the country since the pandemic began, as Americans freed from offices began fleeing major cities in search of more space.\par \par \uc2 BELA BEKOWSKI (PH), THERAPIST: We wanted to get away from the traffic and the people when the pandemic hit, all the work that I do went virtual.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): Therapist Bela Bekowski (ph) decamped from Seattle with her family as Bozeman`s zoom town boom was gaining steam.\par \par \uc2 BEKOWSKI (PH): I just think, oh, my gosh, what an amazing place to bring up a kid.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): One reason, the backdrop.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): I mean, I think it is pretty clear, the appeal of this place, when you see a view like this. This is, like, 15, 20 miles outside of Bozeman.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): Also, outside of Bozeman, the unprecedented pace of urban sprawl.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): Is the old way of life in Bozeman going away?\par \par \uc2 MARK HEIZER (PH), CATTLE RANCHER: Yes. It is.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: It is?\par \par \uc2 HEIZER (PH): Yes, it is going away.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): lifelong cattle rancher, Mark Heizer (ph) isn`t upset with newcomers, just the changes they bring.\par \par \uc2 HEIZER (PH): It has escalated quite a bit in the last two years.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): In town, historic homes overshadowed by new construction.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): Is Bozeman overwhelmed?\par \par \uc2 CYNDY ANDRUS, BOZEMAN MAYOR: Well, I think the answer to that is yes.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): Mayor Cyndy Andrus says Bozeman is in a housing crisis. The medium price for a single-family home reaching nearly $900,000, child care and other costs are skyrocketing.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): Is this turning into a situation where people who work in Bozeman can`t afford to live in Bozeman?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ANDRUS: That is a challenge for sure, yes.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): Then, there are cultural tensions.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): Boze-Angeles. Have you heard that nickname?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sure. I have heard all that stuff. That`s silly.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Will you ever call it Boze-Angeles?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No, I can`t. It hurts my soul.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): The road perhaps a common complaint.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Traffic is doubled out here.\par \par \uc2 HEIZER (PH): Traffic to me is when you can`t get through a stop light in one light change.\par \par \uc2 WALTERS: You might not get through a light immediately. But for me, I`m, like, this is not traffic. I`m not stressed.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): Bozeman`s boom isn`t slowing down. Nick and Kate Serna (ph) came from Cleveland in November, to them, this city still feels small.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): Do you think that small feeling is going to last?\par \par \uc2 KATE SERNA (PH): I do.\par \par \uc2 NICK SERNA (PH): Certain landscape is going to change. But the small town is going to be a small town forever, I believe that.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): Believe it or not, the boom echoes on.\par \par \uc2 For "Sunday TODAY," Maggie Vespa, Bozeman, Montana.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Montana always looks good. Maggie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 [08:45:00]\par \par \uc2 This week, we highlight another life well lived. At the funeral last week for a woman named Lee Klein, seven grateful people stood up to eulogize the beloved cancer crusader who helped to save their lives as children.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST (voiceover): Born and raised in New York, where she began a career in modeling, Klein married and moved to Miami Beach. In 1965, she founded with a group of local women there, an organization called the DEED Club, to provide free pediatric cancer treatment. Her vow was never to turn away any child regardless of need.\par \par \uc2 The DEED Club began with a single doctor and soon became a beacon for Florida families who could not afford the exorbitant expense of carrying for a child with cancer. The glamorous and charismatic Klein raised money to keep the operation running. And personally, saw each child through the difficult process of fighting cancer, often holding their hands during treatment.\par \par \uc2 The all-volunteer group eventually changed its name to the Children`s Cancer Caring Center, with Klein as president and chair. The mother of a daughter herself, Klein also created the Love and Wished Program of the patients under her care and built Camp Fiesta, a place for them to get away in the summer.\par \par \uc2 In 1991, President \uc2 George H.W. Bush\uc2  honored Klein with the President`s Volunteer Action Award. In all of her decades of relentless work, Klein never took a salary, saying her reward was watching children with cancer survive and grow up to have lives and families of their own.\par \par \uc2 Lee Klein, who improved the lives of thousands of children over a half century dedicated to them, died late last month in Aventura, Florida. Her age always was a closely guarded secret.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: it is time for the "Highs and Lows" of the week. And our first high goes to the esteemed kindergarten graduates of Black Street Early Childhood Center in Walter Borough, South Carolina. They marked the end of the school year with a dance party to Taylor Swift`s "Shake It Off," as you do, and one little guy really stole the show.\par \par \uc2 [08:50:00]\par \par \uc2 Oh, he`s just getting started. Our enthusiastic front man there is six- year-old Lajiah Sander (ph) and the dancing just keeps coming. That showcase of moves caught Lajiah`s (ph) teachers and mother by surprise. He had not practiced anything close to this in their rehearsals. He knows and he saved the good stuff for game day. Congratulations on your graduation, Lajiah (ph), but especially on that shoot dance. Very nice.\par \par \uc2 Our first low goes to the inexplicable spoiling of a fairytale moment at the self-proclaimed happiest place on earth.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s awfully nice, a German man kneeling before his princess on a stage in front of Sleeping Beauty`s Castle at Disney Land Paris and taking out an engagement ring. But, wait, a \uc2 Disney\uc2  employee wearing Mickey ears bolts in between the couple mid proposal, snatches the ring and directs them off of his precious platform.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She said yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, that`s great. But over here is going to be even better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Making it worse, the groom to be had asked for and received permission from another employee to use the spot for his once in a lifetime moment. He eventually did slip the ring on his girlfriend`s finger, but not on the stage, he had to move over to a nearby bench. As that moment went viral, \uc2 Disney\uc2  apologized and offered the couple a free weekend at any Disney Land Park to which the man said, no thanks.\par \par \uc2 Our next high goes to a first birthday cake for a North Carolina sixth grader named Abraham Walker. Abraham and his older brother, Joe, were adopted from Sierra Leone last fall by the Walker family in Charlotte.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Happy birthday dear, Abraham, happy birthday to you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Abraham never had a birthday party of his own. So, he didn`t know how to blow out the candles. And he says he isn`t even sure exactly how old he is. His adopted dad there, Joe Walker, met the boys on a trip to an orphanage in Sierra Leone. Later, when the brothers were about to be separated and forced out on to the street, the Walkers and their four kids met and decided quickly and unanimously that Abraham and Joe should join their family in America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM WALKER: All I used to pray for is for me to have a family that can love and care about me. And then, God just said, wait, I have the perfect and beautiful family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Happy birthday, Abraham. And, yes, that is a beautiful family.\par \par \uc2 Our final low this week goes to getting a whole lot more than you bargained for with a good deed. A man named Robert Brantley pulled over the other day when he saw a kitten wandering along the road in Rural Pioneer, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT BRANTLEY: Look. Kitty, kitty. He`s on the side of the road. Going to get killed for sure. Someone just -- oh, no. It`s a whole -- oh, my gosh. I can`t take you all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: It is an ambush. Perhaps the most adorable ambush ever. Brantley did take them all, all 13 of them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRANTLEY: Tactical Honda was not prepared for this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: They now are living with his family including his two little kids and a rescue dog named Cody Pig, who has become something of a mother to the kittens. Brantley and his wife have found 11 of those kittens` adoptive homes. They are keeping the other two, including the first brave little guy who first approached him by the road, they`ve named him Scout.\par \par \uc2 An official correction from our "Sunday TODAY" editorial desk, that story is a high. We regret the error and we love those kittens. We`ll be right back with your "Sunday Mug Shots."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:55:00]\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We got more of your "Sunday TODAY Mug Shots" this week, starting across the top. There is Jessica celebrating her graduation from \uc2 Thomas Jefferson University\uc2  in Philadelphia. Congratulations. Henry in Jerseyville, Illinois. Happy sixth birthday, Henry. Pam and Gary in Martha`s Vineyard. Happy 50th wedding anniversary to you. David and LaDawn on safari in Botswana. That has to be a first for us.\par \par \uc2 Down along the bottom, there is Karsyn in Union, Maine. Then, Alex, Mark, Lauren, Gracie, Alex, Jocelyn, Lydia and Tim at their high school graduation in Columbia, Maryland. Congrats, guys. Olive celebrating her fifth birthday in Leesburg, Virginia. Hey, Olive. And Kim, a second-grade teacher in Golden Valley, Minnesota. Thank you for what you do, Kim, and enjoy that summer.\par \par \uc2 A programming note, today, Major League Baseball is now streaming on Peacock and it gets going early again this morning. Our MLB Sunday Leadoff coverage of the \uc2 Oakland Athletics\uc2  and \uc2 Cleveland Guardians\uc2  begins today at 11:00 a.m. Eastern on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for spending part of your morning with us. We will see you right back here next week on "Sunday TODAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220615ei6c0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for May 22, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Kate Snow, Jo Ling Kent, Kelly O`Donnell, Matt Bradley, Emilie  Ikeda, Vaughn Hillyard, Steve Patterson, Anne Thompson \par \uc2 3,937 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:30:00]\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Breaking news tonight, the first signs of relief for families desperate for baby formula, weeks into the crushing shortage, a delivery from overseas, enough formula to fill half a million bottles. Where this batch is heading and who will be first in line. Our reporting to the secretary of agriculture.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JO LING KENT\uc2 , NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT: Did the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  drop the ball here?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Late today, the Biden administration`s announcement on where the next flight is heading.\par \par \uc2 Monkeypox concerns, more cases overseas, a possible new case now in Florida. The president weighing in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: It is something that everybody should be concerned about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Plus, what he said about COVID and how it could push inflation even higher.\par \par \uc2 A rare joint interview, candid comments from Ukraine`s first family, revealing the toll the war has taken on them and families across Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Weekend weather extremes, the record breaking heat wave for millions on the east coast and a new warning about who is most at risk in these sweltering temperatures, and in Colorado, would you believe snow?\par \par \uc2 The price of hitting the road as the summer travel season begins, gas prices and airfares soaring, and good luck getting a rental car. We have got cost cutting tips as you plan your getaway.\par \par \uc2 And the special surprise for a group of third graders, a shot at a brighter future their families never expected.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. With so many parents still struggling to find infant formula for their babies, today, a first step toward getting all those bare shelves restocked. A military plane landed in Indiana carrying thousands of pounds of formula. These brands specifically for babies with an allergy to cow`s milk. And late today, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  tweeting about a second flight to transport \uc2 Nestle\uc2  specialty infant formula to Pennsylvania. In New York City today, the mayor declaring a state of emergency to give authorities the power to prevent price gouging for formula. And now the big question, how soon will children who don`t have allergies get access to new supplies?\par \par \uc2 Jo Ling Kent\uc2  has been reporting on the shortage all week. She starts us off from Indianapolis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KENT: Today, a welcome sight for anxious parents, a C-17 military aircraft hauling 78,000 pounds of formula landing in Indianapolis amid the crippling formula shortage.\par \par \uc2 TOM VILSACK, SECRETARY OF AGRICULTURE: This shipment provides enough formula to take care of 9,000 babies and 18,000 toddlers for a week. It`s about a half million eight-ounce doses.\par \par \uc2 KENT (voice over): The plane took off from Ramstein Airbase in Germany, the first flight of the Biden administration`s Operation Fly Formula.\par \par \uc2 On board, hypoallergenic Alfamino infant and junior formulas, meant for babies and toddlers with cow`s milk allergies. It will be distributed across the country in waves. The first batch, which \uc2 Nestle\uc2 , the manufacturer, says has already been tested will be sent to hospitals and healthcare providers in a couple of days. They`ll dispense it to families that need it.\par \par \uc2 The rest of today`s shipment, \uc2 Nestle\uc2  says, will be tested and sent out in the next two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Another flight carrying more hypoallergenic formula from Gerber is expected to arrive in a few days and will be trucked to Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 When will shelves be full again or at least fuller?\par \par \uc2 VILSACK: Well, I think that`s a matter of weeks, in part, because of the sequencing of events already taking place, shipments coming in for the other military.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Vilsack says the long-term solution is reopening the shuttered Abbott nutrition plant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Now at least we can breathe a little bit.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Florida mom Alexandra Moskowitz (ph) was glad to see the formula land for the sake of her three-week-old Dylan, who has a cow`s milk allergy. But she isn`t satisified.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s a sense of anger or betrayal that we feel, because if the government was aware of this, we kind of should have been more proactive in getting our hands on the formula before the shortage got to this point.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Did the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  drop the ball here?\par \par \uc2 VILSACK: You know, I don`t think that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  dropped the ball. I mean, certainly, I can speak for \uc2 USDA\uc2 . We moved as expeditiously and as quickly as possible. I think the key lesson for us learned from the pandemic and this is that efficiency is certainly important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: But, Jo, there are so many families waiting with babies who don`t have allergies. When will they maybe get access to formula?\par \par \uc2 KENT (on camera): We don`t know just yet. But we know the White House tonight just now announcing that they received the first two orders for ingredients and supplies under the Defense Production Act.\par \par \uc2 [18:35:01]\par \par \uc2 And that could very well speed up manufacturing here in the U.S. for all the parents who need that formula right now. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Jo, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is about to begin the second leg of his Asia trip, but today his economic and diplomatic agendas were overshadowed by growing concerns over the spread of another illness monkeypox.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Donnell is in Tokyo with the president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voice over): For President Biden, the surprise of this first trip to Asia is not the work to build diplomatic alliances and economic ties, not the visit to thank U.S. troops.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: First of all, I want to say thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The unexpected is that another health threat, growing cases of monkeypox, demands his attention. The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  says monkeypox has been found in at least 12 countries, including the U.S., with confirmed cases in New York and Massachusetts. A third possible is being investigated in Florida. No deaths have been reported.\par \par \uc2 With global economic pain caused by COVID, the president is using this visit to South Korea and Japan to highlight investments headed back home.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We are just going to create more than 8,000 new American jobs.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The pressure is under pressure to confront rising prices for Americans. Commerce Secretary \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2  urges patience, but says China`s ongoing COVID crisis will bring more economic strain.\par \par \uc2 Do you anticipate that will have another ripple effect with more slowdowns?\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO\uc2 , SECRETARY OF COMMERCE: Yes. China is really struggling now. Their economy is struggling. So, I think the ripple effect will continue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And Kelly joins me live now from Tokyo, where, tomorrow, President Biden is going to announce a new trade partnership, Kelly?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (on camera): This will include a number of countries in the Indo- Pacific region. And one main focus, Kate, will be on supply chains that so often originate in this part of the world, to make them more resilient, to reduce delays and shortages that cause economic pain back home. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Kelly, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to Ukraine, where President Zelenskyy and his wife sat down for a rare joint interview, speaking candidly about their family and Russia`s continued assaults.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Matt Bradley.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS (voice over): Tonight, a rare joint appearance by the Ukrainian president and his wife, telling Ukrainian television, our family was torn apart, just like every other Ukrainian family.\par \par \uc2 As Russia continues its assault on Ukraine`s Eastern Donbas region, striking command center and troops and ammunition depots, according to its defense ministry, refugees from the east describe scenes of destruction. The horror, there`s nothing to say, especially the city center, this woman said. The schools are damaged, the monuments.\par \par \uc2 Still, Ukrainians continue to fight.\par \par \uc2 We`re ready to attack, ready to defend, ready to retreat if necessary. He said, we`re ready for anything.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s president now saying only negotiations will end the war. The victory will be difficult, it will be bloody and embattled, but its end will be in diplomacy, Zelenskyy said. But Ukraine`s military is hopeful weapons from the west could break the impasse.\par \par \uc2 U.S. President Biden signed a $40 billion aid package for Ukraine during his South Korea visit. Meanwhile, in Ukraine`s capital, the Kyiv opera held its first performance on Saturday since the war began, striking a defiant note for a nation that`s refused to stay silent.\par \par \uc2 Matt Bradley, NBC News, Lviv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Back here in the U.S., more than 36 million people spent the weekend broiling under a record breaking heat wave. And tonight, we have new information about who is most at risk for health issues and even death as temperatures climb.\par \par \uc2 Emilie Ikeda reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS (voice over): A sweltering heat wave --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s like an oven. I`m like so hot.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: -- hammering more than half of the U.S., and it`s not even memorial day yet.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It feels like we`re in the dog days of August.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: As this weekend melted away record temperatures, 92 degrees in Washington, D.C., 95 in Philadelphia and Richmond and 93 in Atlantic City, New Jersey, a stunning contrast to parts of the Rockies, where late May snow dumped up to 20 inches in Colorado, that same storm system then spawning tornados in Michigan. And tonight, it`s eyeing the eastern seaboard with potential thunderstorms, but not before crowds soaked up the sunshine at the shore.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`ve been at the beach all day.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: While hot weather is welcomed by some as an early start to summer, a new study underscores its dangers, the more days with extreme heat, the more deaths, with the greatest impact on older adults, men, and Black Americans.\par \par \uc2 Is this study a warning for what`s to come?\par \par \uc2 DR. SAMEED KHATANA, PERELMAN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE ASSISTANT PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF PENNSYLVANIA: I think if this isn`t a warning, then I`m not sure what is.\par \par \uc2 [18:40:01]\par \par \uc2 20,000 people may have died due to extreme heat in these ten years that we studied.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: This weekend`s dramatic turn in temperature came just as 20,000 runners hit the pavement in Brooklyn, Saturday. One runner died and another 16 were hospitalized. But authorities have not reported the cause.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Emilie, for those in the heat wave, when can we expect some relief?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (on camera): Kate, this unforgiving heat is starting to break thanks to storms this evening. But the system could bring damaging winds, hail, tornados from Virginia to Maine. 25 million Americans are at risk for severe weather. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Emilie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn to politics and Georgia`s primary showdown on Tuesday. It`s one of the most watched in the nation. The outcome could shake up politics there and have national implications.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn Hillyard explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS (voice over): The Peach State Republican showdown on Tuesday, this year`s most personal race for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Brian Kemp is a turncoat, is a coward and he`s a complete and total disaster.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: The former president is trying to take down Brian Kemp, Georgia`s Republican governor, who, despite Trump`s rage in 2020, certified his state`s election results, a win for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): We need to win and win big on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Kemp now widening his lead among Republican voters, looking to defeat Trump`s handpicked challenger, former U.S. Senator \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. \uc2 DAVID PERDUE\uc2  (R-GA): The election in 2020 was rigged and stolen.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Perdue acknowledging Tuesday`s loss.\par \par \uc2 PERDUE: We might not win on Tuesday, but I can guarantee we`re not down 30 points.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: A Perdue loss would undercut Trump`s standing as his party`s defining kingmaker.\par \par \uc2 If you don`t win, is that an indictment on your focus on the 2020 election and the former president?\par \par \uc2 PERDUE: No, I don`t think so at all. I`m focused on the truth.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Record numbers of voters already casting their ballots ahead of Tuesday`s primary. Also looking for a second term in office, Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , who was pressured by Trump to find more votes for him in 2020.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I just want to find 11,780 votes, which is one more than we have.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: He`s in a tightly contested race with Trump-inspired challenger \uc2 Jody Hice\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the Republican bastion of Georgia, Trump still looms large. His loathing of Kemp could even cost him a general election race against \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Of course, having her, I think, might be better than having your existing governor, if you want to go a little bit forward (ph).\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Kemp, who just narrowly beat Abrams in 2018, rejecting those concerns.\par \par \uc2 Can you win in November without Trump`s support?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Absolutely. I didn`t get in the race not to win in November.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Vaughn joins me now from Atlanta. So, what are these final 48 hours look like on the ground there?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD (on camera): Kate, tomorrow, Governor Kemp will campaign alongside former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . As for Perdue, Trump is slated to join him at one of his final stops but by dialing in over the phone. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Vaughn Hillyard in Atlanta for us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, summer travel, how to save cash as you plan those summer getaways.\par \par \uc2 Plus, now hiring, why it is a great time to be a college graduate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:45:00]\par \par \uc2 SNOW: The final round of the PGA Championship in Tulsa went on without one of its biggest stars. Tiger Woods dropped out yesterday, withdrawing from a major for the first time in his professional career. Throughout the competition, he appeared to be in pain and limping. Woods, of course, is still recovering from life threatening injuries he suffered in a car crash last year.\par \par \uc2 Now to the price you pay. With Memorial Day weekend on the horizon, it`s hard to miss the skyrocketing prices of gas, airfare and car rentals, which could shatter all-time highs.\par \par \uc2 Steve Patterson has more on how to save.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEVE PATTERSON, NBC NEWS (voice over): Nearly 40 million Americans expected to hit to the road or take to the sky ahead of Memorial Day. But with heavy demand, rampant inflation and surging prices, it comes with a warning, brace for headaches.\par \par \uc2 PEER BUELLER, CHIEF FINANCIAL OFFICER AND CHIEF OPERATING OFFICER, KAYAK: It`s a little bit of a perfect storm. We`re just seeing a lot people come back to wanting to travel.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Average domestic flight prices expected to soar, up 46 percent from 2019, with some flights already sold out for weeks. Not much better on the ground. Demand for rentals spiking 130 percent while the gas forecast could average $4.65 by next weekend, a 51 percent over last year.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA REISING, SUMMER TRAVELER: We`re having to go a different route than we usually do.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Amanda Reising`s is driving from Arkansas to Florida to vacation before their son`s football training with rental car inventories so low and gas so high. This year, they`re calling an audible.\par \par \uc2 REISING: It`s definitely is a whole new ball game this time and we`re trying our best to budget things.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: They plan on meal prepping instead of eating out and a game changer, pay for the room in zero interest installments.\par \par \uc2 REISING: You`re not paying at all at one time and you`re not taking that lump sum of money out of your savings that you need for (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Experts say more ways to save include looking for midweek and early morning flights, booking at least six weeks early, and if you`re okay with a staycation, this is the perfect year, getting away without breaking the bank, a summer travel tight rope.\par \par \uc2 Steve Patterson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Still to come for us, why it`s an especially good time to be a college graduate. Wait until you hear with some of their starting salaries are looking like.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the surprise, giving these third graders and their parents a big reason to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:50:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR SWIFT, MUSICIAN: The scary news is you`re on your own now. But the cool news is you`re on your own now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: That`s Taylor Swift speaking at NYU`s commencement, graduation season now fully upon us. This year, the class of 2022 is marching into one of the best job markets in years. And when it comes to accepting a position, they`re not just thinking about salary.\par \par \uc2 Anne Thompson explains what else they`re looking for and why they can be a little more choosey than those in years` past.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS (voice over): the class of 2022 has reason to celebrate. They are entering one of the best job markets in years with employers planning to hire almost 32 percent more grads than a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Which was easier, finishing school or finding a job?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Finding a job, for sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would completely agree. Myself being a pre-med student, finding a job was easier than four years on to graduate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Finding a job was much easier than finishing school for me as well.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Aga Noel got an associate`s degree and is a licensed occupational therapist assistant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I was receiving job offers before my license even came in.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Carolina Ruiz earned a degree in biology and is a clinical research coordinator at a New York hospital.\par \par \uc2 How many job offers did you consider?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, I considered three.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Liam Burke studied supply chain manufacturing. He chose to work for an aerospace company after multiple offers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I landed my position about six weeks after graduation.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: All three are making more than the national average salary for grads, $55,260. A recent monster.com survey shows while salary is the top priority for graduates, career advancement, work/life balance and remote work options are also important.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would really focus on the research project that I would be part of. And the fact that I would be making difference in certain people`s lives, I`m mainly focusing on minorities or any sort of groups that I am very passionate about.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Allowing today`s grads to be choosey, an unemployment rate of 3.6 percent, compared to the daunting 14.7 percent the class of 2020 faced, and almost two job openings for every person unemployed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I really wanted what my career services person would say the perfect fit.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The market giving new grads leverage, and making hiring hard for employers.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BLUMSACK, CHIEF STRATEGY OFFICER, MONSTER.COM: We know that they`re having real difficulties for finding candidates or having to make concessions around things like salary or around flexibility, other benefits that they didn`t have to think about over the past few years.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The class of 2022, joining the working world at just the right time.\par \par \uc2 Anne Thompson, NBC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: When we come back, the surprise announcement, giving these third graders a head-start to a brighter future.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:55:00]\par \par \uc2 SNOW: There`s good news tonight about helping others succeed and the family foundation giving kids the chance for a brighter future a full decade before college even starts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice over): This standing ovation marks a life-changing moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m very proud of you. Give me a high five.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: It`s the instant just weeks ago when parents found out their third graders at Michael Anderson Elementary near Phoenix would all be getting scholarships to college.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You`re going to college. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Tuition, room, and board as long as students are accepted to a program and graduate from a high school in their current district. The giveaway part of a commitment to education in underserved locations, started by Phoenix area business owners Jill and Tom Rostotsi (ph). Tom, inspired by his father, a Hungarian refugee.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My dad was the beneficiary of a lot of people who helped him out along the way. That`s why he was able to achieve the American dream.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Why third grade? The idea is to get kids, their families and teachers thinking now about what could be possible.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I wanted to go to a college. I wanted to go to high school.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: would like to start my own business or be a dentist.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Parents say the opportunity is a lifeline to a brighter future.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: With all the struggles in life and having kids, you know, this is one less worry.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: But this recent award wasn`t the only Rostotsi (ph) Foundation grant. The first one ten years ago helped Demaris Flores (ph) reach her dream.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And right now, I`m a current Arizona nursing student, Arizona College of Nursing. I want to give back to my community.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And in April, another grant.\par \par \uc2 QUINTIN BOYCE, SUPERINTENDENT, ROOSEVELT SCHOOL DISTRICT: For every Bernard black third grader that is right here with us, they are offering a full ride college scholarship.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Roosevelt School District Superintendent Quintin Boyce calls it a game changer.\par \par \uc2 BOYCE: We saw parents liberated of this really tough struggle of figuring out how to create a better life for their child via a college education.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: These generation gifts giving parents, like Tanissa Miller (ph), new hope for the next generation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m so thankful and grateful that someone would have it on their heart to think of my child to want to provide them with a better future and take stress off of me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And, wow, it does sound weird to call them the class -- college class of 2036, but the journey to senior year of high school will go by in the blink of an eye. Trust me. I know. We wish them all the best of luck.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Tom says he hopes to eventually give two more grant promises a year.\par \par \uc2 That is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS on this Sunday night. Lester Holt is back with you tomorrow. I`m Kate Snow. From all of us here at NBC News, stay safe and have a great night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220523ei5m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The Today Show for May 20, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb, Craig Melvin, Al Roker, Carson Daly,  Sheinelle Jones, Tom Llamas, Dylan Dreyer, Gadi Schwartz, Jill martin,  Jenna Bush Hager, Richard Engel, Harry Smith, Keir Simmons \par \uc2 8,278 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: It`s 8:00 on "TODAY". Coming up, feeling the heat. Inside the soaring cost of gas, food, and travel heading into the busy summer season. And it comes as struggling families could soon see relief in the baby formula shortage. What you need to know.\par \par \uc2 Then, sound advice. The inspiring story of a graduating doctor. One of the youngest cochlear implant recipients in the country, now helping to restore people`s hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN WESTMAN, HEARING-IMPAIRED DOCTOR: Nothing can hold you back. You can achieve anything you want if you put your mind to it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Her hopes that her story can help other children receiving implants.\par \par \uc2 Plus, cruise in control. \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2 , honored at the Cannes Film Festival ahead of the London premiere for his new movie "Top Gun: Maverick". The red carpet packed with stars, including the royals, Prince William and Kate Middleton. What this latest blockbuster might be in for the film industry`s recovery.\par \par \uc2 And time to rock. Harry Styles kicked it all off yesterday. And now, we`re ready to reveal our all-star lineup for this year`s summer concert series. So, get ready to book your ticket to the plaza today, Friday, May 20, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Indiana University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Barnard College.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: MV Southern University.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: In Baton Rouge, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Girls` trip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Mobile, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: College roommates.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: To "TODAY".\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Here to celebrate my graduation from Pratt Institute.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hi to my students at Rivercrest Elementary in Hudson, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Celebrating 16.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And 60.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Fargo, North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Here to celebrate my beautiful wife, a cancer survivor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Turning 50 today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Smile out there on our plaza.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Good morning, everybody. Welcome back. It`s Friday. Glad to have you with us. Tom Llamas is here. Craig`s down in South Carolina just having a good old time. Another reason to smile, Jenna Bush Hager is here.\par \par \uc2 JENNA BUSH HAGER, "HODA & JENNA" HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: JBH in the house.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: Hi guys.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Hi. You got something special coming up. What is it?\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: Oh, my gosh. I`m so excited because next week I`m going to be live on the U.S.S. Baton as the ship leaves the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  into New York City for the return of Fleet Week, baby.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, it`s back?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: What?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`ve been waiting.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: OK. It`s the first time since the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: This is a decade-long tradition.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: And I get to be on the ship.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK. And then I also heard you`re getting a helicopter lift --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That`s a how you --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- on Tuesday, that`s how you are getting there? Well, that`s what it says.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: I think that is. OK. And I`m going to get a tour. I`m so excited. A firsthand look at some of the day-to-day duties of these incredible service members on board. We`re going to hear from the ship`s captain and some of the officers. Many of them are women. Oh, I just can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, you`re going to be great. It`ll be a great interview.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: And Wednesday you`re going to be live from the ship?\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: I`m going to be live from the ship.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: I`m going to be live from the ship. The U.S.S. Baton will lead the parade of ship under the Verrazzano Bridge, past the Statue of Liberty --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: -- and the 9/11 Memorial.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I just got chills.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: I know. I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s going to be beautiful.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: I think it`s going to be very touching. I`m really honored. And I know that New Yorkers -- you know that feeling when you look and you see that Fleet Week is back?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Right.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: Service members are in the street?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: I know New Yorkers are looking forward to it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wearing their uniforms, it`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Last time they come to our plaza.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: They do.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Bring some over.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: I know, I will.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We want to say hi.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: So, I wish you all could come but there`s only room for one.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, sure. That`s the theme of the morning, actually.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`re not invited.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thanks, Jenna.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Jenna, we`re looking forward to it. Have a good weekend, hon.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get right to the news at 8:00. Americans are heading into the weekend with no break from rising prices, consumer shortages, and stock market whiplash. And now, word of a threat that we have not faced in quite some time, stagflation. NBC`s Tom Costello joins us with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Tom, this is going back to the bad old days.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, if you`re old enough to remember -- I hate to tell you -- yes, you remember the stagflation reporting from the 70s and 80s, listen, you don`t need me to tell you, we`re all living this every single day. Inflation touching our lives every day. Taking a big bite out of the family budget. We`re talking, of course, about prices for everything from food, of course, to gas prices, airline tickets, hotels, and restaurants across the board.\par \par \uc2 It`s a tsunami of factors driving prices higher. Including, oh, by the way, the China COVID lockdown that is still choking the global supply chain. If you`ve been trying to buy appliances lately, you know what they tell you, wait nine months, wait 12 months, that`s why. Now, some economists and former Fed officials are warning not just of a recession, as the Fed raises interest rates, but the possibility of stagflation where the economy is stagnant, not growing. Yet inflation and unemployment moving higher at the same time. And that`s why the Fed is, right now, acting to try to tamp down inflation just as fast as it can.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 But it is a huge challenge to do that without killing jobs and without killing the economy. Wall Street, clearly worried right now. As you have seen, the S&P, that`s the big broad index down 18 percent so far year to date. The Nasdaq tech-heavy, down 27 percent. That only further unsettles Americans who see their retirement savings dropping by the week. And it`s causing many Americans, of course, to start to rethink their summer travel plans. Do they really need to take that trip on a plane, get in the car with the kids, go across the country? What about the hotels, the restaurants? All of that with rising prices causing people to reevaluate their priorities. Guys, back to you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Tom, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right. Meantime, the Senate has overwhelmingly approved another $40 billion in aid to Ukraine where the Russian invasion is about to enter its fourth month. NBC`s Chief Foreign Correspondent Richard Engel is in Ukraine with an update this morning on one of the most memorable survivors he`s met while covering this war.\par \par \uc2 Richard, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Tom. Yes, you may remember, several weeks ago in a village not far from here, I`m now the City of Kharkiv, we met an 88-year-old woman. And at the time she was traumatized from all of the fighting around her. Well, we went back and followed up to see how she is doing now. And found her and her village very different.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): The first time we saw Prescovia Ivanova, she was sitting outside her home, scared, confused, and overwhelmed. Ukrainian troops had just bombed the Russians out of her village called Mala Rohan. And more than anything else, she needed comfort.\par \par \uc2 Since then, Ukrainian troops have pushed the Russians back even further. Allowing life to return to Mala Rohan. Power lines are being repaired. Shops are still closed but there`s regular food distribution. We decided to check in on Prescovia.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): Hello. How are you?\par \par \uc2 PRESCOVIA IVANOVA, UKRAINIAN ELDERLY WAR SURVIVOR (through translator): Come in.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): It is nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 IVANOVA (through translator): Come in, please.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): It is nice to see you again.\par \par \uc2 IVANOVA (through translator): You see how vibrant I am? I`m still alive.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): The bombings blew out her windows, they`re now covered in plastic. Her roof leaks, too.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): How was it when the Russians were here? What was it like for you when the Russians were in this town?\par \par \uc2 IVANOVA (through translator): When they were bombing, it was so scary. The bombs were whistling and thundering.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): But things are now improving.\par \par \uc2 IVANOVA (through translator): Today, we got the lights back. Before there was nothing. Now we have gas. And I even cooked a borscht.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): After freshening up in some new clothing, Prescovia wanted to show us her borscht.\par \par \uc2 IVANOVA (through translator): Let`s go. I`ll show you how to cook it.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): Onion, dill, parsley, cabbage, all go into boiling potatoes.\par \par \uc2 IVANOVA (through translator): Everyone has their own kind of borscht.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): She checks the seasoning and waits until it`s ready. Prescovia has lived a hard life, surviving World War II and famine. Her husband died nine years ago. Her son and daughter who live in Russian- controlled areas haven`t called in months. Finally, the borscht is ready.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): This is delicious. It`s very good. Thank you. And this is her homemade vodka, which goes with the borscht. Thank you. Cheers.\par \par \uc2 IVANOVA (through translator): For all good things.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): Prescovia was happy for some company.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): Wonderful -- spend some time with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): She seemed sorry to see us go. But now, at least, with the fighting here over, she`s able to smile.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): I think what struck me most about her was her resilience. She really has had an incredibly difficult life. During the periods of famine, she told me that she and her brother and sister would go out in the fields looking for potatoes that the farmers had neglected to harvest. Now, once again, war has returned to her area. But she still gets up every day and now she`s able to cook for herself and take care of herself. Tom.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Richard, you said she`s smiling and we are all smiling back here as well.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Richard --\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: What an incredible story, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- I think you have a friend for life, Richard. It`s so special that you went back and shared that time with her. It seems like it meant as much to you as it did to her. So, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you, Richard.\par \par \uc2 All right. Guys, we`re going to move on now to a personal note. After nearly 30 years with NBC News, our Justice Correspondent Pete Williams has announced his retirement.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, Pete is legendary. He`s been our go-to guy at the Supreme Court, the Justice Department, and Homeland Security, he has covered it all. The biggest court decisions, investigations, the breaking news of our time, including 9/11 and the Boston Marathon Bombing.\par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 NBC News President Noah Oppenheim said, Pete`s reporting has always been ironclad and his ability to break down the most complex and fast-developing situation is uncanny. His decency, kindness, and generosity are unmatched. And that is the truth.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Pete is going to stick around till July. We need him. There are some major Supreme Court decisions ahead. But around our newsroom, we have that saying.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. What`s it?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: In Pete we trust.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: In Pete we 