ts don`t want the stimulation too soon. Sometimes it can be overwhelming. The nerve can be really sensitive. For him, he was at the go, just ready to have that volume. So, it was really exciting for all of us to see that success.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voiceover): Meghan Westman gets it, big time. For without her cochlear implant, she would not be able to hear at all.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (on camera): So, when you`re there and you`re working with someone who has just got an implant and you tell them or they notice that you have an implant too, what does it mean to them?\par \par \uc2 WESTMAN: They light up. And they`re -- they almost -- it`s like finding your own people. When you find somebody else who has the same story or similar story, you kind of feel like you`ve already made a connection with a total stranger.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voiceover): Meningitis put Meghan in intensive care when she was not yet two years old.\par \par \uc2 WESTMAN: That`s when they ordered the auditory brain stem response testing and I had no response. I was profoundly deaf in both ears.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voiceover): She became one of the youngest cochlear implant recipients in the country. And while it helped her hear, it brought unwanted attention to her disability.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 WESTMAN: I spent most of my -- most of my life trying to hide it. Pretend like it wasn`t there. Cover it up with my hair.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voiceover): Junior high was torturous.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (on camera): Were you bullied?\par \par \uc2 WESTMAN: I would say more indirectly. I don`t think kids were mean as to say it straight to my face. But there was also a social delay that comes with anybody with a disability, with a hearing loss especially, you`re not picking up on conversations that are happening around you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (on camera): What was it like then growing up with this?\par \par \uc2 WESTMAN: It`s shaped me into who I am today. It`s made me stronger. And I wouldn`t change anything. Especially now, feeling like I found my purpose and what I`m going to do for my career. And it was all worth it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hi, Christopher.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hi, Christopher.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voiceover): We have all marveled at the videos of children who are implant recipients. Meghan wants to be a symbol of assurance, especially, for parents of those kids.\par \par \uc2 WESTMAN: I can see how much it means to them to see someone who`s had a success story with an implant and it kind of gives them a little hope that their children can have the same outcomes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voiceover): In the final days of her training, Meghan has been under the watchful eyes of audiologist Dr. Jamie Cluna (ph).\par \par \uc2 DR. JAMIE CLUNA, AUDIOLOGIST: She truly will understand what they are going through. We can read it in books and we can talk to our patients and hear about it. But to actually live it is a whole different story.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voiceover): It is amazing, after all, to be an audiologist requires very good hearing. Meghan`s got that and more.\par \par \uc2 WESTMAN: Hearing loss, cochlear implants, disabilities in general, nothing can hold you back. You can achieve anything you want if you put your mind to it. And stay committed, dedicated, and work hard, nothing can hold you back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Amen.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Thanks, Harry.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (on camera): Let`s all choke up as she gets her diploma, a doctor of audiology. Next week, goes to work in an ENT practice in North Carolina --\par \par \uc2 DALY: God bless her.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: -- next month.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Beautiful, Harry. Beautiful story, as always.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I saw tears in your eyes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Of course. It`s a