lifelong fishermen can`t believe what they`re seeing. They`re called the vaquita marina. They`re the smallest of all marine mammals. They`re on the razor`s edge of extinction.\par \par \uc2 [08:40:00]\par \par \uc2 Valeria Towns is a biologist who`s been studying the reclusive mammal for years. Her work is more important than ever because the vaquita population is nearly extinct, declining faster than any other species in modern times.\par \par \uc2 How many vaquitas are left?\par \par \uc2 VALERIA TOWNS, BIOLOGIST: Less than 20.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Less than 20 left on planet Earth?\par \par \uc2 TOWNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Does that terrify you?\par \par \uc2 TOWNS: It is terrifying, definitely.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): The vaquitas are drowning in illegal fishing nets set by fishermen trying to catch another endangered fish called the totoaba, whose bladder can fetch tens of thousands of dollars on the illegal black market in China.\par \par \uc2 And so, the vaquita marina is basically going extinct by accident.\par \par \uc2 TOWNS: Yes, that is the saddest thing about it.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): But recent signings have given researchers some reason to be optimistic.\par \par \uc2 TOWNS: What give us some hope is that, they are still reproducing.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: So there`s been babies, baby sightings.\par \par \uc2 TOWNS: There are some babies. And they`re reproducing at a higher rate than we expect.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): Anu Torezo (ph) is the captain of a research vessel that monitors vaquitas off the coast of San Felipe of Mexico.\par \par \uc2 He says that what we`re looking at right here on the map, this area that is the only place vaquita marinas have been seen in the last years. It`s a tiny little square and it`s right off the coast here, about five miles.\par \par \uc2 International pressure has been building to protect the vaquitas and the threat of sanctions from the United States has prompted the Mexican Navy to step up patrol of the zero tolerance area where boats and fishing gear are forbidden. But as we witnessed firsthand, there has been little enforcement.\par \par \uc2 We`re at the Malecon. This is a main tourist track here in San Felipe. And you`ve got some boats, some trucks coming in. This is probably that 19th, 20th boat that we`ve seen underneath those tarps. You can see that they have fishing nets underneath. These fishing nets are illegal because these are the types of fishing nets that scientists say are drowning the vaquita marina.\par \par \uc2 We saw boat after boat launching from a restricted area. Animal protection group say it`s all to fish under the cover of darkness. In a statement to NBC News, the Mexican Navy says it`s been working very closely with Sea Shepherd, a nonprofit dedicated to marine conservation, supporting them by providing security and exchanging information with their vessels. But everyone agrees on one thing, the time is running out for the vaquita.\par \par \uc2 If things continue as they have been, how long do the vaquita have left?\par \par \uc2 TOWNS: I don`t think that more than a couple of years.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: A couple of years.\par \par \uc2 TOWNS: Yes. Or some might seem like a last class, but I don`t consider it like that. I really consider it like the biggest challenge for protecting the most endangered mammal species in the world. We are able to rescue the vaquita, then there is hope for the humanity future.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): Hope it`s now being reinforced by a coordinated international effort to change the fate of one of the world`s most precious sea mammals.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: And scientists say the most frustrating part about this is, they know exactly what the problem is, they know exactly how to fix it. But right now, it`s all about trying to convince that fishing community to not use those nets. And so far, enforcement of those laws i