IDENTIFIED MALE: We cannot allow this to destroy us. We cannot allow this. We cannot allow those people that are upset and mad about what happened to try to take their own vengeance either.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: A community united in grief.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW (on camera): It is very hard to be here today.\par \par \uc2 We are learning more about the suspect. Authorities have questioned him in the past. We`ve learned, and now investigating his racist online posts.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Emilie Ikeda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS (voice over): Tonight, new details on the man behind Saturday`s stunning attack, authorities confirming this wasn`t the suspect`s first run-in with trouble. Last June, state police say he made a threatening statement at his high school, Susquehanna Valley. School officials alerted police, which resulted in a mental health evaluation. Authorities say he was released shortly later.\par \par \uc2 Now, less than a year later, 18-year-old Payton Gendron is accused of killing ten and injuring another three, streaming his first steps on a gaining platform. Twitch said the feed was removed within two minutes of the violence.\par \par \uc2 What alarms you the most leading to this incident?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): This person intended to have a broad platform livestreaming it, sharing his information, his ideas, his hatred online. There are others who know this now. And that`s what scares me is how far this virus is now spreading and how quickly.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The suspect drove several hours from his home in Conklin, New York, to reach Buffalo. A day before the attack, police say the suspect was canvassing several spots. He was targeting a predominantly black area, according to a manifesto authorities believed the suspect authored. The 180-page document was posted online just two days before the massacre.\par \par \uc2 The author referred to himself as a white supremacist and repeatedly referenced the mass shooter who killed 51 people at a New Zealand mosque.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH GRAMAGLIA, BUFFALO POLICE DEPARTMENT COMMISSIONER: We have evidence that he was here, that he was doing some reconnaissance on this place, that he was at the location and some other locations. The evidence we have right now, we do not believe it was spur of the moment.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Tonight, Buffalo`s police commissioner warned of a lengthy investigation but was firm on the nature of the crime.\par \par \uc2 GRAMAGLIA: This absolutely is a hate crime, there`s no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And Emilie is with me now. What more do we know about the weapons, Emilie?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (on camera): So the suspect`s parents say that he just recently purchased the guns, investigators recovering three on scene here, some with messages of hate and racial slurs. The police commissioner tells me that it does appears that he purchased them legally but he also had in his possession high capacity magazines, which are not allowed here in New York. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Emilie Ikeda, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, I spoke with Grady Lewis, who talked to alleged gunman here outside the store on Friday before Saturday`s shooting. And then yesterday, he was here again during the attack. I asked him what he saw when the shooting started.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I heard the first shot, and one point, seven seconds after that, I looked up, 0.6 seconds, another shot. And he was just shooting people.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Was he just randomly targeting people, do you think, just shooting anybody?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, he was randomly shooting people outside. And then I see the security guard run in. When I saw the security run in, I was like, well, this ain`t no movie, this is for real. And then he went in shooting, shooting more people.\par \par \uc2 And I still see it in my head that this guy was actually just shooting at people standing here. It`s crazy. It`s crazy. It`s crazy. A guy was just shooting at people.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: He targeted black people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: He killed almost everybody. Tell me where you were when you saw him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I saw him walking through the exit door.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Here at the Tops?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Here at Tops on Friday. And I said, he looked out of place. He looks out of place. And I walked up to him, and I said, where are you from? He said two or three hours from now. And then were started talking about race, and I was like, well, there`s only one race. He said, well -- he didn`t he disagree but he said he feels that there`s different races.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: What did you think of him in that moment?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you ask me right now, that kid that I met on Friday wasn`t racist, not the kid I met on Friday. But --\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Super smart?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Super smart. He had a plan, obviously.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: You must have a mix of emotions, right? You could have been there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Very easily.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Five minutes earlier, you would have been there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Two -- I mean one minute and 20 seconds. I just walked across the street. I just opened up my thing, bang, bang, bang, bang, bang.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: He also told me, we need to make some changes in this country.\par \par \uc2 We just learned that President Biden and the first lady will come here to Buffalo on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And even as we cover this mass shooting, we sadly have to report on another one today. This one at a church in Southern California. The details are just coming in. We go to Maggie Vespa. She`s in Los Angeles with the latest there.\par \par \uc2 [18:40:00]\par \par \uc2 Maggie?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS: Kate, we`re hearing about six people shot in this shooting. One killed, four critically wounded. Authorities got the call this afternoon about shots fired inside the Geneva Presbyterian Church in Laguna Woods, that`s in Orange County, California.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff`s office tweeting they`ve detained one person and recovered a weapon. No details yet on who that person is or any kind of motive in this shooting. Kate?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Maggie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Buffalo, the police commissioner today called the deadly attack a racist hate crime. Aaron Gilchrist reports now on what could be fueling the astronomical rise in hate crimes across the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AARON GILCHRIST, NBC NEWS (voice over): Tonight, New York City on heightened alert after Saturday`s deadly shooting in Buffalo, police officers stepping up patrols around black churches for Sunday`s service.\par \par \uc2 It comes just days after three Korean women were injured in a Dallas shooting. Police say it may be part of a string of shootings targeting Asian businesses. And they`re investigating it as a hate crime.\par \par \uc2 EDGARDO GARCIA, DALLAS POLICE CHIEF: We are turning to every resident of Dallas to keep an eye out and safeguard our city. Hate has no place here.\par \par \uc2 GILCHRIST: the U.S. seeing a dramatic spike in hate crimes, rising in eight of the nation`s ten largest cities, an overall increase of 55 percent. And the alarming trend continues.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET HUANG, SOUTHERN POVERTY LAW CENTER PRESIDENT AND CEO: Racially motivated violence is the greatest threat in our country today.\par \par \uc2 GILCHRIST: Saturday`s tragic shooting just the latest in a string of violence. In 2019, a gunman killed 23 people at a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  in El Paso. Prosecutor saying he targeted Mexicans hoping to drive them out of the U.S. In 2018, 11 people died when a shooter opened fire during service at the Tree of Life synagogue in Petersburg.\par \par \uc2 Expects say this hate crimes are being fueled by extreme rhetoric often spread online.\par \par \uc2 HUANG: We have never seen hate more mainstreamed than we see it today. We have to do more to fight against these ideologies.\par \par \uc2 WALTER JACKSON JR. SUSIE JACKSON GRANDCHILD: My first thought was, here we go again.\par \par \uc2 GILCHRIST: Nearly seven years ago, Walter Jackson Jr. lost his grandmother, Susie, and several relatives after a gunman opened fire at the Mother Emmanuel AME Church in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: When you lose a loved one in a fashion such as that by a white supremacist, by someone who is geared with, hate just because of the color of their skin, it`s going to be something you`re going to be dealing with for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 GILCHRIST: Jackson says these moments of tragedy are a call for action for leaders to speak out against hate and the ideologies fueling these attacks. His message for Buffalo`s grieving community --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Continue to love. This was a situation done out of hate. And it`s easy for us to turn around and want to retaliate, do the same thing. But continue to keep your peace, continue to make your loved ones proud.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And Aaron is with me now. Aaron, there are calls for Congress to take action.\par \par \uc2 GILCHRIST (on camera): Kate, that`s right. Right now, there is a domestic terrorism prevention bill moving through the House. Civil rights and human rights groups are calling on Congress to pass it, saying it will create more resources to prevent hate attacks.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right, Aaron Gilchrist for us.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead tonight, we will have latest on the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And also a hero speaking out, Savannah Guthrie`s exclusive interview with a passenger who landed a plane last week after the pilot got ill.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:45:00]\par \par \uc2 SNOW: We`re back with news from overseas. In Ukraine today, officials say Russia has launched more attacks on that battered steel mill in Mariupol where hundreds of Ukrainian forces are still holed up. You can see the munitions raining down on that part of the port city.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile Finland and Sweden both confirmed today their intentions to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  in alliance with the west. It comes as the result of Russia`s invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 A stunning announcement came today from Pennsylvania`s lieutenant governor. He`s also a candidate for the senate right now. John Fetterman, the high- profile Democrat saying he`s recovering from a stroke he suffered Friday. He says he wasn`t feeling well that day, and that his wife urged him to go to the hospital. Fetterman says the stroke was caused by a clot from his heart, but he says he`s feeling much better, and in a statement, says doctors told him he didn`t suffer any cognitive damage. The primary by the way is this Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Now to TODAY SHOW explosive, we are hearing for the first time from that airplane passenger who landed a small aircraft in Florida last week after the pilot passed out. TODAY SHOW`S Savannah Guthrie spoke exclusively to that hero/passenger about how he did it and manage to stay so calm through the terrifying ordeal. Sam Brock has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS (voice over): Staring death in the face --\par \par \uc2 DARREN HARRISON, PASSENGER LANDED THE AIRPLANE: By the time I had moved forward to the front of the airplane, I realize that we had now gone into a dive at a very fast rate.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: -- Darren Harrison speaking for the first time exclusively to Today`s Savannah Guthrie about performing an emergency landing with no experience ever flying and a pilot passed out at the controls.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: All I saw when I came up to the front was water out the right window and it was coming away very quickly.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The plane was in a nosedive at that time?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Correct, yes. And at that point I knew if I didn`t react that we would die.\par \par \uc2 And so I reached over his body because he is, at this point, unresponsive. I kind of put my arm over toward my elbow sitting here and I grab the controls of the airplane and slowly started to pull back on the stick and turn the airplane.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The crisis cropping up midflight when Harrison says the pilot reported not feeling well.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: I`ve got a serious situation here. My pilot has gone incoherent.\par \par \uc2 About the time I stopped the plane, my -- that`s when it hit me. Yes, I was pretty calm and collected the whole time because I knew it was a life or death situation. Either you do what you have to do to control the situation or you`re going to die. And that`s what I did.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Harrison, who is from Lakeland, Florida, is expecting his first child with his wife, Britney, this summer, and says his center of faith and will to live carried him to the ground, even when the plane went off the radar right before landing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did you say the passengers landed the airplane?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh my God. That`s a great job.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: A miracle in every sense of the word.\par \par \uc2 Sam Brock, NBC News, Palm Beach County, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Unbelievable. And you can see much more of Savannah`s interview tomorrow on TODAY.\par \par \uc2 Still to come for us tonight, the nationwide labor shortage and how it could impact your summer vacation plans.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:50:00]\par \par \uc2 SNOW: We`re just weeks away from Memorial Day. And now that many pandemic restrictions have lifted, of course, a lot of families are gearing up for summer vacation. But the problem is a major labor shortage in resort communities that could impact the quality of your trip in a big way.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Rehema Ellis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REHEMA ELLIS, NBC NEWS (voice over): -- is running out for businesses desperate for workers to serve the crowds they expect this season.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I am nervous, yes.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: Jeanette Michaud runs Ben and Bill`s Chocolate Emporium in Falmouth. She`s short-staffed here, but it`s even worse at her shop on Martha`s Vineyard.\par \par \uc2 What do you have now compared to what you would normally have?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We would normally have 25 people in the summer season. Right now, we only have five.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: It`s a problem for Jeanette`s bottom line but also for customers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`re thinking of maybe closing a couple of days a week.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: And it`s not just Massachusetts. Nationwide, summer resort communities are struggling to fill jobs. From Maine, the shoreline explorer trollies, vital transportation for tourists, now slashing most of its roots. Why? Not enough drivers.\par \par \uc2 To Michigan, at Mission Point Resort, on Mackinac Island, they`re so short- staffed, bosses now stepping into positions they simply couldn`t fill.\par \par \uc2 LIZ WARE, MISSION POINT RESORT, SALES AND MARKETING VICE PRESIDENT: This weekend, I will be doing luggage and baggage.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: And along the California Coast, Giovanni`s Fish Market in Morro Bay offering about $20 an hour and still searching.\par \par \uc2 GIOVANNI DECARIMORE, GIOVANNI FISH AND MARKET OWNER: We`ve never had this many job openings ever in history.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: So, why has it been so hard to get help? Some say it`s a perfect storm, fewer local teens willing to work and the pandemic, but that`s not all.\par \par \uc2 Back on Cape Cod, like everywhere else, tighter restrictions on visas mean a lot fewer international workers, and now record housing shortages for seasonal hires mean tourists may find it`s not business as usual but even higher prices.\par \par \uc2 At Flying Bridge Restaurant, Tyler Hayes has just 20 applications instead of the usual 200.\par \par \uc2 TYLER HAYES, THE FLYING BRIDGE RESTAURANT MANAGER: There`s going to be very long waits just to get in. Once you sit down on a table, it`s going to take a long time to get your food.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: People are bracing themselves.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW SEXTON, CAPE COD, MASSACHUSETTS RESIDENT: You just have to kind to pick your times and figure out when they`re going to be open.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: But still some are hopeful.\par \par \uc2 PAUL NIEDZWIECKI, CEO, CAPE COD CHAMBER OF COMMERCE: Cape Coders are a hardy bunch and they`ll figure out how to provide those services.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: Tourists are coming, so is the scramble to give them what they`re used to.\par \par \uc2 Reheme Ellis, NBC News, Falmouth, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: After the break, we`ll be back in Buffalo on this very difficult, remembering a woman who inspired very many here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:55:00]\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Finally, tonight from here in Buffalo, each life lost leaves a gaping hole in a family and in the community. We`re going to hear more about each of the ten victims in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, as we end our broadcast, we wanted to make sure you knew about one of them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice over): You didn`t have to know Pearl Young to know women like her. She was the kind of hero you`re likely to find in communities all over the country. Loved ones on social media remembering the 77-year-old today as the neighborhood mom and the sweetest person. They say Pearl opened her heart and home to those who needed a hot meal, encouragement and kindness. In fact, for decades, she ran a food pantry in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 Those grieving are honoring her as the most beautiful soul who loved her church, God, and family. Pearl Young was the wife of a pastor, a mom, a beloved grandma and aunty. And her family told us today that Pearl and the others gone much too soon in this tragedy will never be forgotten.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She was just so beautiful inside and out, because good always overshadows evil. And to the people in Buffalo community, we extend our condolences to all of the families that lost their loved one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: I said to Zaneta (ph) earlier, the woman whose son was shot, that there really are no words, except we`re sorry and that our hearts are with this entire community.\par \par \uc2 And that is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for this Sunday night. I`m Kate Snow. Please take care of yourself. Lester Holt will be reporting from here in Buffalo tomorrow evening. For all of us, stay safe and have a good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220516ei5f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The U.S. Economy Added More Than 400,000 New Jobs In April Despite Soaring Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Welker \par \uc2 489 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Let`s turn now of the economy and another strong jobs report today. More than 400,000 jobs added in April. Unemployment holding at 3.6 percent. But a worker shortage persists with experts saying wages are not keeping up with rising inflation. Kristen Welker is at the White House to tell us more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With growing dissatisfaction over his handling of the economy, President Biden in battleground Ohio touting today`s jobs report.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have now created a total of 8.3 million jobs in my first 15 months in office.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: With 428,000 jobs added to the U.S. economy in April, the country has gained back 95 percent of the jobs lost early in the pandemic, creating opportunities for people like new graduate Jordyn Whitted who just started a communications role with an arts non-profit.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:08]\par \par \uc2 JORDYN WHITTED, RECENT HIRE: And it gave me an opportunity to really loop in my hobbies and my interest is more money. Different title different organizational structure.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But the country is also facing dire economic headwinds. While paychecks are up five and a half percent, prices are soaring even higher with inflation at a 40-year record. Home prices have spiked 20 percent. Gas prices are back near record highs. And the Dow is down nine percent this year, putting a dent in people`s retirement funds.\par \par \uc2 Republicans today blasting the President`s policies, blaming him and Democrats for skyrocketing prices. Adding to the economic turmoil, there`s a record number of job openings. There are currently two jobs available for every person who is unemployed. Among the reasons, some people are retiring early due to the pandemic. Others are holding out for higher pay.\par \par \uc2 Businesses like precision landscaping in Portland, Oregon say they`ve had to increase salaries and even turn down new customers.\par \par \uc2 TYRONE FOSTER, PRESIDENT, PRECISION LANDSCAPE SERVICE: It has been hard to find employees because it`s become a much more challenging environment to find employees. We get competition from areas that we once didn`t.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Recent polls show just a third of the country approves of the president`s handling of the economy, intensifying concerns for Democrats as the midterms approach, Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programing copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220510ei560005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The TODAY Show for April 29, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Craig Melvin, Erin McLaughlin, Tom Costello, Tom  Llamas, Al Roker, Gadi Schwartz, Sheinelle Jones, Willie Geist \par \uc2 9,279 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: It is 8:00 on "TODAY." Coming up, breaking overnight. A marine veteran reportedly killed in Ukraine after heading there to fight against Russia. What his family is saying this morning as the Capital City of Kyiv comes under attack once again. We`ve got the three things to watch.\par \par \uc2 Then, breed is good. A new study suggests that type of dog has little to do with its personality.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELINOR KARLSSON, GENETICIST: You shouldn`t be putting all of these expectations on a dog just based on those breeds.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`ve got some furry friends in Studio 1A to help put that research to the test.\par \par \uc2 Plus, throw me a bone. An up-close look at a rare velociraptor fossil set to be auctioned for millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They bred raptors.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So, how much will they fetch? We`ll check it out before the bidding begins, today, Friday, April 29, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Sisters` trip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Greenville, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: With my mom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On our first trip to New York.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Howdy, Houston, Texas.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: And to our families.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In Iowa.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I turned 21 today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Bucket list trip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Richmond, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Shout out to our kids.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Watching in Crown Point, Indiana.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From San Diego.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s my 60th birthday.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Three generations.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Winamac, Indiana.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: To the "TODAY" show.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Now, three generations? Whoa. We got to get out there. We`re going to say hi and visit with everyone in a bit. You guys, happy Friday. We are so happy you are ending your week with us.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Savannah`s got the morning off. Good to see everybody.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You all alright? Ready for the weekend?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Fri-yay.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re ready. we`re ready.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Fri-yay. We are ready. But we are already looking ahead to next week here on "TODAY" because Sheinelle Jones is going to be taking us to the Met Gala. You remember last year, Sheinelle rocked the red carpet for us. Well, she`s going to be back at it again on Monday night as the fashion world`s most exclusive party --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I think --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: -- gets underway.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: -- I think Sheinelle should be on the red carpet and not holding a mic on the site. That`s my vote.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: She`s that --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: My vote.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: -- she`s that fabulous.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. And this year`s theme, by the way, is gilded glamour.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I`m not sure what that means but I`m sure it`s going to be fabulous.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It will be hot. All right. Let`s get to our news at 8:00 beginning with the latest from Ukraine, including new attacks on the capital. While an American family is mourning over the reported death of a son who was fighting alongside Ukrainian forces. NBC`s Erin McLaughlin is in Kyiv with the three things we need to watch.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Erin. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Hoda. CNN is reporting that a U.S. veteran was killed while fighting in Ukraine. His mother telling CNN that her son had been working for a private military company. NBC has reached out to both the State Department and his family but have yet to hear back. CNN reporting a U.S. official said they were aware of these reports and are closely monitoring the situation.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, this morning in the capital, they`re cleaning up after two Russian missiles struck the center of the city. Injuring 10 and killing at least one. The attack happened as the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Secretary-General was visiting Kyiv. During his visit, Antonio Guterres acknowledged the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . had failed to prevent the war and stop the bloodshed. He also focused on the besieged City of Mariupol where hundreds of civilian and Ukrainian fighters remain trapped inside an old steel plant. Guterres saying President Putin had agreed, in principle, to allow civilians to evacuate. Hoda.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Erin McLaughlin for us there in the capital city of Kyiv. Erin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Meanwhile, back here, lots of eyes on Wall Street today after the surprising news. The economy actually shrank in the first quarter of this year. But does that tell the whole story? NBC`s Tom Costello is following this one for us from Washington.\par \par \uc2 Tom, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Craig. You know, the news was really stunning because the economy grew at almost seven percent at the end of 2021, then the sudden reversal in the first three months of the year. Analysts say there are many reasons. Number one, Russia`s invasi