ar \par \uc2 BRACE: And the good reminder is making sure your ears are over shoulders, which are over your hips.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Wait. Ears over my shoulders. Everybody at home, look. Everybody`s trying it. Come on, everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: See everybody`s in the studio got their posture right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I see you at home. Ears over your shoulders.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: Right. Good.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Look at that. Literally everyone in the studio just perked up.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: I`m telling you, right?\par \par \uc2 JONES: It goes to show we don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: No, because we`re all in this position all of the time, right?\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. All right. Ears over the shoulders.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: And you know, look. If we`re sitting for long periods of time, every 20 minutes you want to stand up and move around, all right?\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. All right. Tom, your turn.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Robert, finally the last F here, food. Let`s talk about it.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: Yes. Fortifying foods.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: I know you`ve broken this up into categories.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: OK, so when it comes to food you want to make sure, look, bones are made of calicium, right?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: OK.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: So you want to make sure that you`re getting enough calcium in your diet. Typocally when people think calcium they think about cheeses and dairy foods, yogurts right here, but there are more options if you`re either lactose intolerant like me, right?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Right. Be careful.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: Or if you`re vegan, right? So you have the almonds. You have the lentils. Oranges are my favorite because they`re - I have a little sweet tooth.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: OK.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: Right? And you have seeds, sesame, chia, poppy and tuna. OK.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All you need is a little red wine right here. Let`s make this perfect.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BRACE: Fantastic, now --\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: But we see oranges again because we`re talking vitamin D.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: Talking vitamin D. now vitamin D is essentially because it helps you absorb - your body absorb the calcium that you just ate, right?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: OK.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: So all of this stuff is good. We`ve got some beautiful egg yolks there. Some people say egg yolks aren`t healthy but as part of a overall healthy diet you can include there. Oranges there. (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 JONES: Those are the redest egg yolks I`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: You know, what about vitamin D supplements? I heard that`s like a new trend now. Should you be taking them?\par \par \uc2 BRACE: Yes. Listen. You should always consult with your physician first.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: But yes, vitamin D supplements are also great if you need to get that extra boost.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And we only have like five minutes left.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Vitamin K - oh sorry. Those are the whole alphabet. Vitamin K. We got some good stuff here.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: Vitamin K, kale, green vegetables, and the superfoods, bone health superfoods. Salmon because you can see it in many of the different categories there.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRACE: And if you remember those three things - fitness, fierce form, and fortifying foods.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Robert Brace, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you. You`re good.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: You`re good.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: This is dinosaur kALE, right?\par \par \uc2 BRACE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Dinosaur kale.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Perfect segue because up next if all that talk of aging has you feeling old, this guy ought to make you feel young again. He`s been around for at least 108 million years. Not Al Roker. No, no.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JONES: This is really a surreal deal. Hoda and Al are getting up close and personal with one of the most remarkable dinosaur discoveries ever, but first this is "TODAY" on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:51:14]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Oh.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Oh, I remember that one.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I remember that one. Everybody went oh. I mean, from the Jurassic Park.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know. That`s so you. That whole thing is so you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right. The velociraptor.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. So this morning, guys, we have a real treat. That thing was in the movies. We got it in real life. That is a velociraptor behind Mr. Roker and myself, and it`s making its way to Rokafeller Center.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right. We`re right down the street from our Studio 1A. We`re at Christie`s Auction House where this incredibly rare velociraptor is about to be auctioned off.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, and guys, if you`re wondering what all these adorable kids are doing here, they are here on a field trip. They`re from PS59 right here in New York City. They made posters. They came to play. We`re excited. We`re also excited to have James Hyslop. He`s the Head of Christie`s Science and Natural History. It`s so great to see you. This is a stunner. I mean, the first thing you think about is, number one, just how old is this?\par \par \uc2 JAMES HYSLOP, HEAD OF CHRISTIE`S SCIENCE AND NATURAL HISTORY: So these fossil bones are 110 million years old, but the species itself was only known to science in the 60s and it was discovered in the same here as Marilyn`s Warhol was painted just around the corner.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 HYSLOP: And welcomed here at Christie`s. This our spring wide key sale week, and we`re delighted to have this raptor.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No kidding.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And James (ph), where did - where was this found?\par \par \uc2 HYSLOP: So this was found in Montana. There aren`t very many of them. There are only three skeletons known, so it`s a real honor to be bringing something as rare as this to auction.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well you understand how you might find some bones, but like were all of the bones found in that exact location and all just reassembled?\par \par \uc2 HYSLOP: Yes. Painstaking work goes into reassembling an animal like this, and what`s really nice is all of the claws that give the name to this species are there. Look how sharp and fierce they would have been in the lifetime of this animal?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And you know, it`s funny. I thought it would be bigger.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Me, too. It seems little.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: It seems kind of small.\par \par \uc2 HYSLOP: Well if you remember in the film they`re chasing us around the kitchen. Yes, they were on the human size.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I mean, this is - just curious. Someone`s going to bid on this, right?\par \par \uc2 HYSLOP: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And what are you expecting that this will fetch?\par \par \uc2 HYSLOP: So we have a presale estimate of $4 million to $6 million on it, but it could do much more than that. A few years ago we had standard T- Rex and set a world record of $32 million, so we`ll see what we get.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So would this - like this wind up in some kind of a museum somewhere if someone buys it or in someone`s home?\par \par \uc2 HYSLOP: Most things like this end up in museums in due course.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 HYSLOP: I mean, it`s just come from a museum (inaudible) where it was on display in Copenhagen.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Now Al and I have questions, but we don`t have good questions.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: No.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know who has good questions? Some of the kids from PS59. Beatrix (ph), you`re here? Come over here, hnoey. Hi. So you - did you make a sign, too?\par \par \uc2 BEATRIX (ph): Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Where - it`s down there somewhere? OK. Anyway, tell us what your question is, sweetie. Let me see your sign. This is what she made. Look how cute.\par \par \uc2 BEATRIX (ph): Where did the name velociraptor come from?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Oh, good one.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Good question.\par \par \uc2 HYSLOP: So the name velociraptor is from a different animal that was about the size of a chicken, and when they made Jurassic Park, Michael Crayton took the name velociraptor and applied it to this animal. This one is called deinonychus antirrhopus, which means terrible claw and well counterbalanced. And this is the animal that they based what we all know and love as the velociraptor.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Beatrix (ph), do you love - do you love dinosaurs?\par \par \uc2 BEATRIX (ph): Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What do our love about them?\par \par \uc2 [08:55:00]\par \par \uc2 BEATRIX (ph): I love how since that every - since they`re extinct everybody pays so much attention to them because they want to find out more of what happened before humans came.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`re smart. What grade are you in?\par \par \uc2 BEATRIX (ph): Third.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Kids are smart.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: (inaudible) really.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So James (ph) -\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BEATRIX (ph): German School Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You go where?\par \par \uc2 BEATRIX (ph): German School Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, German School Brooklyn. OK, good to know.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So the money that`s raised, some of this is going to go to charity?\par \par \uc2 HYSLOP: We don`t know where the money is going to go from this yet. The owners haven`t yet decided. But we are delighted that we have this raptor here on displays at Christie`s. It`s free for the public to come see it for the next two weeks.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. And can we just point out - I just want to say all the kids who came here today - they`re parents are all in the back - but they all made beautiful signs. This is a big event. It`s happening right here in the heart of Midtown, Manhattan. And what a cool thing for you guys to be able to see it all in real life, so we want to thank you, thank the moms, thank the teachers.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: You guys were great. Really terrific.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You guys were amazing. Thanks again.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And this guy`s on display here at Christie`s until the auction, which takes place May 9 until May 14.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you so much. What a great way to spend a Friday afternoon, right kids? Oh yes, I thought so.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: "3rd Hour TODAY" is coming up next, and, of course, "Hoda and Jenna".\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Don`t forget it. Say bye, Beatrix (ph). Say bye.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Bye-bye, everybody!\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Bye, everybody!\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220429ei4t000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 If You Are Looking To Get Into The Workforce After A Career Break: Some Advice On Making That Resume Gap Work For You\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Llamas, Sheinelle Jones, Al Roker \par \uc2 9,613 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: All right. We are back now with "Consumer Confidential." The pandemic changed ways many companies do business, and that led to lot of people taking a break from careers voluntarily or not.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES: That`s true. In fact, according to a new LinkedIn survey from January, 62 percent of people globally and 64 percent of women have taken a career break. That is a lot. And now, many of those people are looking to get back into the workforce.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER: So, we decided we`d bring in Catherine Fisher. She`s a LinkedIn career expert with some advice on how to get back into the game.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER, LINKEDIN CAREER EXPERT: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, you say right now is a great time if you have taken a break or just thinking about getting into the job market after a period of time, to start looking for a job. How come?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: It is. You know, because right now there`s a lot of jobs out there available but it`s also because the stigma of taking a career break is actually going away. And people are still worried about that. But the world of work is changing. And with that is this idea, this antiquated idea that if you took a break that is a bad thing. It is not. In fact, 50 percent of hiring managers say that that career break is actually common.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Really?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Yes. And so, it is all about how you reframe it and you should be confident that you are skilled to go back into the workforce.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And when you say take a break, is it people who stopped during the pandemic, so maybe a year or two or people who have been out it for years?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: It could be anything. Maybe you took time off to take care of an ill parent, raise your children or you were laid off during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, let`s get to some advice. First of all, you say review and reflect. What do you mean by this?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: It`s really, you know, when you left your job, it could be that what was really important to you then is no longer as important to you. And also, the way employers are treating employees has changed.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: So, there is more flexibility. So, really defining what`s important to you so that you can be intentional about what`s -- what you need from your job and really do that research. Because, like I said, flexibility is much more common than it used to be.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: So, what you are used to may have changed.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Well, that`s -- is there a fine line? Because you`re so right, it has changed. I mean, coming up in this business, we would -- you`d never got to take a break.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Never.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: But now, I feel people take breaks for whatever reasons, mental health breaks, taking care of a relative. So, are employers more open to this? And how do you know that you are not -- I don`t want to say abusing the privilege, but how do you know when to ask and how to ask?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: You know, it`s all about your personal preference. But I think that what we hear from hiring managers is that they actually think that there is a huge untapped pool of candidates who are on career breaks. And that they are more likely to reach out to you if you`ve set context for why you took that career break.\par \par \uc2 The key here is being really specific and addressing it. And so, that people aren`t trying to figure out why did you take this time off? You just can come clean with this is the -- you know, I took time off to take care of my children. Get them into school. And now, I`m ready to go back to the workforce.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: So, if you did take this break, should you -- you know, like right now, before you get back into the workforce, take a side class or do a little something extra that kind of boost your resume to say, well, during this break I did this to better myself in the marketplace?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Absolutely. So, first thing you want to do is reach out to networks. So, it is a great time to reconnect with people that you have worked with in the past. Find out what skills are now required in this job that you used to love and have.\par \par \uc2 So, think about teachers. Maybe you were a teacher, you took time off. You`re coming back. And all of a sudden, you have to deal with like these COVID safety protocols or what does flexible learning look like? So, understand what`s changed in your industry. And there`s many online courses you can take and having those conversations.\par \par \uc2 But don`t undersell your skills. Let`s say you, you know, volunteered at your kid`s school. Well, right there you have got leadership, communication skills, creative thinking. Don`t undersell. Make sure that you are talking about all those skills that you gained during your time off.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s fair.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Is this a time where you -- if you were contemplating, not going back to what you did but something different, is now a good time to be doing this?\par \par \uc2 [09:20:00]\par \par \uc2 FISHER: It absolutely is. A lot of companies, you know, are having a hard time finding people. And so, this is a great time if you want to switch industries. And the transferable skills apply to many different jobs. So, really looking at that -- what the skills that they`re looking to hire you for, and that is what you want to lead within your interview.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We`re out of time. Really quickly, is there a place where somebody can go, well, just even a website for returnships? I think it is important. Like, there are people -- there`s actually programs and companies that will hire you.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: It`s a wonderful program that companies are doing. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , NBC universal, \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 , they offer programs where you can come back to work, they train you, they upskill you and mentor you and then, there is a job for you.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Wow. That`s great.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: That`s really cool.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: NBC Universal.\par \par \uc2 JONES: \uc2 Google\uc2  it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Catherine Fisher --\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: -- from LinkedIn, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Coming up in "She Made It," super model Winnie Harlow opening up to our Jill Martin about the personal reason she`s getting into the sun care business to help other women.\par \par \uc2 And then, later, he played one of the funniest characts on "The Office."\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Ever.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Ever, as Tom says. And now, Craig Robinson is stepping into the role of a leading man in his new comedy series. We can`t wait to check in with him and hear all about it. And we`ll be back in the next 60 seconds. We`re going to stare at you though for the next 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: We are back with a new story in your series, "She Made it." This time, Jill Martin introduces us to a model turn mogul. Jill.\par \par \uc2 JILL MARTIN, LIFESTYLE AND COMMERCE CONTRIBUTOR: I knew you would like that one. Yes. This is really an incredible story but also so inspiring. Winnie Harlow, one of the biggest names in fashion and now, she`s taking her talents from the runway to the sun care aisle. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WINNIE HARLOW, MODEL: I`ve been able to showcase everyone else`s work, the things they have labored on and now, I get to do the same for myself. It is a dream come true.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (voiceover): For years, Winnie Harlow has been blazing a trail in the fashion industry. But now, she`s making strides in business as well.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (on camera): After everything you have been through, and I know this goes back to childhood, how important is that title for you, entrepreneur.\par \par \uc2 HARLOW: My mom is a hairdresser and she had her own salon. My dad is a mechanic in Jamaica, and still runs his own shop. I was thinking, where do I get this from? And I was just like, wait. It is in my blood, you know, it`s from my parents.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (voiceover): As a child, Winnie was diagnosed with a skin condition vitiligo.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (on camera): It`s hard enough being a kid to begin be.\par \par \uc2 HARLOW: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: But kids were so mean and saying names to you. Tell me about your childhood.\par \par \uc2 HARLOW: When you are in a small town, especially as a young kid. It feels like that is the end all and be all. It seems like the end of the world but it is really just the beginning of your life.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (voiceover): After competing on "America`s Next Top Model," Winnie started making a name for herself on high fashion runways and at photoshoots for big brands.\par \par \uc2 HARLOW: Walking for Victoria Secret was incredible for me, life changing. A lot of people don`t know this, but I did try out for Victoria Secret the year before and didn`t get it. And so, getting it the second time was amazing. Like, any "Vogue" cover I`m on, I`m the first model with vitiligo to be on the cover. So, that is mind-blowing to me because I had never seen myself represented growing up.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (voiceover): Winnie says in 2018, an incident on a set inspired her to take action.\par \par \uc2 HARLOW: I had this horrible experience on set at a shoot where no one wanted me to apply sunscreen. It made my skin look purple and gray and it wasn`t great for the photo shoot. So, you know, I went without to get the best shots. But after two days of shooting from sun up to sun down in the Bahamas, I was burnt to a crisp. I was like in so much pain, I had to have doctors give me injections for pain, for inflammation. And I realize that there wasn`t sun care on the market that made you look gorgeous and also be well protected.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (voiceover): Winnie got to work developing skin care products.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (on camera): I think people think you are a super model. Things just -- you know, you just -- you get a line. And it doesn`t work like that.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HARLOW: I had no idea where to start. I had the idea, like it is my brain child. But I had no business savvy. I think some of the most challenging things for me were one hearing all those knows when we started fundraising, especially being a business that was created in a pandemic, where things were already being pushed back with packaging and the formulas and like our factory shutting down for COVID. And, you know, there were so many steps back every time we were taking steps forward.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (on camera): Nearly three years later, Winnie raised $65 million from investors to launch her Cay Skin. A sun care line inspired by the beaches of her family` native Jamaica.\par \par \uc2 HARLOW: I wanted to put things that I`ve used since I was a kid going to Jamaica and staying with my dad that used to cut the aloe vera plant and rub it directly onto our skins for like mosquito bites or sunburns. We also have hydrating nectar, which is from different fruits of botanicals.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Winnie hopes to inspire people to take care of and to love the skin they are in.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN (on camera): What would you say as advice to young girls out there who are going through a tough time who just like want to get through it and pursue her dream?\par \par \uc2 HARLOW: I would say focus on yourself. There`s only two things that you can really do in life. You can change things. And those things that you can`t change, you got to move forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: I love that, like you can either change it up or you can fix what you can control. And that`s what she`s doing.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: So, Cay Skin is available both direct to consumer and in Sephora, which we have some of the products here. Winnie says has been a dream to be in Sephora. And she applied to her local Sephora, age 16. She didn`t get the job.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, really?\par \par \uc2 DREYER: But now, she`s --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Worked out OK.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: It worked out OK. But every no really does lead you to a yes sometimes. So, those no`s are sometimes positive. So --\par \par \uc2 JONES: There`s so many layers to this one. I love it.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: I know. And I really liked her. She`s just such a -- her energy just comes right through the screen.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Strong.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. And she`s anywhere.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I mean, posters.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And confidence. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Signs all over New York City. All right. Thank you, Jill.\par \par \uc2 OK. Coming up, "The Office" and "Brooklyn Nine-Nine" star Craig Robinson is back on TV and starring in a new movie. He`s on the TV.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes. Give us a blue steel.\par \par \uc2 JONES: He`s also got (INAUDIBLE) going on.\par \par \uc2 And then, later, on the (INAUDIBLE) guru George Oliphant. What`s your pose, George? Reveals the mistakes we make in our home including --\par \par \uc2 DREYER: There it is.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- reason you should pick out your furniture before you paint you