ble. You know, they`re like all about the fans. And there really is a community there in Nashville and country music where they root for each other and they`re happy when somebody -- I remember when Chris Stapleton, the great Chris Stapleton, he was a songwriter forever. And he finally had his moment. And you just like Luke Bryan and all those guys who he wrote songs for, they get up on stage and say this night belongs to Chris. I`m happy to win. Like, they want the talented people to win. And they usually do in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I always think the three cities I could live in were New York, New Orleans and Nashville. Those are my three.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Yes, three for three.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Can we talk about Carrie Underwood? This performance was like beyond, it was beyond. It was -- they called it a gravity defying performance.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Wow, look at the outfit, first of all.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Let`s take a look.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Goodness.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She goes up. She didn`t go up in that clip, she goes up. She went up. She went up. She twirled. She`s like, remember when --\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Pink.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I was just going to say.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: That`s what I was thinking.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Pink did that.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And remember Pink like went over the audience and dipped into water and came up. It was like \uc2 Cirque du Soleil\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know when I look at you, you just anchored four hours of "MORNING JOE". You anchored four hours of "MORNING JOE". You came here three minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I did. Well, luckily, we`re across the street. I just ripped off my tie.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You ripped your tie off.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: See, this is me casual. And here we are talking about the CMTs.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Can you believe you`re about to do five hours of television? Do you just --\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I`ll tell you what, sitting next to Hoda Kotb it makes it all OK. The fifth hour will be tough, but here we are. Here we are.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, so if you`ve got your -- your new copy of Vogue magazine, Willie. Guess who`s on the cover?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Bad Girl RiRi.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: RiRi. Look at that. Oh my God, Annie Leibovitz took those pictures. She talks about maternity styles, relationships, everything.\par \par \uc2 [10:05:00]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: By the way, I just think what is the coolest thing in the world is it used to be pregnancy was some -- something that you, you know, kind of hid a little bit, muumuus and all that stuff. And you know, it`s like, no one knows I`m pregnant yet. They always -- and it`s in this moment, it`s just like on the cover of Vogue magazine we celebrate it.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: How long ago was it when Demi Moore went on the cover pregnant? And that was a moment of like, oh, you can do that?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Right. You`re allowed.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: She sort of broke through. Rihanna taken it to a different level.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, she talked about her relationship with ASAP Rocky, what did she say?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: She said, people don`t get out of the friend zone very easily with me. I certainly took a while to get over how much I know him and how much he knows me. Because we also know how much trouble we can land each other in, that`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: By the way, the friend zone, that`s the worst, isn`t it? And you`re like, oh my God, I`m crazy about him. And he`s like, I`m so happy we`re friends. Did you ever?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: You know, I`ve known my wife for so long. I don`t even remember but we were friends first. And I think that`s the biggest --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You are?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Yes, I mean, for people who don`t know, I met my wife in sixth grade. We went to high school together, we went to college together. But we were buddies first.\par \par \uc2 And I think even today, all these years later. Oh, there we are. That`s high school. That`s the foundation of our relationship is that we were always friends.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So, how did you break out of the friend zone? If you were friends in a group, yes.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I have a very romantic secret. I made out with her on the roof of my friend`s house with some warm Bud Light in the red hot fall of `91.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Did you just go in for a kiss and you`re like I`m going for it?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I think we were both -- I hope, I think we`re both sensing it. And I don`t know if, like, her parents know (INAUDIBLE), but it was 30 years ago. I think we can statute a limitation to that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And did you know, OK, so now you had --\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I was so excited because I always liked her I think in my heart. We were truly good friends. And we had the same group of friends. But I always liked her. I didn`t know how she felt until that roof.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So the Monday after the roof or the Tuesday after when you`re in school, did you --\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We were like, was that a thing? Or was it just a moment? Or a thing that turned out it was a real thing that we both felt?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Did you hold hands in school?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I don`t know if I did that. I was like a cool football guy can be seen holding hands. What about you in the friend zone?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I spent most of my life in the friend zone. I feel like I was always seen as the friend. You`re like, oh my God. You`re so fun. You can -- I was always the, you can come to and I was always the -- when someone would approach at a dance and I was like it`s happening, it`s happening. They`re like hey, do you think your friend would dance if I asked?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: No.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: It`s hard to believe sitting here today. It is because of the charisma and the smart and beauty and all of it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Can I say one other super painful story and other people who felt this before. We were playing spin the bottle, we were in seventh grade just for the kids. Spun the bottle, it landed on a guy named Todd and I had never been more excited. Do you know what he said?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Oh, no, please don`t.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: He said, I think that`s going a little too far. I know, it was a bone crusher.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Where`s Todd now? Todd, where you at, Todd?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But it was a bone crusher but I do remember thinking in that moment like oh my God, like, I don`t think I fit. Like, I don`t know where my place is but it happens later on in life when someone asked to dance and you`re still not sure they mean it, do they mean it. But for everybody who`s ever been the weird one, we know what you feel like.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: That the moral of the story is someday you turned out to be Hoda Kotb, it all turns out OK, right? Come on.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK, we got -- there`s a new celebrity master class being offered. If you want to -- if you could learn to sing from one celebrity, who would you pick?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I already mentioned Stapleton, I love his voice. I`m going to go Eddie Vedder.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, Eddie Vedder.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I didn`t know where that came from.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That`s who you want?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: That graveling deep --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, I like that. I think -- I mean, if I could really learn and sing like that person even though I have no skills, Adele. OK, let`s see the videos please.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: That`s the right answer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIAH CAREY, SINGER: OK, ready. Oh, when you walk by every night.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Talking sweet and looking fine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I get kind of hectic inside.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Baby, I`m so into you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Darling, if you only knew.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But it`s just a sweet, sweet fantasy, baby. When I close my eyes, you come and take me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK, that is the most brilliant thing in the world.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Wow, that`s great.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Brilliant.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: They can give you a master class but the truth about something like Mariah is she`s touched by God a little bit. And she`s going into places on the register that nobody else is going.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No matter how hard you try.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, guys. You know the answer, guests who scored an invite to Ina Garten`s Hampton`s home?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Are we doing this again? Jenna was very upset about this.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, we`re going to continue, you`re getting two scoops, OK? Willie is going to give us the third scoop coming up right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:13:58]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Willie is in for Jenna. I know that Jenna ribbed you and was real super mad at you.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: She`s vicious.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, because you went to Ina Garten`s house and we begged her for an invite that she basically kind of said, well, OK. And then, we realized that invite had been taken by you. Anyway, let`s just see how much fun you had.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Oh, it was great.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN, BAREFOOT CONTESSA HOST: The wonderful journalist and writer Willie Geist is joining me for an amazing day at the barn.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I`m about to get on a ferry, cross that body of water and spend the day with Ina Garten.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: I`m baking his favorite salted chocolate chunk cookies.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: That`s perfect.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: I`m giving the garden tour.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Are there any critters in your garden?\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Mine, no.\par \par \uc2 And we`re shaping up Bourbon Sidecar.\par \par \uc2 I can see he`s done this before.\par \par \uc2 That are perfect with spiced pecans to share with our other halves, Christina and Jeffrey.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: That might be my new drink.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: How fabulous is that?\par \par \uc2 As always, you`re the perfect guest.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Oh, you say that to all the guys I`ve met.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: She is the best. I mean, everyone already knows she`s a great hostess, but just the way she actually welcomed me and my wife Christina there into her home. I mean, we were there all day. Christina had some work to do for her real life job. Ina sets her up in her office.\par \par \uc2 [10:15:09]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wait, what?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: They`re like over in the barn, and she`s amazing. And by the way, for this special which called "Be My Guest" on Discovery Plus, she interviewed me. So, I do these interviews on Sundays.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, all the time.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And it is a very different thing but she was so well prepared?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She was?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: She has such great questions, things I`ve never been asked, things I`d forgotten about my childhood and growing up in college and all that. She`s a really good interviewer on top of everything else.\par \par \uc2 And beyond that, she`s just a wonderful person and Jeffrey is great.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I wonder about Jeffrey because I feel like we hear about him as the person who tastes her food and what`s the story with those two?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: They`ve known each other forever. Well beyond she -- well before she was, you know, the Barefoot Contessa.\par \par \uc2 She had -- she actually -- she worked in the Nuclear Energy Department in the -- in the White House way back in the 70s. And she had a dream that she wanted to open a little shop. And she saw for sale, this little place in the handprint. And she said, I think I want to buy that shop and leave everything open. And Jeffrey said, let`s do it. That`s the kind of husband he is.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And then, fast forward, all these years later, she`s the Ina Garten we know with his support all along the way.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She`s like a scientist who went into became.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: She`s brilliant and he`s brilliant.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: He`s still a professor and teaches. But they`re wonderful together because they`ve known each other forever. And we were just lucky to be in their house for a few hours.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`m surprised about your memory. I was just thinking that just now because you were over there a long -- a while ago to shoot. But just listening to all the things that you`ve been remembering. Even when you brought up Demi Moore earlier. Like you were -- I was like, you have a -- your memory is good.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: It`s just triggered something.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Let`s drink, shall we? Did you make a good bourbon cocktail?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We had a Bourbon Sidecar, which I`m a bourbon man.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you. I know you are.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We were talking about Nashville. I picked up that habit in Nashville. Delicious habit.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What is this?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: So, this is -- I can`t remember what`s in Sidecar. There`s bourbon, it`s sweet. It`s got a little sugar on the glass. That`s not salt. And then, these --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: How come guys don`t like to drink out of straws? What`s the problem? What is that about?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: That`s a good question. I always take it out.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Guys always take the straw. What is it?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: I don`t know. For you. Was that cool? It`s good, right?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s yummy. What -- oh, so it`s just sweet and sour.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: We got together I believe it was in the fall, kind of a nice autumn drink. But it`s really anytime here. So, cheers to you, Ina.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Ina, we love you. What`s your bourbon of choice by the way?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: My every day is Maker`s. But then, you know, sometimes on a special occasion, you go up the ladder a little bit.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You do?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Across the state of Kentucky. There`s something called Pappy, it`s very expensive. But you treat your -- Pappy Van Winkle -- you treat yourself once in a while. It`s worth it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`ll try it. All right. Still ahead, guys, no major spring break plans yet?\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Not too late. We`ll tell you about some great getaway deals when we come right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220415ei4c000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Turmoil At \uc2 Disney\uc2 : Workers From Coast To Coast Planning A Mass Walkout Over The Company`s Handling Of Florida`s Controversial "Don`t Say Gay" Bill\b0\par\par\uc2 Craig Melvin, Hoda Kotb, Blayne Alexander \par \uc2 756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN: Now to that dust up at \uc2 Disney\uc2  making worldwide headlines, a number of employees at \uc2 Disney\uc2  planning a massive walkout today.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Yeah, they`re protesting both the controversial bill in Florida that would limit what children are taught in classrooms and how the \uc2 Disney Company\uc2  has responded to it. NBC`s Blayne Alexander joins us from Orlando. Hey, Blayne, good morning.\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well guys, good morning to you. Certainly this is not great timing for \uc2 Disney\uc2 . It comes as thousands are set to descend upon the theme parks for spring break this week. Now, we know that during a town hall yesterday the CEO reiterated his apology but some employees say that`s not enough as this planned walkout is set to impact not only the theme parks, but other parts of the company as well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): This morning turmoil in one of the most famous companies in the world. \uc2 Disney\uc2  which spans iconic properties from \uc2 Marvel\uc2 , \uc2 ESPN\uc2 , and \uc2 Hulu\uc2  to cruises and parks including Disney World, now facing a fierce internal debate with leadership including new CEO \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2  working to write the ship as politics rocks the happiest place on earth. Employees are expected to walk off the job today over Florida so called "Don`t Say Gay" bill. If signed into law, the bill would ban classroom instruction on sexual orientation or gender identity in kindergarten through third grade. Critics say reversing decades of progress on LGBTQ rights.\par \par \uc2 The parental rights and education bill has prompted weeks of protests across the Sunshine State. Now a group of \uc2 Disney\uc2  employees urging their colleagues across the country to stage a walkout today writing an open letter, we must now take action to convince the \uc2 Walt Disney Company\uc2  to protect employees and their families. The group says Chapek did not do enough to speak out against the bill. Chapek at the time explaining --\par \par \uc2 BOB CHAPEK, \uc2 DISNEY\uc2  CEO: Now we were opposed to the bill from the outset but we chose not to take a public position on it because we thought we could be more effective working behind the scenes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): In an employee town hall Monday, the company promised a listening tour, a task force, and efforts to oppose similar legislation in other states. The company tells NBC News in a statement, we know how important this issue is for our LGBTQ plus employees. And we pledge our ongoing support of the LGBTQ plus community in the fight for equal rights. Employee Nicolas Maldonado plans to join his \uc2 Disney\uc2  coworkers in the plan walkout.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS MALDONADO, \uc2 DISNEY\uc2  EMPLOYEE: Once this bill signed, it`s basically forcing people that are LGBT into back into the closet.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): The frustration is extending beyond theme park gates including to \uc2 ESPN\uc2 , where two broadcasters held a two-minute moment of silence on air during the NCAA Women`s Basketball Tournament over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 CAROLYN PECK, ESPN BROADCASTER: We`re going to take a pause from our broadcast to show our love and support for our friends, our families, and our colleagues.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:35:00]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Now guys ask for the timing of this bill, it`s already passed through the state legislature here in Florida. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has not signed the bill into law yet. But he has publicly expressed support for it. If he does sign it, it would go into effect July 1st. Guys?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Blayne Alexander, for us there. Blayne thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Coming up next, calling all car shoppers, if you are in the market, buckle up.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes. The struggle is real. Prices for new and used cars are now at an all-time high. So what`s the best advice if you really need a new ride right now? Vicky Nguyen standing by with some money saving answers right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220326ei3m0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 How Some Companies Are Getting Creative To Keep Their Employees Happy\b0\par\par\uc2 Sheinelle Jones, Craig Melvin, Vicky Nguyen \par \uc2 1,263 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES: We are back, as Carson joins us. Turning to the record number of employees leaving their jobs and it`s leaving employers to get creative to keep their workers.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN: Yes. In some cases, really creative. NBC`s Senior Consumer Investigative Correspondent Vicky Nguyen is here with a look at what some companies are doing to try and stop that revolving door.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`ve been talking a lot about the great resignation.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: It was a fascinating conversation. Really eye-opening. The great resignation has given workers so much more power in the job market. They`re demanding more money, more time off and more incentives. And if they don`t get what they`re asking for, many quitting. They`re finding jobs that will meet their demands. This morning, we have new signs that employers are listening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): The pandemic normalized working from home for millions. And now, workers are tilting the scales in their favor. More life, less work. Prompting some companies to find incentives that can convert the great resignation into the great retention.\par \par \uc2 VICKI SALEMI, CAREER EXPERT AT MONSTER.COM: If employers don`t do what they need to do to retain employees and keep them happily working there, they`re going to constantly have openings to fill.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): The driving force behind career choices, a recent report found 50 percent of employees are focused on compensation, 42 percent on meaningful work.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (on camera): From the data you`ve gathered, what are the things employers need to do to retain their top talent?\par \par \uc2 SALEMI: Number one, is pay them fairly. Number two, is think about compensation beyond the salary piece. And third, is health care benefits.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): Employers like \uc2 Ford Motor Company\uc2  have long offered job sharing. That`s when two employees with similar roles go part-time and split one full-time job. But in a company with 183,000 workers, it can be hard to find the right partner.\par \par \uc2 ENISO NGWENYA, PD BUSINESS OPERATIONS LEADER, GLOBAL OPERATIONS & VEHICLE PROGRAMS, \uc2 FORD MOTOR COMPANY\uc2 : What if it was like a dating app? We should be able to go on an app and look for partners and put a profile in.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): Eniso Ngwenya and Ami Kapadia designed a new app to help \uc2 Ford\uc2  employees find the perfect match.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (on camera): How does it work, Ami?\par \par \uc2 AMI KAPADIA, PLANNING AND STRATEGY SUPERVISOR, \uc2 FORD MOTOR COMPANY\uc2 : You start out by entering your profile. How you want to grow with the company and what your skill set is. And all of that information gets put into the app and the database.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): Employees can now find job share partners worldwide. \uc2 Ford\uc2  says, the app has helped them retain workers and top talent by giving them flexibility to learn new skills or spend more time with family.\par \par \uc2 NGWENYA: Not everybody wants to completely walk away from their role or their career. And that was so important to me as well during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): In Tampa at marketing company PPK, they found something they`ve been doing since 2019, sustained them through the great resignation. Stay interviews. The opposite of exit interviews.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT GARCIA, VICE PRESSIDENT, PPK: There`s a level of trust that`s created by having these meaningful conversations with folks in a regular ongoing basis.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): PPK Vice President Garrett Garcia says, before the stay interviews, PPK retained employees at a rate of 75 to 80 percent. But now, they`re seeing a retention rate as high as 94 percent. At Boston-based Acceleration Partners, a worldwide marketing firm, employees get money for wellness. For things like, a gym membership or a massage. But get this, company founder and chairman Robert Glazer says, if an employee takes time off and truly unplugs, they can convert that into cash for their vacation.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (on camera): Wait a minute. So, when people go on vacation, you pay them not to check their e-mails?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GLAZER, FOUNDER AND CHAIRMAN OF THE BOARD, ACCELERATION PARTNERS: If they come back and then a manager signs off that they have not checked their e-mail, not, you know, been working, then they can use part of their wellness reimbursement towards their vacation.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voiceover): And now, to prevent burnout, the company shuts down for a full week twice a year, so no one can work.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: I think the programs that we have, have certainly been a huge part of our retention. We hired over 100 new employees in the last year. And, you know, we hear a lot of them, that the culture and the programs that we have in place were unique and something that attracted them to our company.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (on camera): And a new buzzword we heard talking about CEOs is work-life integration instead of work-life balance. It`s the idea that employees are happiest and most productive when what they do for work aligns with their values and how they live. And I think a lot of people at that moment --\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s a good idea.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC`S TODAY WEATHER ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: -- over the last two years.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I mean, what if your company doesn`t have any of the programs, like the ones that you mentioned there, what can you do without saying, I`m done, and leaving your job?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: It can be tough, Craig, but talk to your boss. I know people don`t always want to do that. But you`ve got to ask, is there an opportunity for more money or a promotion? If your employer is on the board for that raise, you want it in writing. And also, it`s not always about money. So, consider other options like, maybe you want a more flexible schedule or you want to work remotely or maybe help with childcare.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Even just additional training you could ask to up your skillset, that`s going to benefit you on the long run and your company.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS HOST: Maybe you just want to work on Tuesdays.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Well, there you go. You can --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Wow. So, you`re just asking for a friend?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: It`s Tuesday "POP Start" only.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Tuesday "POP Start".\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Taking lots of notes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Vicky, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Put it in writing.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you, Vick.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Mr. Roker --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Thanks, Vick.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- the weather?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: It`s going to be trouble when "The Voice" is only like one day a week.\par \par \uc2 DALY: We`re headed that direction.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: NBC`s got a lot of hosts to fill there.\par \par \uc2 OK. Let`s show you what we`ve got as far as today is concerned. Some severe storm is firing up through the Gulf into the Mississippi River Valley. Plenty of sunshine out West. Warming up and sunny in the Northeast, Great Lakes into the Mid-Atlantic States.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220324ei3i0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Conversation With Dr. Jenny Yip\b0\par\par\uc2 Sheinelle Jones, Jill Martin, Al Roker \par \uc2 5,229 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 January 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:45:00] SHEINELLE JONES: I like this. This morning, we`re going to share some easy ways to boost your mood. JILL MARTIN: If you`ve fallen into some healthy mindless habits we all have that are leaving you more cranky and irritable, our next guest can help.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER: That`s right. Dr. Jenny Yip is a board-certified psychologist and the executive director of the Renewed Freedom Center.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Yip, good to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNY YIP, RENEWED FREEDOM CENTER EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR BOARD-CERTIFIED PSYCHOLOGIST: Good morning, everyone.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: OK. So, you know, we`re coming -- we`re still in this pandemic. And so, you`re talking anxiety that`s coupled with crankiness, irritability. Is that pretty much how in general we`re all feeling, whether you`re young or old, men or women?\par \par \uc2 DR. YIP: I mean, unfortunately, most of us are still stuck in this, you know, holding place after almost two years and it is creating a lot of anxiety, depression, irritability. And unfortunately, many of us are trying to distract ourselves by engaging in these mindless, unhealthy habits. So - - you know --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. Sorry about that. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 DR. YIP: No, no. I mean, I think what we have to do is recognize when we are engaging in those unhealthy habits and try to create new ones so that we are able to change our mindset.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I thought it was interesting. I was prepping for you and you talk about how unhealthy habits make you cranky. And so, if we dig in specially, working from home, for example, I think at first people thought, oh, this is great, I don`t have to leave the house. But what`s happening for a lot of people is, then their work hours are extended and then that work/life balance kind of falls apart.\par \par \uc2 [09:50:00]\par \par \uc2 DR. YIP: Right. I mean, I think a lot of us thought, OK, we no longer have to commute and we no longer have to get ready for work. And the problem is, then you`re actually taking even more time just for work and you`re not creating that healthy work-life balance.\par \par \uc2 So, back in the days when we were commuting to work, we had time for ourselves, you know, whether we`re on the train or driving in our cars, we had time to decompress, to, you know, get our mindsets ready. However, now, many of us are just rolling out of bed and getting right to our desk and on the computer.\par \par \uc2 And then, the unfortunate thing is that many of us are spending a lot of time just at our desk. So, some of the ways to break that mindless habit is to create that structure, right? So, rather than going to get your lunch and then eating it at your desk, perhaps, eating in a way, eating it outside, in your dining room. Rather than just waking up, rolling out of bed and getting at your desk, going out for a walk before you even start your day. Making sure that you`re taking regular breaks here and there. So, it`s really about creating a more realistic structure to still have that work-life balance.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Boy, I hope one of your senior producers, Cecilia Fan, is listening to this because she needs to really get out more.\par \par \uc2 So, Dr. Yip, social media can be, you know, great, inspiring, it brings folks together. But too much of like anything is a bad thing.\par \par \uc2 DR. YIP: Yes. Unfortunately, many of us are bored. We don`t have the distractors that we used to have and a lot of events have been canceled repeatedly. So, we distract ourselves from those uncomfortable situations or emotions by, you know, scrolling in social media. And unfortunately, that`s not really healthy because you`re avoiding the difficult emotions. And anything that you`re going to avoid will only persist.\par \par \uc2 So, rather than avoiding those difficult emotions, replace them with healthier ones. So, if you`re distressed, if you`re anxious, if you`re overwhelmed, focus on hobbies, tasks that are actually enjoyable. I know that`s a cliche to say right now. However, it really does work. And you just have to make a habit out of it.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: You know, we were talking about this. I feel like right now, we want to be able to control anything we can in this chaos. And so, a big thing that -- I`m a big believer in -- is getting rid of physical and mental clutter. How important is that, even just the physical of it, like going through each drawer and keeping things that aren`t a 10?\par \par \uc2 DR. YIP: You know, once upon a time we didn`t have unlimited access and, you know, availability of photos and e-mails. And, unfortunately, because we have gone into this digital age, you know, we just have so much stuff that we`re collecting, whether they are photos or videos. I mean, I want to ask, how many of you even know what kind of photos or videos you have on your phone.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 DR. YIP: And when space runs out, you probably are purchasing extra space on the Cloud, right? The problem with all of that collection is that it creates distractions. And it keeps you from being able to focus on the things that really matter and it decreases your productivity.\par \par \uc2 So, rather than mindless collecting videos, photos, e-mails, text messages, you know, really dedicate some time during the week, at least once a week, to really go through mindfully and intentionally all of those things that are unnecessary, all of the selfies that you`ve taken, all of the videos you`ve taken of your kids and just delete them because --\par \par \uc2 JONES: You`re right. Just keep the ones you really want. You`re right.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You`re absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Hey, Dr. Yip, I mean, this is all things that are happening to people. But when do you need to maybe seek help, you know, if this isn`t going away?\par \par \uc2 DR. YIP: Obviously -- absolutely. Obviously, if you`re practicing all of these intentional, mindful, healthy habits and you`re still feeling quite overwhelmed with anxiety, depression, you know, then maybe it`s time to seek professional help.\par \par \uc2 So, you can always go to NAMI, you can always go the \uc2 Anxiety and Depression Association of America\uc2 . That`s adaa.org. If you`re suffering from a lot of fears about contamination or illness anxiety, perhaps going on the International OCD Foundation`s website as well.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You know what, I`m glad we talked about this because hopefully we touched somebody today, helped somebody else out.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jenny Yip, thank you for your time as always.\par \par \uc2 DR. YIP: I hope this was helpful. Thank you, everyone.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Absolutely was.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Absolutely. Have a good day. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:55:00]\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, before we go, if you`ve ever wanted to ask us something, now is your chance. We want your questions for our new segment "Q&A TODAY." What do we do during commercial breaks? What time do we wake up? What`s our favorite movie? Literally, you can ask us anything. We may not answer everything. But tweet us @TODAYshow and we will try to answer your questions on the air.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That should be fun. All right. Tomorrow on the "3rd Hour of TODAY," Jim Belushi is live.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Cool.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And coming up on "Hoda & Jenna," the breakout star of the new comedy, "Somebody Somewhere," Bridget Everett.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And you`re going to be back on the "4th Hour," right?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes. I`m going to be back with more of my it list.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, that`s right. You are busy.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I love it. With the Property Brothers?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: With the Property Brothers.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And your streaming show?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes. At 11:30, tune in, today.com. Have a great day.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Have a good day.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [10:00:18]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voice over): Today, remembering comedian Bob Saget. My conversation with his widow, Kelly Rizzo, as she reflects on the love and the laughter.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the breakout star of "Somebody Somewhere" the very funny Bridget Everett.\par \par \uc2 And from "West Side Story" to "The Fallout", the talented Maddie Ziegler.\par \par \uc2 JENNA BUSH HAGER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voice over): From Rockefeller Plaza in New York City, it`s TODAY with Hoda and Jenna, it all starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAGER (on camera): Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Wait a minute, isn`t it Thursday?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s Thursday, the weekend is knocking on the door.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: -- 20th, welcome in. It`s a snowy day in New York -- just started snowing. How about that? How about that?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I like it. And we are listening to Somebody Loves You by Betty Who.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow. So, we`re so happy that you`re with us. It`s so funny when it comes to kids and keeping secrets, OK?\par \par \uc2 Now, when you were a kid, we`re going to talk about this because there`s -- Jenna Dewan is that she`s got a daughter who`s 8 years old, and her daughter can keep a secret, can`t she?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Her daughter can keep a secret in a huge way.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: