ome social dilemmas?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We love social dilemmas. OK. So, let`s start with the first one. OK, first, let`s give it a little second. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 Every time my sister in law visits, she buys what I have, everything from placemats to purses. Should I say something or just be flattered?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I say be flattered. I couldn`t care less if someone bought what I had. But I --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: In fact, I think that`s my shirt.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It is. No, I`m sorry. It was.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, yes. I know, it looks better on you. But I agree, I don`t really care about stuff like that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I mean, if it were chronic, and every time you went, and you notice like there are your placemats, there`s your this, there`s your that. I mean, I can get where it would be annoying. And maybe, you know, but I guess it is flattery. I mean, that`s what it is. So, what if someone --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I think, yes, I agree. I think that just means she thinks you`re cool.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But I think the issue for some of us that were born in the 80s and 90s. Is that movie "Single White Female"?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Like, it can`t get to on that edge. Because then you start to think like --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What are you doing? It`s like, you got the same jeans I have. I don`t -- yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Just like me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Did anyone ever do that?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: No.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, me either.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Of course not.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Of course, they wouldn`t.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: All right, the next one. My boyfriend is a crazy procrastinator when it comes to plans. He doesn`t plan anything. It`s annoying and we aren`t doing anything fun. What do I do?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You plan.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You plan out?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: If he goes along, then