come to this "3rd Hour of TODAY." Craig Melvin, Sheinelle Jones, Jill Martin.\par \par \uc2 Have you noticed something different about our friend?\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY, CO-HOST: What`s different?\par \par \uc2 JILL MARTIN, LIFESTYLE AND COMMERCE CONTRIBUTOR: I do. I think that Sheinelle when -- I think when you travel, you get inspired to try new things. I think it`s when your mind wanders. And look at her -- go like this.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: It`s a braided bun.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s a braided bun.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: It`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Honestly, I was watching (INAUDIBLE) and she had like a cute little look and I just copied it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Well, now, you have to do it every day. We like your braided bun.\par \par \uc2 JONES: When I do the "3rd Hour," sometime we get to play.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: How are you guys today?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: With everything going on, it`s nice to have fun.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: This is -- and honestly, this table is a place where -- it brings me joy. Just talking to you guys and talking to you guys, I feel like you guys are a family.\par \par \uc2 In fact, we have a lot to talk about on this Tuesday morning. And we get started. This one is not such a great one for a lot of people. This is a warming from U.S. Airlines about a "catastrophic" disruption. Here is the deal. This has nothing to do with weather or even COVID.\par \par \uc2 This is what everybody is bugging about this morning, they are worried about new 5G cellphone networks. Let`s go NBC`s Tom Costello. He starts the conversation for us.\par \par \uc2 So, Tom, those new faster 5G cell systems are set to go live tomorrow. But the, you know, aviation industry is urging that all 5G sites within two miles of the airport remain off for now. What is the concern?\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: They are very concerned. And by the way, it`s the \uc2 FAA\uc2 , it`s the airlines, it`s aircraft manufacturers, it`s pilots, all of them concerned about potential interference that may be caused by those 5G ground sites against their in cockpit critical technology, specifically altimeters.\par \par \uc2 Now, they rely on radio altimeters to land in bad weather. If you`re coming into an airport and the visibility is awful and you don`t have a good sense of where is the runway, not only are you relying on autopilot and all of your GPS functions but also on the altimeter to give you an accurate idea of what`s your altitude is.\par \par \uc2 The concern is that the 5G ground sites could interfere with that altimeter and that could make it very, very difficult to get a good accurate reading. So, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  has already said, if those sites are going to be flipped on at midnight, then the \uc2 FAA\uc2  says, you cannot use an altimeter to come into more than 80 airports nationwide starting tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 If it`s good weather, no problem. If it`s bad weather, flights maybe cancelled, diverted, et cetera. Here`s what the airline CEOs are warning about because the warning is kind of stark. They`re saying that without immediate intervention -- is what they say here, without immediate intervention that is needed, they say, to avoid significant operational disruption to air passengers, shippers, the supply chain and delivery of needed medical supplies.\par \par \uc2 They go on to say -- or at least the 5G networks say, the cellphone companies say, 5G networks, they say, operate safely already without interference in nearly 40 countries around the world and it will be no different here. All right. Now, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  weighing in this morning because the \uc2 FAA\uc2 , of course, is worried about keeping everybody safe in the skies, they say, they`re working with aviation industry and the wireless companies to try to limit 5G related flight delays and cancellations.\par \par \uc2 You know, you may be asking, well, wait a minute. If it works in Europe, 5G is active in Europe, no problems with aviation, why do we potentially have a problem here? Well, the aviation industry says, these are -- it`s really not apples to apples. There is a variation, a difference in the frequency that`s used and the strength of the signal and also in the way that the antennae are essentially configured. And so, they don`t want to take any chance.\par \par \uc2 And so, they don`t want to take any chances. And as a result, the airline CEOs are saying, please, don`t turn this on within two miles of an airport. If you do, we`re going to be forced to start canceling flights in bad weather.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s not what we want to hear either. So, clearly, they`re going to have to figure that out.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you, Tom.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you, Tom.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Tom also taught us the plural for antenna.\par \par \uc2 JONES: What was it again?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Antennae.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Is that true?\par \par \uc2 JONES: You learn one every day.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: If Tom Costello says it --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, Tom says it --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: -- it is true.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Antennae.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re going to hop on a flight to the City of Lights where we are getting a glimpse at the painstaking work underway to restore one of the most breathtaking structures on our planet. It has been three years now since fire tore through the Paris`s Notre Dame Cathedral.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Today, Senior International Correspondent Keir Simmons is live in Paris.\par \par \uc2 Because, Kier, you got quite the look at the rebuild. Walk us through these exclusive new images of the repair work there.\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Craig.\par \par \uc2 Yes. And, you know, it`s changed so much already since you were here, my friend, reporting on that terrible fire on that terrible night. And they -- three years ago. Look at it behind me here. You can see just the size of the scaffolding. The back of the church there that you`re looking at, we would able to see the spire right there. Of course, we can`t. But what we have now are these amazing images from inside.\par \par \uc2 Now, the organization that is doing this reconstruction -- resurrection, you could call it, is called the Public Establishment of the Restoration of Notre Dame. They have been working with National Geographic. And in that February issue of National Geographic on natgeo.com, you can see these pictures. And they really are stunning.\par \par \uc2 There you can see the inside of the Cathedral filmed with a drone. So, you could imagine that that steeple dropped down into the inside causing huge damage. They`re having to restore that. And then, on the outside, the photographer I spoke -- and I don`t know if we get to see this picture, actually put a camera right above the church there, right above the cathedral so he could look down into that gaping hole that was caused by the fall of that spire and it really is impactful.\par \par \uc2 You know, the wood just turned to ash is extraordinary. The amount of damage, guys, is so extensive there`s an image there, the amount of damage is so extensive that only now have they finished cleaning up and making it safe for this restoration. They hope though to have it done by 2024 in time for the Paris Olympics.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Incredible progress. And you were there to report on this.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Can you believe it has been three years?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I can`t believe the speed in which they have moved to revive that famous Cathedral. It really is quite remarkable because folks on the ground there, and Kier mentioned this earlier, folks who were watching it burn, they thought it was going to burn to the ground.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And so, incredible. And just switching gears a little bit. In your neck of the woods, you ran into somebody there as you were covering this story. I saw on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Yes. You know, Al Roker, he`s like omnipresent. He gets everywhere.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s so funny.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: We`re here in Paris and then, there`s Al. It is not so much of a coincidence. As you guys know, his daughter studies here. But it was just - - actually, it was lovely. I haven`t seen Al for years because of coronavirus. So, it was great to see him. We took a picture. You can see, Joe, the cameraman and Chuck, our producer there in the shot.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, Chuck Mendel (ph).\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Us all together. There he is.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Hey, Keir. Was Roker wearing --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Is he wearing a beret?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Was he wearing a beret?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: He`s wearing a beret. You know, I think he`s on a flight back there right now. So, he`s watching -- not watching this. So, we can say anything we like about him. That beret, I don`t know, Al, you know.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Here`s the thing, we will talk about this again. We`re going to pull pictures. We`re going to give him a little mustache with the little curly thing. Oh, we`re going to have fun with this.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wearing a beret. What a poser.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Al, did you just call him -- I mean, I just called you Al.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: I`m really into the beret just for the record.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You like it?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wait until he gets back. Thank you, Kier.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Thanks, Kier.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Thank you, Kier.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thanks for the report and the fodder.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: OK. You bet.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. So, we wanted to prepare you for the week ahead with some headlines that certainly grabbed our attention this morning. So, get your calendars out, everybody.\par \par \uc2 So, here is the deal. Today, we are going to get a closer look at a massive asteroid, that`s one. Tomorrow, we can all order those free COVID tests from the government. And next week, she`s back. Dylan returns to Studio 1A with all of her 15 kids.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I know --\par \par \uc2 JONES: I know. I`ve always wanted to do that. She has three kids now too.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: OK. So, first up, there`s no reason to panic. But \uc2 NASA\uc2  says an asteroid twice the size of the \uc2 Empire State Building\uc2  is going to buzz pass the earth at 43,000 miles per hour today.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: This particular asteroid hasn`t been this close in nearly 90 years. But it`s all relative. It`s still about five times further than the moon. If you have a telescope, you might actually be able to catch a glimpse of it. But there`s also something called the Virtual Telescope Project that will live stream the asteroid`s approach. So, that`s pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 JONES: When you see it like that, it looks scarier I think than it is than you see.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, that`s happening today. So, tomorrow, we`ll all be able to put in an order for a free COVID test. Several COVID tests depending on how many folks in your household. There`s a new government website. It`s up and running.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Covidtests.gov. Each household will be able to order four at-home COVID tests. They are free, including shipping. So, you know, we know how hard it has been for most folks to get their hands on a test since the number of cases has skyrocketed. So, hopefully, tomorrow that helps.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s a very big deal. I have friends in Illinois who have been looking for tests and they can`t even find rapid tests. So, that`s a good one.\par \par \uc2 All right. Now, to a date, next week, that`s a big one. Our friend, Dylan, is back from maternity leave on Monday. Does it feel like four months?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Has it been four months?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She posted about her return on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  along with this beautiful picture with her three beautiful boys. We have a lot of catching up to do and we can`t wait to see her again. Aren`t they just cute? Dylan was supposed to be -- her plan was to do the Halloween show and then, go on maternity leave. And then, obviously, Rusty had plans six weeks earlier.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Rusty was like, I`m coming out.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I`m ready now.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She`ll be back on Monday.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, we`ll see her back next week. We will see you back in just a few moments. And when we do come back, it is a job seeker`s market. Some jobs are really, really hot. So, we`re going to share a first look at LinkedIn`s brand-new list of jobs on the rise.\par \par \uc2 And then, a little bit later here on the "3rd Hour," refugees forced to leave their home country, finding support here in the United States from some people who know exactly what they are going through. The "3rd Hour of TODAY" right after this look this. Look at John showing off.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Fancy.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: This morning, we are getting out the crystal ball to get a glimpse into the future of work.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And the good news is, it is a job seeker`s market. And if you`re one of them, listen up, certain careers are booming right now.\par \par \uc2 JONES: \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  is out today with their list of jobs on the rise, a look at which jobs are growing the fastest right now. And here with an exclusive first look is LinkedIn`s editor-chief, Dan Roth. He`s back in this fabulous attic.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL ROTH, LINKEDIN EDITOR-IN-CHIEF: I`m back.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Dan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROTH: Good morning. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You too. So, listen, before we get to which jobs are on the rise, really quickly, can you tell us how you put this list together?\par \par \uc2 ROTH: Yes. We look at the fastest growing job titles over the last two and a half years. And the idea is whether you are starting your career or whether have been working for decades and you`re looking for a new role, these are some of the jobs where companies are investing right now. This is an especially important set of data at a time when people are rethinking how they work because of the pandemic and they`re rethinking where they want to work and what they expect from employers. There`s a lot of good information here if you are reshuffling your career.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And speaking of the pandemic, Dan, you uncovered some trends, perhaps unsurprisingly. Healthcare workers needed more than ever, especially a lot of healthcare workers are distressed, they`re leaving the profession altogether. What does your list say about this field in particular?\par \par \uc2 ROTH: Yes. This is a field that is under particular stress right now. I mean, they have been on the front line for years. But there`s a lot of innovation going on. The number one fastest growing job in America is vaccine specialist. This is something that didn`t exist on our list years ago. This was barely a job. It is now the hottest job title in America.\par \par \uc2 And you are seeing people move into this role from other roles. So, pharmacists, medical sale specialists and nurses in particular are taking this new job and they`re making a whole new career out of it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Forgive my ignorance. What is a vaccine specialist?\par \par \uc2 ROTH: It`s basically anyone who is involved with selling or distributing or thinking about the marketing and development of vaccines, but not someone who is actually making the vaccines. So, everything else vaccine related.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: You would expect that to be the top job right now.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And next up on the list, you say workplace culture and human resources positions. So, what kinds of jobs are those? And are those people -- I feel like a lot of people are struggling to find a job right now. What kind of experience do you need for that?\par \par \uc2 ROTH: Yes. These are jobs that help employers keep employees happy and help find employees. The dynamic for being able to find and keep employees has totally changed. People are expecting new ways to work, they are expecting new benefits coming from their jobs, they`re expecting to be able work remotely. And if they don`t like what they are seeing, they are leaving.\par \par \uc2 One of the -- the number two job on the rise now is diversity and equity and inclusion manager. This was number five on our list before. It keeps moving up. And it keeps moving up because employees are demanding a fair and equitable workplace. You are seeing talent acquisition specialists. These are people who help find new employees. Thinking number one job -- or top job. And then, chief human resource officer.\par \par \uc2 So, you mention that it`s hard to find jobs. It is even harder for employers to find people. So, it`s kind of hard on both sides. But employers right now are like, we`ve never seen anything like this. We need people who are experts at helping us find, retain and keep people.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That makes sense. We have about one minute left, but I want to squeeze in both of these. First of all, really good news for people early in their careers, there are jobs, if you have limited or a little experience for you.\par \par \uc2 ROTH: Yes. A lot of these jobs, business development specialists. There are other jobs that are a user experience researcher. There are jobs where if you have less than four experience, you can go into this. And I`ve said this on here before, but you have to think about your skills that you have. People -- you could have skills that can get you into these jobs. You don`t have to have spent decades being a business development professional, you can actually move into this job with other skills and you don`t have to have years and years of experience. This is especially good for Gen Zers who are jumping jobs in a way that we have never seen before with other generations.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Busy time.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. Well, we are out of time, but the last one we`ll just say, we`ll put more of this on our website and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . Working remotely. So, if you`re interested in working remotely, there are jobs were youo can still work from home.\par \par \uc2 Dan Roth, it was good to see you this morning. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Just ahead, a family who left their lives behind in search of hope here in the United States. How they found instant support from total strangers.\par \par \uc2 And then, a little bit later, best-selling author, Harlan Coben is going to be with us along with actor Richard Armitage. They`re going to be live to tell us about their thrilling new series, "Stay Close." And you should stay close as well because the "3rd Hour of TODAY" back in just 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:20:00]\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: we`re back with our series, "The Upside," shining a light on a special group of volunteers helping Afghan refugees adjust to life in the U.S. NBC News Senior Consumer Investigative Correspondent Vicky Nguyen is here.\par \par \uc2 And, Vicky, I know this is a really personal story for you.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN, NBC NEWS INVESTIGATIVE AND CONSUMER CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good morning, Jill. And good morning to you at home.\par \par \uc2 Yes. Many of you may not know, but my parents and I, we are refugees from Vietnam. So, we know firsthand what it`s like to come to a new country, to begin again with really nothing except for help and kindness of strangers. Well, it turns out there`s a group of volunteers doing just that for Afghan refugees. Here`s their story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (voiceover): Uyen Nguyen and Alley Nguyen are paying a visit to Afghan refugees, Sahib Mashwani (ph) and his family of eight. They`re delivering donated food. Sahib (ph), his wife and their six children have only been in Seattle for a few days. Finally, settling down after spending their first few months on a United States military base in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The family shares tea and their deep thanks with Uyen, Alley and Tam, volunteers with the group Viets for Afghans.\par \par \uc2 When Uyen saw this harrowing imagery of the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan after 20 years of war, it triggered her own memories of her family`s escape from Vietnam as boat people, a journey that took the lives of four family members, including her mother and two siblings.\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (on camera): How did that experience at 10 years old shape who you are now?\par \par \uc2 UYEN NGUYEN, VIETS FOR AFGHANS MEMBER: I would say that it made me realize that how someone`s life can just turn upside down really, really quickly. I have a lot of empathy for one`s life situation when it can change that fast.\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (voiceover): Flashing back to her struggles and the kindness of strangers who helped to resettle in the United States nearly 40 years ago, Uyen didn`t hesitate.\par \par \uc2 U. NGUYEN: I just pick up my phone and I texted my four other friends and just tell them that, I think we need to do something.\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (on camera): So, you just picked up the phone and said, who is in?\par \par \uc2 U. NGUYEN: Exactly. And so far, we have sponsored two of those family here to Seattle and we`re planning to do more.\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (voiceover): Viets for Afghans is about 15 volunteer strong. They use Sponsor Circles Program, a community initiative requiring at least five adults to agree to assist with housing, schooling and jobs for refugees.\par \par \uc2 SAHIB MASHWANI, AFGHAN REFUGEE: We never thought of like kind of Vietnamese family or immigrants in general being in positions where they can help other in the (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (voiceover): Alley is one of Sahib`s (ph) sponsors. She met the family, picking them up at the airport, had furniture, even beds and toys for kids donated.\par \par \uc2 ALLEY NGUYEN, VIETS FOR AFGHANS MEMBER: You see the evacuation from Afghanistan. And just as a human, you can`t help but want to help.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He`s says he`s really thankful.\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (voiceover): I can relate. In 1980, my family and I ended up in Eugene, Oregon as refugees from Vietnam. Sponsors helped us transition to our new life. It is a kindness you can never repay, kindness like when Sahib (ph) learned he and his family will live in this apartment, Tam Nguyen when offering it rent free.\par \par \uc2 TAM NGUYEN, VIETS FOR AFGHANS MEMBER: Let me show you where your house is going to be.\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (voiceover): Tam, also refugee from Vietnam arrived in Seattle in 1980. Today, he`s a pharmacist, developer, restaurateur, and now, a volunteer for Viets for Afghans.\par \par \uc2 T. NGUYEN: This is my second chance. And my chance is to help another family is a privilege.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:00]\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (voiceover): There are currently 18,000 Afghan refugees on military bases across the U.S. waiting to find permanent homes. Sahib (ph) says he worked as a special forces` contractor backed by the United States and feels many more Afghans should be given refuge here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There are a lot of people in Afghanistan that sacrifice so much for the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . They are being killed. So, just calling up on anybody that can help to get them evacuated.\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (voiceover): Refugees helping refugees, paying their debt of kindness forward to a new generation of Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN (on camera): And Viets for Afghans, this is great news, they`ve actually raised enough money to help resettle 75 Afghan refugees. Including last Friday, they brought in two more families. They really credit the greater Afghan community for helping with things like cultural and language barriers, but it really is incredible.\par \par \uc2 One thing I should note, you may have noticed that we blurred a few of the faces in that story. Sahib (ph) asked us not to show his older daughters and his wife because he still fears retaliation from the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . So, this is very serious for a lot of the families here as well.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I mean, talk about paying it forward. Hey, Vick, for folks who are watching or are listening on Sirius XM and may want to help, what can they do?\par \par \uc2 V. NGUYEN: Just get started, Craig, that`s what the volunteers tell us. No act is too small. You can go to the Sponsor Circles` website and check out the details there. Uyen says something as simple as if you got an extra hour or two, Viets for Afghans could use someone to help them with thank you cards to the incredible donors that are making this all possible. There are also other grass roots organizations, welcome.us is another one. So, many, many different ways to contribute whether it`s time or money. Craig.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s so good.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Vicky, thank you so much. I mean, just -- she`s incredibly sharing --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. Personalized it.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: I mean, just really beautiful.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, two guys who know how to keep us in suspense. Bestselling author, Harlan Coben and actor, Richard Armitage are live -- good morning, guys -- to tell us about the new hit \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series, "Stay Close."\par \par \uc2 And then, later, we`re tuning up our skin with some of the best products to add to our routines. We`ll have that for you coming up a little later in the show.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, I use that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: It`s funny, I use that too. And the pimple medicines --\par \par \uc2 JONES: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:30:00]\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Our next guests have already teamed up once. Now, they are back with another megahit. Harlan Coben is the bestselling author of over 30 novels, six of which became \uc2 Netflix\uc2  series.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Richard Armitage has graced the screen and movies like "The Hobbit" and the series, "The Stranger," based on Harlan`s book. And Harlan`s latest \uc2 Netflix\uc2  adaptation is called "Stay Close." It`s all about the secrets people keep, including Richard`s character, Ray, a paparazzi whose past comes back to haunt him?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ARMITAGE, ACTOR: Guy, it`s Ray. Listen, I`ve just seen Simone (ph) and she`s looking for you, mate. So, give her a call, yes? Or give me a call, just get in touch. Look, I know it got a bit messy the other night. If I just -- just call me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good morning to both of you.\par \par \uc2 HARLAN COBEN, "STAY CLOSE" WRITER: Good morning, guys.\par \par \uc2 ARMITAGE: Hey, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s great to see you this morning.\par \par \uc2 All right, Richard, let`s start with you. Ray is this dark and brutty (ph) guy covered in tattoos. He`s got a lot of secrets, some that will be catching up with him throughout the series. What did it take, first of all, to transform into this character?\par \par \uc2 ARMITAGE: It takes a lot of work actually from the makeup team and the wardrobe department and the art department who create the environment and the look for Ray. But it was really interesting to create the look for the character that was very different from the one that I played at "The Stranger." And it was interesting playing a character that has a lack of memory because we were able to sort of use his environment to create, you know, the visual imagery of his world.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Harlan, I saw you watching that video when it came up and I couldn`t help -- do you marvel every time you see what you`ve done on the screen? It feels like you`re like, I can`t believe that still after all the time?\par \par \uc2 COBEN: I really can. It`s been such fun watching people`s reaction to "Stay Close." And especially the last twist, how many people are really kind of getting into it. If you don`t get excited about that kind of thing and working with someone like Richard and a great cast and crew that we had on "Stay Close," you`re in the wrong business.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I still get jazzed every time I see a clip.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes, I bet. And let`s get into it because I know you like controversy and you did when you created these characters. But tell us about Barbie and Ken, because there is an uproar on social media.\par \par \uc2 COBEN: Yes, they are interesting killers, I`ll say. Do you guys remember "Up with People" that used to be on the halftime of football games way back when?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Vaguely.\par \par \uc2 COBEN: I kind of got the idea, what if two of them were psychotic hitmen?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, as one would.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: The way your mind thinks, how do you --\par \par \uc2 COBEN: I get paid to do this, by the way. Just so you know. And so, they`ve been causing a lot of controversy and I kind of love it. Some people love them, some people hate them. But everybody staying on the show and watching, that I guess is they key.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, last time, Harlan was here, "Stay Close" was about to premier. And now, it`s on Netflix`s top 10 list around the World. Richard, tell us what it`s like bringing Harlan`s characters to life and then, collaborating with him for a second time?\par \par \uc2 ARMITAGE: I`ve always been a massive reader. And Harlan`s work is just lending itself to screen adaptation because he has that ability to just create a hook, that means you cannot stop reading and you cannot stop watching. And he did the same again with "Stay Close" that he did with "The Stranger." And I just -- I love the characters he creates. It`s just instantly accessible and just fun to play.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:00]\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And you also, this past new year, you are in Utah and the trip actually has something to do with one of the themes in the novel. Are you living without a little too much?\par \par \uc2 ARMITAGE: I was just up a mountain skiing and sort of enjoying myself for Christmas. But no, I think you know, this story just kind of engages you and Utah was a place I went to retreat.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, there you go. You guys bought have beautiful smiles for the record. Don`t they have nice smiles?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes. It`s nice to have them here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I know. Harlan, we can`t let you go without asking what we should be reading this month. Any suggestions?\par \par \uc2 COBEN: I`m reading "Crossroads" by Jonathan Franzen right now, which is a long book, but it`s just -- it`s wonderful. It really is. So, that`s what I`m reading now. But I`ll be back and we`ll start picking more books in a few weeks, I hope.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Sound good to me. Harlan and Richard, thank you guys so much for joining us. "Stay Close" by the way is available to stream on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  as people all around the world are watching it as we speak right now. Thanks, guys. Have a great day.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 COBEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ARMITAGE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And up next, it is "Tune-Up Tuesday" for your skin with some of the best products you should be using from the second you wake up until you go to bed.\par \par \uc2 And then, later, Chef Laura Vitale is live with a recipe that is a big hit in her house using many raviolis. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I`ll take it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:40:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: This morning, we have a "Tune-Up Tuesday" for our skin with a look at the best and beauty.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Sarah Eggenberger, senior editor at large of "NewBeauty" has her hands on the holy grail of skin care and more. And of course, you know, we love a QR code. Scan your QR code on your screen to see all of her pick.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SARAH EGGENBERGER, NEWBEAUTY SENIOR EDITOR AT LARGE: Good morning. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: So, we`re talking the best of the best here. And we`re doing skincare, which I feel like this time of year we could all --\par \par \uc2 JONES: We need it.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: We need a little (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: So, let`s start with the facial cleanser. Actually, Craig said he uses this and I use this too. Is it pronounce CeraVe or CeraVe?\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: CeraVe. And it is an incredible line of cleansers as well as many products. But their cleansers are really the go to products here. It is created by their dermatologists. It is going to clean the skin without disrupting the acid mantle. So, it is great for everyone to use because you`re going to get that full cleaned feeling when you get rid of all that debris and impurities off your skin. But yet, you`re going to still feel healthy on the skin.\par \par \uc2 And you can see, everyone like this product as we talked about from Craig to you, Jill.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, it`s affordable.\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: It is universal. And it`s so affordable at $13.99. It`s a great price point. And it lasts a long time. And it`s a great cleanser to use, especially in these dry winter months.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I`m looking forward to next pick because I`m obsessed with serums lately. I think I`m using too many different kinds. So, which one do you guys like, especially for aging skin?\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: Yes. So, this contains retinal. And retinal is a key ingredient. It`s really the cornerstone of any anti-aging program. So, I highly recommend you have a retinal in your regiment. Shani Darden has an incredible retina because it is time released, it`s encapsulated. So, it allows you to get the most effective version of the retinal into your skin without the irritation.\par \par \uc2 This became so popular by celebrities. A lot of word of mouth. You can see a little bit goes a very long way. A pea size amount will cover your whole face, neck and (INAUDIBLE). Don`t forget your chest.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: And this is going to give you that cell turnover for your skin. Think of this like exercise for your skin, it`s going to build that skin up. It is the best ingredient for collagen production to help minimize those fine lines and wrinkles and then, also, for breakouts. It`s going to manage your breakouts as well.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: All right. We only have a couple of minutes. I want to get through everything. The best peel. And I know this is not an everyday thing.\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: Peels. Dr. Gross created these at-home pills. It`s a two-step process. It contains alpha and beta hydroxy acids. This is going to help to get rid of those dead skin cells to reveal a beautiful, bright, great texture to your skin. Very easy to use. Actually, it`s sold every three seconds. That`s how popular this is because it just gives you immediate results.\par \par \uc2 And check out the for review because that will sell you alone when you see what he customers are saying about this product.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. This next one grabbed my attention. Really?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Is this what you were talking about?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes, that`s --\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. So, a lot of times you may have breakouts. A lot of people may get mask me under their mask. We`re talking about the best product to deal with breakouts. This is Mario Badescu. You use this?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes, you touch a little bit in. You put a little bit on. It`s pink but it dries it right up, right?\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: Right. It kind of reminds you when you had chickenpox when you had a kid because it contains calamine.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: Sulfur, zinc. It is an incredible product. And it will banish and eradicate any blemishes. Like you can see here, you just take a Q-tip, dab it on that breakout. Don`t pick those breakouts. Use this instead. Leave it on overnight. You`ll wake up to clearer skin. It is a miracle worker for sure, and definitely one of our go to products for blemishes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And sunscreen, best?\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: At the pinnacle of holy grail products is La Roche-Posay`s sunscreen. This is their antioxidant and sunscreen together. So, you have broad spectrum SPF coverage. But then, you`re also going to get antioxidant protection. So, it`s great for bylines or wrinkles, for helping with discoloration. So, it`s a two in one. It`s water base. And so, you`re also going to have an invisible coverage with it. So, it`s a great everyday product.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Looks good.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: This next one I put on everything.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. So, here`s the thing, there are people who just want one multipurpose product. So, if you were -- talking about the best bang for your buck. You say Aquaphor.\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: Aquaphor. It is the winner in that category, right? Because it is going to help to get rid of, you know, any type of dryness, cracking from cuticles to hands, to your feet, to your face, to your lips. It covers everything from young little kids all the way up to grownups.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Sure. That`s what pediatricians recommend, yes.\par \par \uc2 [09:45:00]\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: Yes. It`s great for your lips, especially right now with kids going outside, they need it on their lips. I love that. It`s also wonderful for hands, put it every night to get moisturized hands when you wake up. It is definitely a perfect multipurpose product, and at an incredible price point.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And last up, the dry shampoo for those of us who don`t wash their hair every day.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, nice.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: I`m on third day hair. So --\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: Yes. Right. This is the lifeline as dry shampoo. Klorane is amazing. This product is four decades of research. It is going to give you zero residue and it helps to give you that lift to your hair as well. And it smells amazing. So, it`s going to help to reduce the neutralized odor. It`s going to help to clean the hair, and you get a great lift from it.\par \par \uc2 So, this product I use every day, even when I wash my hair because I just love the volume you get from it. And again, it really helps to extend both washes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Jill just sprayed it a little bit and I already see the --\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: All right. Well, these are great, Sarah.\par \par \uc2 EGGENBERGER: Yes. Isn`t that amazing? sYou dish it up a little up a little bit.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes. Just a little (INAUDIBLE). And to shop these products, head to today.com/shop.\par \par \uc2 JONES: These are a win. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Let`s eat now, shall we? Up next, Chef Laura Vitale is sharing her one of the kind, one of her go to recipes, bringing maximum flavor using many ravioli.\par \par \uc2 And then, tomorrow, Hollywood legend Goldie Hawn is working on something really important. And she`ll be live to tell us all about it. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:50:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: This morning on "Today Food," a warm hearty soup that`s perfect for a cold winter day. Here with one of her family`s favorites is cooking show host and internet star, Laura Vitale of YouTube`s "Laura in the Kitchen."\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 LAURA VITALE, CHEF AND HOST: Good morning. Good morning, ladies. How are you?\par \par \uc2 JONES: We`re doing great. Can I tell you a true story? So, this morning, when I prepping for your segment, I read over the recipe and I was like, you know what, this is doable. So, I took the notes from my desk this morning and put them in my bag so that I can make this today. So, here we go. So, there`s nothing better, obviously, enjoying soup.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about what you`re making today, because it`s more than a soup, it`s like a comfort dish.\par \par \uc2 VITALE: So, I love the idea of like a ravioli dinner or a lasagna dinner, which, you know, it`s a very rich long cooking process that`s just so, so satisfying, right? However, I find myself wanting those comfort dishes on a Tuesday evening where like time and patience is short, and I really want something that quick easy that my whole family is going to lot because I have a very picky four-year-old that just -- that hits the spot.\par \par \uc2 So, this mini ravioli soup really came -- the idea for it really came from a bag of mini ravioli that I got from Trader Joe`s. And I thought, oh, they would be fantastic in a really savory sausage infused tomato broth situation, and that`s kind of where the recipe came from. But it`s also incredibly versatile.\par \par \uc2 Like, today, I`m going to be using pork sausage that I -- all I did was -- in -- for this instance, I went ahead and just crumbled it with my carrots and my celery and I`m going to add my onions to it. You can also just slice in little coins. A good tip there is to put your sausage in the freezer for about 10 minutes before you slice it because it`s just a fatty cut of meat, so it`s hard to get a nice clean cut. But you can just crumble it.\par \par \uc2 If you don`t like sausage but your family preferred meatballs, add some frozen mini meatballs for this. No one is going to judge.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Right. OK.\par \par \uc2 VITALE: If you`re like my sister who, once in a while, she decides she`s a vegetarian --\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s us.\par \par \uc2 VITALE: -- add a can of chickpeas.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s what I was going to do. I was going to do -- yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: I was going to do impossible burger.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, yes. OK.\par \par \uc2 VITALE: Yes. Well, you could do the impossible burger crumbled up in here. That would be fantastic. Well, like I said, a can of chickpeas. And then, I do is I just saute that with your usual aromatic suspects that go in any soup or stew. You`ve got carrots, you`ve got onion, you`ve got celery. To it, I want to add a lot of garlic because again, when we`re talking about something that is quick cookie, easy to put together, you really want to hone in on really strong bold flavors. So, again, lots of garlic, lots of onions.\par \par \uc2 And then, it always makes everything taste like you -- you`ve spent so much more time on it than you really did.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Laura, what do you with the broth? Because we wanted to do chickpeas, that version, but I know she used beef broth. Do we still use the broth? You know what I mean, or what do we do?\par \par \uc2 VITALE: You can use vegetable broth. You can use vegetable broth. If you`re not a vegetarian, you can also use chicken broth if you are going to use chickpeas. But really, I really love getting -- whenever -- every time I come to the city, I will make a quick stop at Italy because they have the most fantastic Italian vegetable broth cubes, you know. So, they`re like bouillon cubes, but they`re vegetarian. And they are so packed with flavor and low sodium down. I love them.\par \par \uc2 And sometimes I`ll just boil that for my daughter with a little bit of pastina and some chicken and she loves it. Lots of parm. So, I like to keep it as easy as possible, especially when it`s just -- you know, it`s cold, it`s miserable and you`re working from home and you have the kids.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Right, and you want it quick. How long do you let it simmer for? And what is that that you`re cutting?\par \par \uc2 VITALE: So, what I`m putting off right now is the rind of some parmesan, right? So, the rind has incredible flavor. And all I`m going to do, now that I`m going to add my tomato sauce and my beef stock, I`m going to add this alongside so that it infuses all of my soup with this lovely salty parmesan cheesy, that it is -- it makes or breaks a soup for me. It`s my favorite.\par \par \uc2 But once you have all of your aromatics and your sausage looking really good, I`m going to go ahead and add some tomato parts. I always have a marinara sauce on hand because I do.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: Sure.\par \par \uc2 VITALE: Because I don`t think any Italian doesn`t. So, I just use a couple of leftover marinara sauce. But this works great with like crushed tomatoes or open up a can of diced tomatoes. It`s really no fuss at all. And then, you`ve got to add your beef stock.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That looks good.\par \par \uc2 VITALE: Now, if you`re adding beef stock, chicken stock, whatever you`re adding, just make sure it`s a good quality. Because again, if you`re using few ingredients in something that cooks kind of quickly, you just want to make sure you`re using the best quality ingredients you can. Then you take your parmesan rind and you bury it in there.\par \par \uc2 I like to go with a little Italian seasoning. If you don`t have it, all it is it`s just dried parsley --\par \par \uc2 JONES: This looks so amazing.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: We`re going to put the full recipe online, Laura. But -- and the good news is, if you don`t have ravioli and you just have pasta in your closet, you can put that in too. So, thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I`ll keep your posted. I`ll post it.\par \par \uc2 VITALE: If you don`t have ravioli, you can go ahead and just use rice or a pasta or anything like that. It doesn`t have to be mini ravioli.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 VITALE: It can be tortellini.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It looks amazing.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: It looks amazing.\par \par \uc2 VITALE: Once that comes to a boil --\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 VITALE: -- you just let --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Laura, we`re running out of time.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Thank you so much. We`ll put everything online.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It looks good. To get this recipe, go to today.com/food. Thank you. Laura. We`ll be will be right back.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: I wish there was food here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I know.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Fun show today, Jill.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You`ll be back here tomorrow with one your favorites. You want to tune in because we have a Hollywood legend. Goldie Hawn will be live.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And up next on "Hoda & Jenna," Ricky Gervais gives us a sneak peak of the new season of "Afterlife."\par \par \uc2 JONES: Nice. Have a great day, everybody. "Hoda & Jenna" up next.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220118ei1i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Thousands Of Grocery Store Employees Walking Off The Job\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Emilie Ikeda \par \uc2 462 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 January 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: We want to turn out of a standoff involving a major supermarket chain. Thousands of employees becoming the latest members of the American workforce to walk off the job. Emilie Ikeda with what they`re demanding.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The so-called year of the worker presses on into 2022 as more than 8000 employees at \uc2 Kroger\uc2 -owned King Soopers are now on strike in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who`s got the power?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We got the power.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: They`re demanding better wages, more robust benefits, and stricter safety measures as essential workers into the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 TONY WHITE, EMPLOYEE, KING SOOPERS: It seems like the people just don`t matter. They just want their profit.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The strike is the latest in a wave of labor union demonstrations from John Deere to Kellogg`s and Nabisco.\par \par \uc2 How has the pandemic awakened to this worker empowerment?\par \par \uc2 ALI BUSTAMANTE, EDUCATION, JOBS AND WORKER POWER DEPUTY DIRECTOR, ROOSEVELT INSTITUTION: It is really very much a product of a tight labor market that has increased the leverage and work -- and power of workers. We`re ultimately seeing this in record levels of quits as well as record levels of hires.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Workers are making their stand at a time when stores nationwide are struggling to stock shelves. And the strike could grow in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 KIM CORDOVA\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UFCW LOCAL 7: They`re essential workers but they`re being treated as sacrificial and disposable workers.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: A new study commissioned by the union indicates more than three quarters of \uc2 Kroger\uc2  workers surveyed in several states are food insecure. \uc2 Kroger\uc2  calls that report misleading releasing its own research showing the company on average pays hourly workers more than its peers as it pushes back against the strike.\par \par \uc2 JOE KELLEY, PRESIDENT, KING SOOPERS: Nobody wins. Not our customers, not our associates, not the union, and not the company.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The grocery chain`s final offer increased some employee`s wages by $4.50 an hour and boosted starting pay to $16 an hour. The Union rejected the proposal leaving \uc2 Kroger\uc2  scrambling to hire temporary workers as Omicron fuel staffing shortages nationwide. Emilie Ikeda, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, the growing threat from homemade bombs.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220115ei1d000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Another Record Wave Of Americans Leaving Their Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 Willie Geist, Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 1,695 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 January 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:15:00] WILLIE GEIST: This week, the Department of Labor reported 4.5 million Americans voluntarily left their jobs in the month of November, breaking the previous record set in September. Economists are calling the ongoing trend of workers walking away, The Great Resignation. So, what is driving that phenomenon? And where are all of those people going? NBC News Senior Business Correspondent Stephanie Ruhle has our "Sunday Focus."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, NBC NEWS SENIOR BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): When it comes to Americans and work, the pandemic has upended just about everything. Nearly two years ago, 20 million people lost their job due to COVID. A year later, thanks to vaccines, employers have been on a hiring spree. A record 6.4 million new jobs in 2021. An average of 537,000 a month.\par \par \uc2 And while some mixed news Friday about the labor market, one good sign, the unemployment rates fell below 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY KLOTZ, TEXAS A&M UNIVERSITY ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR OF MANAGEMENT: In many industries, in fact most, there`s record high job openings. And so, it`s a good time to be an employee in a lot of ways.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): To fill those positions, employers are offering incentives, higher wages, more benefits, putting potential employees in the driver`s seat and many were deciding to take off. A record 4.5 million people quit their job in November.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (on camera): Several months of record setting amounts of people quitting, how do you characterize that?\par \par \uc2 KLOTZ: I would characterize that as remarkable.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): Anthony Klotz is credited with coining the phrase The Great Resignation. He says most people haven`t stopped working. They`re using the pandemic as an opportunity to make a change.\par \par \uc2 KLOTZ: This Great Resignation and the turnover that goes along with it is individuals taking their future, their careers into their own hands and trying to craft a life, it`s more meaningful for them and their families.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): It`s happening across the country. Workers walking away from every type of job. Waiters and cooks, teachers and nurses, retail clerks and real-estate agents.\par \par \uc2 DREWSBIC: It was the scariest yet most accelerating decision I think I`ve made as an adult. And I feel so happy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): Rachel Drewsbic (ph) worked as a police officer in Florida for over seven years.\par \par \uc2 DREWSBIC: You know, just with everything going on with COVID and just everything in this world, I decided that I needed a change.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): Now, running her own advertising marketing company, Rachel is like millions of Americans who decided 2021 was the year to become their own boss.\par \par \uc2 As of November, budding entrepreneur submitted nearly 5 million new business applications, a jump of more than 55 percent compared to 2019 before the pandemic. Beth Forrest (ph) in Portland, Oregon, walked away from a successful 20-year career in hospitality.\par \par \uc2 BETH FORREST, VETERINARIAN ASSISTANT: I was one of those things that was a childhood dream.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): She now works as a veterinarian`s assistant at an urgent care center.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (on camera): Is the schedule easier?\par \par \uc2 FORREST: No, it`s actually worse. It`s -- you know, it`s all sorts of hours. But it`s certainly not 9:00 to 5:00.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK. So, the hours are worse, I`m guessing the pay is worse.\par \par \uc2 FORREST: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK. But you like it a whole lot more?\par \par \uc2 FORREST: I do. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: No regrets?\par \par \uc2 FORREST: No. Not at all.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE (voiceover): And for those in low-wage jobs like retail and hospitality, quitting can mean finding a new job with higher wages, less exposure to COVID and stress dealing with increasingly frustrated customers. Despite the ups and downs over the past two years, it`s a sign of economic strength that people know they can quit and potentially find a better job.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And Stephanie joins me now live.\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning. It`s great to see you.\par \par \uc2 This is just so fascinating. Millions of Americans completely reassessing their lives. This Great Resignation has hit the hospitality and retail industries hard, as you said. But where else might this trend show up to consumers?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Willie, it`s across the board. There are 1.5 open jobs for every unemployed person. So, from lawyers to plumbers, workers now have a lot more bargaining power, even if they`re not planning on quitting. For consumers, what does that mean? It means we`re likely going to be paying more when we go out for Sunday brunch, but we`re going to have to wait more for services we need to get done. That plumber that you need to come over, you might have to wait an extra day because of labor shortages. So, I would say hold your nose and be patient.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Yes. And obviously, Omicron is playing into all of this. We got a new variant. It means COVID has shut down work places once again. How does that affect this resignation?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, we could see the result like we did with Delta. People don`t want to go out to eat as much, go out to eat and travel, that would slow hiring. But you mentioned it, there are all sorts of offices that plan on bringing workers back right around now and in the coming weeks. Since they`ve decided to slow that down and delay it, well, that`s going to impact jobs. Think about cafeterias, all the jobs around commuting to work. Those jobs, those re-hiring are now on hold. And when businesses do bring their workers back because of Omicron, a lot of people are concerned. More people might decide they don`t want to go back to work, they want to do something else.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: You and I see it every day at Rockefeller Center in Midtown Manhattan. Stephanie Ruhle, great report as always. Thanks so much. Good to see you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next here, a favorite "Sunday Sitdown" with a pair of old friends nominated in the same comedy category at tonight`s Golden Globe Awards. Steve Martin and Martin Short on their popular series, "Only Murders in the Building" and four decades of being funny together.\par \par \uc2 And then, a life well lived. The beloved teacher whose daughter was the youngest of four girls killed in the infamous attack on a church in Birmingham, Alabama nearly six decades ago.\par \par \uc2 And as we head to break, our photo of the week as we honor the late Sidney Poitier who died this week after an Oscar winning barrier breaking career on screen and an influential life off of it. The actor placing his hands in the cement outside of the Grauman`s Chinese Theater in Los Angeles in 1967. After news of his death on Friday, the theater placed that same photo on its marquee and blocked off the cement which bears the hand and footprints of the first black man ever to win the Academy Award for best actor. The great Sidney Poitier was 94 years old.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:25:00]\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is a "News4 Today Newsbreak."\par \par \uc2 JUMMY OLABANJI, NBC 4 ANCHOR: It is 8:26 on this Sunday January 9th. Good morning to you, I`m Jummy Olabanji.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a look at our headlines on this Sunday. One person is dead after a triple car crash on the Baltimore Washington parkway overnight. U.S. Park Police were called at the Southbound lanes at the split to Route 50 just after 1:00 this morning. Details of what caused that crash are still under investigation. But we do know one person was killed and two others were taken to the hospital. They are expected to be OK.\par \par \uc2 Also, new details on an investigation involving an accused serial killer in the area. He`s tied to the murders of four women so far. And now, police believe he may have murdered a fifth woman, as well. 40-year-old Sonya Champ from D.C. was found dead in a shopping cart near Union Station in September. D.C. Police believe that she was murdered by 35-year-old Anthony Robinson. Police say he allegedly murdered woman that he met on dating sites and to move their bodies using carts. Police believe there could be more victims and they want to talk to anyone who have met Anthony Robinson.\par \par \uc2 A check of your forecast is coming up next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RICKETTS: Do you see a rainbow today? Usually, we don`t see them a lot in the wintertime because we have clouds for miles across the area, but we`re actually seeing bright and clouds and getting some sunshine. That little bit of rain to come through and some rainbows. But some freezing rain out there right now. North and West of D.C., we will continue to see that skirt by the area. There could be some frozen area, especially given road temperatures that are below that 32-degree mark. So, be careful, this will change to plain rain through the morning.\par \par \uc2 OLABANJI: OK. Lauryn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And I know you`ll watch the burgundy and gold back on the road for the final game of season against the Giants. Kickoff between Washington and New York set for 1:00 this afternoon at Metlife Stadium. Coverage starts at noon over on NBC Sports Washington.\par \par \uc2 [08:30:00]\par \par \uc2 And remember, you can get the latest news and forecast any time in the NBC Washington app. For now, back to the "TODAY" show. Have a great day.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220110ei19000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Is Considering The Fate Of Another Biden Administration Effort Against COVID\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Pete Williams \par \uc2 705 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 January 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Beyond testing, the Supreme Court today considering the fate of another Biden administration effort against COVID. A federal mandate requiring tens of millions of workers to get vaccinated or take weekly tests. As Pete Williams reports, the justices seem skeptical.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The court tonight seems prepared to block what the Biden administration calls a powerful tool for fighting COVID. A mandate that companies with more than 100 employees require their workers to get COVID vaccines or wear masks and take a weekly test. It applies to 80 million people.\par \par \uc2 Businesses in red states sue. The CEO of this Minnesota trucking company says drivers who refuse to get vaccinated will quit and work for smaller firms, and that a testing requirement doesn`t make sense for truckers.\par \par \uc2 ERIC LAWRENCE, CEO, LAWRENCE TRANSPORTATION COMPANY: Where, where are we going to get tested? You know, there isn`t -- there isn`t semi-parking yet at clinics or \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  or CVS.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The rule was imposed by OSHA, the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2 . The White House says it has authority over workplace hazards.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The bottom line, we`re going to protect vaccinated workers from unvaccinated co-workers.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And today, the Supreme Court`s liberal said the need is urgent.\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: It is by far the greatest public health danger that this country has faced in the last century. More and more people are dying every day.\par \par \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: We are now having deaths at an unprecedented amount. Catching COVID keeps people out of the workplace for extraordinary periods of time.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But the courts conservative majority was skeptical that OSHA has the legal authority to issue such a sweeping nationwide ruling.\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL ALITO\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: If you`re vaccinated while you`re on the job, you`re vaccinated when you`re not on the job. Isn`t this different from anything OSHA has done before in that respect?\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , CHIEF JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: Why isn`t Congress have a say in this and going to this and doesn`t this be the primary responsibility of the states?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): But in a separate case, the court seems prepared to rule that the government can require vaccinations for 20 million health care workers. Decisions could come within a few days. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Pete Williams at the Supreme Court, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The President is speaking about COVID today insisting the new normal is not going to be what it is now but better, though he got some disappointing news today with weak jobs numbers that were impacted by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Kristen Welker is at the White House. And Kristen, the President instead highlighted a bright spot in unemployment.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Lester, that`s right. The President went straight to that positive headline touting the unemployment rate fell to 3.9 percent from 4.2 percent last month, saying the drop in the last year is the biggest in history.\par \par \uc2 But the disappointing headline today, the economy only added 199,000 jobs, just half of what economists were expecting. It`s a low figure for the second month in a row now and it comes amid soaring inflation at a nearly 40 year high.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are blaming Mr. Biden`s spending policies for the high inflation. Today, the president firing back calling those claims quote "malarkey." Economists warn today`s disappointing jobs figures came prior to much of the Omicron surge, so there could be more volatility ahead, Lester.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220109ei170002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Firsthand Look At New High Tech Helpful World Where Some Of Us Are Working From Office, Others Working From Home\b0\par\par\uc2 Craig Melvin, Savannah Guthrie, Vicky Nguyen \par \uc2 1,326 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 January 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN: Back now, 8:35 with our series of "Future of Work." And, of course, it is looking different by the day, COVID and the Omicron variant put a pause on return to office plans from coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Back to the Zoom, right? Well, new technology is making it possible for workers to collaborate and build camaraderie from any location. NBC senior consumer investigative correspondent Vicky Nguyen joins us with the first look. Looking forward to seeing this, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN: Yes. Good morning to you all. This is a really cool one, very immersive. You know about virtual reality, now imagine taking that technology into the workplace to really connect you with your colleagues even when you are continents apart. And how about technology that allows you to roam the halls at your workplace from wherever you are. This morning, I`m showing you not only is it possible, but it`s happening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): After nearly two years of working from home and countless remote meetings, including a few blunders.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m here live, but I`m not a chat.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): Which is one reason why multiple Fortune 500 companies are turning to virtual reality.\par \par \uc2 Equipped with this headset and these controllers, employees from around the world can meet and work together. And the idea is they all feel like they`re in the same place at one time.\par \par \uc2 OK. So I have my headset on and, boom, wow. Look at this view. We are in what looks like a really sleek design studio. With me now is Guy Messick. He is the director of design intelligence for IA Interior Architects OK, Guy, where are we right now and how does this help the workforce to be in an environment like this?\par \par \uc2 GUY MESSICK, DIRECTOR OF DESIGN INTELLIGENCE, IA INTERIOR ARCHITECTS: Well, this is a custom version of our new Atlanta office. And it helps by taking what we liked best about real world meetings and transforming that into virtual space, to try to go in a more human way and a more connected way. No matter where you are enrolled.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: I actually feel like I`m here in this space with you, in a weird way that I wouldn`t on a 2D video call.\par \par \uc2 MESSICK: And you find yourself forgetting that we`re in very different places, like it just seems natural very quickly.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: OK. Guy, let`s take this conversation back into the real world for a sec, OK?\par \par \uc2 Well, well, well, there you are. We`re back to reality now.\par \par \uc2 MESSICK: This is quite a bit different, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Do you think this is the future of how we`re going to work?\par \par \uc2 MESSICK: I think it is the now and the near future of how we`re going to work.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): The virtual space allows for more than just meetings. It provides a sense of connection.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got my drink. Cheers.\par \par \uc2 MESSICK: Cheers.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): And check this out, natural features like blades of grass blowing in the breeze and rippling water in the pool. You can make presentations on whiteboards, stick up a post it note, even write messages in mid air. Staff and interact ways they can`t, in real life, changing the scale of furniture with a wave of the hand.\par \par \uc2 MESSICK: So you could bring it up to life size.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MESSICK: Or back down again like that. So a car might be used by automotive company to have a conversation about a new model with your dealers or stakeholders.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Guy, what`s over there on that giant monitor?\par \par \uc2 MESSICK: Well, this is a representation of virtual reality of a new project in Dublin we`re working on for a client.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): From virtual reality to robots.\par \par \uc2 Check this out. From anywhere in the world with an internet connection, I can log on to this robot and feel like I`m in the office. And look, I can even move my little robot body. Hey, Michelle. Hi, Joe. How you guys doing?\par \par \uc2 JOE: Hi, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And it seems like I`m in the office with you.\par \par \uc2 JOE: Kind of. And we`re having a conversation at least.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: This is cool. Let me see how close I can get before it gets weird. Too close?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE: Just a little.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: The personal space will still apply with a robot.\par \par \uc2 JOE: Yes. You kind of in Michelle`s personal space right now.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): The Omni Robot is being used in healthcare for doctors to talk to patients in education, connecting teachers with students, even retail allowing employees to check inventory.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From collaborating with colleagues who are in the office.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): To navigating in a virtual one. Technology is allowing us to work from home and be in the office at the same time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s so interesting.\par \par \uc2 [08:39:57]\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: So this technology is not cheap. Yes. I mean we`re looking at $7,000 to $10,000 for that virtual reality technology. But developers say that when you factor in the office rent, the overhead that comes with running a physical location, it can be more affordable.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So I`m sure the numbers will grow. But for now, Vicky, how many companies are doing this?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes. Sheinelle, we talked to a few more Fortune 500 companies, they have confirmed they are using it, including \uc2 Hewlett Packard Enterprise\uc2 . They`re using it with their attorneys who are stationed worldwide, to connect them and to help them collaborate.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, Vicky, if a company does decide to go VR, how long would it take to set up? Any ideas there?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes. This is interesting, Craig. They say on average, two to three months but it really depends on how complex the office space is. I mean, the one you saw we were in two stories. The outside had views from South Africa and Ireland, it was a fire pit. So a space like that with more bells and whistles will take additional time.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: What about legs, Vic?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: That is cool.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Extra if you want legs in your avatar?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: If you want legs?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, legs. You have no legs. No one have legs.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Like a body, a full body.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: It`s just the Vicky head.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: (Inaudible) working on that. Yes, I forgot. It is a little weird, right, but who needs legs when you`re floating. It`s kind of cool, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, see? Especially when you`re doing conference calls and you sit behind a desk --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: (Inaudible) what it`s worth, I like real life, Vicky, more than (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know. We miss you, Vic.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you, Vic.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: People get in your nerves, you can kind of put a blanket over --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Showed up with a robot. Unplug it. Coming up next from a new work plan to a new one for working out, we`re going to guide you through an exercise routine to start the New Year off right. And you only have to spare 10 minutes every day. But first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220106ei15000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 School Cancelled For Hundreds Of Thousands Of Students In Chicago As Teachers Refuse To Enter The Classroom Amidst A Record Surge In COVID Cases\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 655 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 January 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: All right, busy times. Al, thank you. And now the breaking news overnight tied to COVID`s record shattering surge. Officials in Chicago had to cancel classes for hundreds of thousands of students today after the city`s teachers voted to switch to remote learning. That`s just hours after President Biden address the nation about that current spike in cases. NBC`s Stephanie Gosk with the latest on all of it. Stephanie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, good morning. This is a real showdown in Chicago. So the teacher`s union votes to go remote over concerns about testing and cases. And then the mayor, \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , comes in and raises the stakes, calling it an illegal walkout and cancelling school altogether.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): This morning, another clash between educators and cities over COVID, ending with kids in Chicago staying home. The teacher`s union in the nation`s third largest school district voted Tuesday to switch to remote learning, demanding more testing and adjustments to the new COVID surge. City leaders responded by cancelling school altogether, even remote learning for most of the district`s 330,000 students.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR \uc2 LORI LIGHTFOOT\uc2  (D-IL), CHICAGO: There is no basis in the data, the science or common sense for us to shut an entire system down.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): It`s part of the pressure on schools and parents as COVID cases skyrocket, and some have found guidance from authorities confusing.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:01]\par \par \uc2 The CDC now clarifying its quarantine guidance again after a cut COVID isolation down from 10 days to five for asymptomatic people without requiring a negative test. But the agency is again under fire from medical experts who say the guidelines are still confusing and still don`t require a negative COVID test after sick people are done isolating at home for five days.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the newest guidance unveiled Tuesday simply says people who have access to a rapid test and want to take it, should. The CDC says a positive result on a rapid test after you self-quarantine means you should keep isolating for another five days. Those who don`t have tests and are asymptomatic or feeling better on day five can head out with a well-fitting mask.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAVITA PATEL, NBC NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: That`s why it`s still ambiguous. It`s hard to ask people to judge their own symptoms.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): COVID gazes are exploding from coast to coast. The sheer volume of sick people is taking a toll on the entire country.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURA MCPHEE, PULMONOLOGIST AT ELLIOT HOSPITAL: Right now, we have more COVID patients in the ICU than we ever have.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): Understaffed before the surge hit, hospitals are now dealing with health workers getting sick too.\par \par \uc2 MCPHEE: We`re all pulling extra shifts. We`re all taking care of more patients than we ever have. We are tired and frustrated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOSK: President Biden made a point in his comments on Tuesday to say that kids needed to stay in school. One thing that may help that, the CDC is meeting to likely sign off on the FDA`s authorization of the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  booster for kids ages 12 to 15. Savannah, back to you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Stephanie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220106ei150005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 TODAY for December 28, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Alexander, Kristen Welker, Shaquille Brewster, Gadi  Schwartz, Sam Brock, Jacob Soboroff, Steve Patterson, Jeff Ranieri, Matt  Noyes, Latoyia Edwards, Kerry Sanders, Jeff Ranieri \par \uc2 8,046 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 December 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:22]\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. Back to work, a major update from the CDC cutting the recommended isolation period for people with COVID in half, in part to help with those major staff shortages causing problems from coast to coast, but is it safe? We`ll find out everything you need to know about the new guidance including an urgent call to parents to get their kids vaccinated before they`re back in school.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news, a gunman in Denver is dead this morning after leading police on a wild chase through the city and suburbs. Seven victim shot before the spree came to an end. The big question now, what was the motive?\par \par \uc2 Here we go again, hundreds of new flight cancellations piling up this morning. More stranded passengers now awaiting their fate as a nasty bout of winter weather adds to the misery rain, sleet, and snow set to make a mess across a wide swath of the country. We have complete coverage.\par \par \uc2 Cruise damage control, new COVID outbreaks at sea have the cruise line industry scrambling again. Ships turned away from ports, passengers on edge.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel like I was just in a petri dish waiting to get COVID.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We`ll have a live report. Going maverick, he`s not a movie star. But he`s fooled hundreds of millions with his deep fakes of Tom Cruise. This morning, a today exclusive with a man behind those viral videos now speaking out about the pros and cons of the technology for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Those stories, plus, what`s in the box, a second time capsule from the 1800s unearth in Virginia. Why historians are so excited about this one after a disappointing discovery earlier this month. So what will they find as they get set to open it, today, Tuesday, December 28th, 2021.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And welcome to today, thanks for joining us on this Tuesday morning. I`m Peter Alexander alongside my weekend today co-host Kristen Welker. Our friend Savannah and Hoda are off today.\par \par \uc2 That Maverick that Tom Cruise deep fake is insane. He`s got a pretty good talk to me, goose, I trust.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Sure, does. I can`t believe how much that guy actually looks like Tom Cruise in real life. You have to see this to believe it.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, it`s frightening. We`ll have that coming up in just a little bit this morning. But we begin this morning, with the latest on the Omicron variant shooting up again nearly 250,000 daily cases just shy of the record set for the entirety of this pandemic, numbers not seen since almost a year ago. And experts say the post-holiday spike hasn`t even hit yet.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That`s right. And it comes as travelers are facing another day of potential chaos as the perfect storm of bad weather and staffing shortages due to COVID are set to cause even more cancellations.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The CDC now changing course issuing new guidance on how long people who test positive should remain isolated as more as learned about the Omicron variants and more companies across the country face staffing shortages. We have it all covered for you this morning. We want to begin with NBC`s Shaquille Brewster in Indianapolis. Shaq, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning. Well, positive COVID-19 tests led to so many having to isolate and be away from friends and family during the holidays. But the CDC now says those who test positive may interact with others sooner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): This morning new guidance from the CDC, the agency reducing the recommended isolation period for some people testing positive from 10 days to five if they`re asymptomatic. Adding the time should be quote, followed by five days of wearing a mask when around others. Dr. Fauci telling Kristen Welker the guidelines are partly driven by worker shortages.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: You want to make sure that particularly among essential workers that you get people out there much sooner.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): The change coming out of the highly transmissible Omicron variant is fueling a new nationwide surge, the U.S. once again facing a 200,000 daily case average. In New York, there are now 184 children hospitalized with COVID statewide. Two weeks ago there were 70.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Parents are calling on you. This is the time you have some kids home from school. There`s plenty of vaccination opportunities.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): The new guidance a boost to hospital staff battling full ICUs as many frontline healthcare workers struggle with burnout.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANDREA ROWLAND-FISHER, HENNEPIN HEALTHCARE EMERGENCY MEDICINE PHYSICIAN: And there have been In people leaving medicine at a really higher rate we have people out sick with COVID themselves.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:07]\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): As states continue to see long post-holiday testing lines from Florida to New Jersey to Wisconsin, President Biden in a meeting with governors responding to calls for local action.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Look, there is no federal solution. This gets solved at state level.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): Testing, boosters, and vaccinations just some of the ways Americans are hoping to protect themselves in this time of busy travel and overwhelming surges. Experts also urging a shift to high quality masks.\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA\uc2 , DEAN OF BROWN UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH: The cloth masks do very, very little, I mean, maybe, you know, just sort of on the margin. The surgical masks are a little bit better. A KF94s, KN95s, or N95s, those are all high quality masks and I really think at this point, that`s what Americans should be switching to if they want to avoid getting infected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: The CDC even recommended masking for those who are vaccinated but exposed to the virus. Meanwhile, the military is continuing to surge efforts to those hospitals feeling the squeeze, a 20 person team arrived at this hospital recently with another team being deployed to Wisconsin later this week. Peter?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Shaq Brewster in Indianapolis this morning. Shaq, thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Joining us now Dr. Paul Offit is a member of the FDA Vaccine Advisory Committee and the director of the Children`s Vaccination Center at Children`s Hospital of Philadelphia. Dr. Offit, good morning. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DR. PAUL OFFIT, \uc2 FDA\uc2  VACCINE ADVISORY COMMITTEE MEMBER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: So let`s start with this decision by the CDC to cut isolation periods for people who are infected with COVID or exposed to COVID. But asymptomatic it was, as you heard Dr. Fauci say driven in part by an attempt to cut down those staffing shortages. But does it go enough, far enough to protect public health?\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: I think so I mean, as long as you follow the directives, so usually, when you`re infected with this kind of virus, the virus reproduce itself in your nose, your throat, your upper respiratory tract. And when that happens, if we produce yourself hundreds of times, thousands of times, and then you`re, that`s when you`re most contagious right at the beginning, before you develop some, then your immune system kicks in. And that`s when you have symptoms, as you have more and more symptoms, you actually shed less and less virus.\par \par \uc2 So if you`re asymptomatic, meaning no symptoms, you probably are shedding a trivial amount of virus, if at all at that point. And then the sort of the belt and suspenders approach there is that if you`re truly asymptomatic, then just wear a mask for five more days. So I think that`s fair. Also, it`s hard to get testing. So I think some people are uncomfortable with sort of not testing before you go out mask, but I think that`s OK. But the trick is actually it`s the honor system. Once again, you have to trust people are truly asymptomatic.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And Dr. Offit, amid this rise in cases nationwide, we`re seeing a particularly worrying trend. And that goes to your expertise, which is among children in the hospitalizations, particularly here in New York where the number of kids hospitalized in the last two weeks, nearly quadrupled. So how worrying is that trend? And what is the warning to parents?\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: Well, first of all, it`s winter. And this is a winter virus and this Omicron is particularly contagious. So it`s I think you were going to see an increase anyway. But here`s what I would say, at our hospital, we test anybody who`s admitted to the hospital for whatever reason to see whether or not that they have COVID. And we`re definitely see an increase in cases. However, we`re really not seeing an increase. And people who are children who are hospitalized for COVID are in the intensive care unit for COVID. So again, there`s just association between cases and serious cases, which is all because this is a highly contagious virus. And even if you`re vaccinated, you still have a fairly high chance of exposed or developing mild symptoms. You`re still protected against serious disease. So please get vaccinated, but you`re just going to be seeing a lot of cases and a lot of asymptomatic and mild cases in the United States over the next --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And to follow up on that point, Dr. Offit, millions of children will be returning to the classroom next week in the New Year. Is it safe to be back in the classroom?\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: Well, you want to do everything you can to get children back on. I mean that site learning is so much better than the distance learning and the socialization et cetera, that comes with being in school. So I think you know, if it`s safe, if you can make sure that children who are unvaccinated -- first of all, make sure everybody over five is vaccinated because they can be vaccinated. And you know that people wear masks if there are people in the class who are unvaccinated. So sure, but we need to do that we need to care about our children enough to vaccinate and care about them enough to mask them if, you know, if it requires that, yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Dr. Offit, often finally New Year is just a matter of days away right now. How should folks revisit their plans for the New Year given the infectiousness of this variant?\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: Well, be careful. I mean, I`m about to go visit my mother who`s 93 years old. I think we`re all going to get tested just to make sure that we`re not going to be exposing her to anything. But it`s hard to get test so, you know, just be careful. Obviously if you`re symptomatic, you could be symptomatic with any of the other respiratory viruses that are out there this year but just assume it`s COVID and foreign.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:11]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Dr. Offit, as always we appreciate this great information, always good to be with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Turning down to breaking news from overnight, a terrifying shooting spree in Colorado that left four people dead and injured at least three others, including a police officer. NBC`s Gadi Schwartz has the latest on that this morning. Gadi, good morning. Hey, good morning, Peter.\par \par \uc2 GADI SCHWARTZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, police say the suspected gunman went on a wild rampage shooting people at seven different locations in Denver and one of its suburbs before it was all over. And now the question this morning is why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): This morning, a search for a motive following a shooting spree in Colorado that left four people dead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 1201 Williams (inaudible) Adams. We have a possible shooting.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): It started Monday evening spending at least seven different locations. Officials say the gunman first opened fire in Denver killing two women in one area and a man in another. Denver Police quickly identifying the suspect`s vehicle which led to a wild chase when they tried to take him into custody.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF PAUL PAZEN, DENVER POLICE DEPARTMENT: There was an exchange of gunfire. We believe that the individual after disabling the police car fled into Lakewood.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): Police say the suspect shot and killed another person in that Denver suburb.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROMERO, LAKEWOOD POLICE PUBLIC INFORMATION OFFICER: The driver then fled on foot in the area behind us in the Belmar shopping area where he allegedly felony menaced another business with a firearm.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): Authority say the gunman went into a Hyatt Hotel shooting a clerk inside before he got into a violent gun battle with police.\par \par \uc2 ANNIE WILSON, SHOOTING WITNESS: There was probably maybe like seven or eight gunshots and then like another set of maybe five more.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): One officer was shut during the exchange and is recovering this morning following surgery for unspecified injuries.\par \par \uc2 ROMERO: It`s a tough day for the Lakewood police family, obviously when this happens to one of our own.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ (voice-over): The gun battle quickly coming to an end though police say it`s unclear if an officer took down the suspect or if he took his own life.\par \par \uc2 PAZEN: This is the holiday season to have this type of spree take place is not normal for our community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: And so far police have not publicly identified the gunman and are still unsure of what might have triggered his rampage. Peter?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Gadi Schwartz with the latest. Gadi, thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Meanwhile, another chaotic day of holiday travel is looming, airlines right now in dire need of some reinforcements with COVID call outs and winter weather wreaking havoc this week. But the good news is that shorter quarantine time is expected to help keep more planes in the air. We have complete coverage beginning with NBC`s Sam Brock. Sam, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Kristen, good morning. There is some optimism unfortunately. We`re not off to a great start right now. Let`s get you up to date on the number, so far 680 domestic cancellations and more than 500 delays, Kristen that is on the heels of 1,400 plus cancellations yesterday and 7,700 delays. Travel experts right now say it should get better the next couple of days. But New Year`s is going to be a challenge.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): It`s a brutal one two punch of COVID and winter weather igniting widespread cancellations and flight delays. Both passengers and crews slowed by the rapid spread of Omicron.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: About 4 o`clock in the morning and got to the airport, got on the plane, everything was fine. And then one of the pilots didn`t show up. So we hadn`t a plane.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  says roughly one out of every 30 of its flights was canceled Monday, and widespread delays meant fewer than half of all passengers met it to their destination within four hours of the expected arrival time. For Delta weather snarling traffic at key hubs like Minneapolis, Salt Lake City and Seattle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s pretty tough night. I spent about close to 12 hours in the San Francisco Airport.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): One Delta flight from Tampa to Atlanta had to deal with potentially criminal behavior.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Tell him to mask up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You`ve got your mask down --.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): In the sign of the times, a woman paraded a man for not wearing his mask while not wearing her mask.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As soon as we get to Atlanta, you`re going to jail.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): It devolved into spitting, punching, and scratching. That woman then appearing in a federal courthouse Monday, Delta sang in part the company has zero tolerance for unruly behavior at our airports and aboard our aircraft.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Airlines for America and several airlines CEO supporting the shorter quarantine time just issued by the CDC for sick crew members. But the Flight Attendants Association has deep concerns.\par \par \uc2 SARA NELSON\uc2 , ASSOCIATION 