l anywhere from a half to two inches with this, really stacking up and Kentucky, Nashville, and down to Alabama.\par \par \uc2 That`s a look at your national forecast. We`ll have your local weather coming up in 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MATT NOYES, NBC10 NEWS METEOROLOGIST: You know a couple of slick spots left on back roads, walkways, driveways, sidewalks, things like that this morning. Other than that, we`re doing all right. We`re on our way up into the 40s believe it or not. By the time we get to the afternoon with a couple of breaks of the sun, raindrops and snowflakes again tonight really won`t be a huge deal for us. We`ll trace and that`s about it.\par \par \uc2 [07:20:09]\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, a lot of clouds around, a little bit cooler, highs around 40. But look at this, we`re 40 are better all the way through the upcoming weekend. It looks like right now anyway as long as the timing holds the rain on New Year`s Eve would hold off until after midnight but it will be wet on New Year`s Day.\par \par \uc2 RANIERI: And that is a look at your national forecast. Peter, Kristen, Jacob, look, I think Jacob and I definitely get credit for starting to bring some winter weather from California out here to the east.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: True. We`ve got another California though.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: You get all the credit. There you go.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That`s not the California weather we were hoping you were going to bring here. But thanks \uc2 Jeff Ranieri\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Coming up, just as cruise line travel was rebounding, the chaos returns. We`ll have the latest on the recent COVID outbreaks on board causing several ships to be turned away from their ports.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Plus, Jacob bringing a lot to the table today has an exclusive interview with the man behind the viral deep fake videos impersonating Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I can`t wait to show you guys. He has a fascinating take as this rapidly advancing technology sparks interest both online and from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . But first this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Still ahead with Omicron spreading fast. If you`re not feeling well, how can you tell if you have COVID, the flu, or just a common cold?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Important question. Dr. John Torres will break down the symptoms and the best treatments for each that`s coming up right after your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:26:22]\par \par \uc2 LATOYIA EDWARDS, NBC10 BOSTON ANCHOR: Good morning everybody. It`s 7:26. I`m Latoya Edwards. Thank you for starting your morning with us here at NBC10 Boston. And there was a sprinkling of freezing rain and spots overnight but that`s behind us for now. We want to check in with chief meteorologist Matt Noyes in your First Alert forecast.\par \par \uc2 NOYES: You know what, it was a little tricky in some spots this morning back roads in particular, right. But most of the treated roads held up OK and now today we`re going into the 40s. We`ll do it with some splashes of sunshine. We could push 50 if you`re south of town. Then as we get to the evening and overnight between 9:00 and 10:00 p.m., some light rain and snow showers come through. But look at this, by this time tomorrow, they`re long gone. What do you get out of it? You probably get a little trace of some snow or ice during the overnight.\par \par \uc2 I think for the most part the roads with the treatment they have on them still are going to end up being fine. Tomorrow, it is a little bit cooler, but you`re still around 40. In fact, you`re 40 or better through the end of the week. New Year`s Eve rain on the way, but I`m thinking if the timing holds as it looks right now, we`ll come in till after midnight. That would be nice. It will rain New Year`s Day and could end as snow later on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 EDWARDS: Thank you, Matt.\par \par \uc2 Several stories that we`re following right now, Boston EMS telling us one person has been taken to the hospital after reported stabbing in Hyde Park. Police blocked off Arlington Street overnight as they gathered evidence. We`ll let you know if we learn any more details on this.\par \par \uc2 Another developing story right now Boston police looking for the person who slammed their vehicle into this Dorchester nail salon and then took off shattered glass and debris covered the sidewalk outside of happy nails on Washington Street after the crash. It was reported around 9:00 p.m. And it`s unclear if police have a person of interest.\par \par \uc2 And a heads up for those of you braving the cold for the outdoor dining scene in Boston, this is the last week it`ll be open for the season. December 31st is the end date. For more local news tune into our sister station NACN. Coming up on the Today show, we`ve got a closer look at a really interesting story deep fake technology with a viral creator who looks like Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:23]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We are back now at 7:30 on a Tuesday morning December 28th, 2021. Peter Alexander and I are in for our friend Savannah and Hoda as they enjoy some holiday time off along with Jacob Soboroff. It`s good to be with you guys. Looking at that tree, I can`t believe it comes down soon.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Oh, well, I can and I`m glad my tree came down yesterday out of my house gives that space back and then you still get a holiday just for a little bit.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I`m impressed. You`re ahead of the curve.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: You`re glad your tree came down with your wife doing the hard work.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Shout out to Nicole.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That`s her problem.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Major shout out.\par \par \uc2 All right, well now we do want to get your headlines at 7:30 including some frightening moments in a southern California neighborhood last night when a small plane went down in a residential area. The crash was captured on this doorbell camera a few blocks away. It happened near the \uc2 City of El Cajon\uc2 , northeast of San Diego. Officials say they don`t know where the plane was coming from or how many people were on board. Firefighters didn`t find any survivors. Thankfully, no one on the ground was injured in that crash.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: New developments in the case of that truck driver who was sentenced to 110 years in prison after a deadly crash in Colorado. A judge has now set a new hearing day to reconsider that sentence. In October, Rogel Aguilera-Mederos was found guilty of 27 charges stemming from the 2019 crash that killed four people. Mandatory sentencing guidelines led to the initial 110 year sentence that sparked protests nationwide. Yesterday, the district attorney announced that the state will now seek a sentence between 25 and 30 years.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: And get this, a second time capsule has been found where a statue of Robert E. Lee once stood in Richmond, Virginia. Workers made the discovery yesterday while dismantling the pedestal. It`s believed the box was placed in the foundation all the way back in 1887. There was another time capsule that was found there earlier this month, you might remember. Historian suspect the box will contain dozens of artifacts, including Confederate memorabilia, and possibly a rare photo of President Abraham Lincoln. It`ll be opened later this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I`m obsessed with that story by the way. If there`s a photo of Abraham Lincoln, incredible.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: For sure.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes. All right, well, in other news now multiple cruise ships are back in the U.S. this morning after experiencing COVID outbreaks and being turned away from ports in the Caribbean and Mexico. Another unwelcome setback for the struggling industry, NBC`s Kerry Sanders is at Port Everglades in Fort Lauderdale with the very latest on this story. Kerry, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, good morning. Thankfully, this is nothing like the spring of 2020 when we saw coronavirus, teaming on ships, in some cases trapping passengers on those ships for weeks. But these new outbreaks are troubling because the concern is that while they appear to be asymptomatic, it`s happening at a time when the industry is making a strong return, carrying more than 4 million passengers worldwide on voyages that have been mostly COVID free.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): The cruise ship industry is dealing with a COVID mess once again after multiple ships returned to U.S. ports with outbreaks. the Koningsdam Holland America ship now back in San Diego after getting turned away from Puerto Vallarta, Mexico, because an undisclosed number of crew members tested positive even though the company says they were fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They kind of let us know, hey, look, there`s a -- we let the authorities know that. There`s a -- some people in the crew they`re sick.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Lynn Hoffman (ph) was on board.\par \par \uc2 LYNN HOFFMAN (ph), KONINGSDAM HOLLAND AMERICA SHIP PASSENGER: Everybody on the ship wears, you know, masks, again, this might be one of the safest places because everybody`s vaccinated. We were very careful.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Now back in Florida, the Carnival Freedom was turned away from Bonaire, Aruba after an undisclosed number on board tested positive.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PETERSON (ph), CARNIVAL FREEDOM PASSENGER: I feel like I was just in a petri dish waiting to get COVID.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Ashley Peterson a passenger on the eight day cruise says it was a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 PETERSON (ph): I didn`t feel safe at all. I basically spent most of the time during the day like locked in my cabin.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): \uc2 Royal Caribbean\uc2  International`s Odyssey of the Seas only spent one day at sea before turning back to Fort Lauderdale after a passenger tested positive. Four days later, the same ship was turned away from Curacao and Aruba after more than 50 people came down with COVID. These outbreaks happening despite enhanced COVID safety protocols. All three cruise lines requiring adult passengers to show proof of vaccination and a negative COVID test while encouraging mask use indoors.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I had no concerns at all. The cleanliness standards were really good. I think they responded really quickly to containing everything.\par \par \uc2 [07:35:08]\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Not the holiday season the cruise ship industry was hoping for as they anticipate a busy 2022.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And Kerry plenty of people may be considering canceling an upcoming cruise in light of these latest outbreaks. So what kind of recourse do they have to try to get a refund or at least to reschedule or delay their trips?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Yes, well there`s the answer into your question in that in some cases, the cruise lines will give you a refund, you can reach out to them and tell them you want to cancel, get a refund. But other cruise lines will give you a credit which means that you can get the credit and use it for a future cruise. And right now since people are preparing to go on cruises for the New Year, the cruise lines are warning that itineraries may ship because as we saw some ports of call are now turning ships away.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, another industry that has been hit hard by COVID. All right, Kerry Sanders, thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 And coming up Today exclusive, the first interview with the man behind the deep fake viral videos that appear to show Tom Cruise doing everyday thing.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I`m sure you`ve seen him. He spoke to me about the rapidly advancing technology and the dangers it could open up in the wrong hands, that`s coming up right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:40:41]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: All right, you`ve got to see this we are back with In-Depth Today. And a today exclusive on what they call deep fakes, digitally manipulated videos, images, and audio that are becoming increasingly common, and the fake part can be nearly impossible to detect.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now the man behind the best known deep fake is speaking out for the first time in a conversation with our own Jacob, about how and why he has fooled so many and the threat that technology could pose what a great conversation.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Honestly, you guys this is truly mind blowing. And I think it`s so mind blowing because you all at home have probably seen these TikTok videos that looked like Tom Cruise showing us a day in his life. He`s doing things like playing the guitar, trying on clothes, even eating dinner. They`re pretty captivating. Well, this morning, the guy behind that account, that account that has fooled hundreds of millions of people is opening up about the sensation he created. And what some worry that in the wrong hands could have disastrous consequences.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MILES FISHER, AMERICAN ACTOR: What`s up TikTok?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): All year long, deep Tom Cruise has been posting videos showing what appears to be a different side of the Oscar nominated actor.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: I think there`s bubblegum inside this.\par \par \uc2 People are surprised that I`m a big Dave Matthews guy.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): But believe it or not, that`s most definitely not Tom Cruise. Nor is this with me in our L.A. bureau.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: This is serious breaking news. But a little behind the scenes a little flavor. I`m with one of the most legitimate correspondents on earth. Now if you`re interviewing me, Jacob --\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): Yes?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: You face it. Hold on just sec, there`s a big fire. We`re safe.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: You are, deep fake Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: That`s me. That`s me.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): Miles Fisher may have an uncanny resemblance to the superstar but using advanced technology, what he`s created is far more than an extraordinary impersonation.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Have you guys created the best deep fake that has ever been made?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: I think we have created the first deep fake that`s so realistic, that a large majority of people have seen.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): Not only seen but fooled by including at least one of the world`s biggest celebrities.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: This is the one that Justin Bieber thought was real.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): Fisher says looking and sounding like the leading man made it difficult to chart his own path as an actor. So a year ago, he decided to lean into his stumbling into a role with repercussions far beyond Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: As I find myself the unofficial face of this deep fake movement. It`s important to learn, and I`m fascinated by this. This is the bleeding edge of technology.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): It started when Fisher contacted Belgian visual effects wizard Chris Ume. They began creating viral videos for fun, and they`re only getting better.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Still got it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS UME, BELGIAN VISUAL EFFECTS: Above five days, maximum six days, I could turn around something like this.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): So you`ve gone from weeks to days, in a matter of a year. This technology is advancing rapidly.\par \par \uc2 UME: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): For his part, the real Tom Cruise hasn`t asked to do to stop and they haven`t monetized the TikTok account. Cruise didn`t respond to a request for comment from NBC News. But their work so successful Fisher and Ume`s company Metaphysic is now in business together, looking to convince others it`s worth paying for this technology.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: How can we use this technology by creating kind of identity rights? Let`s say Tom Cruise gave us the consent for this likeness where we could move beyond just small parody clips. Everybody gets paid for that intellectual property.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (on camera): That does raise ethical questions about where you fit in all this.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: The thesis of this company that Chris started begins with ethics.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): They say they`ll only take on projects they consider ethical, but some were relieving companies and individuals to make that decision could be a dangerous precedent. Congress has held hearings on deep fakes and AI and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  tells NBC News they`re following the rapidly developing technology closely, saying we`ll continue to investigate any violations of federal law and actors that may use them for nefarious acts.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Should we understand it as a potential threat to our democracy?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: I think the technology is morally neutral. As it develops, the positive output will so far outweigh the negative nefarious uses.\par \par \uc2 If you like what you`re seeing. Just wait until what`s coming next.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:45:01]\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: And there is more coming next. Ume, Fisher`s partner says that it is Fisher`s talent and he is so talented combined with that AI that makes this possible. But the technology is quickly catching up. They`re both well aware that they are in uncharted territory. And at least one government you guys have reached out to Chris Ume to learn more about this technology, which raises so many questions.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It does. And it`s just mind blowing to see how similar it is. Is there a way to know when this is fake? And to tell when it`s fake from reality?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: The short answer is not really. There are companies that are building tools to try to detect this technology. But there are even people who knew Tom Cruise, have met Tom Cruise who reached out to me after I shared that video on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  after they posted on TikTok and said, Tom is a great guy, isn`t he?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Oh my god.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It was the first thing I asked you when I saw you this week because I had seen that online. This guy does it for entertainment, but the risk is if someone does it to compromise some professional actor or politician. And you guys will relate to this. There were hearings, Adam Schiff held hearings on the Hill about this in Washington D.C. about the effect potentially on election interference, on our democracy, you know, as an entity, what could possibly happen if this gets into the wrong hands? And I don`t think anybody has the answer to that quite yet.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes. And Ume is such a good actor, he could impersonate Tom Cruise without the deep fake but.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: It`s really extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Mind blowing.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes. Great interview, Jacob.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Thank you guys.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That`s something else. I`m still trying to get over that video there. We want to check in with \uc2 Jeff Ranieri\uc2  now. It is the real thing. He has the latest forecast for us. Jeff, good morning.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The real \uc2 Jeff Ranieri\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JEFF RANIERI\uc2 , NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: It is. I know my viewers out at NBC Bay Area in the San Francisco Bay are going, is that the real \uc2 Jeff Ranieri\uc2 ? Yes, I`m out here at 30 Rock filling in for the wonderful Al Roker. So let`s go ahead and get you that forecast and the heat is on from Texas over to the southeast