B: See, there`s back.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: So, don`t you be afraid because (INAUDIBLE) and mustard sparkling wine. So, I`m starting off with this first one. This beautiful one you`ve gotten your first class is called Cremant de Limoux from Gerard Bertrand. So, you`re going to get your French on and you`re Italian on as we speak about the next few wines.\par \par \uc2 And this is from the south of France. This is not champagne. This is from the south of France from a place called Limoux. And Cremant means a style of sparkling wine. So, and actually, this is where sparkling wine was invented, the traditional method down in Limoux.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Now --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: So, it`s - -isn`t that delicious?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s -- yes.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: Do you like that?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s like -- Is this the expensive one because it tastes good?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: Cheers. This is -- no, this is under $20.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, good.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yummy.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: So, this is -- yes, it`s so delicious because it has that lovely complexity and creaminess. But it`s so really wildly affordable for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And what about the splurge?\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: OK. So, the splurge now, I might surprise you I`m actually splurging with Prosecco. So, now, most people think Prosecco is this, you know, light and lively and an inexpensive drink, and it is, primarily. But there`s some absolutely glorious Proseccos, and this being one of them from the hill of Cartizze. So, I want you to say with me, Valdobbiadene di Cartizze.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: Valdobbiadene di Cartizze. And this is the \uc2 Villa Sandi\uc2 , it`s really beautiful. It`s about $45.00 and it`s lovely. So --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK, what pairs with these, Leslie?\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: So, you know, sparkling wine goes with just about everything. But ham is a really great -- if you`ve got -- if you`re serving a ham, sparkling wine is one of the best things to pair with ham. It also goes -- of course, yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK, that`s why probably the mustard goes well with it.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Because mustard and ham are wonderful combination.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: That`s right. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: All right, nobody really thinks rose and holiday seasons.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But you disagree.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: I disagree. It`s rose all year and rose is the perfect. I love this one. This is called Source of Joy. And if you look at the bottle, it`s just such a pretty bottle to serve during the holiday. Source of Joy, right?\par \par \uc2 And Rose is really ideal for food -- be all kinds of food, again, talking about ham, turkey, anything. Because it`s made with red grapes but in a white wine style. So, this is again from the south of France, a beautiful wine that just has this lovely fruitiness, still got some -- you know substance to it because it`s made with red grapes.\par \par \uc2 But again, ham. This is perfect with ham and mustard -- slathered mustard, yes, a perfect with appetizers. So, take a sip of (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The price is right (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, what`s the price of this, baby?\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: Again, under $20 for this one. So --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Good.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We just have about a minute, Leslie, to kind of raise through this. So, let me show --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Let`s move out to Zinfandel.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: OK, oh, gosh. OK. So, we got this beautiful Zinfandel, Blue Quail from Mendocino County, Northern California. Absolutely gorgeous with roast beef if you`re doing kind of beef wellington.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got a lovely chardonnay right here. This is now again with mustard. This is from Sokol Blosser in Dundee Hills of Oregon. And it is a splurge, about $40 but worth it. I call it the goldilocks of chardonnay, not too -- not too full, not too light, it`s just right.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Great.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: And then, we`ve got two beautiful gift wines for you guys. So, those are food wines, those go with everything on the table. These last two are really to impress.\par \par \uc2 So, I have this lovely Rioja. And if you want to impress for less, Rioja is the way to go.\par \par \uc2 [10:35:02]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, OK.\par \par \uc2 It`s a region in Spain. And this is from Ramon Bilbao. This is their limited edition. It`s $25. It tastes like a drinks, you know, twice as much.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s good. Yes, yummy.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: Isn`t that yummy? It`s delicious.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, delicious.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: Rioja, best value. And finally, a little splurge from Sonoma County here, where I live, where I`m calling you from this morning. And it is a beautiful Pinot Noir from Twomey vineyards.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, Twomey vineyards.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: From the Russian River Valley.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I love a Pinot.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: And that`s a part of the Silver Oak family of wine. So, very famous.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh my God, that`s so good.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: And this is a splurge --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: This is delicious.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: It`s like -- it`s like drinking silk, isn`t it? It`s like drinking silk.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Leslie, thank you so much. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SBROCCO: Cheers, ladies. Cheers.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Thank you, Leslie.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: To check out these wines, head to today.com/shop.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Coming up next, I get to go to a very special place helping women feel good, look good, and get back on their feet after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Do you feel good?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK, so, there are more remedies. You all are just really helping, evidently a marshmallow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`m concerned. I`m concerned about that the next set of remedies we`re going to give her for like an upset stomach. She just -- she already eaten mustard, a whole big glob of honey, and now you`re eating marshmallows. OK.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We`ll see. We`ll see. You`re right. We might need those remedies later.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`ll keep you posted. OK, by the way, today, happens to be National Philanthropy Day, the perfect reason to kick-off our gift of gratitude series. There`s no better way to show you we`re thankful for what you have done by serving those who are less fortunate.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And recently, I took a trip to an incredible organization here in New York City. It`s called Bottomless Closet. And I got to see firsthand what they`re doing to uplift women and set them up for success and financial independents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAGER (voice-over): When you look good, you feel good. But at Bottomless Closet in New York City, that`s just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 HAGER (on camera): That`s pretty.\par \par \uc2 MILDRED MARTINEZ, CLIENT, BOTTOMLESS CLOSET: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER (voice-over): Behind every smart blazer and perfectly matched blouse is a woman ready to strut toward success.\par \par \uc2 MIRA GRAETZ-BALL, VOLUNTEER, BOTTOMLESS CLOSET: Our clients have, you know, have challenges. When they come in here, they put those challenges somewhere else on a shelf and they just look forward.\par \par \uc2 [10:40:06]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Bottomless Closet serve disadvantaged women re-entering the workforce by providing them with resources to ace interviews, regain employment, and successfully transition into new jobs.\par \par \uc2 From career workshops to resume helping wardrobe booths. The organization has helped over 46,000 women get on the road toward financial independents.\par \par \uc2 HAGER (on camera): There must be something really meaningful about meeting somebody, seeing them get dressed for an interview, walking them through the resume process, and the interview process.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And then hearing that they got that job.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: And the first thing they do is ring the bell.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I got the -- I got the job. I got hired.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: And when they ring that bell, the entire place just erupts and applause. You know that they`ve got that job, the staff, the other clients, the volunteers, we are all pulling in the same direction. It`s so important for women to help women. We understand the need to build confidence.\par \par \uc2 HAGER (voice-over): Women were disproportionately hit by pandemic-related job losses in 2020, accounting for more than half of the 10 million jobs that were lost. This made Bottomless Closet`s mission more important than ever.\par \par \uc2 One of their recent clients is 57-year-old Mildred Martinez. After working for 16 years in broadcast media sales, Mildred was laid off in August of last year.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: (on camera): When you found out that they were laying you off, how did it -- how did it feel?\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: I was numb, and I realized, for the first time in my life, I don`t have a job. And so, there was panic and fear. And a lot of crying, anger, and what I`m going to do now?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: (voice-over): The job search landscapes change since Mildred was last looking for full-time work. So, with the whole new set of changes ever, Mildred turn to Bottomless Closet.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: I knew about Bottomless Closet from a few years ago. I actually used to bring up my work outfits that I didn`t use. I never envisioned myself as being a client. And I think it wasn`t until I realized that I was one of those women that was considered underemployed. I had to always work a second job to supplement my income.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Attending Bottomless Closet`s virtual workshops has given Mildred a new sense of direction as she seeks to switch industries and re-enter the workforce. So, today, Mira and I are helping Mildred with her elevator pitch.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: Break it down into three pieces: the present, past, future. All right, where am I now? Right now, I want a job in this. Past, what have I done? What are the skills that I have? Just think about that holistically in two sentences. And then third: the future, what are you going to do for that organization?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Do you want to try?\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Well, right now I`m currently unemployed. And I`m actually -- oh, I shouldn`t say that.\par \par \uc2 HAGER (on camera): Oh, what should she say?\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: Right now, I`m looking for a job in --\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: OK.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: That`s such a good little point that I would never think of.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: How does this is -- so, I`m looking to re-enter into the field of facilities manager.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Perfect.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: OK.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: The next piece is the past. What are skills that you`ve acquired?\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: The most important thing that I bring is my understanding of relationships matter.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: Perfect.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I think that`s great.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: That`s great. And then the future.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Future.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: What are you going to do for them?\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: I`m going to bring to your team someone who is easy to work with, someone who`s going to pitch in when things get tight, most importantly, you know, is to leave someone with a feeling that they`re being heard, and taken care of.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: Excellent.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Now that Mildred has elevate her pitch down, next, came the fun parts, putting together an outfit to get her prepared for her next opportunity.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Or maybe just the jacket.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, that looks nice.\par \par \uc2 GRAETZ-BALL: So, let`s try on?\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: OK.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You look great. Do you feel good?\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: I do.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Do you feel more hopeful?\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: I do. I have a lot more confidence. And I feel like, you know what? My door hasn`t opened yet, but it will.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, I love Mildred, and we spoke to her last night. She`s been on a couple of interviews, multiple interviews. She`s hopeful for some opportunities, but she is brilliant and positive.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Can I tell you something? Someone is going to hire Mildred.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, right now.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She -- right now. That was a great, great piece. Tomorrow, we continue our gift of gratitude series with a story about a life-saving friendship.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, I can`t wait to see that. Coming up next, Bunny and Tim. OK, they`ve been hard at work. They`re creating their late landscapes. Evidently, they`re inspired by us.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re going to reveal their New Orleans and Texas tables. Oh, and talk to the filmmaker behind the new series, it`s called Set. You do not want to miss it. Coming up after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:45:00]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK. What would you say if I told you every year, dozens of competitors flocked to the orange county fair in California to compete in the high stakes world of table setting?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK. I would say that sounds like the plot of a movie.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`re right. This cut-throat competition is real. It serves as the backdrop of our November documentary of the month club pick, it`s called Set! The competition features a colorful cast of characters, and let`s just say the knives are out, literally. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BONNIE OVERMAN, COMPETATOR, SETTING THE TABLE: Pete (PH) and I would compare to brain surgery just because the seriousness we put into it. This is my Hall of Fame I guess you would call it. This table here was a vineyard theme. This was my dinner at the White House. Breakfast at Tiffany`s. Phantom of the Opera. Growing a baby takes nine months. This takes six months, so it`s almost like producing a baby, and it`s very annoying when it -- you have your baby and the judges don`t like your baby.\par \par \uc2 TIM WYCKOFF, COMPETATOR, SETTING THE TABLE: Remember, my first table setting still to this day, I did a Lion King Lunch Box social and I put lunch boxes as the plate and I actually got third place my first year. And then I was hooked. Once you got a taste of it, it just fuels the fire of wanting more ribbons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: More ribbons. With us now, the filmmakers, Scott Gawlik along with competitors Bonnie Overman and Tim Wyckoff. OK. I had no idea this was so fiercely competitive, people. I didn`t know it`s like the WWE of table setting. People go down.\par \par \uc2 OVERMAN: We want to win.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Bonnie, have you always been that competitive?\par \par \uc2 OVERMAN: Yes, deep down we really want to win. I mean, I think all of us that participate produce tables that we think are the best in show and hope the judges think so too.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Tim, we`re very competitive.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, fiercely.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But we hold nothing to y`all. How did you get involved with this?\par \par \uc2 WYCKOFF: My mom actually entered art into the fair locally when I was a child.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK.\par \par \uc2 WYCKOFF: And so, I entered art. So, I started doing things at like 7, 8, 9 years old. And then, I saw that the table settings in the youth department got the big ribbon, and I wanted a big ribbon because all I got was the little single one. I didn`t care about that, I wanted a big ribbon. So, I started to do that. So, I`ve been doing it for over 30 years.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Scott, I mean, this is brilliant, by the way. We -- Jenna, this is the first I`ve ever heard of this. I don`t know if this is something everybody knows and we`re just learning about, but how did you decide this would be a perfect subject for a documentary?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GAWLIK, FILMMAKER: Well, I stumbled on to it by accident. I`d went to the orange county fair in Southern California, it was really hot day, 100- degree weather, was a bit over serve on the cold, cold beers, went to the air-condition room, and there`s a bunch of tables. I didn`t really think much of it. Right? Sat my beer down, alarm started going off. People kind a run over, they`re like this is the competitive table setting contest. What are you doing? OK. I`m picked up the beer and looked around and -- I noticed that the tables had ribbons, like first place, no place and I just started really having strong opinions about tables. Well, the whole -- I think this one should have won, not this one. And this is coming from a guy who eats off paper plates, right?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GAWLIK: So, like. For it have this reaction for me was huge. Kind of looked around the room. Everyone was having the same reaction like, oh, I have a feeling that there`s a lot layers to this onion to peel off.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, you know what? I think what you guys do (INAUDIBLE) going to your tables and just getting them all prepped, and you guys can head that way. And then, we`re going to just sit there for a second, just one more second. But how --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But Scott, it usually takes six months.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And they have like tape measures. They are measuring how far the fork is from the whatever .\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And we heard that one person lost because they didn`t put the dessert spoon or whatever.\par \par \uc2 GAWLIK: Yes. But, what`s even more interesting is the way they prepare. Some of people go into sensory deprivation chambers. One of the women in the movie and that`s where she gets inspiration, so she`s not clouded by exterior thoughts. One of the ladies in the film, she doesn`t tear her tables down, she keeps 15 tables up in her -- like in her house at all times. The husband, the family, everyone is just up in arms. It`s just constant nonsense.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GAWLIK: Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: By the way, this is serious business and these two tables, we should say, that these guys were making their tables with 100 bucks in 45 minutes. Not -- no money.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And they are used to six months.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. No money to time. But you create it. So, we`ve got --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Wow, Tim.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Texas and New Orleans. So, we`ll start with Texas?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Tim, this is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 WYCKOFF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Tell us what you did.\par \par \uc2 WYCKOFF: So, I actually locked up what I would think of important places in Texas, things that would drive interest. So, the jukebox represents the continental where a lot of people had their music careers started. The boots, of course, you can`t go without with Texas and the yellow roses of Texas. I also tried to do indigenous flowers if I could of Texas.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, I see some of the wildflowers that we see grow every spring.\par \par \uc2 WYCKOFF: That`s what I tried to do. And then, for the wagon wheel actually represents two places.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, we love this. This is so beautiful.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Bonnie, let`s take us to New Orleans, girl. What you got cooking?\par \par \uc2 OVERMAN: Well, one of my favorite places in New Orleans is the Garden District.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Of course.\par \par \uc2 OVERMAN: So, this is a Mardi Gras Dinner in the Garden District. Kind of an elegant feel, so I tried to include a lot of icons such as the Mardi Gras Mask, my version of the King Cake.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I like your version.\par \par \uc2 OVERMAN: Lanterns. And then, of course, with the Garden District, beautiful, lush foliage and flowers.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Purple, green and gold. Those are the colors of Mardi Gras and that`s what you have on display here. Wow.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You both did a beautiful job.\par \par \uc2 WYCKOFF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OVERMAN: Thank you so much. You`ve got best in show. That`s the one we came here for.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I`m going with Texas and Tim.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right guys. Set, by the way, streaming now on discovery plus. Please check it out.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And we`ll be back after this. Thanks guys.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We have one last remedy. It`s actually right here. Hoda didn`t think I was going to do it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Its pickles and sour cream.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Hoda didn`t think I was going to do. But you know what, Hoda thinks a kid is actually pranking me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I think a kid at home is going is she going to eat it.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I`m going to eat it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: This is for her throat. Remember? Oh, God. Tomorrow Maria Shriver`s stop by.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, we can`t wait for that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020211116ehbf00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for November 4, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Gabe Gutierrez, Miguel Almaguer, Kristen Welker, Ron  Allen \par \uc2 3,958 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Tonight, the Biden administration sweeping new vaccine mandate deadline for 84 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): Workers at private companies with 100 or more employees now required to get fully vaccinated by January 4th or face weekly testing. The backlash, GOP-led states mounting legal challenges. Could they block it?\par \par \uc2 Just in, reports of a shooting at a resort popular with American tourists. What we`re learning.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the election fallout growing pressure on Democrats to pass President Biden spending plans after disappointing losses. Could there finally be a vote this week?\par \par \uc2 Controversy in the trial of three White Georgia men charged with killing Ahmaud Arbery, a Black man. The jury nearly all white, the judge saying it appears to be intentional discrimination. Why he won`t intervene.\par \par \uc2 The season`s first cold snap. 28 million under freeze alerts from Texas to New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Our journey across America to Nashville. The mission to feed hungry families. Our visit to a grocery store created by country music legend Brad Paisley and wife Kimberly Williams-Paisley where the food is free.\par \par \uc2 And inside the mother church of country music. My own chance to play on stage with superstar Michael Ray.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt Across America, reporting tonight from Nashville.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. From one of the most legendary stages in all of music, the Ryman Auditorium here in Nashville. Nashville is stopped number four and our week-long journey across America. We`re going to dive into the rich history of this place and have much more from Nashville shortly.\par \par \uc2 But our top story is one that will soon impact two-thirds of the American workforce. New federal regulations putting the onus and all but the smallest employers to see that their workers are fully vaccinated against COVID-19 or that they are tested weekly. Companies now given two months to comply or face heavy fines.\par \par \uc2 The regulations have follow on to an executive order issued by President Biden in September. The White House tonight vowing to do everything it can to maximize compliance with the mandate. Gabe Gutierrez has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): After months of vocal opposition yet remarkable compliance, the new COVID vaccine mandate covers a staggering 84 million people, two-thirds of the U.S. workforce. It requires companies with 100 or more employees to ensure they`re either fully vaccinated against COVID by January 4th or tested weekly.\par \par \uc2 The order does not require employers to pay for the test though union agreements might require some employers to foot the bill.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH, U.S. LABOR SECRETARY: The idea behind this is not to spend hours and hours and a lot of public money in court. The intent behind this is to get the health and safety of workers in the workplace put that front and center.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: U.S. Labor Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  says the rule will be administered by OSHA, the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2 . Fines could reach nearly $14,000 per violation.\par \par \uc2 How exactly do you plan to enforce this?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: Yes. OSHA has done this work for 50 years, and employers know the way that the -- to work with OSHA on this thing.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Starting December 5th, covered employers will also be required to give workers paid time off to get vaccinated, as well as sick leave to recover from side effects. Today, during a Senate hearing, CDC Director \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2  and \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  defended the Biden administration`s COVID response.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISOR TO THE PRESIDENT: We know that mandates work. If you look at for example, the percentage of people in \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  or in the Houston Medical Association, or another organizations that have mandated, it works 99-plus percent.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: But mandates remain a flashpoint across the country. From Los Angeles today --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No one should lose their job over a vaccine mandate.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: To Lynchburg, Virginia this week were dozens of workers walked off the job.\par \par \uc2 WES GARDNER, BMX TECHNOLOGIES: We like to have the company actually sit down and talk and come to the table with clear, open, and honest communication.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Tonight, some states are planning to sue the Biden administration over this latest mandate after already doing so over a previous requirement for federal contractors.\par \par \uc2 In Houston, \uc2 Brian Fielkow\uc2  is a CEO of a supply chain logistics company who`s concerned about employees leaving in a tight labor market.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN FIELKOW CEO, JETCO DELIVERY: I have real mixed emotions because on the one hand, you know, I know that the vaccine is our one way ticket out of the pandemic. And on the other, there`s a certain reality of managing our employee bases and running our companies and those two may be on a bit of a collision course. We`re going to find out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Gabe, what about states like Tennessee where I am have laws or executive orders banning vaccine mandates?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:06]\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): Yes, Lester, the Biden administration says that because this new rule is under OSHA, it supersedes any local restrictions. Of course, opponents will no doubt take this to court. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Gabe Gutierrez in New York tonight, thank you. We`ll turn to the breaking news now out of Cancun, Mexico, reports of an active shooter at a beach resort popular with American tourists. Let`s get right to Miguel Alma gear. Miguel, what do we know?\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Lester, good evening. We`re getting new details about this developing situation. Here`s what we know right now. The Attorney General in Mexico says there was an altercation between several drug dealers on a beach when shots were fired near the Hyatt Ziva Riviera Cancun Resort.\par \par \uc2 A local news outlet reports at least eight armed gunman then enter the hotel carrying long guns. The commotion sent tourists scrambling. Images coming into NBC News shows some taking cover in the resort`s corridor, and then later guests gathering in the hotel lobby.\par \par \uc2 Mexico`s Secretary of Public Security says two people died, both believed to be drug dealers. At this hour, it appears no tourists were injured and the gunfire thankfully, Lester, seems to be over. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Frightening though. All right, Miguel, thank you. Now, to Washington in the growing drama among Democrats after those major election victories for Republicans. Tonight, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  pushing to pass the President`s massive spending plans suggesting Democrats` failure to act led to those big losses. Kristen Welker now with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): After the dismal results for Democrats on election day, new urgency tonight on Capitol Hill. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  saying she wants a vote on President Biden`s two multi-trillion-dollar spending plans by the end of this week.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): We`re going to pass both bills. But in order to do so, we have to have votes for both bills. And that`s where we are.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Pelosi pressed by NBC, \uc2 Garrett Haake\uc2  on whether Democratic inaction was to blame for the election day debacle.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE\uc2 , NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Do you think Democrats were penalized for having not gotten these things done?\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: There`s no question. If we -- the more results we can produce in a way that is people understand in their lives, the better it is.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It comes after Republicans won stunning victories in Virginia, including for Governor and Lieutenant Governor. And in deep-blue New Jersey, the AP now projecting another surprising upset. The powerful Democratic State Senate President --\par \par \uc2 EDWARD DURR, NEW JERSEY DEMOCRATIC STATE SENATOR: My name is Edward Durr. I`m running for New Jersey State Senate.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Ousted by a little-known Republican truck driver for a furniture store who shot his campaign videos on his phone. Moderate Democratic Senator Joe Manchin arguing the Republican victories are a sign voters don`t want to rush into more massive spending.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): People are concerned. They`re concerned about inflation. They`re concerned about the debt. Now, we don`t talk about it, but they are concerned.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But progressives say the takeaway is just the opposite, that voters want them to go big. And now, they`re backing Pelosi last-minute decision to put four weeks of paid family leave back into the President`s social spending plan despite the fact that Manchin has signaled opposition.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL\uc2  (D-WA): Millions of women across the country are outraged that one man would say we`re not going to do paid leave.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But tonight, top Republicans argue the election was a rebuke of those Democratic proposals.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO\uc2  (R-FL): It`s the craziest thing I`ve ever seen. It`s -- these people are governing like to have a mandate to radically remake the country. It`s built back socialism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER (on camera): Tonight, top House Democrats are scrambling to secure the votes to pass both bills, but some House moderates remain hesitant. And now, we`ve learned President Biden is making phone calls to try to win over those Democrats. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker at the White House, thanks.\par \par \uc2 There is breaking news in the Justice Department filing a lawsuit against Texas over its new voting law. The law signed by Republican Governor Greg Abbott in September limits dropboxes and requires voter ID for absentee ballots among other provisions. The suit alleges the law violates protections for civil rights and voting access. Texas has yet to respond.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, controversy on the eve of testimony in the murder trial of three men accused of killing Ahmaud Arbery, the young Black man who prosecutors say was jogging when he was shot and killed by the men. Just one of the jurors who will decide the case is Black. Ron Allen is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Ahmaud Arbery.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Ahmaud Arbery.\par \par \uc2 RON ALLEN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, passion running high in the community where Ahmaud Arbery was shot and killed almost two years ago. Anger only one Black juror was selected to decide a case that Arbery`s family supporters have called a modern-day lynching.\par \par \uc2 WANDA COOPER-JONES, MOTHER OF Ahmaud Arbery. That`s very disturbing to find out that we had one African American versus 11 Whites.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Do you still think you`ll get justice?\par \par \uc2 COOPER-JONES: I do think with the evidence that the state has, that we will get justice for Ahmaud.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Gregory McMichael, his son Travis, and a neighbor William Roddie Bryan faced nine counts each including murder, aggravated assault and attempted kidnapping, accused of chasing, shooting, and killing Arbery. They`ve all pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 Now, after questioning hundreds of potential jurors in a county that`s about 26 percent Black, 11 White, and one Black jurors selected. The defense using its legal challenges to eliminate 11 potential Black jurors from the jury pool on the final day.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:24]\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHY WALMSLEY, JUDGE, GEORGIA EASTERN JUDICIAL CIRCUIT SUPERIOR COURT: This court has found that there appears to be intentional discrimination.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: He added, Georgia law prevents him from taking action because the attorneys gave reasons other than race for the dismissals, like insisting that prospective jurors could not be impartial.\par \par \uc2 TALI FARHADIAN WEINSTEIN, NBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: That`s really what the court has to decide, are those race-neutral explanations that were offered, are they satisfactory? And in this case, the judge said that he did find them to be satisfactory.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Now, prosecutors expected to cite Arbery`s race as a significant factor in his death. And the defendants are expected to say they are attempting a citizen`s arrest of a robbery suspect acting in self-defense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALLEN (on camera): The judge has told jurors the trial should last two to three weeks with Arbery supporters and advocates who see this as a test for racial justice in America watching closely every day. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Ron Allen, thank you. Millions of Americans are in for their first freeze of the season. 28 million under freeze alerts from Texas all the way up through New Jersey including here in Nashville. Across the Southeast and Mid-Atlantic tomorrow, temperatures are expected to be 10 to 15 degrees below average.\par \par \uc2 In just 60 seconds, we`ll look at the impact of the pandemic on schools here in Nashville, and the announcement by superstar Dwayne Johnson about guns on movie sets.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Here in Nashville, the city and its schools like so many in America are still struggling with the COVID pandemic. Although younger children became eligible for the vaccine, this week, there is resistance here as elsewhere as many kids and teachers are having trouble in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): Nearly two years into the pandemic and school districts across the nation are still struggling with mask mandates, pandemic learning loss, and staffing shortages.\par \par \uc2 DR. ADRIENNE BATTLE, DIRECTOR OF SCHOOLS, NASHVILLE: Our parents have been tremendous partners to us.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Dr. Adrienne Battle is the director of schools here in Nashville, and she is now facing yet another hurdle in the fight against COVID in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 Vaccinations now available for elementary-aged kids. How does that affect you guys?\par \par \uc2 BATTLE: It affects us tremendously. We`ll be partnering with our local health department to provide opportunities for our students, our youngest learners to be vaccinated. It gives us an opportunity to really think what it`s going to take to keep our students safe and keeping the core focus where it needs to be. And that focus right now should be on learning for our students and their social emotional learning space.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tennessee`s vaccination rate is low compared to other states. Less than half the population here is fully vaccinated. So, hesitancy among parents to get their children vaccinated is a concern. Lauren Lowrey covers education for our affiliate WSMV.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN LOWREY, WSMV ANCHOR: The district is starting to roll out these events. It`d be interesting to see how many parents buy into it, right? The Early polling is showing that less than 30 percent of people are actually going to get their kids vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Earlier this year, Nashville was a litmus test for the nation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We were one of the first school systems in the country to go back to school in early August.\par \par \uc2 What would your superpowers be?\par \par \uc2 Jamie Walensky wasn`t sure what to think when her daughter`s iris and Eloise stepped back into their classrooms.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When I first sent the girls back to school, I felt like I was sending them out into the wild, wild west.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Jamie and the girls adjusted but --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I do feel like they`re starting a little bit behind. But I will tell you that I feel like everyone is. The whole country is going to see this generation have to adapt to learning and education in different ways.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: More than 97 percent of educators reported some learning loss over the past year when compared to the previous year. At the end of the 2020-21 school year, students are on average four months behind in reading and five months behind in math.\par \par \uc2 Have you been able to chart quantify the learning loss as a result of all this?\par \par \uc2 [19:15:14]\par \par \uc2 BATTLE: What we`ve been able to identify is that their progress was not what we would like to see year over year.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Mary Holden has been teaching English for 19 years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m more concerned with the loss in social emotional areas. The school started and we`re seeing a lot of really immature behaviors.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Just one more hurdle in an already tense environment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This year has turned out to be the most challenging of my career. We are being stretched in ways that I did not anticipate. Everyone is expecting things to be normal. And it`s not normal. We actually need a lot more support.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: What are you doing for teachers who feel that they`re being stretched too thin?\par \par \uc2 BATTLE: We`re layering in additional social emotional learning for our adults, not just our students, but our adults addressing their mental health needs as well.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Despite it all, Mrs. Holden still loves teaching.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When you are in your classroom, and your doors closed, and you`re with your students, that`s the highlight of my day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): All right, one of the Hollywood`s biggest stars is making a big change in the wake of that deadly shooting on the set of Rust, Dwayne Johnson pledging to ban real guns from his sets after the death of cinematographer Halyna Hutchins. Johnson says he will use only rubber guns now and use special effects to simulate gunshots in post-production.\par \par \uc2 Up next for us, the country music stars on a vital and innovative mission to feed the people of Nashville.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back in Nashville now, a booming city, yet as we`ve seen elsewhere, some are left behind including the food insecure. But thanks to a reckoning and their own family, two the city`s more famous residents have adopted an idea that is helping lift their greater Nashville neighbors.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): From the outside, it looks like any corner store with fresh vegetables, canned goods, and fresh pick apples. But if you look closely, something is missing, cash registers. Welcome to The Store, a Nashville nonprofit that seeks to feed the food insecure. Here, an estimated 15 percent of residents need help to put food on the table. The store`s two famous founders, country music star Brad Paisley and his actress wife Kimberly Williams-Paisley.\par \par \uc2 Can we talk about the idea behind this particular store, where it came from?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY WILLIAMS-PAISLEY, ACTRESS: Our kids were acting out a little bit and I said to Brad, we`ve got to get them into service.\par \par \uc2 BRAD PAISLEY, SINGER: She`s like, these spoiled brats are going to volunteer somewhere right now. They need to know that there are hungry people.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Brad and Kim took them to volunteer at Unity Shop, a free supermarket in California that sparked an idea. Did you understand how prevalent hunger and food insecurity was in your own community?\par \par \uc2 B. PAISLEY: I knew there was definitely need but it`s not until you open something like this that you really see it.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The store is different from a food pantry. Customers who can prove need are able to shop for the foods they choose from a variety of fresh healthy choices.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Let`s get a few cases of it.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Courtney Vrablik, The Store`s executive director, knows what it`s like to need help and the emotions that come with it.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY VRABLIK, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, THE STORE: What we want families to know is that they`re safe here. They`re valued here. That no one is going to question their food choices.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The store opened in March 2020. Four days later, the pandemic forced them to shut the door.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Its delivery day at The Store and we`re about to deliver.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Brad and Kim joined with volunteers to pick, pack, and deliver groceries, providing meals to nearly 800 households weekly, more than 1.5 million meals in 18 months.\par \par \uc2 B. PAISLEY: You know, people were in tears. It was really a rewarding thing in the middle of one of the most challenging times we`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The Store reopened to customers in July.\par \par \uc2 K. PAISLEY: So, our idea is to get people back on their feet on the road to self-sufficiency so they won`t need us beyond a year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): The couple hoping to see ideas like this take off in other cities in the country.\par \par \uc2 Up next, I get to take the stage where some of the biggest stars have perform here in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Welcome back to Nashville, everyone. Finally, tonight, many a young musician has traveled to Nashville with a few dollars in their pocket, a guitar in hand, and dreams of standing on this very stage at the Ryman Auditorium. After all, stars from Elvis, to Johnny Cash, to Duke Ellington played here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): Music is the soul of this city from Broadway to the back alleys and beyond.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s the mother church right there.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And if these walls could talk, they probably sing an anthem to music history.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA BRONNENBERG, CURATOR AND TOURS MANAGER, RYMAN AUDITORIUM MUSEUM: This building has been and continues to be the gathering place of the city.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The Ryman Auditorium was built in 1892 as a place of worship. Since then, it`s evolved into the mother church of country music, and much more.\par \par \uc2 BRONNENBERG: You name it. We`ve had it, comedy, gospel, blues, rock and roll.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Jazz greats and rock and roll legends performed here. Presidents attended events. And those who have taken this stage say there`s no other experience quite like it.\par \par \uc2 B. PAISLEY: 1999-2000 was first time I ever walked out on that stage and it`s -- you`re hit with the gravity of it.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 1943, its most famous actor arrived, the \uc2 Grand Ole Opry\uc2 , the show`s home until 1974. Stars like Dolly Parton and Charlie Pride made their Opry debuts right here on this stage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Johnny Cash.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Carried into living rooms, the Opry show became America`s longest- running radio program, helping cement Nashville`s reputation as Music City.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL RAY, SINGER: Where we`re standing as the most historical stage and country music.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: I met up with country music star Michael Ray.\par \par \uc2 Do you remember the first time that you played in this auditorium?\par \par \uc2 RAY: I do, yes. You never forget it. You feel that energy from the men and women that made this room what it was back in the day.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And then grabbing our instruments, I got to experience my own Ryman moment.\par \par \uc2 All right, man.\par \par \uc2 RAY: That was great, man.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): And Michael is right. Nothing like playing this stage. Big thank you to everyone here at the Ryman Auditorium. Tomorrow, our journey across America takes us to Phoenix where we`ll take an in-depth look at the opioid crisis, the changing face of the electorate, and we`ll catch up with the family of that baby who was handed over a fence in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 That`s NIGHTLY NEWS. Thank you for watching, everyone. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211105ehb400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press for October 17, 2021, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, John Podhoretz, Kimberly Atkins Stohr, Garrett Haake,  Anne Thompson \par \uc2 9,373 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [10:30:22]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST (voice-over): This Sunday, our struggling recovery.\par \par \uc2 NOEL HACEGABA, PORT OF LONG BEACH: Everything we`re seeing today was induced by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Higher prices, slower job creation and a supply chain backlog with no end in sight.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you ever seen a backlog like this ever?\par \par \uc2 HACEGABA: Never. We`ve never seen anything like this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And with workers feeling empowered, more than 180 union strikes already this year.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LAURSEN, JOHN DEERE WORKER: It`s a resurgence of the labor movement. You know, the cheap labor bubble is finally busted.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My guest this morning, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 . Plus, the battle over vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I guarantee you at least half the department is staying home come Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Companies and municipalities requiring vaccines, and the battle lines are drawn --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Vaccination or termination.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- while some Republican governors see a political advantage in opposing mandates.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS\uc2  (R-FL): Unfortunately, this has become about politicians wanting to control people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll talk to Arkansas Governor \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 . And God and politics. While most evangelicals are now aligning with former President Trump --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think God is using President Trump?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Absolutely. I think President Trump is a miracle.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- others remain wary of mixing religion with outright partisan politics.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP NORDSTROM, PASTOR: The bible belt is unbuckling.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Looking at the divide in the evangelical movement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me for insight and analysis are Amy Walter, editor-in-chief and publisher of "The Cook Political Report," NBC News Senior Capitol Hill Correspondent \uc2 Garrett Haake\uc2 , Kimberly Atkins Stohr, senior opinion writer for "The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ," and John Podhoretz, editor of "Commentary."\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And a good Sunday morning. The economy`s inability to fully recover from the shock of COVID-19 is both an economic story and a political one. So what is holding the economy back?\par \par \uc2 In the spring, we thought we were turning the corner on COVID, but when we turned that corner, the Delta variant was waiting on the other side to hit us hard in the face. \uc2 Delta\uc2  and vaccine refusals sent cases to their second highest peak of this pandemic this summer, before they finally began to trend downward. Let`s hope there`s not another variant out there.\par \par \uc2 A post-COVID supply chain back-up has spread across the globe, causing cargo ship traffic jams, slowing delivery of goods and, yes, driving up costs. In fact, those higher costs have helped feed a rise in prices all over the map, leading to the sharpest rise in inflation in 13 years.\par \par \uc2 On top of that millions of Americans have decided they don`t want to return to their old high-stress, low-wage jobs. So, that`s keeping job creation stubbornly low for the last two months and takes some important workers out of the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 All those economic problems add up to a big political problem for the president. Is all of this his fault? Of course not, but it is now his responsibility. And he and fellow Democrats are in real danger of suffering some serious political consequences.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden ran on a promise of a basic return to normal, or at least a path to normalcy. But with the midterm elections just over a year away, unless voters see evidence of this new normal, they may decide to return power back to the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HACEGABA: Everything we`re seeing today was induced by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The slow economic recovery is hurting President Biden`s standing, eroding voter confidence in his competency and threatening to overshadow his agenda.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you ever seen a backlog like this ever?\par \par \uc2 HACEGABA: Never.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s the crush of cargo in the nation`s ports.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS GOLDSBY, RETAIL AND SUPPLY CHAIN EXPERT: It`s something like the python trying to swallow the alligator. That`s the kind of volume that we`re trying to send through our ports right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On Wednesday, President Biden addressed the supply chain crisis, announcing the ports of Long Beach and Los Angeles, which handle 40 percent of the nation`s cargo, will now be open 24/7.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Our goal is not only to get through this immediate bottleneck but to address the longstanding weaknesses in our transportation supply chain that this pandemic has exposed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Retailers are warning of price hikes and shortages over the holiday season. Products are not just harder to get but more expensive. Prices for kids` shoes up 12 percent in the past year. Beef up 17 percent. New and used cars up 24 percent. And gas up 42 percent.\par \par \uc2 ED BASTIAN\uc2 , CEO, DELTA AIRLINES: Fuel costs are a bit of a headwind. I think you`re looking at inflation across many industries.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Then there`s the turmoil in the labor market itself. Americans are quitting their jobs in record numbers, 4.3 million in August alone. Those who remain are demanding better pay and working conditions with strikes rippling across the country.\par \par \uc2 [10:35:01]\par \par \uc2 LAURSEN: Cheap labor bubble is finally busted, you know, and part of that is due to the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MARIE RITACCO, VICE PRESIDENT, ST. VINCENT HOSPITAL: I think the pandemic has awakened the union movement again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Those economic aftershocks are taking a toll on President Biden`s approval ratings.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When these guys get into office, you know, it`s a job that has to continue, not just when you win the election.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People don`t know what to do and who to vote for. I mean, they made a change, doesn`t seem to be really working, because he`s a good vice president but I don`t think he`s a president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The president is pitching his social spending plans as a solution.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Despite the attacks and misinformation, my plan still has the overwhelming support of the American people when they`re told what`s in it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But Republicans, sensing an opening, are stepping up criticism.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is killing this economy.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN BRADY (R-TX): This is an economic crisis of their own making.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): It`s driving an inflation bomb throughout the economy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: With the first test of this political environment in the Virginia governor election, just over two weeks away, the Democratic nominee is nervous.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2  (D), VIRGINIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Now if I were running the show here I`d get everybody in a room, lock the door, what do you need, what do you need, and let`s get this thing done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is the secretary of transportation, \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 . Secretary Buttigieg, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG\uc2 , SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION: Thanks for having me back.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to start, obviously, with this issue of the supply chain. You`re secretary of transportation. The ports, all of this is under your purview here. The big news you made was trying to establish a 24/7 operation now at as many of our nation`s ports as possible.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you, this supply chain issue has been a problem for months. Why wait until this week to try that? Why weren`t we at a 24/7 operation nine months ago, 10 months ago, a year ago?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, I can`t speak to a year ago before this administration was in office, but what I can tell you is that as soon as the president came in, he issued an executive order to, I think, in February to look at the supply chain from all angles. Now, one of the things that we started looking into earlier this year was these expanded operations.\par \par \uc2 If you think about the scale and the complexity of these ports, obviously, it`s not something that you can do overnight. But after bringing together the players, agreeing that this is a good approach, seeing the pilot launched in Long Beach, and now being able to announce, as the president did last week, that L.A. is also going 24/7, we`ve got major progress on that front.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s just one piece of a very complex puzzle where you`ve got the terminals, the rail piece, you`ve got the warehouses, the drivers. And we`re working on all of those angles. But let`s remember, these are private sector systems. This is a capitalist country. Nobody wants the federal government to own or operate the stores, the warehouses, the trucks, or the ships, or the ports. Our role is to try to make sure we`re supporting those businesses and those workers who do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me ask you about various ideas that industry leaders are going to be asking the federal government for. I`m going to put up four ideas that we have found in our research. There are some that would like to see deploying of the National Guard, maybe even use of the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  at the ports themselves, suspension of some of the tariffs that, right now, have added to the cost of shipping, fill some of these vacant jobs using some temporary visas with labor from overseas, and possibly even tap some national defense funding.\par \par \uc2 So let`s unpack this. The National Guard. The state of Massachusetts is using the National Guard to fill a gap of school bus drivers, right? Is there any reason why you wouldn`t use the National Guard to try to get more truck drivers on the road?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, right now, we`re focused on some other steps that we think are making a difference, including my department, working with the state DMVs to cut some of the red tape for issuing commercial drivers licenses. Look, we`re constantly going to reevaluate all of our options. But the steps we`re taking right now are going to make a difference. And let`s also acknowledge we have some more profound issues in our economy.\par \par \uc2 Look at truck driving. If you look at the large employers of truck drivers, the annual rate of turnover is 90 percent. That tells us that there`s something deeper going on than any short-term fix is going to address. Truck drivers want to be paid. They want to be respected.\par \par \uc2 The secretary of labor, \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2 , and I have been speaking out about this from earlier this year. And if we`re not dealing with these deeper issues in our economy, just like we`ve got to deal with deep issues in our infrastructure then, you know, these shorter-term measures are really not going to be enough. That`s why we have things like the infrastructure bill, like the Build Back Better plan that are really going to set up America for success in the long term.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that, but we`ve got a short-term problem here, just like -- look, we`ve got -- most of our ports are out of date technologically, right? So, you know, it`d be great to sort of build some new ports and have some -- but some of these things take time. What you`re describing takes time. It doesn`t mean there are going to be gifts on the shelves to buy for Christmas this December. It sounds like what you`re setting up the country for is -- do you agree -- do you concur that this is going to get worse before it gets better?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, what I can tell you is that, you know, we`re doing everything we can for the short term and the long term.\par \par \uc2 [10:40:04]\par \par \uc2 And there are some X factors out there on everything from, you know, pandemic-related closures of factories and ports in Asia, to things that are going on through the chain on the U.S. side. Now, of course, the other thing we`ve got to talk about is that this isn`t just a matter of supply. This is a matter of supply and demand. Every item you see, every container on every ship that is waiting at anchor in the ports of L.A. and Long Beach or anywhere else is there because an American company or consumer purchased it. And that`s because demand has surged. Retail sales are off the charts.\par \par \uc2 Remember, these ports are already bringing through record amounts of goods this year. And that`s a sign, of course, of the successful work that`s been done to bring the economy out of the teeth of the recession that we were facing earlier. So we`ve got that issue of demand that`s mismatched with supply. And now, we`ve got to make sure that the supply chains in the short term and the long run are positioned to catch up. Now, a lot of this should be resolved by markets, but we`re not waiting for the markets to take care of it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. One other thing that the government can do is tariff relief. Is it on the table?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Again, I think that any opportunity to make a difference will be looked at. But let`s also remember that the president`s overall policy of Buy America is constantly pushing for us to make and source more goods in America, would be a big part of the solution on a lot of these issues. All right. If we didn`t need to go overseas for so many of our products, we would have a lot more resilience in our supply chain when there is, you know, anything from a typhoon to a COVID outbreak shutting down a factory overseas. And so it`s one more reason why the president is rightly calling on America and working for America to build up more of that domestic manufacturing and other supply capacity.\par \par \uc2 TODD: One of your -- one of your tasks these days is also trying to sell the president`s agenda, particularly what you`ve referred to it a few times on Build Back Better. There seems to be some news over the weekend that some of the biggest, perhaps the most important climate change -- climate provisions in the bill have been struck down because of some negotiations with Senator Manchin.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned that the lack of an aggressive action on climate, that it looks like this bill no longer will have in order to get 50 votes, is going to end up costing you progressive support? I mean, is what it takes to get Manchin on climate is it going to lose progressives on climate?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, look, the president is committed to major climate action. He has been from day one. You saw that in everything from rejoining Paris, to the achievements of the Climate Summit, to the preparations for the talks that are going to take place next month in Glasgow. And this is important not just morally, but also economically, something I think we need to recognize, that like any maintenance issue, and this is basically a maintenance issue for the planet. The longer you take to deal with it, the more there will be costs in livelihoods and in dollars and, of course, in lives. So this remains a core commitment of this administration. And, of course, my slice of it as transportation secretary is one that we`re working very hard on to prepare America for a future with more electric vehicles and to decarbonize other sectors of transportation, which amounts to the biggest part of our economy when it comes to emissions right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are we going to hold up the bipartisan infrastructure bill any longer than October 31st if there`s no deal on the larger -- on the larger agenda?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Look, I`m not going to predict legislative mechanics. What I will say is that we`ve got to get this done. And we`ve got to get this done for the American people and the American economy.\par \par \uc2 Look, the Build Back Better vision and the infrastructure bill are supported by a majority of Americans. They`re going to make lives better concretely in this country. They`re going to strengthen the economy. And one under -- I think underappreciated aspect of the president`s agenda is that it will also lower the inflationary pressures that we`re experiencing in this economy by making it easier for people to thrive and do well in the workforce and by, of course, improving our infrastructure that is so important to the supply chains that we`ve been talking about this morning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know you`ve been under some bizarre attack for taking paid leave by some loud mouths in our political system. But I want to ask you about paid family leave in general, because it apparently may not be in the final part of Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 You took it. The federal government offers it to federal government employees. What does it say if the president can`t get paid family leave into his agenda?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, it`s on the president`s agenda. It`s in the Build Back Better plan and I`m proud to work for an administration --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t think -- but it may not last.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: -- that walks the walk on family values.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It may not last in the plan.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: You know, let me tell you, it is in the president`s vision. And we`ll see what the legislative process is going to bring. But the president`s been very clear on what he`s calling for. And the American people want this too.\par \par \uc2 [10:45:00]\par \par \uc2 And frankly, I view this as something, or I wish, at least, that this was something the Republicans could join Democrats in calling for. Look, paid family leave is important. It`s important as a matter of family values. It`s important to our economy.\par \par \uc2 And one more thing that I think is maybe underappreciated. When somebody welcomes a new child into their family and goes on leave to take care of that child, that`s not a vacation. It`s work. It`s joyful, wonderful, fulfilling work, but it is work. And it`s time that our nation join pretty much every other country in the world and recognize that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Secretary Buttigieg, appreciate you coming on this morning and sharing the administration`s view.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s turn now more directly to the pandemic. There`s been a national tug of war of sorts going on between municipalities and some states and private companies that are issuing vaccine mandates, and the many workers who are vehemently opposed to them. In some cases, it`s Republican governors like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida and Greg Abbott of Texas. They`ve been outspoken opponents of mandates of all kinds, choosing to side with their more conservative and a growing group of anti-mandate voters, and against the private sector companies.\par \par \uc2 But Governor \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2  of Arkansas, who agrees that vaccines save lives, is taking a bit of a nuanced approach, I guess you could call it. Last week, he effectively approved a new state law that would allow workers to opt out of mandates even while saying the law -- he thought the law was harmful in getting more Arkansans vaccinated. Well, Governor Hutchinson joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome back to MEET THE PRESS. And let me just dive right into this issue. You did not think this was a good law passed by your Republican-controlled legislature to essentially dictate to the private sector how they do these things. But you decided not to veto it. You decided not to have the, the veto fight with the legislature. Why?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON\uc2  (R-AR): Well, the principle reason is that the whole debate on mandates takes away from the efficacy of the vaccines themselves and our push to increase vaccination rates. Ever since the president reversed his position and said -- first said that we weren`t going to have federal mandates and then he switched and said, we are going to have federal mandates, and then some of the states came back and said, "Well, we ought to have state mandates to push back against the federal mandates." All of this slowed down acceptance of the vaccine and increased resistance.\par \par \uc2 And so, I`d like to see us get back to, without the mandate battle. Let`s just encourage the vaccine acceptance, build confidence in it. And that`s the direction we need to go.\par \par \uc2 My heart goes out to these workers that many of them say, "We`re not anti- vaxx. Were just anti-mandate." And they`re making a principled stand. And that sort of makes the point that the mandates are not being beneficial.\par \par \uc2 So, I pushed back here in Arkansas that we don`t need to counter a federal mandate on our employers with a state mandate on our employers. That`s just the wrong direction and I made that point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I want to ask you, though, if -- we got some evidence here that mandates work. Let me show you some statistics in your state. On August 1st, 36 percent of the state was fully vaccinated. After the vaccine mandate was announced and you had companies like Tyson and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  in your state, essentially, announce their own vaccine mandates, vaccine acceptance went up 30 percent, you got 46 percent of the state now. Is that not evidence that the mandates by Tyson and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  have had an impact?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, absolutely, it does indicate that. And it`s not just there, but our health care workers. Many of our hospitals have put in a requirement for vaccines and the rate goes up. So, yes, there is an effectiveness there.\par \par \uc2 And so, let me make it clear that when I say I don`t believe we ought to be engaging in mandates, I`m speaking of the government mandates, whether it`s a federal government mandate or a state government mandate. And the states are sometimes coming in and saying, "Employers should not have the ability to impose a vaccine requirement on their workers." To me, that`s the wrong direction as well. It`s not practical in terms of creating that debate, but it`s not principled either.\par \par \uc2 So, I am a defender of the employer`s right to provide a healthy workplace. You would have just as many workers say, "I don`t want to work there because it`s not a healthy workplace, because not everybody`s going to be vaccinated." The employers are in a tough position. They should have the prerogative to make those decisions and I support that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, there is a lack of consistency now in your party, not with you, on this issue, but this sort of government intervention with the private sector. Republican state legislatures all across the country have gotten really aggressive at trying to insert government either into overreach on local government decisions or on private sector decisions.\par \par \uc2 [10:50:03]\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned about the direction of your party? That there is this sense of it`s not a small government party anymore. It`s a my government, my way, party, if you will, at least in these -- in these state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, it`s an important debate on liberty. And my view has always been, which I believe is consistent with the principles of our party, that let`s stay out of interfering with those private business decisions. These are not all large employers. They can be a small employer that wants to protect their business and their employees and their customers. They ought to have those decisions.\par \par \uc2 To me, if you`re going to say the government ought to come in and tell the employers what to do, the next thing is they could say we shouldn`t have drug-free workplaces, where you can`t require drug testing in the workplace by employers. I think that`s wrong.\par \par \uc2 I`m former head of the DEA and I believe employers ought to be able to make that decision. And if somebody doesn`t comply and it`s a sensitive workplace, they lose their job. We require that in many different sensitive businesses across the country.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I think when you`re talking about a restraint on government, let`s be consistent. I don`t want the federal government doing the mandate. I don`t want the state government doing the mandate. Let`s try and be a little bit more consistent in that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, but it is noticeable you chose not to fight. Why didn`t you fight your Republican colleagues on this in the legislature?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, that`s the uniqueness of Arkansas. You can override my veto in Arkansas with a simple majority and I didn`t want to bring them in for that same debate because every time we debate and spend a week debating vaccine mandates and efficacy, our resistance increases and our acceptance goes down. And so, I didn`t want to recreate that. Let`s just move on and let`s try to get back to the business of encouraging vaccines across the board.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quickly, it was among the bizarre statements that the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has released this week was this one where he said this, "If we don`t solve the presidential election fraud of 2020," his bizarre characterization, "which we have," he claims, "which we have thoroughly and conclusively documented, Republicans will not be voting in `22 and `24."\par \par \uc2 Many -- there are some Senate Republicans that I`ve talked to who believe President Trump`s actions in Georgia encouraged people not to vote. Are you concerned that this is only going to hurt Republican turnout in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Re-litigating 2020 is a recipe for disaster in 2022. Let`s talk about the future. The election is passed, it`s been certified, the states made decisions on the integrity of each of their elections and made improvements where it need be. It`s about the future, it`s not about the last election, and that -- those kinds of comments are not constructive. We can win in 2022. And we`re going to, but let`s focus on the important issues of our supply chain, of getting over this pandemic, about freedom, and not the, not the last election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , Republican from Arkansas, I appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us, sir.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Thank you. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And sorry about those Hogs yesterday. I know I was pulling for them.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, how worried should Democrats like Virginia`s Terry McAuliffe be about this slow economic recovery and what that could mean for the 2022 midterms. The panel is here and it`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:55:46]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The panel is here. Amy Walter, the editor-in-chief and publisher of "The Cook Political Report," NBC News senior Capitol Hill correspondent, \uc2 Garrett Haake\uc2 , John Podhoretz, editor of "Commentary," and Kimberly Atkins Stohr, the senior opinion writer for "The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Garrett, I want to start with you because this entire issue, right, we have this sort of sluggish economy due to COVID. The president`s agenda feels a bit sort of clogged up a little bit. What is the mindset of Democrats on Capitol Hill? Do they see the forest right now through these trees of obstruction, if you will?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE\uc2 , NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: I think they do. I mean, Democrats promised the world to their voters coming into the last election cycle. And now, they`re trying to figure out what they can still deliver on. They`ve left some priorities by the wayside, the guns bill, the voting rights bill that`ll get a vote this week that they don`t think they`re going to pass. But -- all the marbles are on these two big spending bills right now. And they`re just pretty well stuck.\par \par \uc2 Now, I think everyone understands that the reconciliation bill is probably too big to fail. They will all survive or fail. And if they don`t pass that big bill in some capacity, probably everybody`s going home.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Amy, is it already though -- has it already got this weird, negative vibe to it even if they pass it? Here`s what "Politico" wrote yesterday. I thought it was a bit harsh, this framing, but maybe they`re right. "Here`s the cold reality," they write. "It now appears all but impossible for Democrats to enact a reconciliation package that`s anywhere near as far reaching as what polls suggest a large majority of Democratic voters want. And that effectively means that for a lot of Dems, it will be hard to see whatever ends up coming out of this process as a success -- with potentially dire implications for the party in 2022."\par \par \uc2 Specifically, they were writing about the climate provisions, basically capitulating to Manchin`s demands here, which, basically, Secretary Buttigieg didn`t want to touch.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE COOK POLITICAL REPORT: See, I think the challenge really goes back more to what Garrett is saying, is that I don`t know that Democratic voters are particularly excited about Build Back Better or infrastructure bill. I think what --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Don`t you see all the individual polling? They tell you this? I just -- it makes me nuts --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: I know it. I know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just because something polls well, doesn`t mean it`s on the tip of the tongue.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Doesn`t mean that it`s salient. It`s not salient. What is salient, and I`ve been sitting in these focus groups, especially with younger African American voters. You know what they`re talking about, exactly what Garrett said, which is, where`s the police reform? We talked about that a lot in 2020. Voting rights. This is an existential threat to our country and what -- we can`t pass it? You`re in charge, Democrats. You have a chance to do this.\par \par \uc2 And so I think they`re sort of missing out on the things that do really energize Democrats on the one hand. And then the other is everything you just talked about, which is, there`s just a sluggishness in the economy, in the optimism that he`s also the president and Democrats aren`t delivering on the sort of bread and butter that they were hoping they would.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kimberly, how demoralized do you think the Democratic base could get if this gets fraught?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, SENIOR OPINION WRITER, THE \uc2 BOSTON GLOBE\uc2 : Oh, I think it`s already. I think it is very existential at this moment for all the reasons that Amy was talking about. I don`t think it`s just a matter that the Democrats promised the world to their voters, it`s voters demanded these things from Democrats. And now, they`re saying, "OK, well, you have the White House. You have Congress. And after the end of two years, we have nothing."\par \par \uc2 I think that will be absolutely devastating to the Democratic base if they walk away without -- not just with nothing, but yes, without voting rights particularly, without anything. Without, you know, making sure everyone has health care, the expansion of Medicaid. I mean, if they walk away with nothing, they can expect, as you said, to pack up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: John, I`m going to guess that you`re going to agree with something Charlie Cook wrote. I should`ve made Amy respond to this --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes, and made me in the hot seat.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But I`m not. No, no, no, no, because here`s what Charlie wrote, "Democrats push for way too much without having the political capital to make it stick. It`s killing them on overreach. It`s killing them on competence. If misreading a mandate is a sin in politics, pretending that you have one when you don`t is a mortal sin." Is that the issue?\par \par \uc2 JOHN PODHORETZ, EDITOR, COMMENTARY: In March, \uc2 Jon Meacham\uc2  convened a bunch of historians at the White House who said to Biden, you can be FDR. You can be LBJ.\par \par \uc2 [11:00:02]\par \par \uc2 Lyndon Baines Johnson won election in 1964 with a 155-seat majority in the House and 69 --\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS MODERATOR, "MEET THE PRESS": Say that again.\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: -- 155-seat majority and 69 --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is that more than five? Sorry.\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: And 69 Democratic senators. And in 1965, they passed 70 major pieces of legislation. If you go to the Johnson Library, there are 70 pens lined up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: That`s the Great Society. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has a majority of, nobody even knows. Is it three? Is it five? Is it four, in the House, and a 50/50 Senate. Only a 50/50 Senate because of bizarre machinations by Trump in November and December that depressed the Republican vote.\par \par \uc2 Democrats went nuts in the winter. They went crazy. They were at the point of having $8 trillion of new spending with no consensus. Forget the Democratic base, there was no mandate to do any of this from the American people writ large.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE\uc2 , NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: But they`re locked in structurally, right? I mean, you can`t do voting rights with a 50-vote threshold. You can`t pass a guns bill with a 50-vote threshold. You can spend money with a 50-vote threshold, and you have to say the price tag first. That`s the way reconciliation works.\par \par \uc2 And so they locked themselves into this messaging nightmare by saying, "Okay, we have to set the price tag," and then tell you all the good stuff it`s going to do. And that`s not --\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, EDITOR IN CHIEF, COOK POLITICAL REPORT: And then take it all out.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right. And then take it all out. That`s not a way you build a coalition around anything but it`s what they were forced into doing.\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: And they had a coalition for a bill. They had a huge spending bill, a $1 trillion infrastructure bill. That`s a big bill. There was a time when, saying you were going to spend $1 trillion on a bill was, like, "What are you, crazy?" Obama didn`t even spend $1 trillion on the stimulus package in 2009.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: But I`m not sure. I`m not sure a trillion dollar spending package would satisfy the people who spent the pandemic marching in the streets for real social justice reform. So I don`t think that that would solve all their problems either.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I get that, but it gets to the point. I almost wonder, was Manchin and Sinema too easy on COVID relief because it made the progressives think, "Oh, we got them for this. We`ll get them for more"?\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: I will not try to say what`s in Senator Manchin`s mind. I think he`s enjoying being at the center of this conversation. I do think that the American people and Senator Manchin should realize that West Virginians make up one half of 1 percent of the American population.\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: But the West Virginians who voted Joe Manchin in, voted almost 40 percent for Trump. Trump`s margin was 40 points in 2020 in West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well --\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: He is representing his constituents.\par \par \uc2 Can I just say though? In 2001, when \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  had a Senate almost exactly analogous to this, he got one major piece of legislation through that was a bipartisan piece of legislation, shepherded by \uc2 Teddy Kennedy\uc2 . That was No Child Left Behind. That was an education bill for which there had to be bipartisan support. They have that bill. They have the infrastructure bill. And Democrats are tanking the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Amy, how much is all of this malaise, if you will, in Washington, going to impact Mr. McAuliffe in Virginia?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes. I mean, you can already feel it. And again, I think it`s much more of the broader environment. And it`s also very important for Glenn Youngkin that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has stayed out of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But he didn`t.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: I mean, he didn`t -- but he was not --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, I will say this -- have you watched TV in the last weekend?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Well, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Because \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  is now --\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- is making sure that --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: That people know he called in --\par \par \uc2 TODD: And said his name.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: -- to a rally.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He said the name, Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: I know it. I know it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s a pretty important moment.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: It is. Except I think -- all right, well, let`s put it this way: he is more important, I think, to turning out the Democratic base than whether a bill passes --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Than if things pass.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: -- Congress. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that is I think potentially a long term problem for the Democrats. They can`t keep going back to the Trump well.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Unless \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  keeps running.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair point.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, God and politics: the debate among Evangelicals about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and whether he represents their values.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEN PETERS, SENIOR PASTOR, PATRIOT CHURCH: One thing I`ve learned about scripture and the Lord is God can use anybody. In the Bible, he even used a donkey. And if God can use a donkey, he can use President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [11:07:13]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 One of the more unexpected developments in American politics in the last five years has been the enormous popularity of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  among the so- called values-voting evangelical community. Mr. Trump won 77 percent of the evangelical vote in 2016 and then 84 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 On "Meet the Press Reports", our weekly one-topic streaming show on NBC News Now and Peacock, my colleague Anne Thompson traveled just outside Knoxville, Tennessee, to report on the friction over what it means to be an evangelical today.\par \par \uc2 Her report was so revealing, we wanted to share part of it this morning right here on "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Under this flag painted roof -- religion and politics mix --\par \par \uc2 PETERS: I want Trump to come back sooner than later. But you know who`d be a lot better than Trump coming back? Jesus coming back\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: With gusto --\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Biden you trouble Israel, leftists, you trouble Israel --\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: This is the Patriot Church, on the outskirts of Knoxville, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: You unvaccinated people. you are causing the trouble in the land, that what`s what they say --\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Founded by Pastor Ken Peters.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: This nation was founded on predominantly Christian values by predominantly Christian people, we just want to keep that in play. We just want to keep our roots alive and not let this reconstruction, this tearing up of our nation`s roots and a new set of values is being pushed on us. It literally is.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: So, whose values are being pushed on you?\par \par \uc2 PETERS: These are leftist worldly values. They can`t stand Christian culture. Why? Because we believe marriage is between a man and a woman. We believe that there are only two genders. We believe that life in the womb is actually human life and they`re murdering human life for money.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: He is not afraid to take sides, and thinks God does too.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: One thing I`ve learned about Scripture and the Lord is God can use anybody. In the Bible, he even used the donkey, and if God can use a donkey, he can use President Trump.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Do you think God is using President Trump?\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Absolutely. I think President Trump is a miracle. I think God picked \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , an imperfect vessel, to be the champion of his people.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: That intertwining of patriotism, politics and religion is attracting a devoted following.\par \par \uc2 JAYNE MILLER. CONGREGANT, PATRIOT CHURCH: When you`re in a culture where everybody`s trying to silence you, it`s great to be able to share how you really feel with people who feel the same way.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA GAI, VISITOR, PATRIOT CHURCH: We`re just checking it out. We actually saw it on CNN. And if they say something bad about it, it might be good.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Peters leading one side of the battle for the soul of the Evangelical Church.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Some of the evangelical church, I think, is soft. I think they`re cowardly and they`re trying to ride the fence between the left and the right. And so that`s kind of where the divide is.\par \par \uc2 [11:10:03]\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Here in the heart of the Bible Belt --\par \par \uc2 PHIL NORDSTROM, LEAD PASTOR, LIFE CHURCH: I would say, as a person of faith who has been around church all my life, that the Bible Belt is unbuckling.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Across town, Pastor Phil Nordstrom leads the Life Church.\par \par \uc2 What do you mean that the Bible Belt is unbuckling?\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: The branding of Christianity has suffered. I think that our association with political extremism has especially turned off a younger generation toward evangelicalism. So one of the challenges we face right now is, who are we? What does it mean to be an evangelical?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Nordstrom is decidedly old school, a pro-life pastor pointedly staying away from partisan politics on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: The funny thing is I`m probably personally pretty conservative. So I don`t come across as like I`m a liberal evangelical, but, but people get the spirit -- get the feeling pretty quick that we`re a pretty inclusive church, that everyone`s welcome, that we`re trying to not fight the culture wars from the pulpit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: In reporting this story, I also sat down with Dr. Russell Moore, a high-profile conservative evangelical opponent of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. Moore is now the public theologian at Christianity Today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s fascinating is that if you look at some of the Pew studies, more people are identifying as white evangelical and yet fewer people are going to church.\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL MOORE, PUBLIC THEOLOGIAN, CHRISTIANITY TODAY: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It is, it is not, it is not as if these people are finding religion in the sense of what that phrase would have meant just a decade ago. What is happening here?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, that`s what concerns me, I`m talking every day to church going evangelical Christians who don`t want to use the word evangelical because it`s become merely a political word and then those who don`t go to church but who are using evangelical in a political sense as a way to own the libs. That`s not a -- that`s not a good development in my -- in my view.\par \par \uc2 At once, evangelical Christianity is defined not by the gospel, but by some sort of cultural or political movement. We`re in a really dangerous place. Evangelical Christianity is meant to be the good news of Jesus Christ and handing that over to a political agenda, no matter what the political agenda is a bad idea. And that`s especially true when we`re in this crisis moment where the moral credibility of virtually every institution is being challenged and the church is certainly no exception.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you hear that the former president told a Christian broadcast - - he went on television and told them that he did more for Christianity or for evangelicals or for religion itself, that nobody else had done more. What do you think when you hear him say this?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: You know, I think that`s the tragic part about the past several years, if I had heard that in 2015 or 2016 or 2017 even, I think I would have been -- I would have been outraged all day. I would have flinched immediately when hearing it.\par \par \uc2 I wasn`t really even surprised. I think I gave it a half second`s thought - -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: -- and moved on because we have so, so many things like that being said that we`ve become used to it. And I think that`s, that`s part of the problem right now, the coarsening and the trivialization of rhetoric in American life. We`re, we`re getting used to it, and that`s dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Outrage fatigue, a huge problem. You can see our full report on the split in the evangelical movement streaming anytime on Peacock. These are considered 30 minute documentaries, if you will.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the events that led to this traffic jam off the coast of California... and what it could mean for your holidays and your pocketbook.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [11:16:57]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Data Download time, and a look at what`s causing the supply chain backups. We`re going to follow just one product, an Apple iPhone. But it shows you the example. It`ll show you how the breakdowns at every step of the process have led to shipping delays for you, really no matter the product.\par \par \uc2 This is the iPhone, but it`s even running shoes. Eson Precision Engineering, they make some component parts for the iPhone. Well, they had to shut down for a couple weeks due to both some health concerns and some power issues. This is not the only factory in China that`s had these issues, but this is one that`s impacted the iPhone. So there`s your delay in manufacturing the iPhone.\par \par \uc2 Now, to get the iPhone done, now they`re putting it on a container ship. Well, guess what`s happened, particularly as we`ve seen this sort of pent up demand? The amount of cargo being sent from China to the United States is up some 27 percent. That has led to quite the backlog of big container ships outside on the west coast of the United States. Trust me, you fly in and out of LAX, look out the window, you`ll see this traffic jam, not just on the 405.\par \par \uc2 But let`s say you get the container ship empty. You`ve got to actually take that from the ports to the store shelves, whether it`s your Apple Store, \uc2 T- Mobile\uc2 , \uc2 Sprint\uc2 , \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , et cetera. Well, we have a huge truck driving issue. In fact, since 2019, there are 22,000 fewer people in the truck driving industry, and this was an industry that was already struggling to find drivers even pre-COVID. Now, even harder than before. So you have a hard time getting the actual product to the shelf.\par \par \uc2 Then -- this has been a problem not just for iPhones, really for anything manufactured in China. So what has it meant overall? Pre-COVID, it basically took an average of 40 days to get a product from China to the shelves in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Two years later, it is almost double. It now takes 73 days. And the real fear is that this is only going to get worse. Why? Demand`s only going to grow. And we`re still going to have this problem.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see. Some people think this could take years, not months to unravel.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, what happens when workers feel empowered to demand better wages and conditions? Striketober, what else? Tens of thousands right now are walking the picket line.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have more next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [11:23:48]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Kimberly, workers have never had more leverage. And when you look around, this is like one of those things that, like, oh, every national news organization discovered strikes this week. But the fact is, it is happening.\par \par \uc2 Look, we`ve seen it in our own organization, more openness to organizing, more openness to unions. This is one of the more interesting consequences of COVID.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: Yeah. Well, I think it`s an accelerator actually, because we saw that sort of movement toward unionization in places like newsrooms coming up for a couple of years before this. And then you add COVID, where people realize, okay, if I`m not making enough money, if I don`t have enough benefits and you`re calling me essential and forcing me to go to the job, I`m rethinking about what this means. And that really put more leverage on the part of workers.\par \par \uc2 Before, companies could pay them or not pay them whatever they wanted to. And things have changed in a really rapid way. And that`s what this Striketober is representing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, John, this is not -- you know, there was always to me a very distinct line, you know, on labor unions between Democrats and Republicans. Not with the Trump base.\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: Well, look, I think this is a generational moment. For the last 20 years, people have been frightened to lose their jobs. It`s not just because of the economic dislocations of the meltdown of 2008. Because of health care and the worries about changing your job, because of the stuff that happened as a result of 9/11, all kinds of things, almost all the power in the United States has been in the hands of employers in part because of the anxiety of the workforce. And that goes from the very poor to the very wealthy. That is an anxiety shared by everybody.\par \par \uc2 And suddenly, we have 9 million, or 10 million, or 11 million jobs open in the United States. And that`s new. And I think the psychology of the employers or these corporations has yet to shift.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they know they need to pay people a lot more money. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is advertising paying people a lot more than $15 an hour and all of that. But I think of John Deere and some of these other places.\par \par \uc2 [11:25:01]\par \par \uc2 My guess is that the corporate culture has not yet shifted into the idea that, we`ve got to be a lot nicer to our workforce.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yeah, I mean, look, people who in Washington are surprised by this dynamic have fundamentally misunderstood what happened in the pandemic, when people with white collar jobs stayed home and complained about hard it was to work on Zoom, and people with blue collar jobs who were essential went to work every day and braved the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you think you had a hard time working from home, or you still are while you were working on Zoom and you don`t understand why folks who`ve been out in it every day are striking and are very upset, I don`t know what to tell you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And even our service economy is a very labor intensive --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- economy.\par \par \uc2 And now we`re having -- you know, let me put up some of the companies here. \uc2 Instacart\uc2  is one of the places that`s having one of these labor. I mean, I do think that this new sector of the economy that factory workers were really important to our economy in the `50s and `60s --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Now, it`s the service economy --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, the deliverers, you know--\par \par \uc2 WALTER: -- like, \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , the \uc2 Instacart\uc2 , people who are --\par \par \uc2 TODD: And they`re not paid very well.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: They`re not paid very well.\par \par \uc2 And so, right, are we going to have a generational change that this is COVID, we`re going to look back 20 years from now and say, that was the instigator for this? Or, are we going to wake up three years from now and say, oh, well, it was COVID. And guess what, we`re going to go back to normal because of all these other structural challenges we still have, which is, you know, getting back to health care. That is still a fundamental problem. Or, you know, so many of the other inequities that we have in our system.\par \par \uc2 So look, I`m very interested to see where this all goes because the disruptors that we`ve expected in the past to make significant changes at least in our politics, whether it was 9/11 or whether it was the pandemic, didn`t make big structural changes to our politics. But our economy, that`s a whole different story.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, and go ahead.\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: I`d just say speaking of the economy though, this is all happening at a time of rising and apparently non-transitory inflation. And if wages have to go up significantly, that`s inflation too. I mean, there are inflationary -- this is a potential inflationary spiral. So you want to talk about political consequences, I`m sorry --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Oh, the short term --\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: -- that people can talk about this might be --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: There`s short term, and then --\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: -- great --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: -- and then there`s much longer term --\par \par \uc2 PODHORETZ: -- for Democrats, the new activism and all of that.\par \par \uc2 This is not good for Biden either way. It`s not good for Democrats either way. They have the House, the Senate, and the presidency. And we could be looking at inflation continuing to go up, in part justifiably in order to pay these people --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kimberly, it`s a weird economy. We have plenty of jobs, and it costs more to just keep your house.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: Yes. I mean, but one part of that is, yeah, you can have fears about inflation, but the structure was unsustainable. You have this gig economy, where people were working second and third jobs just to keep food on the table, just to pay for the health care that the law requires them to have.\par \par \uc2 That was going to be unsustainable. So this, you know, conflagration of issues really brought it to the fore. And yes, you have these open jobs.\par \par \uc2 But I think when you look at it study after study, it shows if you pay people more, they will take the jobs. It`s really shifting from that corporate perspective that they can have large profits to saying, no, we actually -- to be profitable, you need to treat your employees well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And one thing I would say, if labor unions are getting stronger, I wouldn`t assume one party has the advantage on this one because we know in the particularly blue collar labor unions, they`ve been pretty open to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 All right. Nice panel. That`s all we have for today. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching. We`re going to be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press". "Meet the Press Reports" is on Peacock. We got "Meet the Press" every day of the week. But we`ll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020211017ehah00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Police Standoff In Chicago, The City Hitting Its Deadline For All Officers To Disclose Their Vaccine Status\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Kathy Park \par \uc2 811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART, NBC NEWS HOST, NIGHTLY NEWS: Tonight, the police standoff in Chicago, the city hitting its deadline for all officers to disclose their vaccine status. The union leader calling for the police to refuse those orders, sparking concerns of major shortages on the force. What a judge has now ruled.\par \par \uc2 Booster confusion. An \uc2 FDA\uc2  panel approves booster shots for the J&J vaccines, but not mixing and matching. We`ll guide you through the new patchwork of rules.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HOPKINS, \uc2 JOHNSON & JOHNSON\uc2  VACCINE RECIPIENT: I`m a scientist and I`m confused.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: An all-out manhunt in Texas. Police officers shot, one killed in what`s being called an ambush.\par \par \uc2 MARK HERMAN, HARRIS COUNTY PRECINCT 4 CONSTABLE: What happened tonight was evil.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: The moving tribute to the fallen.\par \par \uc2 New details in the killing of a British member of parliament. Authorities now call the murder terror-related.\par \par \uc2 Critics attack transportation secretary Pete Buttigieg for taking paternity leave to be with his newborns. He`s firing back.\par \par \uc2 Retail madness, the huge challenge stores face in getting staffed up for the holidays. How it will affect your shopping.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood cliffhanger. Negotiations down to the wire to prevent a labor strike that could shut down your favorite TV shows.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the man who`s become famous for dancing alone now inspiring millions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (Voice over): This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Jose Diaz- Balart.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Good evening. We have breaking news on both sides of the vaccine debate tonight. For those who don`t want the vaccine, the first major showdown with severe consequences. In Chicago, a deadline for all police officers to report their vaccine status passed just hours ago. The police union leaders calling for them to ignore the order and he`s warning that up to half of the force could stay home.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the debate, for those who want the vaccine, more confusion today over booster shots with those who got the supposedly one- shot J&J vaccine wondering what they should do next. We have two reports tonight and we begin with Kathy Park.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (Voice over): Tonight, a showdown in Chicago over vaccine mandates between the mayor and the police union.\par \par \uc2 LORI LIGHTFOOT\uc2 , (D) MAYOR OF CHICAGO: I expect the police officers to honor their oath, not tarnish their badge and to follow lawful orders and directives.\par \par \uc2 JOHN CATANZARA, UNION PRESIDENT: They can take us to court all they want.\par \par \uc2 PARK (Voice over): All city employees, including roughly 13,000 Chicago police officers, were required to declare their vaccine status by Friday. Those who didn`t meet the deadline could be put on unpaid leave. But earlier this week, the Fraternal Order of Police urged officers to ignore that mandate, warning the mayor ...\par \par \uc2 CATANZARA: I guarantee you at least half the department`s staying home come Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 PARK (Voice over): On Friday, Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  filed a complaint against the police union for engaging in, supporting and encouraging a work stoppage or strike and overnight, a Cook County judge slapped a temporary restraining order against the union president to stop making public comments encouraging members to defy the city policy. But just today, he shared this message on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CATANZARA: To the great officers, detectives and everybody else that is literally bombarding me with information, thank you. Hold the line, keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 PARK (Voice over): The police union also filed its own countersuit. The battle over vaccine mandates is playing out across the country. In Leesburg, Virginia, some officers say they may quit if the vaccine mandate moves forward.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This decision has the potential to decimate LPD.\par \par \uc2 PARK (Voice over): In Seattle, a similar fallout ahead of Monday`s deadline.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any loss of an officer right now would be detrimental to our city`s current public safety crisis.\par \par \uc2 PARK (Voice over): The nationwide standoff over vaccines intensifying with both sides holding the line.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And, Kathy, will vaccine mandates be expanded to police in New York City?\par \par \uc2 PARK: Jose, Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  says that he is weighing all options at this point. Currently, \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers have to be vaccinated or provide a negative COVID test each week. Jose?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211017ehag000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hollywood On The Verge Of Shutting Down\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Erin McLaughlin \par \uc2 445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Also tonight, Hollywood on the verge of shutting down. The entertainment industry could be days away from a crippling strike, just one of several labor disputes erupting across the country. Erin McLaughlin is in Los Angeles with more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Tonight, Hollywood is on the brink of calling cut. With 60,000 behind-the-scenes workers threatening to walk off set, potentially halting production of your favorite series films and live shows.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m not nervous about the strike. I`m excited.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: The Union IATSE alleging the explosion of streaming combined with the pandemic has elevated and aggravated working conditions, bringing those behind the scenes to the breaking point, creating what it says are excessively unsafe and harmful working hours and unlivable wages. Also noting a consistent failure to provide reasonable rest.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:14]\par \par \uc2 CHELCIE PARRY, IATSE FILM LOADER: So, they expect us just continue working no matter that conditions, no matter how long we`ve been at it.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Now, Hollywood heavyweights are speaking out in support of the workers.\par \par \uc2 OSCAR ISAAC, ACTOR: It`s staggering. I mean, the -- I mean, the amount of abuse that I`ve seen. No one gives a (BLEEP), you know, and it`s -- I`ve seen a lot of that.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: The \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  saying it deeply values the crew members and is committed to reaching a deal. The Alliance includes \uc2 Universal Pictures\uc2 , part of our parent company, NBC Universal. Anger over workers` rights is hardly confined to Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re on the right side of history.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: From auto and agriculture to health care workers, it`s all part of a growing nationwide showdown between employees and employers sparked in part, experts say, by the pandemic now spilling on to the silver screen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (on camera): If ongoing negotiations fall through, thousands here in Hollywood are set to join the picket lines starting Monday. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Erin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how to deal with potential holiday shortage is even for products made here. And big increases ahead heating your home.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211017ehaf0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 COVID Fear Still Making Americans Cautious About Going Back To Work\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Peter Alexander \par \uc2 625 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: In September, buffeted by the Delta COVID variant, the country added 194,000 jobs, fewer than expected. The unemployment rate dropped however to 4.8 percent as more workers exited the job market. But will an improving COVID outlook give the economy the boost it needs? Our team taking a hard look tonight and we begin with Peter Alexander.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Tonight, help wanted. Just when America was expected to usher in a fall season of new hires, instead U.S. job growth dropped to its slowest pace of the year, with a recent spike in the Delta variant and an ongoing shortage of workers holding the economy back.\par \par \uc2 President Biden tonight insisting it`s a snapshot from nearly a month ago when the COVID case count was much higher.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Since then, we`ve seen the daily cases fall by more than one-third and they`re continuing to trend down. We`re continuing to make progress.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The economy in September adding 194,000 jobs, a disappointing figure for a second straight month. Unemployment dropping below five percent for the first time since before the pandemic, with more people giving up looking for work. Republicans blame President Biden`s policies. One key leader saying, if this were a football team with his losing record, the coach would be searching for a new job.\par \par \uc2 Caitlyn Tolkien was laid off at the height of the pandemic. Since then, she`s applied for nearly 300 jobs, but can only find in-person work when she feels safer working from home with her 10-month-old baby.\par \par \uc2 How frustrating has this been for you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s challenging and it definitely wears you down mentally. It start to feel like am I ever going to get something? Is this ever going to work?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: According to the job search website Indeed, the number one reason people are not going back to work is fear of catching Coronavirus. Still, many economists predicted job gains with the expiration of enhanced unemployment benefits and school reopenings. But that has not happened.\par \par \uc2 If you had your way, how many more people would you hire right now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Five, at least.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: In Sarasota, Florida, Mike Martin owns Reef Cakes Restaurant, but these days he`s juggling multiple jobs himself because it can`t find anyone to hire. And he`s postponed plans to open a six-day a week.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have a head chef here and he`s working about 65-plus hours a week. I`m working upwards of that, more than that. And so, it`s been -- it`s been very challenging.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Peter, what are some of the industries that are still looking to hire right now?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Yeah, Lester. Among them retail, restaurants, transportation, and healthcare that have all been raising wages and offering better benefits to try to attract and keep workers. The U.S. right now is still five million jobs below pre-pandemic levels, meaning there`s a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Peter Alexander at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020211010eha80002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Former CEO Of Pepsico Sitting Down With Sheinelle And Opening Up About Her Most Painful Parenting Moment During Her Rise To The Top\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Sheinelle Jones \par \uc2 1,296 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: We`re back. 8:36, here is Ms. Sheinelle. And you have a really empowering story on a challenge a lot of working parents know well, the attempt to balance that career and family lives.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: I can`t wait to introduce you to our next -- our guest, if you will. Indra Nooyi is the trailblazing former chairman and CEO of PepsiCo. As a woman of color and an immigrant, she says she worked twice as hard to reach the top. Her journey wasn`t an easy one but she hopes the next generation will benefit from lessons learned along the way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): As a little girl growing up in India, Indra Nooyi had big dreams. And it was the support of her family that allowed her to soar. Despite cultural expectations for women at the time, Indra`s parents prioritized her education.\par \par \uc2 INDRA NOOYI, FORMER CEO, PEPSICO: The belief was a good education is the foundation for your future.\par \par \uc2 JONES: How do they balance the cultural tug of war of making sure, as a woman, you are going to eventually find someone to get married versus education and career.\par \par \uc2 NOOYI: you know, one foot on the brake, one foot on the accelerator. I was among the early group of women to go to college, to go into business schools. And my family it was like let the girl dream.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Indra`s dream led her to the United States, in the Yale School of Management. She graduated in 1980 and spent the next decade climbing the corporate ladder, at a time women were rarely seen at the top.\par \par \uc2 When you joined \uc2 PepsiCo\uc2  in 1994, 15 of the top 15 executives were American men, not one Fortune 500 company had a female CEO. You are a woman, an immigrant, a person of color, what was it like sometimes in those boardrooms when you didn`t see faces that looked like you?\par \par \uc2 NOOYI: It actually made me work harder and better because I`d feel like people are looking at me like she must not belong here. So I always came over prepared. That would quickly establish that I was a force to be reckoned with, that I deserve to sit at the table.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): It wasn`t long before she sat at the head of the table. In 2006, she was named chairman and CEO of PepsiCo. But she already had another important title, mom. Her success at work meant sacrificing time with her family. Her husband, Raj, and their two daughters, Preetha and Tara.\par \par \uc2 NOOYI: When I became CEO, my kids were all young. So it was a difficult time, believe me. But the good thing is my office was 10 minutes from home, and the young one school was 10 minutes from the office. So I lived in that triangular bubble if you want to call it that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Trust me, a lot of us can relate.\par \par \uc2 NOOYI: That made a huge difference now because if there was a problem in school, I just ran out. I didn`t care what meeting I was in, I ran out.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I know so many women who feel like, OK, if I mastered my work today, then I feel like I failed at home. And if I was all in at home, I feel like I failed at work. And I feel like there are a lot of people who feel like they just can`t balance it all.\par \par \uc2 NOOYI: It`s a real challenge. And I don`t even use the word balance anymore. It`s like juggling. You just have to juggle everything. I think the best you can do is to say, at the end of the day I`m going to do the best I can for my family, the best I can for my job.\par \par \uc2 [08:40:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: There was a time that really kind of pulled on my heartstrings where your daughter wrote you a letter.\par \par \uc2 NOOYI: The note goes, mom, please, please, please, come home. I love you more if you came home, please, please, please.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Did it just break your heart?\par \par \uc2 NOOYI: Break my heart is understatement, but that`s reality, it`s hard. And you do the best you can.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Indra believes that accessible and affordable childcare will not only help ease the balancing act for families, but it`s also a win for society at large.\par \par \uc2 NOOYI: The big blessing was, as I moved up in the corporate career, I had money to afford my own health. Lot of people today don`t have it. Our frontline didn`t have it during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 JONES: What has to change in order to make this a reality for so many women around the world?\par \par \uc2 NOOYI: The first thing we have to do is when we talk about future of work, we have to just stop talking about technology and start to talk with families, family builders, women. Women are 70% of the high school valedictorians. They graduate in college at 10 points higher than the men. We need these women in the workforce. This whole field of woman, dream big. The world needs you, the country needs you. We`re going to create the system to support you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONES: We`d like to thank the Moxy Hotel for allowing us to film our interview there. And for more of my conversation, she was so fantastic. When we were finished, I was like will you be my auntie? (Inaudible) auntie. So we put our entire conversation, we`re going to stream it today on Today All Day at 2:30 and 6:30 Eastern.\par \par \uc2 She`s a very busy woman. She`s really trying to focus on making things better for the next generation of women. And not just women but for families because we know this. When you`re -- when you feel like you`re doing well at home, you can do better at work. So it`s not just lip service for her. She`s really trying to team up with thought leaders and try to help with --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You said the memoir was good. It wasn`t like one of these CEO boys.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s a real memoir.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I told her, we got a little teary and you`ll see this on Today All Day. When I read about her grandfather and how close she was with her grandfather made me think of my grandfather. And there were so many people who poured into her in India, who can be so proud of her.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: She was ahead of the game.\par \par \uc2 HODA: She was a trailblazer, we really need it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HODA: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Thank you, Sheinelle.\par \par \uc2 HODA: Coming up next, how great does this sound? Enrique Iglesias and Ricky Martin together in concert.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Two of the biggest names in Latin music are kicking off a worldwide tour and opening up to us about why that is so special. But first this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020210928eh9r0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Growing Debate Over Working From Home Versus The Office\b0\par\par\uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, HODA KOTB, SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, VICKY NGUYEN \par \uc2 4,198 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:13:05] CRAIG MELVIN: We are back now, coming up on 8:13, with our special series, "The Future of Work".\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Yeah, from employers to employees, we are all trying to figure out the new workplace, including the perks and the compensation.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: NBC`s investigative and consumer correspondent Vicky Nguyen with ways you can navigate the constantly changing landscape.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Vicky. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN, NBC NEWS INVESTIGATIVE AND CONSUMER CORRESONDENT: Hi, good morning you to.\par \par \uc2 Well, as the delta variant continues to surge, many companies have delayed their return to work dates. But one thing is still certain. Right now, it is an employee market. That means it`s primetime to negotiate for what you need in terms of your quality of life and monetary compensation. This morning, we are helping you get what you want from your employer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s hard to find a balance between work and home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am anxious returning to the office.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don`t know if I want to go back to the office nine to five.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): Hybrid, remote, working in the office full time. Whatever the setup, many of us are doing some career soul searching.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER, CAREER EXPERT, \uc2 LINKEDIN\uc2 : With this great reshuffle, people are really re-evaluating what they want in life.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Catherine Fisher a career expert at \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What do employees want now?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Flexibility. We have found that really one of the main factors that they are looking for in a new job is that flexibility.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: In fact, 89 percent of workers who are returning to the office say that flexibility to decide where they work is most important. And cities are taking note. Small towns across the country from Tulsa to Topeka and cities in northwest Arkansas all offering up to $10,000 to lure remote workers to move.\par \par \uc2 How specifically do you bring up this conversation with your boss so it`s successful?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: What you want to do is when you ask your boss just as you are asking for flexibility is that you are being flexible.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Give examples of how productive you`ve been working remotely. If you can`t work from home every day, ask to to start with a few days and revisit the discussion. If you are not in the office, participate in video conferences, keep in weekly contact your boss and share your successes.\par \par \uc2 [08: 15:03]\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Even if it`s just texting, hey, I closed this deal or, I wanted to make sure you saw this, it`s really important to stay top of mind.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: But choosing flexibility could come at a cost. A pay cut.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Many employers are revisiting their entire compensation scheme.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Johnny Taylor (ph) is president and CEO of the Society for Human Resource Management. What can they expect when it comes to compensation and perks?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Many organizations are saying as we would give you a premium for moving to a more expensive market, we are going to reduce your pay to move a less expensive market.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  have been doing this for years. Adjusting compensation depending on where an employee is based. \uc2 Google\uc2  telling employees who move to less expensive locations, they may lose up to 25 percent of their salaries. Despite the potential cut, since June, 10,000 \uc2 Google\uc2  employees signed up to relocate.\par \par \uc2 There are things happening to incentivize people to return to the office. People working from home want to know what are their incentives or perks. Is it equal? Should it be?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s never going to be equal. Employers should be clear about that. There are pluses and minuses to both. Coming to work or being allowed to work remotely.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: But Taylor says don`t stop asking for what you want and need.\par \par \uc2 What are the top things employees are asking for?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Dependent care number one. And that`s not just childcare. It could mean eldercare for your parents.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, flexibility. The traditional nine to five, five days a week gig done.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, mental health and wellness coverage. They want to know it`s more than just their physical bodies that come to work, but minds as well.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: What do you say to people who are skeptical, who think things are just going to go back, it`s going to be business as usual, we won`t see meaningful change?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We had a great reset called the pandemic. Nothing will be the same post-2020. So we are now saying we`ve got to change and employers are embracing it because in a knowledge-based economy, you need people and engaged people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: And \uc2 Google\uc2  told us their compensation packages have always been determined by location and that they pay at the top of the local market.\par \par \uc2 Now, something to keep in mind. Pay adjustments could also be based on a broader review of your circumstances as a employee. But, right now, there really is a war for talent.\par \par \uc2 While some companies are going to try to have you take a pay cut to relocate, you can and should negotiate. Just be polite and firm and ask for what you want.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Nice advice.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s an employee`s market. I love that.\par \par \uc2 Are there certain industries that are hiring more than others?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes, Savannah. According to the folks at \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 , job postings for financial advisors, store associates, product designers, technical project managers and tellers have had the greatest month over month increase. So those are jobs in high demand. But we are coming up on the seasonal worker time, November to December, and we are hearing they are going to need 350,000 employees in retail alone.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: And \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  is predicting the median hourly wage is going to be up from $13 last year, to $19. So it`s a good time to get out there.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I do wonder about some of these companies whether they are trying to use the adjustment in compensation to get folks back into the office.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: They are using all sorts of incentives, but what we heard in the piece and I think it`s true is, if you work from home, don`t expect to get the same perks in the office and vice versa. They are two different animals.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Thank you, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Let`s check on the weather with Ms. Dylan.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Dylan.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Good morning, again, guys.\par \par \uc2 And taking a look across the country, we do have a chance of some stronger storms today, especially through the Northern Plains. Those will fire up this afternoon. Also that flood threat on the Gulf Coast with the remnants of Nicholas still producing the potential for some storms that could end up really dumping two to three inches of rain per hour.\par \par \uc2 Texas it is still hot. Still hot in the southwest with that fire risk continuing out west as well.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s happening around the country. Here is a peek out your window.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK BELL, STORM TEAM4 METEOROLOGIST: Hey, good morning. I`m Storm Team4 meteorologist Chuck Bell. Outside of our windows here in the northwest it`s a mostly cloudy sky.\par \par \uc2 There`s a little bit more sunshine breaking through across northern Maryland and eastern West Virginia, thicker clouds across southern Maryland. Rain chances today at 60 percent, but they are a little higher if you go to southern Virginia and lower if you go out to the north and west. Temperatures today will be noticeably cooler than we haven been. We were 90 yesterday, we`ll be 82 today and for tomorrow. The sunshine and the warmer air start to return over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: And be sure to check out us out on TODAY Show radio, our SiriusXM channel 108.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you, Dylan.\par \par \uc2 You know what? It`s time for Mr. --\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Off the rails.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Best time of the day.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Favorite show ever.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Hit it.\par \par \uc2 DALY: We got a lot to get to today, a lot to debate. I hope we have time for this.\par \par \uc2 This is a "POPStart" friends loaded with lists. Who doesn`t like a good list? I know you do. We`re going to get to the top music songs in a minute.\par \par \uc2 First, we`re going to start with the 2021 finalists for the Toy Hall of Fame. That includes 12 special nominees.\par \par \uc2 We have battleship and risk, to unconventional choices this year like sand.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: What?\par \par \uc2 DALY: Sand is a toy now.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wait, is it the kind -- come on.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 DALY: Maybe next year, air will get a shot. Pinata is also on that list.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a few potential Hall of Famers here, guys, right here. The cabbage patch, 1979.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DREYER: I am surprised --\par \par \uc2 DALY: They came with their own name and adoption papers. It smells like baby powder?\par \par \uc2 No. Smells like storage.\par \par \uc2 If it`s a battle between the dolls, who would win between Cabbage Patch and American Girl doll?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Cabbage Patch.\par \par \uc2 DALY: American Girl in 1986, 18 inches tall. They come with storybooks exploring different time periods. This is Kristen, mid 19th century America.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, she is?\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yeah, how about that?\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Cabbage Patch --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: Look at her.\par \par \uc2 And last we have to mention the \uc2 Fisher-Price\uc2  corn popper. Remember this thing? This thing has been around since 1957.\par \par \uc2 Generations of toddlers have been pushing this lawnmower-esque toy around the living room, bumping into walls for many, many years.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Cool.\par \par \uc2 DALY: And then my iPhone. This toy.\par \par \uc2 Only three of these guys are going to make it into Strong`s National Toy Hall of Fame this year. The honorees will be announced may 4th. We will stay tuned for that.\par \par \uc2 Maybe "POPStart" should go live.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We should.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I agree. A special show, a very special TODAY show.\par \par \uc2 DALY: All right. Let`s get into the big list that everybody is talking about. \uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2  magazine for the first time in 17 years released the 500 greatest songs of all time. More than 250 artists, musicians and producers created this list. Write them letters. Not us.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a lot at some of the top picks. Coming in at number three, what a great choice --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Number ten.\par \par \uc2 DALY: This is not -- oh, okay. Let`s listen to a little bit of "Change is Gonna Come".\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I love this song.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 DALY: Crazy thing about this song, it came out two weeks after Sam Cooke was shot and killed at a motel ion L.A. It would have been recorded a year before. It`d be his farewell song and a song that would be anthem for civil rights.\par \par \uc2 This was his answer to Bob Dylan`s `Blowing in the Wind". There is a lot to this song. Glad that it`s number three. I think it`s a great choice.\par \par \uc2 Landing at number two, I love this one, too, this is my jam, written for the soundtrack of the Spike Lee film, "Do the Right Thing" in 1989, here is a clip.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 DALY: Savannah, fight the power.\par \par \uc2 Talk about timely, right? Everything that`s going on in the world. This is -- this song`s got lyrics, I`m black and proud and hyped because I am amped. Most of my heroes don`t appear on the stamp.\par \par \uc2 This is war cry song. This an anthem commissioned from Public Enemy for the movie which the theme was to fight white supremacy. So, you want to -- plus, this is a brand-new, really, a modern day we shall overcome, if you will. That song was 330 on the list last time. Now at number two.\par \par \uc2 Number one on the list -- yeah, so far for sure, top 500 songs. A classic. Here is number one. You can`t help but sing along. Look a this.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 DALY: That is "Respect", Aretha. 2021 shaping up to be Aretha`s year. Jennifer Hudson and Cynthia Erivo recently portraying the legend.\par \par \uc2 A few surprise from the \uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2  list of 500, I love the Outkast. "Hey Ya!" was a great song. It ends up at number ten of all time.\par \par \uc2 It is not the tenth greatest song of all time. "Imagine" by John Lennon at 19, right? Also, "Lorde" the radio hit --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: It`s a fine song. It came at number 30.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: No!\par \par \uc2 DALY: But it is not better than Bob Marley`s "Redemption Song" at number 42.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Or "Hungry Like the Wolf" at 482.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Actually, that was a good pick. That was about where it should be.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: No, stop that.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That was the only thing that got -- no Eagles, no Led Zeppelin, no Elvis -- in the top 100. "Stairway to Heaven," "November Rain," "Hotel California", nowhere in the top 50.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And that "Royals" song made it?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Who did they poll again?\par \par \uc2 DALY: I love get your freak on by the like the next guy, but better --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: "Stairway to Heaven"?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Where is get your "Freak On" on the list?\par \par \uc2 DALY: Top ten.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: No, it`s not.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Is it "Rapper`s Delight" is on the list, too?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I love "Rapper`s Delight".\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: I love Missy. I love Outkast?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You should do more of this on TODAY all day today.\par \par \uc2 DALY: I can`t. My heart hurts. I can`t breathe.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yeah, nobody do --\par \par \uc2 DALY: I forgot to meditate this morning. I didn`t do my calm app this morning, so I`m hyped up right now. My anxiety is coming through.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DALY: Anyway, "West Side Story." We have time for this?\par \par \uc2 We played a little bit of this yesterday, the trailer. I don`t think I have seen a better trailer in years. This is incredible. After several pandemic delays, Spielberg`s reimagining of the `61 classic film on its way to theaters. In a thriller for the film, we get a peek at the big screen debut of Rachel Ziegler playing Maria and Ansel Elgort as Tony. Take a look at this trailer.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO CLIP PLAYS)\par \par \uc2 DALY: "West Side Story" set to hit theaters December 10.\par \par \uc2 Michael Jackson`s Billie Jean is at 44.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: He`s still --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Billie Jean, one of the greatest opening three seconds in music history, shout out Quincy Jones, love you Outkast but --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Where is Prince?\par \par \uc2 DALY: Prince is repped. Prince is okay.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You got everybody worked up.\par \par \uc2 DALY: A little Pearl Jam, "Running with the Devil". It`s so much. Where are you?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Straight ahead, we`ve got greatness in the house this morning eager to talk about the greatest of all time. Mohammad Ali`s daughter Rashida and legendary filmmaker Ken Burns sharing a new look at the icon`s life and legacy right --\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is a News4 Today Newsbreak.\par \par \uc2 JUMMY OLABANJI, NEWS4 TODAY ANCHOR: Good morning. It`s 8:26 object this Thursday, September 16. I`m Jummy Olabanji.\par \par \uc2 In our news today, security fencing back up around the U.S. Capitol. Crews worked to protect the building ahead of a protest set for Saturday. Authorities are taking an all-hands on deck approach just in case tensions rise.\par \par \uc2 And there is new vaccine mandate for adults working in Prince William County Schools. The school board voted on that rule last night. Staff will have to show proof of vaccination by November 1 or agree to weekly COVID testing. New employees will have to do within 30 days of hire.\par \par \uc2 A check of your forecast is up next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BELL: Good morning. It`s awfully cloudy outside. There is a little filtered sunshine get through enough to cast a little bit of a shadow out there. However, these clouds will thicken again later into the afternoon and you should be expecting a chance for rain today. Temperatures are in the 60s to low 70s now. Highs today in the low 80s.\par \par \uc2 OLABANJI: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 The Washington football team will try to get the first win of the season tonight. The Burgundy and Gold hosting the \uc2 New York Giants\uc2 . Taylor Heinicke getting the start. Kickoff at 8:20 at \uc2 FedEx\uc2  field.\par \par \uc2 All right. Let`s go get `em.\par \par \uc2 You can get the latest news and forecasts anytime in the NBC Washington app. We`ll see you in 25 minutes for another update. Have a great day.\par \par \uc2 [08:30:24]\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: And welcome back to Today on this Thursday morning. Friday eve, it is September 16, 2021. And we`re all sat with a loud fantastic crowd. This is an exciting batch.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: This is a great crowd, they`re so lovely, gosh. And, guys, look who`s waiting for us inside. This is just so lovely to have these folks here with one and only Ken Burns and Rasheda Ali. They`re going to share a sneak peek at Ken`s new must see series. It`s all about one of the greatest icons of all time, Rasheda`s dad Muhammad Ali.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Plus, we are stepping up our "Come with Us" celebration of Hispanic Heritage Month. This morning, we`re checking in with Telemundo`s chef Alfredo Oropeza to get his recipes for a delicious menu of quesadillas and Mexican red rice.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Nice.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Coming up in just a few minutes, by the way, in the third hour, we`re getting ready for another celebration. That`s right. Ladies an