our window.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK BELL, STORM TEAM 4 METEOROLOGIST: Good morning. I`m Storm Team 4 Meteorologist Chuck Bell. Temperatures around 70 right now, will be up in near 82 for a high today. You`ll notice that that`s eight degrees cooler than that 90 that we had yesterday. There`s also a 60% chance of rain today.\par \par \uc2 Rain starting to develop now across parts of Central Virginia, will pinwheel up in our direction, could even have a rambler to have thunder as we get into the afternoon. No severe weather to worry about just a couple of thunder showers today. Tomorrow, no Thunder at all but it`s still another chance for a little passing raindrop or two. And most of your weekend is going to be warm and dry.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: That`s your latest forecast.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re just commenting the song I can`t get out of my head is hey.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s "True."\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s "True" right? All right, guys, something big happened this.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But higher than "Billy Jean." No, I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Daily Mail TV is returning for its fifth season.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And back as host, our good friend, our pal Thomas Roberts. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS ROBERTS, DAILY MAIL HOST: It`s so good to see all the heya (ph).\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Don`t you love that, come on.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It`s so good to see you guys all in-person. And Dylan`s pregnant, I guess.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Again.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It`s amazing. But yes, we are fired up for season five and we got started off with premiere week this week. We have an exclusive with Jake Paul today. Jake Paul is just 24 years old, has been part of our kind of everyday lexicon for the past decade. He`s kind of this bad boy. Well, now he`s in the ring.\par \par \uc2 He`s a good fighter. He`s really good fighter. And he is trying to get a little redemption tour going. We`ll see what happens there. But then, an old colleague of ours here at NBC, that then went over to ABC and just left "The View."\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Meghan McCain.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, she`s with you?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: She`s with us, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: What she`s going to do? She`s going to correspond --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That`s what I`m calling her to do, yes. But she`s going to be a columnist. Her column starts next week on Daily Mail, but I`m going to convince her to come on with me as much as possible. But I`m going to first sit down with her to talk about what it`s like to leave "The View" and the way that she did, and what she wants to do next.\par \par \uc2 And she is typically, you know, straight from the hip. Straight from the hip. And she is authentically Meghan as Meghan ever was, but she feels very free right now. That`s what I`ll say.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She`s a darling, new baby too.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: She does "\uc2 Liberty\uc2 ," yes. There`s about to be one.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: This should certainly does have something for everyone. What else you have coming up this season?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Everything. It`s like TV dinners when we were kids, right? So we had the brownie, right? We had the meatloaf , we have --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The Salisbury steak.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes. We got the vegetables, anything like that. And sometimes we do start with a dessert. We start with the brownie first.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, I like that.\par \par \uc2 [08:35:05]\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And then sometimes we start with the meatloaf, and we`ve got a lot of exclusives coming up and we`re coming out of the gate hot. It is season five, my second season back.\par \par \uc2 Melvin: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So it`s very exciting. And plus, I get to come in now and see you guys in person.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: I know, it`s great.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And by the way, we can let you leave without commenting on how beautiful that suit is. That is a beautiful suit.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I tweeted you. I was going to wear the bluest thing I got.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That is beautiful. You always bring a smurf. Thomas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 By the way, folks, be sure to look out for Daily Mail TV, just check your local listing.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Thank you, Thomas. Coming up next, Ken Burns and Rasheda Ali on the greatest, Muhammad Ali and a legacy that extends far beyond the boxing ring. But first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END \par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020210918eh9g0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Billions In Wages Are Stolen From Workers In The U.S. Every Year\b0\par\par\uc2 Tom Llamas, Cynthia McFadden \par \uc2 1,175 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS: Stephanie, thank you. With the spotlight on jobs this Labor Day, imagine working and never getting paid. It`s a growing problem called wage theft, especially during rebuilding efforts after disasters. Our Cynthia McFadden started investigating last year after a powerful storm with through Iowa.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA MCFADDEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL AND INVESTIGATIVE CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Last summer, in the midst of the pandemic, a historic storm last through Cedar Rapids, Iowa, leaving billions of dollars in damages across the state.\par \par \uc2 When you could tell, how many buildings were damaged.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, there was another banner right there at the corner. Yes, they got completely destroyed.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Pablo Ramirez hired a group of construction workers from Texas and Mexico to come help. Most of the workers did not speak English. He says he did not ask their immigration status. Ramirez hired the man as a labor broker for Blue Sky, a restoration construction company based in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 Ramirez says he and the worker stayed here at one of the sites they were supposed to rehab. The condition, deplorable.\par \par \uc2 They were not rentable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They were not rentable.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:04]\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: For over three weeks, the men say they work without getting paid by Ramirez or Blue \uc2 Sky\uc2  which had estimated revenues last year of $100 million. By the end of the month, they`ve run out of food.\par \par \uc2 Royce Peterson is the head of the local carpenters union. In desperation, the workers turned to him for help.\par \par \uc2 ROYCE PETERSON, BUSINESS REPRESENTATIVE, UNITED BROTHERHOOD OF CARPENTERS: We found out that these six workers hadn`t eaten for days.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Nothing?\par \par \uc2 PETERSON: Nothing. I said go buy them some groceries. Let`s get these guys fed.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Even though they weren`t union members.\par \par \uc2 PETERSON: We`re out there to fight for the workers against unscrupulous contractors. And no matter who you are, for union or not.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: When Ramirez told Blue \uc2 Sky\uc2  the Union was involved, he says, they told him to fire the workers who now had no food, no cash, and no place to live.\par \par \uc2 This is where the guys stayed, this -- you welcomed the six.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. This is where they came.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Reverend John Green opened his church to the workers who slept among the pews that first night.\par \par \uc2 How long did it take for you to decide you were going to cook them a meal?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Less than five minutes.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: You gave them the keys to the church?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I did.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Literally.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They weren`t strangers. You know, they were just people I didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They asked for full and immediate payment --\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Meanwhile, a picket line was set up outside the worker`s job site. The demand --\par \par \uc2 PETERSON: Pay them. Pay them the back wages and pay them for their travel expenses.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Facing mounting backlash, two Blue \uc2 Sky\uc2  executives flew in agreeing to pay the workers after a day of negotiations in exchange for non-disclosure agreements, which the victims are usually low wage workers, sometimes undocumented, who are often too intimidated to complain.\par \par \uc2 Experts say some companies hide behind labor brokers claiming they never promised the workers anything. In a statement to NBC News, Blue \uc2 Sky\uc2  says their labor broker was the problem. The issue in Cedar Rapids arose because Mr. Ramirez failed to pay the people he personally hired despite Blue \uc2 Sky\uc2  having paid Mr. Ramirez for that same work.\par \par \uc2 Blue Sky says you`re the one who messed this up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, they can say whatever they want, but they know the truth.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: It`s estimated over $15 billion is stolen from workers every year.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN CLARK-BENNETT, DIRECTOR, UNIVERSITY OF IOWA LABOR CENTER: It truly is an invisible epidemic. Some of the wealthiest investors in the world are profiting from the work of some of the lowest paid workers.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Investors like Dominus Capital, a New York-based private equity firm which is the majority owner of Blue \uc2 Sky\uc2 . Dominus did not respond to numerous requests for comment. And Blue \uc2 Sky\uc2  declined to be interviewed. But Pablo Ramirez who says, because of all of this, he has lost his home, did agree to talk.\par \par \uc2 Did you feel guilty?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I feel guilty for bringing my own people here, to have them work and not getting paid.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN (0n camera): When we contacted Blue \uc2 Sky\uc2 , they announced they`d be expanding their existing oversight program to ensure that partners paid workers fairly accurately and on time. Meanwhile, we`ve learned that city officials have Blue \uc2 Sky\uc2  under scrutiny in this city of Minneapolis. Tom?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Cynthia, thanks for that. We are back in a moment with incredible footage of a rescue of a little boy lost in the Australian wilderness for more than three days. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Back now with news of an incredible rescue in Australia where a 3- year-old boy has been missing for three days. A helicopter crew finding little A.J., you see him there, who has autism and is nonverbal sitting by a creek drinking water. More than 130 people assisted in this rescue which AJ`S father called in Miracle.\par \par \uc2 Now to our TV exclusive. Her allegations of sexual assault sent Bill Cosby to prison. Now, more than two months after Cosby`s conviction was overturned, and he was set free. Andrea Constand is breaking her silence to our Kate Snow.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA CONSTAND, ACCUSER OF BILL COSBY: I was really shocked. I was really shocked, disappointed.\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: What does it say about the American justice system?\par \par \uc2 CONSTAND: That it`s flawed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: And you can see much more of Kate`s interview with Andrea Constand tomorrow on Today. And right here on NIGHTLY NEWS.\par \par \uc2 And tributes are pouring in tonight for actor Michael K. Williams, famous for his breakout role as Omar Little on TV`s The Wire, one of the series` most memorable characters. Williams also starred in Boardwalk Empire and his Emmy nominated right now for his work on Lovecraft Country.\par \par \uc2 Williams was found dead in his Brooklyn apartment. No cause of death has been released. He was 54 years old.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, NBC`s Al Roker remembers his mentor, Willard Scott and the lessons and legacy he left behind. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020210907eh960005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Working From Home Has Become The New Normal And Many Want To Keep It That Way\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Tom Costello \par \uc2 421 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 August 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HOLT: For millions, working from home has become the new normal and many want to keep it that way. But as Tom Costello reports, that could mean a pay cut for some.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Paul Sherman says moving to Florida during the pandemic was life-changing. Leaving snowy Chicago behind to work for the same employer from home in Sarasota.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I feel like I live in paradise. Blood pressure is lower, family is happy. We spend so much time outside.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Millions of Americans have made similar moves leaving big city offices for the suburbs or smaller towns, even when it`s meant to taking a pay cut. \uc2 Google\uc2  telling employees who moved to less expensive locations, they may lose up to a quarter of their salaries. \uc2 Google\uc2  tells NBC News, "Our compensation packages have always been determined by location. And we always pay at the top of the local market based on where an employee works from."\par \par \uc2 Other companies including \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  have made similar announcements. Since June, 10,000 \uc2 Google\uc2  employees have signed up, even though working from home is not cost-free.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:06]\par \par \uc2 TSEDAL NEELEY, PROFESSOR, HARVARD BUSINESS SCHOOL: There`s a big cost to working. You`re giving up real estate. You`re displacing parts of your household. And you`re dedicating space for work that companies have to account for in their calculus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Still, a recent survey found 75 percent of employees asked we`re willing to give up benefits like cash bonuses and time off in exchange for the freedom to choose where they work. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Tom Costello, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A long-awaited victory for Britney Spears tonight. Court documents show the pop stars father Jamie Spears has agreed to eventually step down as her conservator after 13 years though the conservatorship remains in place. Last month, Spears accused her father of abusing the arrangement. He has denied any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020210813eh8c000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House To Develop Antiviral COVID Pills As Delta Variant Spreads\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Tom Costello \par \uc2 447 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART (00:08:14): Federal health officials announced a major effort today to develop medication that could one day be used to treat COVID and other viruses. We get more from NBC`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO (00:08:26): With COVID likely to remain a threat for years to come, the White House and \uc2 NIH\uc2  say they will spend 3.2 billion dollars to develop new anti-viral over-the-counter pills to treat COVID and future viruses at home, keeping people out of ERs. Meanwhile, the dangerous new COVID delta variant is spreading fast. Now ten percent of all U.S. cases. But in young people, the early symptoms can be confused for a cold, headache, sore throat, runny nose and fever.\par \par \uc2 DR. VIVEK MURTHY (U.S. Surgeon General) (00:08:55): If you are vaccinated, you are protected. If you are not, the threat of the variants is real and growing.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO (00:09:01): But vaccination rates are slowing across the country. Just fifty-two percent of those twelve and older fully vaccinated. States with the most fully vaccinated citizens are in the Northeast and the West, while the South and Mountain West have the fewest. Among Latinos, only twenty-eight percent have received just a single dose. Meanwhile, many employees working from home seem reluctant to return to work. One report says less than a third of white-collar workers are back at the office in big cities. Morgan Stanley`s CEO told employees this week he expects most of them back by Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 JAMES GORMAN\uc2  (Morgan Stanley CEO; \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 ) (00:09:35): You can go to a restaurant in New York City. You can come to the office. But Labor Day, I`ll be very disappointed if people haven`t found their way into the office and then we`ll have a different kind of conversation.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO (00:09:46): But after a year of working from home, many workers are reluctant to give up that freedom, start looking for childcare and resume their normal commutes. Jose.\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART (00:09:57): Tom Costello, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and program