e a work space tour with the help of a germ meter to find out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN (08:10:06): As many as seventy percent of employees reported working from home this past year. And now some companies say employee can say work remotely as long as they want. In many offices, even before the pandemic, cleaning crews sanitized desks daily. But can we say the same for our home offices? You know, the ones we share with kids, pets and our meals?\par \par \uc2 (08:10:28): So how dirty is your work-from-home desk? To find out, we`re using this device. It tests for invisible living cells. In short, it`s a germ meter and the higher the number, the dirtier the surface.\par \par \uc2 (08:10:39): I swabbed my keyboard.\par \par \uc2 (08:10:41): This is where I let my fingers do the walking. Mouse conveniently located next to my open food. And desk.\par \par \uc2 (08:10:48): All right, here we go. It`s not promising when there`s actually visible dirt on the swab.\par \par \uc2 (08:10:56): Next, I prepped the samples by breaking this tube. It releases a reagent that mixes with whatever is on the swab.\par \par \uc2 (08:11:02): I know the results are going to be bad because I`ve cleaned this area exactly this many times.\par \par \uc2 (08:11:06): In a matter of seconds-- (08:11:07): I got a result of one thousand five hundred thirteen.\par \par \uc2 (08:11:12): For reference, hospitals are expected to keep their high-touch surfaces below one hundred. We don`t expect to have the same level of cleanliness in the average home, but fifteen hundred indicates my keyboard could use some cleaning. My house slightly lower at eleven forty-four, but my desk?\par \par \uc2 (08:11:28): Whoa, this one by far, seventy-five hundred six.\par \par \uc2 (08:11:33): To help me out my generous teammates on the consumer investigative team agreed to swap their work spaces. Many of their numbers were even higher than mine.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1 (08:11:42): Oh, gross. Thirty-