kay.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:23:09): Right.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:23:08): You`re right after your local news. In fact, we`ll see you in just a few.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS) CRAIG MELVIN (08:30:02): Welcome back. Eight-thirty on this Wednesday morning, the 9th of June, 2021. We appreciate you stopping in for a bit.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:30:08): I know our little sunshiny corner of the world this morning. Coming up, Harry Smith will be along. He`s talking to Tribeca Festival founders Robert De Niro and Jane Rosenthal ahead of opening night for its twentieth year. Harry is going to show us how its highlighting New York in a whole new way this time around. Good morning, Harry. We`re looking forward.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS (08:30:27): Yeah, plus, a special story as we celebrate Pride Month. Joe Fryer will introduce us to a couple who went to great lengths to get married fifty years ago, now sharing their journey and a history-making milestone.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY (08:30:38): Oh, very cool. Also ahead the restaurant industry is bouncing back. You need proof? Well, our friend Anthony Scotto is live at Fresco by Scotto for the reopening.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:30:45): Yay.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:30:46): All right. Anthony. (Cross talking)\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY (08:30:49): And this morning he`s sharing the recipe for one of his most popular dishes. Can`t wait for that.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:30:55): Yeah, I miss Anthony.\par \par \uc2 (08:30:56): And then coming up in just a few minutes on the 3rd Hour, guys, one of the newest members of Team USA, superstar surfer Carissa Moore, she is going to join us live and tell us how she`s preparing to make waves in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES (08:31:09): Wow.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:31:08): She makes that look easy, doesn`t she? SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:31:09): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:31:11): Very cool. And speaking of fun, tomorrow we`ve got the fun and it is going to be epic. Dylan and Kerry Sanders heading to Universal Orlando Resort for the grand opening of the Jurassic World Veloci-Coaster.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY (08:31:25): Nice.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:31:26): It is going to be Florida`s fastest, tallest and most intense launch coaster. And, Kerry is going to try it with a good buddy who brings the laughs, SNL`s Kenan Thompson. We should also mention Universal Orlando Resort, part of our parent company NBC Universal.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:31:45): Okay. Good. Okay, Kerry and Kenan will ride the coaster.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:31:47): Yes. AL ROKER (08:31:48): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:31:47): Because Dylan is pregnant.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS (08:31:49): Yeah, I was wondering about that.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:31:50): No coaster for you. But she likes a coaster, though.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:31:52): She loves it.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:31:53): Jealous.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:31:54): Coming back next year.\par \par \uc2 (Cross talking)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:31:55): Al, let`s get a check of your forecast.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:31:55): You bet. But don`t we want to celebrate something?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:32:00): Yeah. we did show this morning.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:32:01): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:32:01): Our little show.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:32:03): Oh, what? I actually don`t know this.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:32:04): Our little show.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:32:05): It`s the winner of the Gracie Award for best news program.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:32:08): Remember that?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:32:09): Did you know that?\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS (08:32:10): I`d only--\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:32:10): Did you know that?\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS (08:32:12): I did not know from eight o`clcok.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:32:12): For winning in media foundation, the Gracies honoring The Achievements of Women in Media.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY (08:32:18): Nice.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:32:18): And we are delighted to be among this year`s honorees. Although, this morning it seems like--\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS (08:32:24): --celebrate,\par \par \uc2 (Cross talking)\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:32:24): Singing it`s raining men.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:32:27): Yeah, I know exactly, ironic, but that`s wonderful.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:32:30): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:32:30): Congrats to the TODAY Show, the best team in the business. We all know it, our producers and staff, everybody. Wow.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:32:34): Way to go, guys.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:32:35): Yay.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:32:35): There you go.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:32:36): Mister Roker, how about a check of the weather now?\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:32:38): Now we`ll take a look and show you what`s going on as far as your weekend is concerned. We like to look ahead. Heavy rain along the mid-Atlantic coast on Friday, but cooler in the Northeast. Record heat continues in the Southern Plains. Saturday spring sunshine, heat wave still. Warmer out west. And then on Sunday, Sunday, we`re looking at some gulf storms down through the Gulf Coast. Hotter Out West. We`re looking at fire danger continuing throughout much of the West Coast, although the Pacific Northwest, we`ll be looking at some rain.\par \par \uc2 (08:33:04): That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (Weather follows)\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:33:39): And you know what put us over the top on the Gracies? The Savannah-Versary.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:33:42): No. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS (08:33:43): Savannah-Versary\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY (08:33:42): That`s a steal the deal.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (08:33:44): It`s over. Okay.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (08:33:45): It`s never over, never over.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (08:33:47): When we come back, we are going to mark twenty years of the Tribeca Festival. Harry Smith getting a sneak peek at tonight`s opening, and why this opening is so special for Hollywood. He sat down with the founders of the festival, Robert De Niro and Jane Rosenthal. But first this is TODAY on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020210714eh69000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Battle Now Erupting At Some Of Country`s Biggest Companies Over The Return To The Office\b0\par\par\uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, TOM LLAMAS, CRAIG MELVIN, HALLIE JACKSON \par \uc2 992 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (07:32:13): Now to a question a lot of people are grappling with as the country begins to reopen, have you been happier working from home? Yes. TOM\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS (07:32:19): Yeah. I think a lot of people are saying, yes. After a year unlike any other, many companies and their employees are now trying to figure out what the new normal look like. And it`s leading to a raging debates at companies both big and small.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (07:32:30): NBC`s senior Washington correspondent Halie Jackson has a closer look for us this morning. Hallie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON (NBC News Senior Washington Correspondent) (07:32:35): Hey, guys, good morning. So some of the recent polling on this shows one in four employees would rather keep working remotely full-time but the question now is whether their bosses will actually let them with some companies looking to get staffers back at their desks and making it mandatory.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN (07:32:52): I don`t know how to remove it. HALLIE JACKSON (07:32:54): From video conferencing mishaps and Zoom fatigue to sweatpants and an easy commute, many Americans have found working from home a mixed blessing. Some eager to go back, others feeling a little like this.\par \par \uc2 (07:33:07): (Excerpt from The Office)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON (07:33:09): Bloomberg reporting a survey and a thousand adults showed thirty-nine percent would consider quitting if their employees were not flexible about remote work. And some may not be. Some high-profile bosses recently suggesting they`re looking for employees in the office, like the head of \uc2 JP Morgan Chase\uc2  who`s ready to say so long to video chats for good.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2  (Wall Street Journal) (07:33:30): I`m about to cancel all my Zoom-ins. I just think there are just-- I`m done with it.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON (07:33:34): In DC, the staff of the Washingtonian magazine went on a short strike after its publisher wrote an op-ed suggesting corporate managers have a strong incentive to demote employees who choose to keep working from home. There may be a generational divide too. Bloomberg reporting millennials and Gen Z feel more strongly about remote work flexibility. One worker say, "They feel like we`re not working if they can`t see us. It`s a boomer power-play." In a hard-charging Silicon Valley, a back-to-work backlash brewing at Apple with some employees upset they`ll have to return to the office three days a week starting in September. According to a Verge report, Lindsay Duran, chief marketing officer at Zilliant, believes office incentives could help make the transition easier, which is partly why her company is restarting their Taco Tuesday tradition.\par \par \uc2 LINDSAY DURAN (07:34:25): Some work activities in particular are-- are easier to do in-person such as brainstorming on a white board. For example, that`s a bit more difficult over a Zoom call or a Team`s meeting.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (07:34:39): But Hal-- it`s such an interesting debate, Hallie. But when people do come back to the office, if they do, it`s probably going to look a little different?\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON (07:34:46): Yeah, probably. You`ve got some offices hoping it will feel a little more like the before times, right? Whether it`s Taco Tuesday like you just heard or events like Happy Hours, etc. But you`ve got some companies that might choose to keep in place certain safety precautions. You might see something like staggered seating, staggered schedules. Teams or Zoom, I hate to be the bearer of bad news, probably is not going to disappear altogether, but the shared office birthday cake might or at least the blowing out of candles getting on people`s comfort levels. Guys.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (07:35:14): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (07:35:15): Well, and tacos. You know, tacos do make a lot better. Thank you, Hallie. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (07:35:19): Do you guys enjoy working from home?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (07:35:21): No, I missed everybody. CRAIG MELVIN (07:35:22): No.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS (07:35:23): I had little kids, so it was hard.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (07:35:24): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS (07:35:24): Definitely hard.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (07:35:25): I loved it.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (07:35:27): As Roker famously says though, I can`t miss if you don`t leave.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (07:35:30): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (07:35:31): So. You didn`t enjoy working home?\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (07:35:33): No, I absolutely did. It was great.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (07:35:35): He loved it.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER (07:35:36): It was fantastic. I had the studio set up in the garage. It was fantastic.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS (07:35:41): The great debate. SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (07:35:42): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS (07:35:43): Up next, everything you need to know about the first new treatment for Alzheimer`s to be approved in the U.S. in nearly two decades.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN (07:35:51): The hope it`s offering to millions of families this morning and why critics are worried it could actually do more harm than good. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020210714eh680002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The President`s Massive Infrastructure Plan, Which Called For Nearly Two Trillion Dollars In New Spending\b0\par\par\uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, GEOFF BENNETT \par \uc2 922 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (07:09:04): All right. We got a lot more to get to this morning, including another source of debate in Washington. The President`s massive infrastructure plan, which called for nearly two trillion dollars in new spending. Well, after weeks of negotiation, the White House is now offering concessions to Republicans on Capitol Hill. NBC`s White House correspondent Geoff Bennett is in Delaware traveling with the President, and has got the details this morning. Hi, Geoff. Morning.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT (07:09:28): Hey, Savannah, good morning. President Biden is looking to strike a deal on what he hopes will be his signature legislative achievement, that`s infrastructure. He`s set to speak again today with Senate Republicans` chief negotiator, Senator \uc2 Shelley Moore Capito\uc2  of West Virginia. Now, both sides say the talks are moving forward but they`re still very far apart on how much to spend and how to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT (07:09:50): This morning, pressure is mounting on President Biden and Senate Republicans to make a deal on infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (07:09:57): Does anyone doubt this whole nation will be better off with these investments?\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT (07:10:02): But both sides appear miles apart. The President`s latest offer carries a 1.7 trillion-dollar price tag, targeting traditional projects like roads and bridges, along with billions for other areas, including caregiving for the elderly and disabled, modernizing the electric grid and public school upgrades. Republicans` counteroffer is nearly a trillion dollars for roads and bridges, and rail. But unlike Mister Biden`s plan, nothing for projects outside of traditional infrastructure. The biggest sticking point remains how to pay for it all. The President`s latest offer to republicans, a fifteen percent minimum corporate tax rate along with tougher tax enforcement on the highest earners. That`s without rolling back the 2017 tax cuts, widely considered the GOP`s red line.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  (07:10:51): What the President believes is one that corporations can afford to pay a little bit more, and that`s a way that can-- we can pay for a range of the bold proposals that he has put forward.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT (07:11:02): And while the Senate`s top Republican says he`s hopeful a deal can get done, he`s flatly dismissing the President`s offer, arguing any tax hikes are out of the question.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY; Senate Minority Leader) (07:11:12): I don`t think that`s going to appeal to members of my party, and I think it will be a hard sell to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT (07:11:20): With time running short, many Democrats are now focusing on passing the President`s plan using the process known as reconciliation, which requires only fifty-one votes, without Republican support. But Senate Democrat Joe Manchin tells NBC`s \uc2 Garrett Haake\uc2  that`s a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (07:11:36): Right now, basically, we need to be bipartisan. If we can`t become-- I`ve always said this, I`ve never seen a pothole that had a Republican or Democrat name on it. (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT (07:11:45): Now, the White House says it`s willing to give these bipartisan talks until early next week before they decide whether to focus on trying to pass this with just democratic votes alone. Savannah.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (07:11:55): All right. And another topic, Geoff, while we`ve got you, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  in the headlines and making some comments about the January 6th riots and the former president. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT (07:12:05): Yeah. So the former VP is now offering his most direct comments yet about what transpired on that day. You`ll remember that he was rushed from the Senate Capitol as pro-Trump rioters calling for his death stormed the halls. Now, Pence last night called that day a dark day in the history of the U.S. Capitol, and he said that he and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  don`t agree on what happened.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE\uc2  (07:12:26): You know, President Trump and I have spoken many times since we left office, and I don`t know if we`ll ever see eye-to-eye on that day.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT (07:12:38): Now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has continued to criticize Pence and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  for allowing Congress to certify the 2020 election results. And those comments last night from Pence in New Hampshire, Savannah, are adding speculation to the notion that he might run for president himself in 2024. Savannah, Hoda.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE (07:12:54): All right. Geoff Bennett with all the headlines, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB (07:12:56): All right. Coming up on thirteen minutes after the hour, now to some mixed milestones when it comes to the coronavirus. Craig is here with that.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Copy: Content and programming copyright 2021 \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2 . ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content.>\par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020210710eh64000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Visit To Zoom Towns, The Places Where Americans Are Moving For A Better Quality Of Life With Remote Work Freeing Them From Expensive Cities\b0\par\par\uc2 WILLIE GEIST, CATIE BECK \par \uc2 2,103 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST (08:41:03): When the country ground to a halt in March of 2020, most of us thought our lives were on a temporary hold. But as the months wore on, we began to wonder if we were changing for good. Would work, school, travel, social life or even home life ever be the same? Some Americans have taken the pandemic as an opportunity to reevaluate and to pull up stakes. NBC`s Catie Beck has our Sunday Spotlight.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:41:32): Daily life for the Sherman family might look a lot like a vacation.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SHERMAN (08:41:36): We spend most of our time outside or by the pool or boating. It`s true paradise.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:41:41): But this is a permanent paradise after the family relocated to Sarasota, Florida from Chicago.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SHERMAN (08:41:48): When COVID hit, it was pretty much like being in lockdown, you know, like being in prison in our house.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:41:56): So when Paul`s company told employees to work from home, the Sherman family decided to quickly find a new one.\par \par \uc2 (08:42:03): So basically uproot the two kids, the two dogs and you guys and move from Chicago to Florida in forty-five days?\par \par \uc2 CINDY SHERMAN (08:42:09): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SHERMAN (08:42:10): Yep, that was how quick it was.\par \par \uc2 CINDY SHERMAN (08:42:11): Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:42:11): The move brought them closer to family in Florida and closer to each other. Thanks to all the family activity outdoors.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SHERMAN (08:42:18): The first thing everybody asks is what is that noise of water running in the background.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:42:22): As for work, Paul now does Zoom calls from his backyard pool.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SHERMAN (08:42:25): I wouldn`t change it. I love it.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:42:29): Remote possibilities created by COVID now creating major migration across the country. Virtual employees resettling where they want to live instead of where they have to work. From Sarasota`s beaches to Butte, Montana`s mountains, these desired hot spots from Austin to Charleston now termed Zoom towns, named by the popular videoconferencing app and a throwback reference to the boom towns out west. KRISTEN SRUR (Florida Realtor) (08:42:55): They`re just reaching out and we are trying to find them the properties. Some of them buy it sight unseen.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:43:00): Some Zoom towns reporting home sales up thirty percent this year. \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  reports similar trends as guests seek longer term stays. A \uc2 New York Times\uc2  survey found one in three workers would move to a new city or state if remote working continues.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN SRUR (08:43:15): It`s super competitive. I think we are attracting anybody from a big city or anybody looking for outdoor space.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:43:24): Irene Becklund and her husband lived in San Francisco for thirteen years.\par \par \uc2 IRENE BECKLUND (08:43:28): COVID actually changed everything for us.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:43:31): Their seven-hundred-square-foot apartment too close for comfort. The couple said goodbye to the Golden State and bought a house in Bend, Oregon.\par \par \uc2 IRENE BECKLUND (08:43:40): I just was craving a little more nature and a little more hikes and little more just outdoor activities.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:43:47): Now they`ve got it. Plus, a bigger more affordable home.\par \par \uc2 (08:43:51): Do you feel a certain new freedom being able to sort of pick up and go like this?\par \par \uc2 IRENE BECKLUND (08:43:55): Absolutely. It just, you know, was a game- changer I think in our personal lives.\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK (08:44:00): Many now working from a better version of home.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SHERMAN (08:44:03): We love it. We`re always outdoors and active. And I`d say my blood pressure and stress level has dropped so much. CATIE BECK (08:44:09): Finding balance and bigger joys in everyday life.\par \par \uc2 (08:44:14): For Sunday TODAY, Catie Beck, Sarasota, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST (08:44:18): Catie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (08:44:20): This week, we highlight another Life Well Lived. Just before eleven o`clock Eastern Time on the night of July 20, 1969, Neil Armstrong became the first person ever to walk on the moon.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NEIL ARMSTRONG (08:44:34): That`s one step for man, one giant leap for mankind.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST (08:44:40): Buzz Aldrin followed shortly after and the pair became household names around the world. But that moment in human history would not have been possible without a third astronaut on the mission named Michael Collins. Lieutenant Colonel Collins piloted the Columbia command module from which Armstrong and Aldrin descended to the moon. While they walked the surface, Collins orbited at thirty-seven hundred miles an hour, losing communication and going completely dark each time he passed to the far side of the moon. He later wrote about his quiet terror that a technical problem might strand Armstrong and Aldrin on the moon, a quarter of a million miles from home. The docking, though, was perfect and the astronauts returned home as heroes, receiving the Presidential Medal of Freedom from President Nixon. Collins, who was born into a military family, graduated from West Point in 1952 and became a fighter pilot in the Air Force. While serving as a test pilot, he dreamed of going to space, inspired by President Kennedy`s 1961 pledge to put a man on the moon and by John Glenn`s solo orbit of earth a year later. In 1963, Collins was chosen by \uc2 NASA\uc2  in the third ever class of astronauts. He spent three days in space with the Gemini 10 mission before joining the Apollo 11 team bound for the moon. After retiring from \uc2 NASA\uc2 , Collins became the inaugural director of the Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum in Washington while actively avoiding the celebrity status earned by Aldrin and Armstrong. Michael Collins, who flew America to the moon, died on Wednesday in Naples, Florida. He was ninety years old.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST (08:49:36): It is time for the Highs and Lows of the week. And our first high goes to golfer Michael Visacki who this week lived out his dream of competing on the PGA Tour. On Monday, Visacki sank a dramatic twenty-foot putt on the last hole of a qualifier to earn a spot in his first ever PGA tournament. The twenty-seven-year-old turned pro in 2013 but never had qualified to play with the big boys. Visacki stepped off that green and called his dad to tell him the big news.\par \par \uc2 (08:50:07): (Excerpt from internet video)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST (08:50:31): That father-son clip went viral this week, but Visacki was quick to point out he also talked to his mom who he says cried even more. The golfer lives with his parents in Sarasota, Florida, says they have sacrificed everything for him over the years to help him pursue his dream. And it turns out Big Mike, as he`s known, wasn`t just happy to be there, he played well. On Thursday, Visacki made his tour debut at the Valspar Championship by blasting a three-hundred-thirty-four-yard drive off of the first tee and finishing the hole with a birdie. His first hole on tour. He shot a seventy-four that day and improved to an impressive seventy-one on Friday. But just missed the cut for the weekend. I hope we see a lot more of Big Mike out on tour.\par \par \uc2 (08:51:16): Our first low goes to getting stuck at the top of one of the world`s tallest roller coasters and even worse having to walk back down to earth. Riders in the front row captured the ordeal showing the group stranded on a coaster called The Big One at Blackpool Pleasure Beach Theme Park in the UK. They were just seconds from the ride`s two-hundred-thirty- five-foot peak when it suddenly screeched to a halt. Honestly, they seemed pretty relaxed for being suspended two hundred feet in the air, just kind of taking in the view, making sure they grab some content for social. The riders eventually were informed the only solution here was to climb out of the cars and to walk down step by step, clinging to the railing of a narrow staircase. Oh, my goodness. Park staff made sure that they could get up there and made the trek to escort riders down as the harrowing sight was captured by visitors on the ground. Some real action park energy there. My Jersey people know what I mean.\par \par \uc2 (08:52:16): Our next high goes to a sight that never gets old, grandparents reuniting with their grandchildren after this long year apart. Bonnie Pittman had been sick and had not seen any of her grandkids since the pandemic began. This week, her son David and his seven-year-old daughter Frannie flew from New York to Dallas to surprise Bonnie for her seventy- fifth birthday.\par \par \uc2 (08:52:39): (Excerpt from internet video)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST (08:53:02): It had been fourteen months since Frannie got to hug her nana. Bonnie said she`s never been so surprised in her life, and her heart exploded from the joy she was feeling in that absolutely beautiful moment. Ours are exploding, too, Bonnie.\par \par \uc2 (08:53:18): Our final low this week goes to the latest in a series of in- studio incidents where meteorologists are attacked by giant winged creatures. Those big monitors piping in the live outdoor weather camera have led to scares with birds, bees and spiders which are attracted to the camera and then magnified to the horror of weathermen and women around the country. This week, the victim was Fox40 Sacramento meteorologist Adam Epstein. Watch your back, Adam.\par \par \uc2 ADAM EPSTEIN (08:53:47): Today would be a nice day to eat outside but you might want a wind screen later in the afternoon. Whoa, whoa, whoa. What is going on? Never seen that before. Never.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST (08:54:01): As the studio erupted into laughter, Adam checked his vitals and then soldiered on with the forecast, like the pro he is. I just love that for a split second in all of our brains it is possible there suddenly is a ten-foot pterodactyl flying into the studio.\par \par \uc2 (08:54:18): We`ll be right back with your Sunday Mug Shots.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST (08:57:40): We got more of your Sunday TODAY Mug Shots this week starting straight across the top there`s Tunde, Jeannie and Abbey with service dogs Jacob, Baldy and Charlie as Veterans Moving Forward headquarters in Dulles, Virginia. Great work, guys. Glenn and Patti in Carlsbad, California. Marie and her horse Dublin taking a sip out of the mug in Monetta, South Carolina. Laura and her co-workers at UNC Health Care Chapel Hill in North Carolina. Thanks for what you do, guys. Down along the bottom, there is Kassandra in Maryland; Ginger and Nancy in Portland, Maine; John and his new baby girl Kooper in Webster Groves, Missouri. And a first for us as the Star Wars-themed Sylvia and Russell check in, they say, from Tatooine drinking a Sunday Mug of Bantha milk. May the force be with you as well, Sylvia and Russell. The bar has been raised. Send us a photo of you and your mug with the #SundayTODAY. You might see yourself next week. And remember, you can get the Sunday TODAY mug online at today.com/shop.\par \par \uc2 (08:58:43): Thank you for spending part of your morning with us. We`ll see you right back here next week on Sunday TODAY.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020210607eh52000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Is Rush Hour Traffic Gone Forever?\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Jacob Ward \par \uc2 697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 March 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART (00:21:32): Now to the future of travel. Until the pandemic, daily rush hour traffic is one of the few guarantees in life. Last year, traffic plunged as much as seventy-seven percent in some cities. And as our Jacob Ward finds, traffic is now on the uptick, but not in the ways you might think.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD (00:21:51): If you had forgotten rush hour gridlock after a year of easy pandemic commutes, traffic now is back, but it`s not the same--\par \par \uc2 MAN (00:22:02): \uc2 Texas Department of Transportation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD (00:22:04): --Something they`re tracking inside Houston`s traffic command center.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG REBORN (\uc2 Houston-Galveston Area Council\uc2  Transportation Director) (00:22:06): Well, in total levels, it`s getting very close, but we think that the pattern of that travel has changed a fair amount.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD (00:22:14): Changed in that the traffic is more spread out, less concentrated to morning and evening rush hours.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG REBORN (00:22:19): With more telecommuting, more people working from home, we think that there are more trips that they`re taking during the day as errands that otherwise would have happened at the beginning or at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD (00:22:30): Cities across the country are seeing this shift with fewer workers heading into the office and fewer children at school. Our Houston station`s traffic expert sees a difference.\par \par \uc2 ANAVID REYES (KPRC Traffic Anchor) (00:22:39): In terms of speed, the gap is closing between pre-pandemic and post-pandemic speed. Today, we`re only traveling about five miles per hour faster than we did back in 2019.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD (00:22:51): While in Washington, DC--\par \par \uc2 ADAM TUSS (NBC4 Washington Transportation Reporter) (00:22:53): Where it used to be crammed and crowded all times of the day, and now in a lot of cases, it`s wide-open spaces.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD (00:23:00): But Los Angeles is basically back to its normal gridlock.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN WINSTON (TODAY in L.A. Traffic Anchor) (00:23:03): Here in Los Angeles, we have some of the most congested freeways in the country, even during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD (00:23:09): Here`s the thing, traffic has been back since the summer, and yet, it`s plateaued. On the Golden Gate Bridge, it`s holding steady at about seventy-five percent of pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 EVERETT LOTT (Washington, DC Transportation Department Interim Director) (00:23:18): Well, as we`ve seen fewer cars on the road, we`ve seen the cars actually travel a little bit faster.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD (00:23:24): In the U.S., that means collisions are down thirty percent but fatality rates are up eight percent. So, how long will these changes last?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG REBORN (00:23:32): We`re actually seeing a fair amount of evidence that a lot of the changes that we`ve seen are going to continue after what we could call a return to normalcy.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD (00:23:42): But to keep this going as more return to the office, experts say it requires commuters returning to public transportation and carpooling. The payoff could be worth it.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG REBORN (00:23:51): There`s a lot of evidence that shows that the less time people spend in their commute, the healthier they are, the happier they are.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD (00:24:00): Jacob Ward, NBC News, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART (00:24:04): When we come back, the gift that brought this woman to tears and the remarkable way she`s helping thousands.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020210427eh3k000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Time To Start Filing Your Taxes\b0\par\par\uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, KRISTEN WELKER, STEPHANIE RUHLE \par \uc2 3,402 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 February 2021\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (07:40:02): We`re back on a Saturday morning with our series, The Price You Pay. And it is that time of year again, believe it or not, tax season officially began on Friday. And this year, you probably have a lot more questions than usual because of the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (07:40:14): That is for sure. NBC`s senior business correspondent Stephanie Ruhle ha some great information to help you file correctly and maybe safe some money in the process. Steph, it`s so great for you. Thanks for joining us. So, you know, most people have spent the past year indoors. So how does that change things for the vast majority of people tax-wise?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE (07:40:32): I`m so happy to see the both of you. I feel like I`m about to disappoint you in the next three minutes. So for so many of us who have been working from home were thinking, hey, could I be deducting, you know, my home office even though my kitchen is now my office? Can I deduct this printer, my internet? Most likely, the answer is no. If you have a full-time job, meaning an employer, you get a W-2 at the end of the year, you cannot deduct any of your home office expenses. You can potentially talk to your employer, maybe they will give you some sort of stipend. But as it relates to your taxes, the answer is no. If, however, you`re a consultant, you work for yourself, you have a small business, you can. But that`s always been the case. But if you thought, hey, working from home, let me deduct my internet, the answer is negative.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (07:41:18): And, Steph, as you`ve been reporting, I think sixteen million people filed a change of address form this past year. So what do you need to know if you`ve moved or if you`ve worked in one state but then moved to another? STEPHANIE RUHLE (07:41:30): This is super important because, as you said, sixteen million people, lots of recent college grads moved home with their parents to save money. People left cities to move to the country. I mean, we were all packing up. Very few of us thought about our taxes. But if you work in one state and live in another, there`s a good chance you owe taxes in both of those states. You need to contact your employer. Now, it depends state by state. Some states have reciprocal relationships. Some states, like New York and New Jersey, offer a credit. This might be the year because of this very reason that you want to hire a tax professional because the last thing you want to do is get a penalty because of this.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (07:42:08): Yeah, or just call up my dad which I do every year.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE (07:42:10): Either one.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (07:42:12): Steph, right, exactly. What can you take advantage of this year that you haven`t been able to in years past?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE (07:42:18): So I would say this is ish good news. Because of the coronavirus pandemic and the CARES Act, people are allowed to take up to one hundred thousand dollars out of their 401ks without facing any sort of penalty. Normally you face a ten percent penalty if you take that money out early, meaning before you`re fifty-nine and a half. But if you were impacted by COVID, you lost a job, you got sick, your spouse got sick, you lost childcare, you can take that money out, not pay a penalty, and you won`t actually have to pay taxes on it for up to three years. If, over the next three years, you decide to put that money back in, you`ll get the taxes back.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (07:42:56): And, Steph, stimulus money and unemployment benefits, how does that affect our taxes?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE (07:43:00): Okay. If you received a stimulus check, you don`t owe taxes on that. Good news. If you qualify and didn`t get a stimulus check, you can take it as a credit on your taxes. And here`s the third one, in 2000-- last year, if you got a stimulus check, that was based on what money you made in 2019. If your situation changed in 2020 and you lost your job and then you qualified, well, file your taxes right now and you will be getting a check.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (07:43:27): Steph, I got to ask you about that--\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE (07:43:27): Oh, and unemployment? Unemployment? If you got unemployment benefits last year, you do owe taxes on them.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (07:43:33): All right. Glad you added that. I have to ask you about tomorrow, Valentine`s Day. What are some tips if you haven`t gotten started getting your loved ones presents yet to get some good deals? STEPHANIE RUHLE (07:43:43): I know we want to love our loved ones. I want you to love your local neighborhood. This is the year to shop local. Think about what a terrible, terrible year this has been for small business. So I`m going to ask you with one day to go, don`t go online, go to your local florist, go to your local card shop, go to your local restaurant. Even if you can`t go out to eat, buy a gift certificate. Plan a hot date in a few months.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (07:44:05): Yeah, Stephanie Ruhle aka Doctor Ruth meets Suze Orman, we appreciate your advice this morning. Have a great Valentine`s Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (07:44:12): Thanks, Steph.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE (07:44:13): Great to see you guys.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (07:44:13): Have a great one. Great tips.\par \par \uc2 (07:44:15): All right. Let`s turn now to Dylan. Dylan, what`s coming up in POPStart?\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER (07:44:19): Well, guys, you know, we love talking about them, Chip and Joanna Gaines. They have a big announcement about their Magnolia Network. We`ll have that after these messages. (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (07:47:32): And we`re back on a Saturday morning with POPStart. Dylan, what`s cooking?\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER (07:47:35): All right, guys. First up, Chip and Joanna Gaines. We just teased it. If you are looking forward to the couple`s new Magnolia Network, unfortunately you are going to have to wait a little bit longer. The Gaines now say it won`t premiere for almost a year, in January of 2022. I guess they were kind of getting used to everything getting pushed back. Good news though. Chip and Joanna will launch a Magnolia app in July and that`s going to have access to the network`s new show as an exclusive content ahead of the premiere. But there`s more. The Fixer Upper family finally unveiled their latest project, their own home. Joanna showed off the new addition to the family`s farmhouse calling it everyone`s happy place, complete with a roaring fire and really cool bookshelves. I`m trying to look there. There`s shiplap? I don`t see I see shiplap. Not sure.\par \par \uc2 (07:48:20): All right. Next up, Dave Chappelle. The comedian shared a ten- minute standup special to his \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . It`s called Redemption Song where he addressed his January coronavirus diagnosis, the Capitol riots and also shared some good news for fans. Chappelle`s show is back on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  again. The streaming giant had pulled the show back in November as Chappelle`s request because of a contract dispute over royalty. Chappelle says the issue is resolved and Chappelle`s show is back on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  now.\par \par \uc2 (07:48:48): And finally, Dwayne Johnson. Just in time for Valentine`s Day, The Rock is showing what a big heart he has. He posted this picture to \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , a pause for the awws. He`s walking down a path and holding his daughter`s hand writing "Every man wants a son but every man needs a daughter. All my girls have become the great equalizers in my life. I`m surrounded by estrogen and wouldn`t have it any other way." He went on to say he hopes his daughter never gets tired of holding his big old dinosaur hands. By the way, he`s a girl dad of three. And that photo already has more than six million likes. And I will say you never get tired of holding your dad`s hand. That`s your POPStart. KRISTEN WELKER (07:49:26): That is for sure. Dylan, that picture melts my heart. It`s so sweet. And you`re a girl dad, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (07:49:32): I was going to say as the dad of daughters, I used to always say I wanted to be surrounded by women. I should have been more specific. But I have-- I got no complaints on those bunch of girls.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER (07:49:39): Take what you can get.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (07:49:40): They are the best.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (07:49:41): Peter`s got a whole lot of roses that he just bought for tomorrow. That`s for sure. All right. Dylan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (07:49:46): Still to come right here, why Justin Timberlake says he failed and why he`s now apologizing specifically to both Britney Spears and Janet Jackson. That`s next. We`re back after this. (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (07:52:04): Still to come right here on TODAY, the latest on former President Trump`s second impeachment trial that could wrap up by the end of today.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (07:52:10): Then, what might just be the most adorable national anthem you have ever heard.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER (07:52:15): And I am going to introduce you to the New York dance city-- New York City dance group braving the elements so that they can keep on dancing. Don`t worry, I don`t try to dance. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:00:02): Good morning. (08:00:03): Trump Trial Twist: New details this morning about a heated call between the former President and the Top Republican in the House who tried to get Mister Trump to stop his supporters during last month`s Capitol riot. And the former President`s lawyers now arguing Mister Trump never knew Vice President Pence was in harm`s way.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL VAN DER VEEN (08:00:23): The answer is, no, at no point was the President informed the vice president was in any danger.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:00:28): But a Republican senator and Trump ally saying that`s not true.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOMMY TUBERVILLE (08:00:32): I said, Mister President, they`ve taken the vice president out. They want me to get off the phone. I got to go.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:00:38): This morning, a new push to hear from witnesses. We`re live with the overnight developments.\par \par \uc2 (08:00:44): "I failed:" Justin Timberlake apologizes to Britney Spears and Janet Jackson for his actions nearly two decades ago. The superstar speaking out after facing heavy backlash following the release of a new Britney Spears documentary. Is it too little too late? (08:01:01): And Staying On Their Toes: Through wind and snow and more, Dylan introduces us to a group of dancers who aren`t letting a little winter weather stop them.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER (08:01:11): Do you ever look at the forecast and think, oh, I don`t want to do this today?\par \par \uc2 ELLA WASSERMAN-SMITH (08:01:15): When you see it go from forty to thirty to twenty, every loss of ten degrees, it`s like, okay, how am I going to add another layer and still be able to move?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:01:24): Why they`re still moving through it all, today, Saturday, February 13, 2021.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison) (08:01:32): Happy Saturday!\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1 (08:01:34): We are at the Holly Performance Academy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2 (08:01:36): From the Holly Theater in Dahlonega, Georgia. And we hope you have a great day.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1 (08:01:38): And we hope you have a great day.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (in unison) (08:01:40): Bonjour, TODAY Show from Paris.\par \par \uc2 SIRI BRANSON (ph) (08:01:44): Hi, Siri Branson. And I`m tuning in to TODAY from Hollandale, Wisconsin. WOMAN #3 (08:01:50): Happy birthday to our son, AJ, who turns one today.\par \par \uc2 MAN (08:01:56): Happy birthday, son. Look at the camera.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:02:01): Happy first birthday. Big one for that little cutie. Good morning, and welcome back to TODAY on this Saturday morning. Dylan, I am looking forward to your dance story. I want to see you get out there in that cold weather with those moves.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER (08:02:13): You always see me out there in the cold weather, but maybe not, maybe not with the moves. We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (08:02:17): I`ll just say better you than me. That`s a pulled hamstring as best I can tell watching that video.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:02:22): Exactly. Well, that will be a good one. We`ll look forward to it. But first, let`s get right to today`s news. Former President Trump`s second impeachment trial set to resume in just a few hours. News of a contentious call overnight between Mister Trump and the top House Republican as rioters stormed the Capitol could lead to a call for witnesses today. NBC`s Kelly O`Donnell is at the White House with more on these developments. Kelly, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`CONNELL (08:02:46): Good morning, Kristen. There is a new push to halt the impeachment trial after new information about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew and what he said during the insurrection. Democratic Senator Sheldon Whitehouse says the way to resolve these news-- new questions is to pause the trial and depose. Alabama senator and Trump ally, Tommy Tuberville, who says he was on the phone with Mister Trump during the crisis and told him the vice president was in danger directly contradicting the Trump defense that claimed he told-- he had not been told that Pence was in any-- any trouble at all. And could GOP leader Kevin McCarthy be called as a witness after new information about an angry call when he spoke to Mister Trump urging him to get his supporters to stop the riot? Mister Trump told McCarthy that, well, I guess these people are more upset about the election than you are, according to lawmakers briefed on that call. Now, senators do have the power to call witnesses and change their trial schedule, but it`s unclear if enough senators will agree to do that. And if not, closing arguments are expected today and then a vote to convict or acquit. Kristen. KRISTEN WELKER (08:03:51): Yeah, we should-- we should know more in just a few hours. And, Kelly, there`s also news about White House deputy press secretary being suspended for a week without pay because of comments he apparently made to a reporter. What can you tell us about this?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`CONNELL (08:04:04): So this is a one-week suspension without pay from TJ Ducklo after news broke that he threatened to, quote, "destroy" a reporter who was working on a story about Ducklo`s personal life, that he was dating a separate unrelated reporter who had covered the Biden campaign. On Inauguration Day, President Biden said he would not tolerate disrespectful conduct from his staff.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (January 20) (08:04:28): If you ever work with me and I hear you treat another colleague with disrespect, talk down to someone, I promise you I will fire you on the spot, on the spot.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`CONNELL (08:04:39): Ducklo, who previously worked for NBC, has apologized and the White House says it considers this one-week suspension serious though it does not meet the standard we just heard the President said just last month. Kristen.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:04:52): All right, Kelly O`Donnell at the White House. Kelly, thank you. And you can see NBC`s coverage of the impeachment trial starting at ten Eastern on MSNBC and NBC News Now. PETER ALEXANDER (08:05:02): Now, the debate over how and when to reopen schools across this country. The CDC issuing some long-awaited guidance about how to get kids back in the classroom safely. NBC`s Meagan Fitzgerald is in Los Angeles with the latest on that. Meagan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MEAGAN FITZGERALD (08:05:17): Peter, good morning. The CDC is saying returning to the classroom can be done safely, and they`re out with new guidelines on how to do it. They`re recommending masks to be worn properly and at all times, social distancing should be enforced, hands should be washed frequently, cleaning and proper ventilation is essential. And finally, the CDC requires contact tracing, isolation and quarantine protocols to be followed inside schools. Some doctors say virtual learning is taking a big toll on kids causing weight gain and leading to mental health issues. But returning to school is an uphill battle in some communities where educators want to be vaccinated before going back. Meanwhile, supply of the vaccine is still an issue. Mass vaccination sites like the one at Dodgers Stadium ran out on Thursday. And now, cities like New Orleans are enforcing strict measures to stop the spread of the virus ahead of Mardi Gras on Tuesday. Parades and traditional parties have been canceled and the city is putting in measures to stop mass gatherings. Health experts are especially concerned about superspreader events because of these more contagious variants that are quickly spreading across the country. Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (08:06:26): All right. Meagan, thank you very much. And NBC News, by the way, wants to help you with everything you need to know to get the COVID vaccine for you or for members of your family. You can learn more about your state`s vaccination plans and eligibility at planyourvaccine.com.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:06:41): All right. And now it`s time for a little weekend morning boost. We could use it after a long week. Check out this extra special performance of the national anthem.\par \par \uc2 (08:06:50): (Excerpt from internet video)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:07:03): Those are some pipes. That`s five-year-old Lucy Walz captivating the audience before the Louisville women`s basketball team against \uc2 Georgia Tech\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (08:07:11): So Lucy`s dad is Louisville head coach, Jeff Walz. He actually taped Lucy singing the anthem a few days earlier. She had no idea they were going to play it on the big screen right before the game. Dad says he might get some grief for this at home, but we think that Lucy did a pretty darn good job. By the way, she`s also good luck for the Cardinals. They won the game.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:07:31): Way to go. She is too adorable. She`s got a future.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER (08:07:36): She`s good.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER (08:07:37): Yeah. We`ll see her before the Super Bowl one day.\par \par \uc2 (08:07:38): All right. Well, still ahead, why Justin Timberlake is apologizing to ex Britney Spears and also Janet Jackson right after this.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \